window.SEARCH_INDEX={"built":null,"pages":[{"f":"discussion.html","t":"Discussion: Instructor's Notes","s":[{"id":"t1","h":"Why a wider margin is worth having","t":"Topic 1 Why a wider margin is worth having The argument differs between hard and soft classification, and in the soft case it is explicitly a trade rather than an improvement. What this page is These are the instructor’s own summaries from the discussion forums, kept in full. They anticipate and extend the Module 2 material — support vector machines, k-nearest neighbours, scaling, validation, and where classification stops and clustering starts — but the reasoning is judgement rather than mechanics, and none of it reduces to a rule you can memorise. The page is deliberately unfinished. Topics get added whenever the instructor posts something that goes beyond the lecture, so what is here is the current set rather than a complete one. Each topic carries a few checks to confirm the point landed; the full multiple-choice banks sit on the Exam Prep page, under Topic 10 for Topics 1 to 4 here and Topic 13 for Topics 5 and 6. Why a wider margin is worth having Take the two settings separately, because the argument is not the same in each. The circled point is the whole trade. Whether accepting it is worth the extra room depends on whether you believe that point is signal or noise. Hard classification A wider margin gives more surety that each point is classified correctly. It also buys tolerance for data that is not quite right: if someone reports income as $50,000 when it is really $49,872, a wider margin makes it more likely that the point still lands on the side it belongs on. Soft classification A wider margin — even one that induces more errors on the data you are fitting to — can give a model that is more certain about how to classify new points, and less influenced by the specific location of points that may, through an incomplete model or randomness in the data, be sitting in the “wrong” place. Against that: those specific points might carry important information, and putting less weight on them can hurt the precision of the model in close cases. Notice that the two arguments are not the same one. In the hard case the gain is robustness to measurement error in the inputs. In the soft case the gain is robustness to the particular sample you happened to draw , and it is explicitly paid for with training errors. Neither argument says a wider margin is always better; the second says outright that it can cost you precision where it matters most. Check yourself — margins A short set. The full bank for this material is on the Exam Prep page. quick check answered In the hard-classification argument, what is the $50,000 / $49,872 example actually about? People rounding their answers when surveyed. Recorded values carry error, so a tight boundary can put points on the wrong side. Income being unbounded, so it needs standardizing. quick check answered The two arguments for a wider margin are not the same argument. What is the difference? Hard: tolerance for input error. Soft: less influence from points that may be in the “wrong” place, paid for with training errors. Hard: tolerance for sampling variability. Soft: tolerance for input error. There is no real difference; the same argument is stated for two audiences. quick check answered What is given up by widening the margin in a soft-classification setting? Speed — wide-margin solutions take longer to compute. Fit to the boundary points, which may carry real information about close cases. The ability to produce a probability estimate at all. discussion write, then compare A colleague widens the margin on a soft classifier, sees training accuracy fall from 96% to 91%, and concludes the change was a mistake. What would you say? Your answer click to type; saves automatically Show a strong answer The training figure is the wrong evidence. Inducing more errors on the data you are fitting to is the expected consequence of widening the margin, not a symptom of failure — the whole argument is that you accept those errors in exchange for a model less influenced by points that may be in the wrong place through randomness or an incomplete model. The right evidence is performance on data used for neither fitting nor choosing. If that improves, the wider margin was correct. If it falls, the colleague may be right for a different reason than the one they gave: those points may have carried genuine information about close cases, and down-weighting them cost precision exactly where it mattered. The core trade What exactly are you giving up when you widen the margin in a soft-classification setting? Show answer Fit to the data in front of you — more points end up misclassified during training — and with it, whatever real information those points carried. If they were genuine signal about how close cases behave, precision on close cases falls."},{"id":"t2","h":"Scaling versus standardization","t":"Topic 2 Scaling versus standardization Both put attributes on a comparable footing. Which one suits depends mostly on whether the data has bounds that mean something. Scaling versus standardization Both put attributes on a comparable footing. Which one suits depends mostly on whether the data has natural bounds. The deciding question is not “which is more sophisticated” but “does this attribute have a floor and a ceiling that mean something?” Scaling is often more appropriate when there are bounds on the data — age, SAT score, a success probability, RGB values — and especially when there is data close to or at those bounds. Standardization is often more appropriate for data that is not as bounded — distance, income — and that is a reasonable fit for a normal distribution. If the data fits a different distribution, standardizing to that distribution instead is also an option. Combination cases exist. Data might sit mostly in a nice bounded distribution with a few cases far away — income is a good example — where it can be best to remove the outliers and then scale the rest. Or mix the two: instead of standardizing the values, take a reasonable range of z-scores, say −3 to +3 standard deviations, and scale that window from “−3 to 3” onto “0 to 1”. Check yourself — scaling and standardization Three checks, then the example the instructor actually asked for. quick check answered Which attribute is the clearest case for scaling rather than standardization? Household income SAT score Distance driven per week quick check answered What is the hybrid technique described at the end of this topic? Scale to 0…1 first, then take z-scores of the scaled values. Take a z-score window such as −3σ to +3σ and map that window onto 0…1. Delete everything beyond ±3σ, then standardize what is left. quick check answered An unbounded attribute clearly fits some distribution other than the normal. What is the option raised? Drop it, since standardization assumes normality. Standardize to that distribution instead. Always fall back to 0…1 scaling, which assumes nothing. discussion write, then compare The instructor invited examples of when you would scale versus standardize. Give one of each from your own domain, and one genuine combination case — say which technique you would use and why. Your answer click to type; saves automatically Show a strong answer Scale: pixel intensity in an image. Hard bounds at 0 and 255, and real data sits at both ends — pure black and pure white are common, so the bounds are informative rather than accidental. Standardize: time on site per session. No ceiling, roughly bell-shaped once you take logs, and the interesting question is how far from typical a session is. Combination: transaction amount. Almost everything falls in a narrow band, but a few wire transfers are thousands of times larger. Either strip those and scale the rest, or clip to a ±3σ window and map that window to 0…1 — the second keeps the extreme cases in the dataset instead of discarding them, which matters if the extremes are the fraud you are hunting. Strong answers say what the technique does to the extremes, not just which one they picked. Exam target Given an unfamiliar attribute, how do you choose? Show answer Ask whether meaningful bounds exist and whether data sits near them — if so, scale. Ask whether it is unbounded and roughly normal — if so, standardize. If it is bounded-ish with a long tail, consider handling the outliers first, or clipping to a z-score window and scaling that."},{"id":"t3","h":"KNN or SVM? What actually decides it","t":"Topic 3 KNN or SVM? What actually decides it Six considerations, none of which is about which algorithm is better in the abstract. KNN or SVM? What actually decides it Six considerations, none of which is about which algorithm is “better”. Consideration What it implies More than two classes KNN is more flexible. SVM methods for multiple classes exist but are not commonly used — for example, for each colour k , run a model separating “colour k” from “not colour k”. Pockets of one class inside another KNN handles meaningful pockets of one colour surrounded by the other; a single separator cannot. Computation at prediction time SVM is easier to compute with. Every new point KNN classifies requires finding the k nearest training points. For a model answering card-transaction queries in real time, that cost matters. Heuristics help, but are beyond this course. Balance in the dataset With many more “yes” points than “no”, KNN leans toward predicting “yes”, and more so as k grows. You have to even the data out first. Probability estimates KNN gives one for free: neighbours in a class divided by k . SVM is harder — probability might be a function of the distance from the classifier to the point. Being deliberately conservative Both can do it. SVM shifts the separator; KNN shifts the threshold. Fixing an unbalanced dataset Two basic approaches. Scale up the rare class — duplicate each point some number of times, or create new points of that colour that are similar to the ones in the dataset. Or scale down the common class — randomly take a small sample of the full set, run your model, and repeat for many different small samples. None of these approaches, or others like them, are perfect and all have potential problems; but an unbalanced dataset is sometimes reality and we have to do the best we can. KNN probability, and its limits With k = 7 and five “yes” neighbours to two “no”, you can quote 5/7. The estimate is not so reliable, especially at the extremes: a point whose seven nearest neighbours are all one colour is probably not really 100% likely to be that colour. Neighbours can also be weighted by how close they are. Conservative classification with KNN Change the threshold rather than the method. With k = 7, instead of calling anything with 4 or more red neighbours red, you might call anything with 2 or more red neighbours red — and require 6 or 7 out of 7 before classifying a point as blue. That is the KNN equivalent of the more-conservative SVM solutions from the lessons. One more, and it is a meta-point rather than a method choice: if the points are very mixed, no model will save you. If the predictors do a bad job of separating the classes even with nonlinear-looking separators, so that everything is thoroughly intermingled, that is a sign to go back and question the underlying assumption that these particular features are good predictors of the classification at all. Check yourself — KNN against SVM Four checks covering computation, imbalance, mixed classes and conservative thresholds. quick check answered Why does the computational difference between KNN and SVM matter in the credit-card example specifically? Because each new point must be classified immediately, and KNN searches the training set for every one. Because KNN takes much longer to train than an SVM. Because the model has to be retrained after every transaction. quick check answered With far more “yes” points than “no” points, what happens to KNN as k grows? The bias toward “yes” gets worse. The bias toward “yes” washes out. Nothing, since KNN corrects for class frequencies. quick check answered Your classes stay thoroughly mixed under every separator you try, linear or not. What is the correct conclusion? You need a more flexible model. These features may simply not be good predictors of this classification. You need more data of the same kind. quick check answered With k = 7, which rule makes KNN deliberately conservative about calling a point blue? Call it blue on 4 or more blue neighbours. Call it red on 2 or more red neighbours, and require 6 or 7 blue neighbours to call it blue. Raise k to 15 and keep the majority rule. discussion write, then compare You are building the real-time card-transaction approval model. Argue for KNN or SVM, addressing computation, class balance and probability estimates. Your answer click to type; saves automatically Show a strong answer The defensible answer is SVM, but only if it engages with what is being given up. Computation. Decisive here. Every KNN prediction requires finding the k nearest training points, and this system answers a continuous stream of queries in real time. An SVM has already condensed the training data into a separator. Class balance. Fraud is rare, so a KNN would lean heavily toward “allow”, worsening as k grows. You would have to rebalance first — and every rebalancing option has problems. Probability. This is the cost of choosing SVM. KNN would hand you an estimate for free; with an SVM you have to derive something from distance to the separator, which is harder — and you do want a graded score here, because the threshold should be conservative in the direction of not declining legitimate transactions. Credit also for noting that KNN would be defensible if the fraud pattern forms pockets that no single separator can enclose. discussion write, then compare Your KNN model on an imbalanced dataset predicts the majority class almost always. Walk through your options and the specific risk each one carries. Your answer click to type; saves automatically Show a strong answer Duplicate the rare points exactly. Simple, but every duplicate is the same point — you have not added information, and a single unrepresentative rare point now has several votes. Create similar new rare points. Better coverage, but you are inventing data, and any structure your invention assumes gets baked into the model as if it were observed. Subsample the common class repeatedly. Keeps all real data honest but throws away information in each run, so results vary between samples — which is why you repeat across many samples. Shift the threshold instead. With k = 7, require far fewer rare-class neighbours to call the rare class. Cheap and reversible, but it does not fix the underlying neighbourhood composition. The correct framing to close on: none of these is perfect and all have potential problems. An unbalanced dataset is sometimes just reality. Diagnosis Your classes are hopelessly mixed whatever separator you try. What is the correct conclusion? Show answer Not that you need a fancier model. It is evidence against the meta-model — the assumption that these features predict this classification. Go back and look for better predictors."},{"id":"t4","h":"Why units silently weight your distances","t":"Topic 4 Why units silently weight your distances The scale each feature happens to be recorded on is acting as a weighting scheme, whether or not anybody chose it. Why units silently weight your distances Each feature — predictor, variable, factor, whichever word your text uses — might naturally sit on a different numeric scale, and those scales are essentially weighting the distances. The worked example Suppose you use “distance from warehouse to customer” and “distance from factory to warehouse” as factors in estimating transportation cost. If one factor is measured in millimetres and the other in kilometres, the first will have numbers roughly 1,000,000 times as large. Differences in that factor will therefore look bigger, and a KNN-type algorithm will weight that factor far more heavily when deciding what counts as “nearest”. The unsettling part is that nothing about the real world changed. The same two quantities, recorded in different units, produce a different notion of “nearest” and therefore a different model. This is why scaling is a cross-cutting concept rather than a detail: it decides what your distance metric actually means. Class discussion — running notes click to type; saves automatically Check yourself — units and distance Two checks, then a translation exercise. quick check answered One factor is recorded in millimetres, the other in kilometres. What does a KNN-type algorithm do? Weights the millimetre factor far more heavily when deciding what is “nearest”. Weights the kilometre factor more heavily, since kilometres are the larger unit. Nothing — it normalises units automatically. quick check answered What is the unsettling implication of that example? A recording convention nobody deliberated over is silently weighting the model. Metric units should be avoided in analytics. Distance-based methods should not be used on physical measurements. discussion write, then compare Explain to a non-technical manager why recording one column in millimetres instead of metres changed which customers the model flags. Then say what you would do about it. Your answer click to type; saves automatically Show a strong answer The explanation should avoid jargon and land the mechanism: the model decides who is “similar” by adding up differences across columns. A column recorded in millimetres produces numbers a million times larger than the same quantity in kilometres, so its differences swamp everything else in that sum. The model is not weighting that factor because it matters — it is weighting it because of the unit somebody chose. The fix is to put every attribute on a comparable footing before computing distances: scale the bounded ones onto a common range, standardize the unbounded roughly-normal ones, and handle the awkward ones with a combination approach. Worth adding that the choice of scaling technique is itself a modelling decision, not a formatting step. Why it bites KNN hardest Which methods are most exposed to this problem? Show answer Anything that adds or compares attributes to compute a distance or an inner product — KNN, SVM, k-means. A decision tree, which splits one attribute at a time, is largely indifferent to units."},{"id":"t5","h":"Choosing on validation, and what it costs","t":"Topic 5 Choosing on validation, and what it costs The model that wins on validation is the right one to pick and its winning score is the wrong one to quote — and the reason is the same in both cases. Two kinds of pattern, in every dataset Any set of data holds real patterns , which reflect the world and will show up again in new data, and random patterns , which are accidents of that particular sample and will not. A score measured on a dataset reflects both, and the number alone doesn’t say which did the work. Instructor’s note If the magnitude of the random patterns is high enough compared with the differences between models’ ability to capture real patterns, then it’s likely the best model wouldn’t be the one chosen. However, it’s still the most-likely one to be best, which is why we choose it. The model with the highest validation performance would be most likely to be best, but that probability it actually is best would still be below 50% if there are several almost-equally-good options. Nothing here is a defect in either dataset. The four models differ barely at all in real quality, so which one comes top is decided mostly by which random patterns it happened to match. Why the winner’s error rises on the test set Instructor’s note When you select the best model based on validation error, you are introducing a selection bias in favour of models that got a random ‘boost’ on the validation set, particularly when there is a close call for the best model. When moving to the test set, that advantage is likely to disappear, resulting in a higher than expected error. Just to make sure it’s clear, there isn’t anything inherently better or worse between the validation data and the test data. The difference is that they contain different random patterns, and each model might be a better fit to one or the other of those random patterns (in addition to having different fits to the real patterns). So, whatever performs best on one of those data sets might not perform best on the other — but there’s no way to tell which is better. Two things follow, and they are easy to state backwards. The winner’s validation score is optimistic, because that score is what selected it. The test score is the unbiased one, not because the test set is better data, but because the selection never touched it. And why not use both sets to choose Instructor’s note The reason we generally don’t use the two data sets combined to pick the model is because then we don’t have an unbiased estimate of the model’s quality. Instead, we’d be combining the validation and test data into one large validation set (to pick the right model) and we wouldn’t have any data left over to get an unbiased quality estimate for whatever model we chose. If you’re in a situation where you really do need to use more data to pick a model and the test data is all you have left, you can use it that way — just understand (and make sure others understand) that the test data will no longer give an unbiased estimate of model quality; it’s likely that performance on the test data will now overstate the model’s quality, and you shouldn’t expect it to do that well on new data. Note what that last paragraph is and isn’t. It isn’t a prohibition — it is a permission with a disclosure attached. The failure mode is not using the test data for selection; it is using it and then quoting the resulting number as though nothing had changed. Check yourself — selection bias A short set. The full bank for this material is on the Exam Prep page, under Topic 13. quick check answered Five candidate models score within a hair of one another on validation, and the noise in the data is large. What is the probability that the top scorer is genuinely the best model? less likely than the others to be best. It remains the single most likely choice — the point is only that 'most likely' can still be under a half.\">Lower than for the other four, because winners are usually flukes Essentially certain — that's what winning on validation means Possibly well below 50%, though still higher than for any other single model Exactly one in five, since the scores are indistinguishable quick check answered Your chosen model scored 91% on validation and 87% on test. What is the most likely explanation? Something went wrong in fitting, and the model should be refitted before use The truth is the average of the two, so quote 89% The test set happened to be a harder sample, so it is a pessimistic estimate Selecting on validation performance made the 91% optimistic; 87% is the unbiased estimate quick check answered A colleague argues that since the test error came out higher, the validation set must have been the better-quality data. What is wrong with that? selected on one of them — not from a quality difference between the datasets.\">Neither set is better; the gap comes from selecting on one of them, not from data quality Nothing, provided the validation set was the larger of the two The claim can be settled by reshuffling the split and comparing again Nothing is wrong except the direction — the test set is the higher-quality data discussion write, then compare Your chosen model scored 91% on validation and 87% on test. A stakeholder asks which number to put in the deck, and whether the 4-point drop means something went wrong. Write the answer. Your answer click to type; saves automatically Show a strong answer Quote the 87%. Nothing went wrong: the model was chosen because it scored best on the validation data, so any random boost it got from that particular sample is built into the 91%. The test set holds different random patterns, that boost doesn’t transfer, and the score comes down. This would happen in a flawlessly executed workflow. The one thing worth adding is that the size of the gap is informative in a rough way. A large gap suggests the candidates were closely matched and the winner owed a lot to luck, which is also a warning that a different split might have crowned a different model. What you cannot do is average the two, or present the 91% as an expectation for new data. The core point Is the validation set or the test set the better data? Show answer Neither. They hold different random patterns, and there is no way to tell which gives the truer ranking. The only asymmetry is which one the selection touched — and that is what makes the untouched one worth protecting."},{"id":"t6","h":"Classification against clustering","t":"Topic 6 Classification against clustering One has a correct answer for every data point and the other has none — and every other difference between them follows from that. Supervised and unsupervised Instructor’s note Classification is a “supervised” model where you know the “correct answer” for each data point. Clustering is an “unsupervised” model where you don’t know any “correct answer”. [Note: In our clustering homework I did give you correct answers for each data point just so you could see how well the algorithm performed, but in practice you usually wouldn’t have that information.] That bracketed aside is the part worth holding on to, because the homework arrangement is the thing most likely to mislead you. Labels sitting in the file do not make a method supervised; what matters is whether the algorithm learns from them. In the homework it doesn’t — they are there so you can grade the result afterwards. Where the categories come from Instructor’s note Here’s another way of making the distinction. In classification, where you do know the “answer” for each data point, the categories are a different variable from the predictors (e.g., “did or didn’t repay loan” is different from the predictors “income” and “credit score”). On the other hand, in clustering, the categories/clusters are defined by the predictors through the model. Change which predictors you feed in and the clusters can change entirely. The classification response cannot, because it was never a function of the predictors in the first place. The phrasing test Instructor’s note Question-wise, a classification question is usually “is each data point this or that?” or “is it, or isn’t it?” while a clustering question is often more like “which groups of these data points are similar to each other?” This is the fastest of the three tests to apply in an exam, because it works on a sentence rather than on a dataset. If the question names the categories, they exist independently and it is classification — however many of them there are. If it asks which points resemble each other, no categories have been named yet and the model will have to invent them. Check yourself — supervised or not A short set. The full bank for this material is on the Exam Prep page, under Topic 13. quick check answered The clustering homework gives you the true label for every data point. Does that make it a classification problem? No, because clustering cannot run on labelled data at all Yes, because a labelled dataset is by definition a classification dataset Yes, it makes it a hybrid of the two, which is what semi-supervised means No — the labels are used to check the result, not to build it quick check answered Which statement describes the relationship between categories and predictors correctly? In classification the categories are a separate variable; in clustering they are defined by the predictors through the model In classification the categories are produced by the model; in clustering they are recorded in the data In both cases the categories are just another predictor column In classification the predictors must be quantitative; in clustering they must be categorical quick check answered Which question is a clustering question rather than a classification question? Is this email real or spam? Which of our customers behave similarly to one another? Will this applicant repay the loan in full? Which species does this bone belong to? discussion write, then compare A colleague runs a clustering algorithm, inspects the groups, names them, and reports “92% accuracy”. What has gone wrong, and what could they legitimately report instead? Your answer click to type; saves automatically Show a strong answer Accuracy needs a correct answer to compare against, and clustering has none. The clusters were defined by the predictors through the model, and the names were attached afterwards by the same person, so the 92% is the model being scored against itself. The homework case is the one exception, and only because the labels were observed independently and withheld from the algorithm. What they can report instead is how well separated and how stable the groups are, whether the grouping survives a change of attributes or of the number of clusters, and whether the clusters correspond to anything already known about the customers. If reliable labels do exist, the honest move is to stop clustering and run a classification model against them. The core point Does having labels in the file make a method supervised? Show answer No — only using them to build the model does. Labels used purely to score the result afterwards leave the method unsupervised, which is exactly the arrangement in the clustering homework."},{"id":"t7","h":"Which outliers are hard to find, and which are hard to judge","t":"Topic 7 Which outliers are hard to find, and which are hard to judge Detection difficulty and handling difficulty are two different rankings, and the types that top each one are not the same. Finding them is not the same problem as deciding about them The three types are usually introduced as a difficulty ladder, which invites the assumption that one type is hardest at everything. It is not. Sort them once by how hard they are to detect and once by how hard they are to adjudicate , and the two orderings disagree. The circle in the first two panels is a point you can put a finger on. In the third there is nothing to circle — which is exactly the difficulty. Point outliers — easiest to detect A single observation that sits far from everything else. It can be identified by looking at the distance of a point from all the others — for example, calculating the distance between each point and the nearest other point to it. A point with an unusually large nearest-neighbour distance is a candidate. Contextual outliers — often detectable the same way A value that is unremarkable in general but wrong for its surroundings. Potential contextual outliers can often be found by the same distance reasoning, once “nearest” is taken within the relevant context. Detection is not usually the hard part here. Collective outliers — hardest to detect A set of points that is anomalous as a group while no individual member looks unusual. These are tougher because we might know something is missing, but it is hard to know exactly what or where. There is no single observation to flag, so the distance test that works on the other two has nothing to point at. The other ranking: which are hardest to deal with Contextual outliers are the difficult ones once found. It is often quite hard to work out whether such a point is bad data, data that is correct but should not be used in your model, or data that is correct and should be used. Collective outliers are hard to see ; contextual outliers are hard to rule on . An exam answer that names only one type as “the hardest” without saying hardest at what has answered half the question. Once you have decided a point is an outlier Finding it settles nothing by itself. Every flagged point then needs one of three verdicts, and the first two look identical in the data: Bad or incorrect data — a genuine error. Throw it out. Good, correct data that is not relevant to the question at hand — real, but not about what you are modelling. It can be thrown out. Good, correct data that is relevant to the question — and therefore should be retained, however inconvenient it is. Note that the second and third verdicts both concern correct data, so no test of data quality can separate them. Only knowing what question the model is answering does. This is why deleting everything a detector flags is not a defensible policy: it silently applies verdict 1 to points that belong in category 3. Check yourself — kinds of outlier Three checks. The full bank for this material is on the Exam Prep page. quick check answered Which type is hardest to detect , and why? Point outliers, because one bad value can be hidden among thousands. Contextual outliers, because context is subjective. Collective outliers, because no individual point looks unusual — only the group does. quick check answered Which type is hardest to deal with once identified? Contextual outliers. Collective outliers. Point outliers. quick check answered Two of the three verdicts on a flagged point concern data that is correct. What separates them? How extreme the value is. Whether the point is relevant to the question being modelled. How large the dataset is. discussion write, then compare A teammate builds a cleaning step that drops every point whose nearest-neighbour distance exceeds a fixed threshold, and calls the data “outlier-free”. Say what is wrong with both halves of that claim. Your answer click to type; saves automatically Show a strong answer “Outlier-free” is not established. A nearest-neighbour threshold catches point outliers and, applied within context, many contextual ones. It cannot catch collective outliers at all: every member of an anomalous group can sit comfortably close to its neighbours, so the rule finds nothing while the group as a whole is wrong. The data is free of the outliers this test can see, which is a much weaker claim. “Cleaning” is the bigger problem. Dropping a flagged point applies the verdict “bad data” to every one of them. But a flagged point may be correct data that is irrelevant to the question — fine to drop — or correct data that is relevant, which should be retained. Those last two look the same in the data, so no automatic threshold can tell them apart; only knowing what the model is for does. The rule is fine as a detector and indefensible as a decision. Two rankings, not one Which type is hardest, and hardest at what? Show answer Collective outliers are hardest to detect — nothing individual looks wrong, so you may know something is missing without knowing what or where. Contextual outliers are hardest to deal with — separating bad data from correct-but-irrelevant data from correct-and-relevant data is a judgement the data cannot make for you."},{"id":"t8","h":"What C and T actually do in a CUSUM","t":"Topic 8 What C and T actually do in a CUSUM One parameter filters noise out of each observation; the other sets how much surviving evidence you need before calling a change. They are not interchangeable. Notation used here Detecting an increase , the running total is S t = max(0, S t−1 + (x t − μ − C)) , and a change is declared when S t ≥ T . For a decrease , reverse the subtraction: S t = max(0, S t−1 + (μ − x t − C)) . Everything below is written for the increase case; the argument reverses unchanged for decreases. Outside this course you will meet C under the names reference value, allowance or slack (often written k ), and T as the threshold or decision interval (often h ). T — how much evidence before you call it When T is higher it takes longer for CUSUM to detect a change, because the running total has to get larger before it trips. In exchange, a higher T makes it less likely that the model declares a change that has not happened — that the total is only high through randomness. Lower T detects changes faster and falsely declares changes more often. So T is the parameter governing how sensitive the model is to changes . The wandering section before the dashed line is the problem. It is random variation, and with T set low enough some of it will reach the line on its own. C — how much variation you agree to ignore When adding to the running total, C is subtracted from the deviation. So even when an observation comes in above the mean, the total will not increase unless the observation is more than C above the mean. C removes the effect of ordinary variability. Lots of natural random variation in the data → a higher C helps you ignore the occasions when randomness alone pushes an observation above the mean. Little variability → C can be lower, because randomness on its own will not push observations very far above the mean. C at or near zero, with noisy data → several random above-mean observations in a row are enough to run the sum past the threshold, and the model reports a change that never happened. The question everyone asks: why not set C = 0 and raise T instead? If C only absorbs noise, and a large T also stops noise from triggering an alarm, it looks as though one parameter would do. It will not, and the reason is worth being able to reproduce. The argument Observations will sometimes be randomly above the mean for several periods in a row — not unlikely at all when you are taking many observations. With C = 0, every one of those excursions is added to the total in full, and they accumulate. To avoid declaring a change on that basis, you would have to set T high. And now a real change — a fast one, where the data suddenly sits far above the mean — takes longer to detect, because T has been raised artificially to cope with noise. Use C to handle the variability instead, and T can stay low, so the genuine fast change is caught sooner. The two parameters act at different stages, which is why one cannot stand in for the other. C filters each observation before it enters the total, so ordinary wobble never accumulates at all. T sets the bar on the evidence that survives the filter. Collapse them into T alone and you are asking a single number to both tolerate noise and measure evidence — and it can only do that by being large, which is precisely what costs you detection speed. Do not update the mean as new data arrives A second common question: should μ be recalculated as each new observation comes in? No. To detect whether a change has occurred you have to compare against the old mean. Updating μ as new data arrives masks exactly the change you are looking for. The salary example Take average yearly salary data from 1970 to the present. Salaries have risen a great deal — there has plainly been a change. But if μ is recalculated every time a new data point arrives, μ rises along with the data, each new observation sits close to the freshly updated mean, the running total never builds, and the method reports no change at all. The detector has been tuned to agree with whatever is happening. Worth separating from a legitimate neighbour: a baseline with known, predictable structure — strong seasonality, say — does need handling, because a chart run against a flat μ will accumulate every summer and alarm on the calendar. The fix there is to remove the known pattern before monitoring, or to use a seasonally appropriate baseline. That is not the same as letting μ drift toward the data, which is what destroys the test. Where this gets used CUSUM is often helpful for tracking the performance of models you have put into production. Model performance usually does change over time, because the situation or the underlying data changes, and a sustained drop is the signal that maintenance is due: retrain on newer data, or reconsider whether a different type of model now suits the data better. This is a common use in practice — residuals or an accuracy metric are monitored per period, and a sustained shift triggers the retraining pipeline rather than a fixed calendar schedule. The same machinery is used in public-health outbreak surveillance, in manufacturing quality control, and in tracking defect rates in software builds. Applications fall into rough categories, and which one you are in should set the parameters: Early detection of big long-term changes — the change is large and you want warning as early as possible. Detecting very short-term changes — the change may not persist, so a slow detector misses it entirely. Distinguishing small changes from random noise — the hardest case, and the one where the C-versus-T argument matters most. There are cases in between, but naming the category first is what makes a parameter choice defensible rather than arbitrary. Conventions you will meet outside this course Standard practice in statistical process control expresses both parameters in standard deviations and gives starting values: set C to roughly half the shift you want to catch — C ≈ 0.5σ if a 1σ shift is what matters — and T to about 4σ or 5σ, then tune. The trade is usually quantified as average run length : how many observations pass before an alarm. ARL with no change present is the false-alarm rate; ARL once a change has occurred is the detection delay. Raising T improves the first and worsens the second, which is the same trade described above with a number attached. Two further practical notes: the sum is normally reset after an alarm has been investigated, so old evidence does not carry into the next decision; and CUSUM earns its keep on small sustained shifts — for a very large sudden jump, a simple “is this observation extreme?” chart signals just as fast. Check yourself — CUSUM parameters Four checks. The full bank for this material is on the Exam Prep page. quick check answered What happens when you raise T? Changes are detected sooner, with more false alarms. Changes take longer to detect, but false alarms become less likely. The mean is re-estimated from more data, so both improve. quick check answered The data you are monitoring is very noisy. What should you do to C, and why? Raise it, so that randomly high observations do not add to the running total. Lower it, so the model is more sensitive to the noise. Set it to zero and handle everything with T. quick check answered What is lost by setting C = 0 and using a large T to cope with noisy data? Nothing — false alarms are controlled either way, so the configurations are equivalent. Detection speed on a genuine fast change, because T was inflated to absorb noise. The ability to estimate the mean at all. quick check answered Why is the salary example a warning against updating μ as data arrives? Because a long-run trend violates the assumptions of any statistical test. Because μ rises along with the data, so an unmistakable long-run change is never detected. Because salary data must be inflation-adjusted before any CUSUM is valid. discussion write, then compare You are monitoring a fraud model in production. Weekly precision is noisy, and the failure you fear is a slow drift that costs money for months before anyone notices. A colleague proposes C = 0 with a high T, and recomputing μ each week from the last twelve weeks so the baseline “stays current”. Respond to both halves. Your answer click to type; saves automatically Show a strong answer On C = 0 with a high T: it will control false alarms, so it is not absurd — but it is the wrong trade here. With weekly precision noisy, runs of random above-mean weeks will accumulate in full, so T has to be set high to survive them. That inflated T then has to be climbed by the real drift as well, and since the drift is slow the total builds slowly, so detection arrives months late — the exact failure we are trying to avoid. Use C to absorb the weekly noise, set it around half the drop that would actually matter to the business, and T can then be low enough to catch the drift while it is still small. On the rolling twelve-week μ: this does not trade anything off, it disables the detector. A slow drift is precisely a change the rolling mean will follow: each week's precision sits close to a baseline computed from the recently degraded weeks, the deviation stays near zero, the total never builds, and the chart certifies that nothing is happening while performance decays. This is the salary case — an enormous real change made invisible by a mean that chases the data. μ should be fixed at the performance level established when the model was validated, because that is the thing we want to know we have departed from. The distinction worth making explicit: if weekly precision has genuine known seasonality — holiday fraud patterns, say — that is a real objection to a flat baseline, and the answer is to remove the known seasonal pattern or use a season-appropriate baseline. It is not to let μ follow whatever the data happens to be doing. Two knobs, two jobs In one line each: what does C do, and what does T do? Show answer C is subtracted from every deviation, so ordinary variation never enters the running total — it sets how much noise you agree to ignore. T is the level the total must reach before you declare a change — it sets how much accumulated evidence you demand, and so how sensitive the detector is. C filters; T judges. And μ stays fixed, or there is nothing to detect a departure from."},{"id":"ex","h":"Worked examples for every topic","t":"Reference Worked examples for every topic Each topic on this page is a judgement call, and judgement is easier to reproduce from cases than from definitions. These are concrete instances of each one, including the cases that cut the other way. How to use this Read a case, decide before reading the verdict, and pay particular attention to the ones marked cuts the other way — they are the ones that separate an answer that reproduces a rule from an answer that understands it. Sources beyond the instructor notes are general reference material, not lecture content. Topic 1 — Margin width Self-reported income on a loan application. An applicant writes “$50,000” when the true figure is $49,872. A boundary drawn tightly through the training points may put that applicant on the wrong side purely because of the rounding. A wider margin absorbs the displacement. Hard-classification argument: robustness to measurement error. A lab assay with ±2% instrument error. If the separating boundary sits inside the instrument's own error band, the classification is partly a readout of the instrument's noise. The margin should exceed the known measurement error before the decision means anything. A spam filter trained on one month of mail. Borderline messages reflect that particular month. Widening the margin reduces the influence of those specific points, at the price of training errors. Soft-classification argument: robustness to the sample you drew. Cuts the other way: a filter separating aggressive marketing from actual spam. The borderline cases are the commercially important ones, and they may carry real information about where the line belongs. Widening the margin here can cost precision exactly where the model earns its money. Topic 2 — Scaling against standardization Scale: exam score out of 100, RGB channel 0–255, humidity as a percentage, battery charge, an estimated probability, age. All have a real floor and ceiling, and real data sits at them. Standardize: household income, commute distance, transaction value, wind speed. No natural ceiling, and often a reasonable fit to a normal distribution. A different distribution? Standardize to that one instead. Combination case — income with a handful of extreme earners. Scaling to 0–1 against a billionaire crushes everyone else into the bottom sliver of the range. Remove the far outliers and scale the rest, or clip at ±3 standard deviations and map that window onto 0–1. Cuts the other way: columns that are already indicator variables taking values 0 and 1. They are on a common footing already; standardizing them rescales each by how rare its category is, which is a weighting decision you probably did not intend to make. Topic 3 — KNN against SVM Ten classes, modest data, no time pressure — handwritten digit recognition on a research dataset. KNN handles many classes without any extra machinery. Binary decision, millions of rows, an answer owed in milliseconds — card authorisation at the point of sale. Training an SVM is expensive but happens once; prediction is a single arithmetic comparison. KNN inverts that cost profile, since every prediction searches the training data. Severe imbalance — fraud at 0.2% of transactions. A new point's nearest neighbours are almost all legitimate whatever the truth, so the raw vote is near-useless without weighting or resampling. No balancing approach is perfect; say so. Cuts the other way: if you need a probability rather than a label, note that the KNN fraction is coarse — with k = 5 the only available answers are 0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.8 and 1 — and least reliable at exactly the extremes where a confident answer would matter most. Topic 4 — Units and distance The headline case: one feature recorded in millimetres, another in kilometres. The millimetre numbers are around a million times larger, so differences in that feature swamp the distance calculation and the model is effectively deciding on one feature. The quiet everyday case: age in years (roughly 18–80) alongside annual income in dollars (roughly 20,000–200,000). Income differences are thousands of times larger. Nothing errors out; the model simply ignores age. House prices: bedrooms (1–6) against floor area in square feet (500–5,000). Choosing to record area in square metres instead changes which neighbours are “nearest”, and so changes the prediction. Scope: this is not a KNN quirk. Anything deciding by distance inherits it — k-means, SVMs with a distance-based kernel, PCA, anomaly scores built on nearest-neighbour distance. Choosing units is choosing weights. Topic 5 — Validation bias Thirty near-tied candidates. The winner scores 94% on validation and 91% on test. Nothing broke: you selected the model that caught a favourable roll on that particular set, and the favour did not travel. The public-leaderboard effect. In competitions where a public score is computed on one slice and the final standing on another, rankings routinely rearrange at the reveal. That is this phenomenon at scale, with a paper trail. Slow leakage. Fifty rounds of “tweak, re-score on validation, keep if better” is selection against that set even without a formal model comparison. The validation figure stops being an estimate long before anyone notices. Cuts the other way: the test set is not better data. It contains its own random patterns. Its value comes entirely from having been untouched while you were choosing — spend it and it becomes another validation set. Topic 6 — Classification against clustering Classification: is this email spam; will this customer churn within 90 days; which digit is this. A correct answer exists for each data point, and someone can in principle check it. Clustering: what natural groupings exist among our customers. There is no correct answer per customer, so the output is a proposal about structure, not a set of facts about individuals. The phrasing test: “which of these categories does it belong to” supplies the categories — supervised. “What categories are there” asks for them to be discovered — unsupervised. Cuts the other way: naming your clusters afterwards (“bargain hunters”, “loyalists”) does not convert them into labels. The names are your interpretation of the grouping, and the grouping would change with the distance measure, the scaling, or k. Claims your method cannot support are exactly what this topic is about. Topic 7 — Kinds of outlier Point: a single $40,000 charge on a card that normally sees $80 purchases. A sensor reporting 300° in a system that runs between 60° and 80°. Easy to detect — the nearest-neighbour distance is enormous. Contextual: 20°C is a pleasant summer reading and a remarkable one on Christmas Day in a cold climate; the value is ordinary, its position is not. A $5,000 transaction is routine on a corporate account and striking on a personal one. Detectable by the same distance reasoning applied within context. Collective: fifty failed logins from different addresses against one account — each attempt is unremarkable, the set is an attack. Every car on a motorway changing to the left lane at the same moment; lane changes are ordinary, simultaneity is not. A stretch of an ECG holding a value that is itself perfectly normal for far too long. Nothing to circle, which is the difficulty. The three verdicts, worked on one flagged transaction: if the amount was mis-keyed by a data-entry error, it is bad data — drop it. If it is a genuine corporate bulk purchase sitting in a model of consumer spending, it is correct but irrelevant — it can be dropped. If it is a genuine fraudulent charge in a model built to catch fraud, it is correct and relevant — and dropping it removes the very thing you are modelling. Cuts the other way: note that the last two cases are indistinguishable by any test of the data itself. Only the question the model answers separates them. Topic 8 — CUSUM parameters Small change against noise — weekly precision of a deployed fraud model. Week-to-week variation is large relative to the drift you fear, so C does the heavy lifting: set it near half the drop that would actually matter, then keep T low enough to catch the drift while it is still small. Early detection of a large long-term change — daily emergency-department visits during outbreak surveillance. A missed outbreak costs far more than an investigated false alarm, so T is set low and the false-alarm rate is accepted as the price. Short-lived changes — a transient equipment fault that lasts hours. A slow detector does not report it late; it never reports it at all, because the total decays back to zero before reaching T. Manufacturing and software — tablet weight or coating thickness on a production line, defect counts per software build. Both are the classic small-sustained-shift case that CUSUM was designed for. Cuts the other way: for a very large sudden jump, CUSUM offers little over simply asking whether the latest observation is extreme. Its advantage is on small shifts that accumulate, which is also why the C-against-T argument matters most there. discussion the pattern across all eight Every topic above has a “cuts the other way” case. What do those cases have in common, and what does that suggest about how these questions get examined? Your answer click to type; saves automatically Show a strong answer Each counter-case is the point where the rule stops being a rule and turns back into a question about the data or the purpose. Widening the margin is right until the boundary points are the informative ones. Standardizing is right until the column was already on a common footing. The test set is cleaner only until you spend it. Dropping outliers is right until the outlier is the phenomenon. CUSUM beats a simple extremeness check only while the shift is small. What they share is that the deciding fact is never in the technique — it is in what the data is like and what question is being asked. That is a strong hint about examination: a question that supplies a context is usually testing whether you noticed which side of the counter-case it falls on, and an answer that states the general rule without engaging with the supplied context will miss the mark even when the rule is stated correctly. The habit to build What should you ask before applying any rule on this page? Show answer Two things: what is this data like — bounded, noisy, imbalanced, measured with error — and what question is the model answering? Every topic here resolves from one or the other, and never from the technique alone."},{"id":"sec-10","h":"What these have in common","t":"What these have in common The through-line across the discussion topics so far Topic 1 Margin width is a bet about how much you trust the position of the points nearest the boundary. Topic 2 Scaling or standardizing is a bet about whether the bounds of an attribute mean anything. Topic 3 KNN against SVM is a bet about the shape of the classes, the balance of the data and how fast you owe an answer. Topic 4 Units decide what “nearest” means, so an arbitrary recording choice becomes a modelling choice. Topic 5 The score you selected with is a bet you already cashed, which is why it can’t also serve as the estimate. Topic 6 Whether a correct answer exists at all decides which family of method you are even in. Topic 7 Hardest to find and hardest to judge are different rankings, and finding an outlier settles nothing on its own. Topic 8 C decides how much noise you ignore, T decides how much evidence you need, and a mean that chases the data detects nothing. Things worth noticing None of these has a default answer. Every one resolves from a property of the data — measurement error, bounds, balance, throughput. Anyone who answers without asking about the data is guessing. Two of them are really the same problem. Scaling and the units example are one issue seen from opposite ends: units weight distances, and scaling is how you take that weighting back under control. The honest verdicts are part of the material. No balancing approach is perfect. The KNN probability estimate is unreliable at the extremes. A wider margin can cost precision. Reproducing the caveat is usually worth as much as reproducing the technique. One finding overrides the method choice entirely. If the classes stay mixed under every separator, the problem is the predictors, not the model. Two of them are really about honesty rather than technique. Topic 5 is a rule about which number you are allowed to quote, and Topic 6 is a rule about which claims your method can support at all. Neither changes a line of code, and both change what you are entitled to say. Judgement and detection are separable skills. Topic 7 makes the point explicitly — the type that is hardest to find is not the type that is hardest to rule on — but it applies further back too. Spotting that a validation score is inflated is easy; deciding what to report instead is not. Parameters encode the same trades as model choices. Topic 8 is Topic 1 in a different costume: C buys tolerance for noise the way a wide margin buys tolerance for misplaced points, and both are paid for in sensitivity to real signal. Anyone asking for faster detection and fewer false alarms at once is asking for the trade to go away. This list is not closed. Every topic here arrived because the instructor thought the lecture version was too clean. More will arrive on the same terms, and the pattern of what gets singled out — judgement calls the lectures state as rules — is itself a hint about what gets examined. Take these to the exam bank Once this page makes sense, two banks on the Exam Prep page test it. Topic 10 covers margins, scaling, method choice, imbalance, probability estimates and conservative thresholds — the material in Topics 1 to 4 here. Topic 13 covers selection bias, the role of the test set, and classification against clustering — the material in Topics 5 and 6. Outlier types covers point, contextual and collective outliers and the three verdicts on a flagged point — Topic 7 here. CUSUM parameters covers C, T, and why the mean stays fixed — Topic 8 here. New discussion topics get a matching bank there as they are added. Discussion — my own summary click to type; saves automatically Written by an AI model. Not reviewed by any instructor or institution — verify anything you rely on. The topics on this page follow instructor notes posted to the class discussion forums, and the page is added to as more are posted; the worked examples, figures, checks and feedback around them are written for these notes and are not lecture content."}]},{"f":"exam-prep.html","t":"Exam practice","s":[{"id":"validation-test","h":"Validation vs. test data","t":"Validation vs. test data Why the winner on validation isn't reliably the winner overall. A model comes out on top on the validation set. Why might it still not be the genuinely best model? Every dataset carries its own random patterns alongside the real ones. A model can look best simply because it happens to fit the validation set's noise well. If those random effects are large relative to the real differences in model quality, the true best model may not be the one that wins. Several candidate models perform almost equally well. What's the chance the top validation performer is actually the best of them? With several near-ties, the probability that any one of them is truly best is spread thin â the leader's share can fall below 50%. It remains the highest-probability choice, which is exactly why we select it. Being the best bet is not the same as being certain. The model you selected does worse on the test set than it did on validation. What's the standard explanation? Picking the validation winner tilts your choice toward models that got lucky on that particular set, especially in a close call. On new data that luck disappears, so error comes in higher than the validation number led you to expect. Is the validation set or the test set the more trustworthy measure of a model's quality? There's nothing intrinsically better about either set. Each contains different random patterns, and a given model may fit one set's noise better than the other's. Whatever wins on one may lose on the other, and there's no way to tell from the data which verdict is closer to the truth. The test set's value comes from being untouched during selection, not from being better data. Why not merge validation and test data into one large set for choosing among models? Merging gives you a bigger validation set and no test set. You'd select a model but have no untouched data left to estimate how good it actually is â and the estimate from the data you selected on is biased upward. You genuinely need more data to make the model choice, and the test set is all you have left. Describe what you would do and what you'd have to tell your team. Using the test data for selection is allowed â but you lose the unbiased quality estimate, and everyone relying on the result needs to know that. Points to hit: What you gain: more data for the selection decision, which reduces the chance of picking a model that only looked good on a small sample. What you lose: no held-out data remains. Performance measured on data you selected on will most likely overstate the model's quality. What to communicate: state plainly that the reported figure is not an unbiased estimate and that performance on genuinely new data should be expected to be worse. The failure here isn't using the data this way â it's letting others read the number as if it were clean. A colleague argues: \"Our model scored 94% on validation, so it'll score about 94% in production.\" Write your response. The 94% is the score of the model that won a competition judged on that specific data. Two things inflate it: Selection bias. You didn't pick a model at random â you picked the one that scored highest, which favors whichever model best fit the validation set's random patterns as well as its real ones. The boost doesn't transfer. New data has different random patterns, so the portion of that 94% attributable to luck won't repeat. The honest estimate is the test set score â untouched during selection, so no model earned an advantage on it. Production performance should be expected at or below that, not at the validation number. Validation data is inherently lower quality than test data. Random patterns in the validation set can favor one model, on top of how well it fits the real patterns. Validation sets are always too small to be meaningful. Any model that wins on validation has overfit the training data by definition. Effectively certain â that's the point of validation. Exactly 50%, by symmetry. Possibly under 50%, though it's still the most likely single candidate and so still the right pick. Unknowable, so the choice should be made at random. Selection bias â you chose the model that got a random boost on validation, and that boost doesn't carry over. The test set is drawn from a different population than the validation set. The model continued learning from the validation data. Test sets are conventionally harder. Validation â it's used first, while the model is still being refined. Test â it's held back until the end, which makes it cleaner data. Neither is inherently better; they just contain different random patterns. Whichever is larger. Merging datasets is statistically invalid. You'd have nothing held back to produce an unbiased estimate of the chosen model's quality. Test data should never be examined under any circumstances. It would cause the model to overfit the training data."},{"id":"classification-clustering","h":"Classification vs. clustering","t":"Classification vs. clustering Supervised and unsupervised, and how to tell which question you're being asked. What is the core distinction between classification and clustering? Classification is supervised: each training point comes with its known correct label. Clustering is unsupervised: you have no correct answers, and the algorithm groups points by similarity alone. How do the categories relate to the predictors in each approach? In classification, the label is a different variable entirely â \"repaid the loan or not\" is separate from predictors like income and credit score. In clustering there is no separate label variable: the clusters come out of the predictors via the model itself. Which is phrased as a clustering question? Classification questions sound like \"is this point this or that?\" or \"is it, or isn't it?\" â they presuppose defined categories. Clustering questions sound like \"which of these points are similar to each other?\" â the groups aren't known in advance. A clustering homework assignment hands you the correct label for every data point. What does that tell you about clustering in practice? The labels are a teaching device: they let you check the algorithm's output against the truth. Real clustering problems generally arrive without any correct answer to check against â which is what makes evaluating a clustering result harder than evaluating a classifier. For each, say whether it's classification or clustering and why: (a) flagging transactions as fraudulent using past confirmed fraud cases, (b) segmenting a customer base into groups for marketing, (c) predicting loan repayment from income and credit score. (a) Classification. Past confirmed cases give you the correct answer for each training point, and \"fraudulent or not\" is a variable separate from the transaction's features. The question is \"is it, or isn't it?\" (b) Clustering. No correct segment exists for any customer beforehand. The groups emerge from the predictors through the model. The question is \"which customers resemble each other?\" (c) Classification. Repayment history is a known outcome, and it's a different variable from the predictors income and credit score. The useful test: ask whether the categories exist independently of the predictors, or whether the model has to invent them. Classification is supervised â the correct answer is known for each point. Clustering is unsupervised â no correct answer is known. Classification handles two categories; clustering handles three or more. Classification uses linear boundaries; clustering uses nonlinear ones. Classification works on numeric predictors; clustering works on categorical ones. In both, the categories are a separate variable from the predictors. In both, the categories are derived from the predictors. In classification the category is a separate variable from the predictors; in clustering the clusters are defined by the predictors through the model. In classification the category is derived from the predictors; in clustering it is supplied externally. Will this applicant repay the loan? Which of these customers resemble each other? Is this transaction fraudulent? Does this email belong in spam? Clustering requires labels; they're just usually implicit. The labels are there so you can see how the algorithm performed â in practice you usually wouldn't have them. The assignment is really a classification problem in disguise. Labels make clustering supervised whenever they're present."},{"id":"margins","h":"Margins in SVM","t":"Margins in SVM What a wider margin buys you, and what it costs. In hard classification, why prefer a wider margin? A wider margin gives more surety about each classification, and it absorbs data that isn't quite right. If someone reports income as $50,000 when the true figure is $49,872, a wide margin makes it more likely the point still lands on the correct side. Someone reports their income as $50,000 when it's really $49,872. What does this example illustrate about margin width? Recorded data is often approximate. Under a narrow margin, small reporting errors can push points across the boundary. A wider margin keeps points correctly classified despite that imprecision. In soft classification, a wider margin may produce more errors on the data you're fitting. What's the argument for accepting that? Widening the margin de-emphasizes the exact position of individual points. Since some of those points sit where they do because of randomness or because the model is incomplete, being less influenced by them yields a model more confident about classifying new data. What's the cost of down-weighting those individual points? Not every awkwardly-placed point is noise â some are genuine signal about where the boundary belongs. Discounting them costs you precision exactly where decisions are tightest. Lay out the full trade-off in choosing margin width for a soft-classification problem. When would you lean wide, and when narrow? Lean wide when: your measurements are imprecise or self-reported; you suspect the model is missing predictors, so odd points are likely artifacts rather than signal; you care most about confident classification of new points; the data is noisy enough that individual positions shouldn't be trusted. Lean narrow when: the data is accurately measured; boundary-adjacent points are believed to carry real information about where the division belongs; precision in close cases is what the decision depends on. The underlying tension: a wider margin trades fit on the data you have for robustness on data you don't. Accepting more errors on the training data buys a model less hostage to any particular point's location. The judgment call is whether awkward points are noise (widen) or signal (narrow) â and that's a question about your data and your model, not one the algorithm can settle for you. It reduces the number of support vectors needed. It gives more confidence points are classified correctly and tolerates imprecision in the recorded data. It guarantees zero training error. It makes the model train faster. That income should never be used as a predictor. That measurement error means a point near a tight boundary may be classified wrongly â a wider margin absorbs the imprecision. That the data should be rounded before modeling. That the model needs more training data. Training errors don't matter in soft classification. The resulting model is more certain about new points and less swayed by individual points that may sit in the 'wrong' place due to randomness or an incomplete model. It always improves accuracy on the test set. More errors mean the model is more conservative by construction. The model becomes computationally expensive. The margin can no longer be computed. Those points may carry real information, so giving them less weight can hurt precision in close cases. The model can only handle two classes."},{"id":"scaling","h":"Scaling vs. standardization","t":"Scaling vs. standardization Which transformation suits which kind of data. When is scaling generally the more appropriate choice? Scaling suits bounded data. Age, SAT scores, probabilities and RGB values all have known floors and ceilings, and the case is strongest when observations sit close to those limits. When is standardization generally more appropriate? Standardization suits data without hard bounds that's a reasonable fit for a normal distribution, such as distance or income. If the data fits some other distribution, standardizing to that distribution instead is an option. Income is mostly well-behaved but has a handful of extreme values far from the rest. What's a sensible approach? Combination cases are common. When data is mostly in a nice bounded distribution but a few points sit far away, removing those outliers and scaling the rest often works better than forcing one transformation on everything. What does it mean to take z-scores from â3 to +3 and map that range onto 0 to 1? Rather than standardizing the raw values and stopping there, you take a reasonable band of z-scores â say â3 to +3 standard deviations â and scale that band to a 0-to-1 range. It blends both approaches and bounds the result. For each, pick scaling or standardization and defend it: (a) SAT scores, (b) household income, (c) probability of a device failing, (d) distance from a warehouse to a customer. (a) SAT scores â scaling. Hard bounds at both ends, and real students score at or near the ceiling, so the bounds are meaningful rather than theoretical. (b) Household income â depends on the tail. Unbounded above and reasonably normal through the bulk, so standardization is the default. But with a long tail of extreme earners, removing outliers and scaling the rest may work better. This is the textbook combination case. (c) Failure probability â scaling. Bounded to [0, 1] by definition, with real observations sitting at or near zero. (d) Distance â standardization. No natural upper bound and a plausible fit to a normal distribution. The general test: are the bounds real and does data actually reach them? If so, scale. If the values run free and the distribution is roughly normal, standardize. If it fits a different distribution, standardizing to that distribution is also on the table. When the data is roughly normally distributed. When the data has natural bounds â age, SAT score, a probability, RGB values â especially with points at or near those bounds. When the data contains many outliers. Whenever the units are large. For unbounded data that reasonably fits a normal distribution â distance, income. For data confined to a fixed interval. For categorical predictors. Only when features share the same units. Scale the raw data including the extremes. Drop the feature entirely. Remove the outliers, then scale the remainder. Standardize twice. A mistake â the two methods can't be combined. A hybrid: standardize to z-scores, cap at a sensible range, then scale that range to a fixed interval. A way of removing the mean without changing the variance. Standard practice for categorical data."},{"id":"knn-svm","h":"KNN vs. SVM","t":"KNN vs. SVM Multiple classes, computation, imbalance, and probability estimates. You need to distinguish among five classes. Why does this favor KNN? KNN handles multiple classes naturally. SVM approaches for multiple classes do exist â for example running a separate model for each class k to separate \"class k\" from \"not class k\" â but they aren't commonly used. The data has meaningful pockets of one class surrounded by another. Which method suits this better? KNN classifies by local neighborhood, so a pocket of one class embedded inside another is no problem. A separating-surface approach has more trouble carving out enclosed regions. A credit-card system must approve or decline transactions in real time, at high volume. Which method has the computational edge, and why? SVM is easier to compute with at prediction time. KNN has to locate the k nearest training points for each new point it classifies, which becomes a real constraint when queries arrive constantly and answers are needed immediately. Heuristics can help, but the fundamental cost is there. Your dataset has far more \"yes\" points than \"no\" points. What happens to KNN? With one class dominating, any given neighborhood is likely to be majority \"yes\" simply by prevalence, and larger k pulls in more of the abundant class. The bias toward the common class gets worse as k increases. Which are legitimate approaches to an unbalanced dataset? Two families of fixes: build the rare class up (duplicate its points exactly or approximately, or synthesize points similar to existing ones), or cut the common class down (take small random samples, run the model, repeat across many samples). None is perfect and each has drawbacks â but unbalanced data is often the reality, and you do the best you can. How does KNN produce a probability estimate? The estimate comes free: neighbors in the class over k. With k=7 and 5 \"yes\" neighbors, that's 5/7. You can also weight neighbors by proximity. The estimate is unreliable at the extremes though â 7 out of 7 doesn't really mean 100%. And for SVM? SVM is a little harder here. There's no built-in count to convert into a proportion, so probability tends to be modeled as some function of how far the point sits from the separating boundary. You want conservative classification â very reluctant to call something red unless it clearly is. How do you do this in KNN with k=7? SVM gives conservative solutions when you want to be sure you're not erring in one particular direction, and KNN can do the same by moving the threshold off the majority rule. Instead of 4-of-7 deciding either way, demand 6 or 7 red neighbors for a red call while 2 blue neighbors suffices for blue. Whatever model you try, the classes stay thoroughly mixed. What's the most likely diagnosis? If the predictors fail to separate the classes even allowing nonlinear separators, more modeling won't rescue it. That's a signal to reconsider the underlying assumption that these particular features predict the classification at all. You're choosing between KNN and SVM for a fraud detection system. Walk through the factors and land on a recommendation. Points toward SVM: classification must happen in real time at high volume, and KNN's per-query search for the k nearest training points is expensive exactly where latency matters. SVM does its work up front. Points toward KNN: if fraud forms distinct pockets within otherwise normal activity, neighborhood classification captures that well. It also hands you a probability estimate directly, which is useful if the decision is a threshold on risk rather than a hard yes or no. The imbalance problem: fraud is rare, and KNN under imbalance will lean heavily toward the majority \"legitimate\" class, worsening as k grows. You'd need to rebalance â build the fraud class up or sample the legitimate class down â before KNN is usable. The conservative-direction question: which error is worse, blocking a good transaction or allowing a bad one? Both methods can be tuned for asymmetry â SVM naturally, KNN by shifting the neighbor threshold. Recommendation: the throughput requirement is usually decisive, so SVM. If probability estimates or local pockets matter enough to override that, KNN with rebalancing and a shifted threshold â accepting the computational cost and any heuristics needed to manage it. SVM cannot handle more than two classes under any formulation. KNN is more flexible with multiple classes; multi-class SVM methods exist but aren't commonly used. KNN converges faster with more classes. SVM requires classes to be balanced. SVM, since the pockets become support vectors. KNN, which handles pockets of one color inside another. Neither can represent pockets. Both handle it identically. KNN â classification is just a lookup. SVM â once trained, classification is cheap, whereas KNN must find the k nearest training points for every new query. KNN â it requires no training phase. They're equivalent at prediction time. Nothing â KNN is unaffected by class balance. It becomes more likely to predict \"yes\", and increasingly so as k grows. It becomes more likely to predict \"no\" to compensate. It refuses to classify points in sparse regions. Increase k until the imbalance averages out. Scale up the rare class by duplicating points or generating similar new ones, or scale down the common class by repeatedly sampling from it. Delete the rare class and model only the common one. Switch to a distance metric that ignores class counts. By the distance from the point to the decision boundary. By the count of neighbors in a class divided by k â 5 of 7 gives 5/7. It cannot produce probabilities. By the training-set base rate of each class. Straightforward â the margin width is the probability. Harder; probability might be based on the point's distance from the classifier. Identical to KNN's method. SVM outputs calibrated probabilities directly. Reduce k to 1. Shift the threshold: require 6 or 7 red neighbors to call it red, while 2 or more blue neighbors calls it blue. Widen the margin. Weight all neighbors equally. The model needs a nonlinear kernel. The chosen predictors probably aren't good predictors of the classification â the problem is the meta-model, not the model. k is set too low. The data needs rescaling."},{"id":"feature-scales","h":"Why feature scale matters for distance","t":"Why feature scale matters for distance How units quietly become weights. Why must features be put on comparable scales before a distance-based method like KNN? Features arrive on whatever scale their units imply, and those scales act as weights inside the distance calculation. A feature that happens to produce larger numbers dominates what counts as \"nearest\" â a weighting you never chose. You're estimating transportation cost from warehouse-to-customer distance in millimeters and factory-to-warehouse distance in kilometers. What goes wrong? The millimeter measurements are on the order of a million times larger than the kilometer ones. Differences in that feature look correspondingly bigger, so a KNN-type algorithm weights it far more when deciding what's nearest â even though nothing about the problem says it deserves more weight. The predictors are also called features, variables, or factors. Which of these methods is sensitive to their relative scales? The issue is structural rather than specific to one algorithm: as soon as a method decides using distances between points, the units of each feature become weights in that calculation. Explain to someone new to modeling why a feature measured in a small unit can silently take over a KNN model, and what to do about it. The mechanism: KNN classifies a new point by finding its nearest training points, and \"nearest\" means summing differences across all features. A feature measured in millimeters produces differences roughly a million times larger than the same physical quantity in kilometers. Those large differences dominate the total, so the model is effectively deciding on that one feature. Why it's easy to miss: nothing errors out. The model trains, predicts, and reports a number. You've applied a heavy weighting you never chose and probably wouldn't have. The fix: transform every feature to a comparable range before computing distances â scale bounded features, standardize unbounded roughly-normal ones. Then the distance reflects what you intended rather than an accident of units. The general lesson: in any distance-based method, choosing units is choosing weights. Making that choice deliberately is part of the modeling. Because the algorithm can't process negative numbers. Because each feature's natural numeric scale effectively weights its contribution to the distance. Because distance can only be computed on standardized data. Because unscaled features slow computation. Nothing â both are distances, so they're comparable. The millimeter feature produces numbers roughly a million times larger, so its differences dominate the distance and KNN weights it far more heavily. The kilometer feature dominates because it covers more ground. The model fails to converge. Only KNN. Only SVM. Any method whose decisions depend on distances between points. None â scale never affects model behavior."},{"id":"outliers","h":"Kinds of outlier, and what to do with one","t":"Kinds of outlier, and what to do with one Hardest to detect and hardest to judge are two different questions. Which type of outlier is hardest to detect, and why? A collective outlier is a set of points that is anomalous as a group while each member looks ordinary. There is nothing for a distance-based test to flag. You may know something is missing without being able to say what or where, which is exactly what makes them the hardest to find. How can potential point outliers be identified? Looking at the distance of a point from all the others â for instance the distance to its nearest neighbour â exposes points sitting far from everything else. Contextual outliers can often be found the same way, once 'nearest' is taken within the relevant context. Which type is hardest to deal with once you have identified it? Detection difficulty and handling difficulty rank differently. Collective outliers are hardest to find; contextual outliers are hardest to rule on, because deciding which of the three verdicts applies is a judgement the data cannot make for you. You have determined that a point is an outlier. What are the three possible verdicts? The first should be thrown out as an error. The second is real data that is not about what you are modelling, and can be thrown out. The third is real and relevant, and should be retained however inconvenient it is. Two of the three verdicts concern data that is correct. What distinguishes them? Both are correct data, so no test of data quality can separate them. Only knowing what question the model answers decides whether the point belongs in the analysis â which is why an automatic deletion rule cannot make this call. A cleaning script drops every point whose nearest-neighbour distance exceeds a fixed threshold, and the data is declared outlier-free. What is the most serious problem? Two failures at once. The test cannot see collective outliers, because their members sit close to their neighbours, so 'outlier-free' is unsupported. And deleting on detection applies the 'bad data' verdict to every flagged point, including points that are correct and relevant to the question. A teammate says collective outliers are 'the hardest type, full stop'. Explain what is right and what is missing in that claim. What is right: collective outliers are the hardest to detect . A set of points can be anomalous as a group while every individual member looks ordinary, so the distance-based reasoning that finds point outliers â and, applied within context, many contextual ones â has nothing to point at. You may know something is missing without knowing what or where. What is missing: detection is only half the problem. Once an outlier is identified it still has to be adjudicated, and there contextual outliers are the difficult ones: working out whether a point is bad data, correct data that should not be used in the model, or correct data that should be used is often genuinely hard. The correction: 'hardest' needs a qualifier. Hardest to find is collective; hardest to judge is contextual. An answer that names one type without saying hardest at what has answered half the question. Work the three verdicts on a single example: a $40,000 charge flagged on a credit card whose typical transaction is under $100. Show how the same flagged point can land in any of the three categories. Verdict 1 â bad data. The amount was mis-keyed, or a currency conversion was applied twice, or a test transaction leaked into the production feed. The value never happened. Throw it out. Verdict 2 â correct but not relevant. The charge is genuine: a corporate account settling a supplier invoice, sitting in a dataset you are using to model ordinary consumer spending. The data is right; it is not about the question. It can be thrown out. Verdict 3 â correct and relevant. The charge is a genuine fraudulent transaction and the model is being built to catch fraud. Here the outlier is the phenomenon. Dropping it removes the signal you are trying to learn. The point of the exercise: the second and third look identical in the data â both are correct values, both are extreme. Nothing about the number distinguishes them. Only the question the model answers does, which is why 'flagged, therefore deleted' is not a defensible cleaning policy. Point outliers, because a single wrong value hides easily in a large dataset. Contextual outliers, because context is a matter of opinion. Collective outliers, because no individual point looks unusual â only the group does. All three are equally hard; detection difficulty depends only on dataset size. By calculating the distance between each point and the nearest other point to it, and flagging unusually large values. By retraining the model without each point and comparing accuracy. By checking whether the value is physically possible. Only by manual inspection â there is no systematic method. Point outliers, because removing them changes the mean. Contextual outliers, because it is often hard to tell bad data from correct-but-irrelevant data from correct-and-relevant data. Collective outliers, because they are hardest to detect. None â once an outlier is identified, the handling is mechanical. Keep, delete, or impute a replacement value. Point, contextual, or collective. Bad/incorrect data; correct data not relevant to the question; correct data that is relevant to the question. Statistically significant, borderline, or negligible. How far the value sits from the mean. Whether the data point is relevant to the question being modelled. Whether the value was measured or self-reported. Whether the dataset is large enough to absorb the point. The threshold should have been a percentage rather than an absolute distance. Collective outliers go undetected, and every flagged point is treated as bad data when some may be correct and relevant. Nearest-neighbour distance is not a valid outlier measure. Nothing â this is standard practice."},{"id":"cusum","h":"CUSUM: C, T, and the fixed mean","t":"CUSUM: C, T, and the fixed mean One parameter filters noise, one weighs evidence, and the mean must not move. In a CUSUM set up to detect an increase, what is the effect of raising T? A higher T means the running total has to get larger before a change is declared. Detection is slower, but it is less likely that a total driven up by randomness alone will trip the threshold. T governs how sensitive the model is to changes. What role does C play when the running total is updated? C is subtracted from the deviation, so even an observation above the mean adds nothing unless it is more than C above it. That is how C removes the effect of ordinary variability before it can accumulate. The process you are monitoring has a lot of natural random variation. What follows for C? With high variability you want to ignore the occasions when randomness alone pushes an observation above the mean. A higher C does that. Where there is little variability, C can be lower, because randomness will not push observations far above the mean on its own. Why not simply set C = 0 and use a larger T to control false alarms? Observations are sometimes randomly above the mean for several periods in a row, which is not unlikely across many observations. With C = 0 those excursions accumulate, so avoiding a false alarm requires a high T. A genuine fast change then also has to climb that inflated threshold, so it is detected later. Handling the variability with C lets T stay low and catches the fast change sooner. Which description of the division of labour between C and T is correct? They act at different stages, which is why one cannot substitute for the other. C stops ordinary wobble entering the running total at all; T judges the evidence that survives that filter. Collapsing both into T alone forces T to be large, which is what costs detection speed. Should the mean be recalculated as each new observation arrives? If the aim is to detect whether a change has occurred, the comparison has to be against the original mean. A mean that moves with the data keeps each new observation close to the baseline, so the running total never builds and the change becomes invisible. Why is average yearly salary from 1970 to the present used as a warning example? There has clearly been a change over this period. But if the mean is recomputed with each new data point it climbs alongside the data, every observation sits near the current mean, and the method reports nothing. The detector has been tuned to agree with whatever is happening. Which application category most strongly favours a low T, accepting more false alarms? When the cost of missing or being late to a real change dominates the cost of investigating a false alarm, you buy speed with a low T and pay the false-alarm rate. Naming the application category first is what makes a parameter choice defensible rather than arbitrary. Explain why CUSUM is useful for monitoring a model already running in production, and what a signal should prompt. Why it applies: model performance usually changes over time, because the situation or the underlying data changes. That degradation is typically gradual and noisy rather than a single dramatic failure â exactly the small-sustained-shift case CUSUM is built for. Monitoring an accuracy metric or the residuals period by period accumulates evidence that no single period would provide. What a signal means: it is time to do maintenance on the model. Retrain on newer data, and consider whether a different type of model is now a better fit given how the data has changed. The signal is a prompt to investigate, not an automatic verdict. Why it beats a schedule: retraining on a fixed calendar retrains when nothing has changed and waits when something has. Tying the trigger to detected change spends effort when it is warranted. Parameter choice follows the failure you fear: for a slow drift buried in noisy weekly figures, let C absorb the noise â set it near half the drop that would actually matter â so T can stay low enough to catch the drift while it is still small. A colleague proposes monitoring weekly model precision with C = 0, a high T, and a mean recomputed each week from the trailing twelve weeks. Evaluate both parts of the proposal. C = 0 with a high T â a bad trade, not a broken one. It will control false alarms. But with noisy weekly figures, runs of random above-mean weeks accumulate in full, so T has to be set high to survive them. The real drift then has to climb that same inflated threshold, and since the drift is slow the total builds slowly, so detection arrives long after the damage. Use C to absorb the weekly noise and T can come down. The rolling twelve-week mean â this disables the detector. A slow drift is precisely what a trailing mean will follow. Each week's precision sits close to a baseline computed from the recently degraded weeks, the deviation stays near zero, the total never builds, and the chart certifies that nothing is wrong while performance decays. This is the salary example in production form. What to do instead: fix the mean at the performance level established when the model was validated, since that is the thing you want to know you have departed from. If the metric has genuine known seasonality, remove that known pattern or use a season-appropriate baseline â that is a real objection to a flat mean, and it is different from letting the mean chase the data. Changes are detected faster, and false alarms become more likely. Changes take longer to detect, but the model is less likely to declare a change that has not happened. The running total resets more often. The mean is re-estimated using more data. It is added to each observation to correct for bias. It is subtracted, so the total only grows when an observation exceeds the mean by more than C. It sets the number of observations kept in the running window. It scales the threshold in proportion to the sample size. C should be lower, to make the detector more responsive. C should be higher, so that randomly above-mean observations do not build the total. C should be set to zero and the variability handled entirely by T. C is unrelated to variability; only T responds to it. Because a CUSUM is mathematically undefined when C = 0. Because T cannot be set above one standard deviation. Because random runs above the mean accumulate in full, forcing T high â which then delays detection of a real, fast change. Because C and T must always be equal. C filters each observation before it enters the total; T sets how much accumulated evidence is required. C sets the alarm level; T absorbs noise in each observation. Both do the same job, so either one can be tuned and the other fixed. C applies to increases and T applies to decreases. Yes â a current mean keeps the detector calibrated to recent conditions. Yes, but only after a change has been declared. No â detecting a change requires comparing against the old mean; updating it masks the change. It makes no difference to the result. Salary data is too noisy for CUSUM to be appropriate. Salaries have risen enormously, so a mean recalculated as data arrives rises with them and hides an unmistakable change. Salary data has no meaningful mean. The example shows that C must be larger than T for trending data. Distinguishing a small change from random noise in a stable process. Early detection of a large long-term change where a missed detection is far more costly than an investigated false alarm. Any process where the mean is known exactly. Processes with very low variability."},{"id":"bias-variance","h":"Bias, variance, and what error is made of","t":"Bias, variance, and what error is made of Why a more flexible model is not automatically a better one. A team keeps adding variables and interaction terms. Training error falls steadily; validation error falls, flattens, then rises. What is the rise? Flexibility lowers bias and raises variance. Early on the bias reduction dominates and validation error falls. Past a point there is little bias left to remove, and each new term mostly lets the model chase quirks of the particular records it was fitted on â quirks that do not recur. That is variance, and it shows up only on data the model did not see. Two analysts model the same process. One reports test error of 0.08, the other claims a method that will reach 0.00 with enough tuning. What is wrong with the second claim? Expected test error decomposes into variance, squared bias and irreducible error. The first two can in principle be driven toward zero; the third is the part of the outcome that the available inputs simply do not determine. A model reporting zero error on new data is either measuring on data it was fitted to, or the outcome is a deterministic function of the inputs. Which change moves a model toward high bias and low variance? Larger k averages over more neighbours, so the prediction at any point is smoother and less sensitive to which particular records landed nearby â lower variance. It is also less able to follow genuine local structure â higher bias. The other three options all add flexibility, which runs the other way. A stakeholder asks why you are not simply using the most powerful model available. Answer in terms of bias, variance and what the data can support. Points to hit: Power is flexibility, and flexibility is paid for in variance. A more flexible model can represent more shapes, which reduces bias. It can also represent the accidental shapes in this particular sample, which raises variance â and variance is error on new data just as surely as bias is. The right amount depends on how much data there is. Variance falls as records accumulate, so the flexibility that overfits a few hundred records may be exactly right for a few hundred thousand. There is no model that is best independent of sample size. There is a floor either way. Irreducible error means some part of the outcome is not predictable from the inputs held. Past that point extra capacity buys nothing and costs interpretability and compute. How the question gets settled: not by argument but by validation â fit the candidates, compare them on held-out data, and keep the one that wins there rather than the one that wins on the fitting data. Bias increasing as the model grows. Variance increasing â the extra flexibility is fitting patterns specific to the training sample. The irreducible error growing with model size. A sign the validation set is too small to trust. Nothing â zero error is achievable with a flexible enough model. Test error cannot fall below the irreducible error, which no model can remove. Zero error is only possible for classification, not regression. The second analyst must be using a larger dataset. Increasing k in k-nearest neighbours. Decreasing the regularisation weight toward zero. Switching from a linear boundary to a flexible kernel. Adding interaction terms."},{"id":"cross-validation","h":"Cross-validation, and the ways it goes wrong","t":"Cross-validation, and the ways it goes wrong What k changes, and the four situations where resampling quietly lies. A pipeline standardises every record, then splits into ten folds and cross-validates. What has gone wrong? Any quantity estimated from the data is part of the fit. Computing the mean and standard deviation over all records lets information from the held-out fold influence the transformation applied to the training records, so the fold is no longer genuinely held out. The same applies to imputation values, variable selection, and any binning or encoding learned from the data. The fix is to derive these inside each fold, from the training part only. Moving from 5-fold to leave-one-out cross-validation on the same dataset does what to the estimate? Each leave-one-out model trains on nâ1 records, so it is almost the model you would fit on everything â little bias. But the n models differ from one another by a single record, so their errors move together, and averaging quantities that are highly correlated stabilises very little. Five or ten folds sits between the two effects, which is why it is the usual default. Records are repeated measurements on the same forty patients. Folds are assigned at random across all records. What is the consequence? Cross-validation assumes the held-out records are new in the sense that matters. Splitting within a patient leaves the model with other rows from that same patient, so predicting the held-out one is much easier than predicting a patient it has never seen â which is the task it will actually face. Group the folds by patient instead, so every record from an individual falls on the same side of the split. You inherit a model reported at 91% accuracy from 10-fold cross-validation. List what you would check before repeating that number to anyone. Things to establish, roughly in order of how often they are the problem: Was any preprocessing done before the split? Scaling, imputation, encoding or variable selection computed over the full dataset leaks held-out information and inflates the score. Was the model chosen using the same cross-validation? If several candidates or parameter settings were compared on these folds, 91% is a selection winner, not an unbiased estimate. That needs either a held-out test set or nested cross-validation. Are the records independent? Repeated measures, households, sessions from one user, or near-duplicate rows all break the assumption that a held-out record is genuinely new. Is there a time order? If so, random folds train on the future and the number is meaningless for forecasting use. Are the classes balanced, and is accuracy the right measure at all? At 91% accuracy with a 90/10 split, the model may be little better than always predicting the majority class. What was shipped? If a single fold model went to production rather than a refit on all the data, the deployed model is not the one that was measured. Nothing â standardisation is a fixed transformation. The mean and standard deviation were computed using the held-out records, so each fold's score is optimistic. Standardising before splitting changes the class balance across folds. Ten folds is too many for standardised data. Lowers both bias and variance. Lowers bias, because each model trains on nearly all the data, but raises variance, because the models are nearly identical and their errors are correlated. Raises bias and lowers variance. Leaves both unchanged; only the runtime differs. None, as long as each fold has roughly equal size. The estimate is optimistic, because a patient's other measurements sit in the training data and the model can recognise the individual rather than the pattern. The estimate is pessimistic, because repeated records add noise. It only matters if the outcome is numeric."},{"id":"bootstrap","h":"The bootstrap","t":"The bootstrap Resampling with replacement, and the question it actually answers. What question is the bootstrap designed to answer? The bootstrap measures the variability of a statistic. Resampling n records with replacement, recomputing the statistic, and repeating many times produces a spread of estimates that approximates how much that statistic would move if you could collect fresh samples. It is the general-purpose answer for quantities â a median, a ratio, a model coefficient under a complicated procedure â where the textbook standard error formula does not exist. In a single bootstrap sample of size n drawn with replacement from n records, roughly what share of the original records appears at least once? A given record avoids selection on one draw with probability (1 â 1/n), and across n draws with probability (1 â 1/n)â¿, which converges to 1/e â 0.368. So about 63.2% of the distinct records appear in any one sample and about 36.8% are left out. Those omitted records are the out-of-bag set, and they can serve as a free held-out sample for the model fitted on that draw. Drawing without replacement instead would break the method. Why? Drawing n records from n without replacement returns exactly the same set every time. The whole mechanism depends on some records appearing more than once and others not at all â that variation between samples is what stands in for the variation between hypothetical fresh datasets. A colleague says bootstrapping gives a model more data to learn from. Correct the misconception and say what the method does provide. The correction. Resampling creates no new information. Every bootstrap record is a copy of one already held, so the total information about the process is fixed by the original sample. Nothing about the dataset's coverage, its biases or its blind spots improves. What it does provide. A way to observe how much an estimate moves when the sample composition shifts. If the statistic is stable across resamples, it is well determined by the data at hand; if it swings widely, the single number from the full dataset is not something to lean on. Where the limit bites. The bootstrap can only reflect variability that the original sample is capable of showing. If the sample missed a segment of the population entirely, every resample misses it too, and the confidence interval will be confidently wrong. It measures sampling variability, not sampling bias. Which of several candidate models is best. How uncertain an estimate is, when no formula for its standard error is available. Whether a dataset contains outliers. How many folds cross-validation should use. About 50%. About 63%. About 90%. All of them â that is what resampling means. It would be computationally slower. Every sample would be the original dataset in a different order, so all the estimates would be identical and the spread would be zero. It would introduce bias into the statistic. It would only work for numeric outcomes."},{"id":"distance","h":"Choosing a distance measure","t":"Choosing a distance measure What each norm treats as close, and when the choice changes the answer. Two customers have identical spending profiles across categories, but one spends roughly ten times as much overall. Which pair of verdicts is right? Euclidean distance measures the gap between the two points in space, and a tenfold difference in magnitude puts them a long way apart. Cosine distance compares only the direction of the two vectors, so identical proportions across categories register as the same profile regardless of size. Which is correct depends entirely on the question: segmenting by taste favours cosine, segmenting by value favours Euclidean. A delivery service cares about the worst single leg of a route rather than the total. Which norm matches that concern? As p rises in the p-norm, larger coordinate differences dominate the total. At the limit only the single biggest difference contributes at all. That is exactly the right measure when what matters is the worst case along any one dimension, not the aggregate across all of them. Attributes are age in years, income in dollars and number of purchases. Euclidean distance is computed without transformation. What effectively happens? Distance sums squared differences, so an attribute measured in tens of thousands contributes thousands of times more than one measured in tens. The model then reflects the units the data happened to be recorded in rather than any judgement about what matters. Standardising or scaling first makes the weighting deliberate instead of accidental â and if one attribute really should count more, weight it explicitly. You are grouping hospitals by their patient-mix. Some attributes are percentages, some are counts, and two of them are strongly correlated with each other. Work through how you would set up the distance calculation. Put the attributes on comparable footing first. Percentages and raw counts cannot go into the same distance untransformed â the counts will dominate. Standardise, or convert the counts to rates so that every attribute is in the same kind of unit. Decide whether size should matter. If two hospitals with the same case mix at different volumes should count as similar, work with proportions or use cosine distance. If volume is part of what distinguishes them, keep it in and use Euclidean. Handle the correlated pair. Two attributes measuring nearly the same thing effectively vote twice, quietly doubling that dimension's weight. Options: drop one, combine them into a single index, or use a distance that accounts for the correlation structure (Mahalanobis) rather than treating dimensions as independent. Say what you assumed. The distance measure is a modelling choice, not a neutral preliminary. Two defensible choices can produce different groupings from identical data, so the choice belongs in the write-up alongside the results. Euclidean distance calls them close; cosine distance calls them far apart. Euclidean distance calls them far apart; cosine distance calls them nearly identical. Both call them close. Both call them far apart. The 1-norm, which sums absolute differences. The 2-norm, which is straight-line distance. The infinity norm, which takes the largest single coordinate difference. Cosine distance. Each attribute contributes roughly equally. Income dominates almost entirely, because its numbers are orders of magnitude larger. Age dominates, because it has the narrowest range. The result is identical to computing distance on the standardised data."},{"id":"kmeans-behaviour","h":"How k-means behaves","t":"How k-means behaves Why two runs disagree, what makes it stop, and the shapes it cannot find. Two runs of k-means on the same data with the same k produce different groupings. What does this indicate? k-means converges to a configuration no record wants to leave, which need not be the arrangement with the lowest total distance overall. Where it lands depends on the initial centres. The standard response is to run it from several different starts and keep the best result, and to use a spread-out initialisation so that a poor start is less likely in the first place. What condition tells the algorithm it has finished? If no record moves, the membership is identical to the previous round, so the centroids computed from that membership are identical too, so the next assignment step produces the same result again. The algorithm has reached a fixed point and further iterations change nothing. The true groups in a dataset are two long, curved, interleaved bands. k-means with k = 2 splits each band across both clusters. What is the explanation? Assigning each record to its nearest centre divides the space into straight-edged convex regions. A curved band cannot be captured by such a region, so the algorithm cuts across the true structure no matter how well it is initialised. This is a limit of the method rather than a tuning problem: a different algorithm, or a transformation of the space, is what the situation calls for. A single record in your dataset sits far from everything else. Trace what it does to k-means, and set out your options. What it does. A cluster centre is a mean, and a mean is pulled by extreme values. The outlier drags whichever centre claims it away from the bulk of that cluster's records, which distorts the boundary and can shift memberships that have nothing to do with the outlier. If k is large enough, it may capture an entire centre to itself, spending one of your groups on a single record. The options, and what each costs. Investigate first. If it is a recording error, correcting or removing it is straightforward and uncontroversial. Remove a genuine extreme. Cluster without it and assign it afterwards to the nearest resulting centre. Clean, but you have decided it is not worth a group of its own â sometimes wrong, since rare cases are occasionally the interesting ones. Use a method less sensitive to means. Choosing an actual record as each centre rather than a computed average limits how far a single point can pull. Keep it and report it. Legitimate if the extreme record is genuinely part of what you are describing, as long as the write-up says the grouping was shaped by it. What should not happen is silent deletion: the decision changes the output, so it belongs in the record. A bug â the algorithm is deterministic. Normal behaviour: it is a heuristic that converges from wherever it starts, and different starting centres can converge differently. The data has no cluster structure at all. k has been set too low. The total distance stops decreasing by more than a set tolerance. No record changes cluster on an iteration â so the centres are unchanged, and the next assignment would reproduce itself. A fixed number of iterations has elapsed. Every cluster has roughly equal membership. Too few restarts. k-means assigns by proximity to a single centre, which carves the space into convex regions â it cannot represent elongated or curved groups. The data needs standardising. k should be raised to 4."},{"id":"choosing-k","h":"Choosing the number of clusters","t":"Choosing the number of clusters Why the obvious objective fails, and what to use instead. A colleague proposes selecting k by choosing whichever value minimises total distance from records to their cluster centre. What will this produce? Total distance falls monotonically as k rises, because adding a centre can only shorten the distance from records to their nearest one. Taken to its conclusion, every record becomes its own centre at distance zero. The objective is useful for comparing solutions at a fixed k and useless for choosing k. What is the elbow method actually looking for? Since total distance always falls, the informative quantity is the rate at which it falls. A pronounced bend suggests that up to that point each new cluster was separating something real, and after it each one is mostly subdividing groups that were already coherent. It is a judgement read off a curve, not a test â and on many real datasets the bend is ambiguous or absent. A record has a silhouette value of â0.4. What does that say? The silhouette compares the mean distance to a record's own cluster against the mean distance to the nearest other cluster. Values near +1 indicate a comfortable fit, near 0 a boundary case, and negative values say the record would look more at home elsewhere. Averaging the silhouette across all records scores a whole clustering â and unlike total distance, it does not automatically improve as k grows, which is what makes it usable for choosing k. You are clustering retail locations to decide how many regional depots to build. The elbow is ambiguous and silhouette scores are similar for k between 3 and 7. How do you settle on a number? Accept that the data is not going to decide it. Ambiguous diagnostics across a range usually mean the structure is genuinely graded rather than sharply grouped. Forcing a statistical answer out of that is false precision. Let the decision supply the constraint. The question is not really how many groups exist but how many depots to build, and that has its own answer: the capital available, the maximum acceptable distance from a depot to a location, the staffing each site needs. Clustering at k = 3 through 7 and costing each option is a better use of the ambiguity than picking one and presenting it as discovered. Check stability across the range. If certain locations group together at every k, that grouping is a real feature of the data and can be relied on. If memberships reshuffle completely between k = 4 and k = 5, none of the solutions is describing something solid. Report the ambiguity. A note that k between 3 and 7 fits the data about equally well, with the choice made on cost grounds, is more useful to whoever inherits the analysis than a bare assertion that there are five regions. The natural number of groups in the data. k equal to the number of records â every record becomes its own cluster at zero distance. k = 1, since one cluster has the fewest centres. An unstable answer that varies between runs. The k with the lowest total distance. The k past which additional clusters stop producing a worthwhile reduction in total distance. The k at which all clusters contain equal numbers of records. The largest k that still runs in reasonable time. It sits comfortably inside its cluster. It is on the boundary between two clusters. It is on average closer to the records of a neighbouring cluster than to those of its own, so it is probably assigned to the wrong one. Its cluster has too few members."},{"id":"classifier-metrics","h":"Measuring a classifier","t":"Measuring a classifier When accuracy is the wrong number, and what to report instead. One record in 200 is fraudulent. A model reports 99.5% accuracy. What is the first thing to check? With a 199:1 imbalance, the majority-class rule alone scores 99.5%. Accuracy cannot distinguish that from a model with real skill, because the metric is dominated by the class that is easy to get right. Recall on the rare class â what share of actual fraud was caught â is the number that separates the two, alongside precision to show how many false accusations it took to get there. A screening test is tuned so its threshold flags more cases as positive. What happens to precision and recall? Lowering the bar for a positive verdict catches more of the true positives â recall rises â while also sweeping in more records that were not positive, so a smaller share of the flags are correct and precision falls. The two move against each other as the threshold slides, which is what the ROC and precision-recall curves trace out. There is no threshold that maximises both; the choice depends on which error costs more. A missed case costs roughly twenty times what a false alarm costs. How should the model be evaluated? Accuracy and F1 both treat the two mistakes as comparable, which is exactly what the situation says they are not. Weighting each error count by its cost and minimising the total makes the asymmetry explicit, and it is what justifies shifting the decision threshold away from the default. A model that is worse on accuracy can easily be better on expected cost, and that is the one to deploy. You are asked for a single number summarising a classifier for a non-technical audience. Explain why you would resist, and what you would provide instead. Why a single number misleads here. Any one metric hides the trade-off the model actually embodies. Accuracy hides class imbalance; precision hides how much was missed; recall hides how many false alarms it took. A model is a threshold sitting somewhere on a curve, and reporting one point as if it were the model's quality conceals that the threshold is a choice someone made. What to offer instead. The confusion matrix itself, in the audience's own terms: of 1,000 cases, this many caught, this many missed, this many false alarms. That is concrete, needs no statistical vocabulary, and lets the audience apply their own sense of what each error costs. Then attach the consequence. If the costs of the two errors are known even roughly, expected cost converts the matrix into one number that genuinely means something â and if the audience disagrees with the cost assumptions, the disagreement is now about the right thing. And say what moves. Make clear the threshold can be retuned: more catches at the price of more false alarms, or the reverse. That is usually the decision the audience is actually being asked to make. Whether the model simply predicts not fraudulent for every record, which would score 99.5% and catch nothing. Whether the training set was large enough. Whether the model was cross-validated. Whether accuracy was computed on the test set. Both rise. Recall rises and precision generally falls. Precision rises and recall falls. Neither changes; the threshold affects only accuracy. By accuracy, which already accounts for both error types. By expected cost â the false positive count times its cost plus the false negative count times its cost. By the F1 score, which balances the two errors. By precision alone."},{"id":"data-prep","h":"Missing values, encoding, and leakage","t":"Missing values, encoding, and leakage The preparation choices that quietly become modelling choices. A column is 30% missing. Which consideration matters most in deciding what to do? Filling in the mean is defensible when values are missing for reasons unrelated to what you are predicting. When the reason is related â an income field left blank more often by those with no income, a test result absent because nobody ordered the test â imputation invents data that systematically differs from the truth and erases a real signal. Adding an indicator column recording that the value was missing keeps that signal available to the model. A categorical column holds four unordered values. Someone codes them 1, 2, 3, 4 and feeds it into a distance-based method. What does the method now believe? A distance calculation reads those codes as positions on a line. The arbitrary assignment of numbers has invented an ordering and a set of spacings that do not exist in the data. Indicator columns â one per category, each 0 or 1 â leave every pair of categories equidistant, which is the honest representation of an unordered variable. Which of these is a leakage problem rather than an ordinary modelling weakness? Variable selection is part of fitting. Doing it across all records means the held-out folds influenced which variables the model contains, so the cross-validation score reflects a model that has already seen them. The effect can be severe: selection on noise alone can produce impressive cross-validated scores on data with no real signal whatsoever. The selection step has to happen inside each fold. Write down the preparation steps that must be carried out inside a cross-validation fold rather than once over the whole dataset, and explain the common principle. The principle: any step that learns a quantity from the data is part of the model. If it is computed using records that are supposed to be held out, those records are no longer held out, and the resulting estimate is optimistic. Steps that must go inside the fold: Scaling and standardisation â the minimum, maximum, mean and standard deviation are estimated quantities. Imputation â mean, median or model-based fills are learned from the training records. Variable selection and dimensionality reduction â which columns survive, and any components derived from them. Encoding schemes learned from the data, such as replacing a category by its average outcome. Outlier removal rules based on data-derived thresholds. Any parameter tuning, which is why nested cross-validation exists. What can safely happen once: transformations fixed in advance and independent of the values, such as converting units, parsing dates, or applying a log to a variable chosen for subject-matter reasons rather than because the data suggested it. The percentage itself â above a fixed threshold the column should be dropped. Why the values are missing, since missingness that relates to the outcome carries information and cannot be filled in neutrally. Whether the column is numeric or categorical. Whether the dataset is large enough to drop the affected records. Nothing problematic; the codes are just labels. That the categories are ordered and that the first and fourth are three times further apart than the first and second. That the column is numeric and should be standardised. That there are four clusters in the data. Using a model that is too simple for the underlying relationship. Selecting predictor variables by testing them against the outcome over the whole dataset, then cross-validating the resulting model. Having fewer records than you would like. Choosing Euclidean distance where cosine would suit better."},{"id":"smoothing","h":"Exponential smoothing and its parameters","t":"Exponential smoothing and its parameters What α, β and γ trade against each other, and which variant a series calls for. A forecast consistently lags several periods behind turning points in the series. Which adjustment addresses that? The weight on the observation k periods back is Î±(1âÎ±)áµ. A small Î± spreads weight far into the past, which smooths noise and makes the estimate slow to acknowledge genuine change â visible as a lag at every turning point. Raising Î± shortens that memory. The cost is that the forecast also reacts to noise, so the adjustment is a trade rather than a fix. Monthly sales show a seasonal swing that grows proportionally as the overall level rises. Which formulation fits? Additive seasonality assumes December adds a fixed quantity regardless of the level; multiplicative assumes December runs at a fixed ratio to the level. A swing that grows with the series is the multiplicative pattern, and forcing an additive form on it will under-predict peaks late in the series and over-predict them early on. Fitting Î±, Î² and Î³ by minimising squared forecast error yields Î± = 0.95 on a visibly noisy series. What is the concern? An Î± near 1 means the forecast is close to the naive last-value rule. On a noisy series that can genuinely minimise one-step-ahead squared error while producing forecasts that simply repeat the most recent fluctuation. Check the fitted model against the naive benchmark on held-out data, and consider whether the objective being optimised matches the horizon you actually forecast over. Hourly demand at a call centre shows a daily pattern, a weekly pattern, a slow upward trend and occasional spikes from marketing campaigns. Discuss how you would approach forecasting it. Separate the components deliberately. There is a baseline, a trend, and seasonality â but two seasonal cycles, one of length 24 and one of length 168. Standard Holt-Winters carries a single seasonal length, so either model the dominant cycle and accept residual structure, or use a formulation that supports multiple seasonalities. The spikes are a different kind of thing. Campaign effects are not seasonality and not noise: they are driven by a known external event. Leaving them in the series corrupts the seasonal factors, since the smoother will attribute a one-off surge to whatever hour and weekday it fell on. Either flag those periods and handle them separately, or include the campaign as an input rather than letting the smoother absorb it. Set the parameters against the horizon. If the forecast is used to staff tomorrow, one-step-ahead accuracy is the right objective. If it staffs next month, fit and evaluate at that horizon â the Î± that is best for one is frequently not best for the other. Validate forward. Hold out the most recent stretch, forecast it period by period refitting as you go, and compare against both the naive and the seasonal naive rule. A model that cannot beat last-Tuesday-at-this-hour has not earned the complexity. Lower Î±, so the forecast is smoother. Raise Î±, so more weight falls on recent observations. Lower Î³, so seasonal factors update more slowly. Increase the differencing order. Additive seasonality, subtracting a fixed seasonal amount. Multiplicative seasonality, dividing by a seasonal factor. Simple exponential smoothing with a higher Î±. Differencing the series once. None â the optimiser found the best value. The fitted parameters may be chasing noise, since one-step-ahead error can be reduced by tracking the last observation closely, which does not imply useful forecasts further out. Î± above 0.9 is mathematically invalid. It means the series has a trend that has not been modelled."},{"id":"arima","h":"Stationarity, differencing and ARIMA","t":"Stationarity, differencing and ARIMA What the I does, what p and q describe, and what GARCH is for. What does differencing a series accomplish? Modelling with past values assumes the relationship between them is stable over time. A series wandering steadily upward violates that, because the mean depends on when you look. Working with period-to-period changes rather than levels removes the drift and yields a series whose statistical behaviour is consistent â which is what the model requires. d is simply how many times this was necessary, and d = 1 handles most series that need it. On a differenced series, the autocorrelation function falls away gradually while the partial autocorrelation cuts off sharply after lag 2. What does this suggest? The two functions are read as a pair. An autoregressive process produces a gradually decaying autocorrelation and a partial autocorrelation that cuts off at the model's order â so a sharp cut at lag 2 points to AR(2). The mirror image, a sharp cut in the autocorrelation with gradual decay in the partial, points to a moving-average process. When both decay gradually, a mixed model is likely and small p and q are compared on an information criterion. An analyst wants to forecast how volatile a daily return series will be next week, not what the return will be. Which tool fits? ARIMA and exponential smoothing both forecast the level of the series. GARCH models the variance, which is the right target when volatility itself clusters â calm stretches followed by turbulent ones â and when the size of the uncertainty is the quantity the decision depends on. Risk limits, option pricing and position sizing all want the second thing, not the first. You have 26 monthly observations of a new product's sales and are asked for a twelve-month forecast. Discuss what is realistic. State the limits before the method. Twenty-six observations is a short series, and a twelve-month horizon is nearly half its length again. Any forecast that far out rests mostly on an assumed trend rather than on evidence, and the honest deliverable includes a wide interval rather than a line. Why ARIMA is a poor fit here. It estimates autoregressive and moving-average structure from the data and generally wants a substantially longer history â commonly quoted as around forty observations or more â before those estimates mean anything. With 26 points it will fit something, and that something will largely be noise. What is more defensible. Exponential smoothing with a trend, kept deliberately simple, copes better with short series. A single seasonal cycle cannot be estimated from two years of monthly data with any confidence, so either leave seasonality out or borrow a seasonal profile from a comparable established product and say that you have done so. Frame the output honestly. Give a range rather than a point, note that a new product's trajectory frequently changes character as it saturates its early market, and propose revisiting the forecast as observations accumulate rather than treating it as settled. It removes noise, leaving the underlying signal. It removes drift in the level, so that the mean and variance no longer change systematically over time. It converts a seasonal series into a non-seasonal one. It reduces the number of parameters that need fitting. A moving-average model of order 2. An autoregressive model of order 2. That further differencing is required. That the series is white noise. ARIMA with a higher differencing order. A GARCH model, which forecasts the variance rather than the value. Holt-Winters with weekly seasonality. A larger Î± in exponential smoothing."},{"id":"forecast-validation","h":"Judging a forecast","t":"Judging a forecast Why the usual validation machinery fails on time, and what replaces it. A forecaster is evaluated with shuffled 10-fold cross-validation and scores well. Why is the result meaningless? Random folds scatter the held-out periods through the series, so the model routinely has both neighbours of a held-out point in its training data. Interpolating between known values is a much easier problem than extrapolating past the end of what you know, which is what forecasting actually is. The reported error reflects the easier task. Four forecast errors are +8, â8, +6 and â6. What do the mean error and the mean absolute error say? Signed errors cancel, so a mean of zero says only that the forecast is not systematically high or low. It says nothing about accuracy: a forecaster that alternates large overshoots and undershoots scores the same zero as one that is right every time. Absolute or squared errors are what measure size, and the mean signed error is worth reporting only alongside them, as a check on bias. What is the purpose of comparing a forecasting model against the naive rule that next period equals this period? The naive rule and its seasonal counterpart cost nothing to build, need no data beyond the last observation, and are surprisingly hard to beat on many real series. Reporting a model's error without that comparison leaves the reader unable to tell whether the number is good. On a strongly seasonal series the seasonal naive rule is the benchmark that matters, since beating the plain naive rule can be trivial. Set out how you would validate a weekly demand forecast that will be used to place purchase orders, and what you would report. Validate in time order. Fit on an initial stretch, forecast the next week, record the error, add that week to the training data, refit, and repeat to the end of the series. Average the recorded errors. Every forecast scored this way used only information available before the week it predicted, which is the condition that makes the number mean something. Match the horizon to the use. If orders are placed three weeks ahead, evaluate three-week-ahead forecasts, not one-week-ahead. Accuracy degrades with horizon and a one-step figure will flatter a model that will never be used that way. Report error in decision terms. MAE in units is directly interpretable as typical shortfall or excess. RMSE is worth adding where large misses hurt disproportionately, since it penalises them more heavily. MAPE is convenient for comparing across products of different sizes but becomes unstable when demand approaches zero. Report bias separately. Mean signed error shows whether the forecast systematically over- or under-orders, which is a different and often more expensive problem than imprecision. Include the benchmarks and the residual check. Give the naive and seasonal naive errors alongside the model's, and confirm that what is left over looks like noise â remaining structure in the residuals means there is signal the model has not used. Ten folds is too many for time-series data. Most folds train on periods that come after the periods being predicted, and adjacent periods are related, so the task is far easier than forecasting. Forecast errors cannot be averaged across folds. Cross-validation requires a categorical outcome. Both are 0, indicating an unbiased and accurate forecaster. Mean error is 0 and MAE is 7 â unbiased on average, but wrong by about 7 every period. Mean error is 7 and MAE is 0. Both are 7. It is a formality; any fitted model beats it. It establishes whether the model's complexity buys anything, since a model that cannot beat a rule requiring no fitting has not earned its place. It calibrates the confidence intervals. It determines the correct differencing order."},{"id":"least-squares","h":"Least squares and what the coefficients mean","t":"Least squares and what the coefficients mean What the fit minimises, why, and how to read a coefficient, a transformation or an interaction. Under which assumptions does minimising the sum of squared errors give the maximum likelihood estimates of a linear regression? Write down the normal density of each error, multiply them because they are independent, and the product is a single exponential whose exponent is minus the sum of squared errors divided by 2ÏÂ². Maximising it is the same as minimising the SSE. Change the error distribution â heavy tails, unequal variance, dependence â and a different criterion becomes the likelihood-maximising one. A model is fitted as y = aâ + aâx + aâxÂ². A colleague says this canât be linear regression because the fitted curve bends. Who is right? âLinearâ refers to how the coefficients enter the equation, not the attributes. Computing xÂ² for every row creates a new column of data, and least squares fits aâ, aâ and aâ exactly as it would any three coefficients. The same holds for logs, square roots and products of attributes. In y = aâ + aâxâ + aâxâ + aâxâxâ, what is the change in predicted y for a one-unit increase in xâ? xâ appears twice: on its own and inside the product. Raising it by one adds aâ from the first and aâxâ from the second. The effect of xâ therefore depends on the level of xâ, which is precisely what an interaction term is for. aâ alone is the effect only where xâ = 0. You are asked to build a regression for the price of used equipment from its age, hours of use and manufacturer. Discuss how you would decide on the form of the model before trusting its coefficients. Look before fitting. Plot price against each numeric attribute. Depreciation is often steep early and then flattens, which argues for a log of price or of age rather than a straight line. A residual plot after a first fit will show any curvature the model missed. Handle the category properly. Manufacturer enters as a set of 0/1 columns, one fewer than the number of makers, not as a single coded number. Consider interactions. Hours of use may matter more for some makers, or more as equipment ages â a product term lets the slope differ. Choose among candidate forms on evidence. Compare the versions on AIC or BIC fitted to the same data, or better on held-out data, rather than on RÂ², which always rises with more terms. Then read the output. Only once the form fits well do the coefficients, their intervals and their p-values mean what they claim. Whenever the predictors are measured on the same scale. When the errors are independent and normally distributed with mean zero and constant variance. Whenever the sample is large, regardless of the errors. Only when the response has been log-transformed. The colleague: linear regression can only fit straight lines. Neither: it is a separate method called polynomial optimisation. The model is linear regression, because it is linear in the coefficients; xÂ² is simply another column. It is linear only if aâ turns out to be zero. aâ aâ + aâxâ aâ + aâ aâ Â· aâ"},{"id":"information-criteria","h":"Likelihood, AIC and BIC","t":"Likelihood, AIC and BIC Charging a model for its parameters, and turning a difference in criteria into a statement about which model to prefer. Two models fitted to the same data have AIC values of 450 and 454. What is the relative likelihood that the higher-AIC model is the better one? The relative likelihood is exp((AIC low â AIC high )/2) = exp(â2) â 0.135. Only the difference matters; the absolute size of AIC values depends on the data and carries no meaning on its own. Forgetting to halve the difference gives exp(â4) â 1.8%, a common slip. Why does BIC usually select models with fewer parameters than AIC on the same data? Both criteria are â2 ln L* plus a penalty. AIC charges 2 per parameter; BIC charges ln(n). With a few hundred observations ln(n) is around 5 or 6, so a parameter has to improve the log-likelihood far more to survive under BIC. When the two disagree, the extra parameters are buying only a modest improvement in fit. A model with 8 parameters is fitted to 14 observations. Which statement is most accurate? AICc adds 2k(k+1)/(nâkâ1) = 2Â·8Â·9/5 = 28.8, a correction as large as the differences it is meant to judge. Plain AIC assumes plenty of data and BIC is a large-sample approximation, so neither is dependable. The real message is that fourteen observations cannot support eight parameters: the model is very likely fitting noise. Explain why maximum likelihood on its own cannot be used to choose between models with different numbers of parameters, and how the information criteria address this. Likelihood never falls when parameters are added. A model with an extra parameter can always reproduce the smaller model by setting that parameter to zero, so its maximum likelihood is at least as high. Choosing by likelihood alone always picks the most complex model available, including parameters that only fit random effects. The criteria add a price. AIC = 2k â 2 ln L* and BIC = k ln n â 2 ln L*. A parameter is kept only if it raises the log-likelihood by more than its cost: 1 per parameter under AIC, (ln n)/2 under BIC. AICc adds a correction for finite samples. Their differences are informative. An AIC gap converts to a relative likelihood through exp(âÎ/2); BIC gaps have rule-of-thumb bands. Comparisons are valid only between models fitted to the same data. They are guides, not verdicts. AIC leans toward prediction, BIC toward parsimony; looking at both, and at performance on held-out data, gives a more trustworthy choice than any single number. About 99%, since the values are nearly equal in size. About 13.5%. About 1.8%. It cannot be computed without the number of data points. BIC uses a different likelihood function. BICâs penalty per parameter is ln(n), which exceeds AICâs penalty of 2 once n is 8 or more. BIC ignores the fit of the model entirely. BIC only works for models with a single predictor. AIC is the most reliable criterion here because it has the smallest penalty. BIC is reliable at any sample size, so use it. AICcâs correction is large here, and all the criteria are on shaky ground because there are too few observations per parameter. Maximum likelihood alone is best, since penalties are unnecessary with small samples."},{"id":"regression-inference","h":"p-values, significance and causation","t":"p-values, significance and causation What the output table can claim, what it can’t, and the traps that come with large data and many tests. Which statement correctly describes the p-value of a regression coefficient? A p-value is computed assuming no effect, and asks how surprising the observed estimate would be under that assumption. It is not the probability that the assumption is true, and it says nothing about the size of the effect. In practice small p-values justify keeping an attribute and large ones cast doubt on it, but the formal statement matters on a written answer. A regression fitted on several million records finds that nearly every candidate predictor has p < 0.001. What is the most important next step? With very large samples, tiny effects become statistically significant. Significance then stops distinguishing important predictors from trivial ones, and moving the threshold barely helps. What does distinguish them is practical size: how much the prediction actually changes over a realistic range of the attribute. Regressing y on x and regressing x on y using the same data give identical p-values and RÂ². What follows? The fit statistics are symmetric, so they cannot show which variable drives which, or whether both are driven by something else. The usual rules of thumb â the cause precedes the effect, the mechanism makes sense, and no outside factor explains the relationship â have to come from knowledge of the system. Randomised experiments are the strongest way to satisfy the last of these. An organisation screens 150 possible drivers of employee turnover, one regression each, and presents the 12 with p < 0.05 as âthe causes of turnoverâ. Discuss what is wrong and what you would do instead. Multiple testing. If none of the 150 drivers mattered, about 150 Ã 0.05 â 7.5 would still pass by chance. Twelve is not far above that, so a sizeable share of the list may be noise, and the p-values canât say which. A correction â Bonferroni for strict control, a false-discovery-rate method for a screen â or replication on new data is needed. One-at-a-time regressions. Each driver was tested alone, so overlapping drivers (pay and seniority, say) each take credit for the same effect. Fitting them together shows which carry independent information. Correlation is not causation. Some âdriversâ may be consequences of an intention to leave, or share an outside cause such as a poor manager. Calling them causes needs time order, a plausible mechanism and an argument against outside factors â ideally a trial of an intervention. Size. Report how much each surviving driver moves turnover, not just that it is significant, so effort goes where the effect is large. The probability that the coefficient is exactly zero. The probability, if the true coefficient were zero, of obtaining an estimate at least as far from zero as the one observed. The proportion of the response explained by that predictor. The probability that the model is correct. Keep every predictor, since all are significant. Lower the threshold to 0.0001 and keep the survivors. Judge each predictor by the size of its effect â coefficient times a realistic change in the attribute â relative to the response. Discard the model, since the p-values must be unreliable. The causal direction must be decided from outside the model â timing, mechanism and possible third factors. Both variables cause each other equally. The direction with the larger coefficient is the causal one. Neither variable is related to the other."}]},{"f":"index.html","t":"Home","s":[{"id":"","h":"Cram sheet — The three questions","t":"The three questions Descriptive — explains a pattern already in past data. Clustering, change detection, dimensionality reduction. Predictive — a value not yet observed. Classification, regression, time-series forecasting. Prescriptive — a course of action, usually with an objective to optimise. Optimization, simulation, probability models. The test — ask what the answer would be: an explanation of the past → descriptive; a value → predictive; an action → prescriptive. Definitions Modeling — expressing the key parts of a real-life situation in math, so the math can be analysed and the analysis turned back into a real-life answer. Three legs — situation → math · analysis · math → recommendation. Teaching covers the middle; projects fail at the ends. \"Model\" has 3 zoom levels — (1) the method (\"regression\"); (2) method + its chosen variables; (3) the fitted equation with numbers in it. \"Compare five SVM models\" = level 2; \"retrain the model\" = a level-2 spec producing a level-3 equation. Cross-cutting (used by every method) Data preparation · scaling · distance metrics · validation · missing data · variable selection · outliers · measuring model quality. Project shape — 7 steps Frame the question → get & clean data → prepare it → fit candidate models → validate & choose one → estimate its true quality → interpret it for the organisation. Loop back when it isn't good enough. Only one box is \"doing analytics\". Acquiring and cleaning takes the largest share of the time."},{"id":"","h":"Cram sheet — Terms","t":"Terms Classification — putting things into categories. Response coded y = ±1 (not 1/0) so both classes collapse into one inequality. Hyperplane — the boundary. Parallel lines share a₁…aₙ and differ only in a₀ — which is why the intercept is the thing you slide when costs are unequal. Margin — the gap between the two parallel lines touching the nearest points. Support vectors — the points on the margin edges; only they determine the boundary, and that is where the name comes from. Hard vs soft margin — hard requires perfect separation; soft pays a penalty (hinge loss) for violations. KNN — no boundary at all. Keep every training point, classify by majority vote of the k closest. Multi-class needs no extra machinery. Equations a₁x₁ + … + aₙxₙ + a₀ = 0 the hyperplane blue: Σⱼ aⱼxᵢⱼ + a₀ ≥ +1 red: Σⱼ aⱼxᵢⱼ + a₀ ≤ −1 yᵢ ( Σⱼ aⱼxᵢⱼ + a₀ ) ≥ 1 for every i the two merged — why y = ±1 margin = 2 / √( Σⱼ aⱼ² ) coefficients in the denominator: small aⱼ ⇒ wide margin hard margin: min Σⱼ aⱼ² s.t. yᵢ(Σⱼ aⱼxᵢⱼ + a₀) ≥ 1 errorᵢ = max{ 0 , 1 − yᵢ(Σⱼ aⱼxᵢⱼ + a₀) } hinge loss: 0 when comfortably right soft margin: min Σᵢ max{0, 1 − yᵢ(Σⱼ aⱼxᵢⱼ + a₀)} + λ · Σⱼ aⱼ² scaling: xᵢⱼ' = (xᵢⱼ − x min,j) / (x max,j − x min,j) standardizing: xᵢⱼ' = (xᵢⱼ − μⱼ) / σⱼ cost 2:1 → intercept = ⅔(a₀ − 1) + ⅓(a₀ + 1) weighted blend, pulled toward the costly side Turn the knob λ ↑ — a wide margin outweighs mistakes on known points: simpler, more robust, more training errors. λ → 0 — avoiding mistakes outweighs a wide margin: narrow margin, fits noise, overfits. aⱼ ↓ — margin widens; they sit in the denominator. k ↑ (KNN) — smoother, more bias, less variance. k ↓ — jumpy, fits noise. Choose k by validation , not by a formula. Unequal costs — don't change the slope; slide the intercept toward the expensive mistake. Scaling vs standardizing Scaling → bounded [0,1], keeps the distribution's shape, easily dragged by one outlier. For another range [a,b]: take the 0–1 value, ×(b−a), +a. Standardizing → mean 0, sd 1, unbounded; reads as \"how many σ from the mean\"; better with roughly normal data or when outliers exist. Do it before fitting. Coefficients of 5 and 700 mean a 1% nudge to the big attribute moves the objective as much as a 600% change to the small one. Only after scaling can |aⱼ| be read as variable importance. Three different k's KNN: how many neighbours vote. k-means: how many clusters. k-fold: how many parts the data splits into."},{"id":"","h":"Cram sheet — Terms","t":"Terms Real vs random effects — every dataset holds both; a model fits both, but only the real ones reappear in new data. That is why a training score always flatters. Training set fits the model · validation set chooses among models · test set estimates the chosen model's true quality, once. Overfitting — fitting random effects. Leakage — held-out data influencing the fit. k-fold CV — split into k parts, train on k−1 and score on the held-out part, rotate, average. LOOCV — the extreme case, k = n. Bootstrap — resample n observations with replacement , B times, to measure how uncertain a statistic is. Equations & numbers E[test error] = variance + (bias)² + irreducible error all ≥ 0 — error can never beat the irreducible term CV(n) = (1/n) Σᵢ MSEᵢ , MSEᵢ = (yᵢ − ŷᵢ)² classification: CV(n) = (1/n) Σᵢ Errᵢ , Errᵢ = 1 if misclassified LOOCV shortcut (linear fits): CV(n) = (1/n) Σᵢ [ (yᵢ − ŷᵢ) / (1 − hᵢ) ]² hᵢ = leverage, between 1/n and 1 P(obs appears in a bootstrap sample) = 1 − (1 − 1/n)ⁿ → 1 − 1/e ≈ 63.2% ≈ 36.8% out-of-bag each fold model trains on (k − 1)/k of the data Turn the knob k ↑ — bias ↓ (each fit sees more data), variance ↑ (fold models near-identical, so their errors are correlated), cost ↑. k = 5 or 10 is the empirical compromise. k = 2 — trains on 50%, badly pessimistic. k = 5 → 80%. k = 10 → 90%. k = n — bias ≈ 0, highest variance, n fits. more models compared — the winner's validation score is more inflated by luck, so the separate test set matters more. Rules that carry marks Typical split: 20% test; the other 80% divided into k folds for train/validate. Quote the validation figure, not the training one (the lecture's 80%, not 90%) — and only if it was the single model you built. After choosing: retrain on all the data. Never deploy a fold model; never average fold coefficients. In four-fold CV each point helps train 3 of the 4 models and is held out exactly once. CV breaks when — preprocessing happened before the split; the data is ordered in time; rows aren't independent; the future won't resemble the past. Nested CV — needed when the same data both tunes and assesses."},{"id":"","h":"Cram sheet — Terms","t":"Terms Clustering — unsupervised: no labels given, the groups are discovered. Classification's categories exist beforehand. Centroid — the mean of the points in a cluster; k-means' \"center\". Heuristic — not guaranteed optimal, but fast and usually close. k-means is one. Voronoi diagram — assign every location to its nearest center and the space divides into regions; that is how a new point is predicted. Elbow — the kink in total-distance-against-k, where extra clusters stop buying much. Equations Euclidean: d = √( Σᵢ (xᵢ − yᵢ)² ) straight line Manhattan / rectilinear: d = Σᵢ |xᵢ − yᵢ| street grid p-norm (Minkowski order p): d = ( Σᵢ |xᵢ − yᵢ|ᵖ )^(1/p) ∞-norm: d = maxᵢ |xᵢ − yᵢ| only the biggest single gap counts k-means objective: min Σᵢ Σₖ yᵢₖ · d(xᵢ, zₖ) s.t. Σₖ yᵢₖ = 1 for every i cosine distance = 1 − (u·v)/(‖u‖‖v‖) (3,4) vs (6,8): Euclidean 5, cosine 0 Notation xᵢⱼ — attribute j of point i. Given. yᵢₖ — 1 if point i is in cluster k, else 0. Unknown. zₖⱼ — coordinate j of center k. Unknown. y and z depend on each other, which is why k-means alternates. The algorithm Pick k starting centers → assign every point to its nearest center (the \"maximization\" half — it minimises distance) → recompute each center as the centroid of its points (the \"expectation\" half — it is a mean) → repeat. Stop when no point changes cluster : membership unchanged ⇒ centroids unchanged ⇒ the next assignment repeats itself. A fixed point. Turn the knob k ↑ — total distance to centers always falls, so \"lowest total distance\" is a useless rule: it picks one cluster per point. Use the elbow, or a practical constraint (budget for four fire stations, not six). p ↑ — bigger coordinate differences dominate; at p = ∞ only the single largest gap matters (the warehouse case). restarts ↑ — k-means is a heuristic and depends on its starting centers; run it several times and keep the best. outliers — drag centroids, because a center is a mean. Deal with them first. Scale before any distance. Units silently weight every dimension."},{"id":"","h":"Cram sheet — The three problems, before any model","t":"The three problems, before any model Mismatched scales — income ≈ 10⁵ against credit score ≈ 10². Fixed by scaling / standardizing (Module 2, Lesson 7). A few extreme points — one or two points bend the answer. This module. Extraneous information — columns carrying noise, not signal. Fixed by variable selection / dimensionality reduction: by dropping or combining columns , not by transforming values. Terms Data preparation — everything done between collecting the data and handing it to a model. What \"assume the data is given\" is quietly assuming. Outlier — a point very different from the rest of the dataset. Context-dependent: a value can be ordinary on its own and impossible in its neighbourhood. IQR — 75th percentile minus 25th. Box and whisker plot Box edges = 25th and 75th percentiles. Line inside = the median, the 50th. Whiskers = a chosen reasonable range — 10th/90th, or 5th/95th. Every point beyond a whisker is drawn individually: those are the candidates , not verdicts. software default whiskers: furthest point within Q1 − 1.5·IQR … Q3 + 1.5·IQR P(|x − μ| > 2σ) = 4.55% · P(|x − μ| > 3σ) = 0.270% two-sided; ≈ 2,700 per million Turn the knob whiskers wider (5th/95th, or the 1.5·IQR rule) — fewer points flagged. narrower (10th/90th) — more flagged, more to investigate. The plot does not decide the count; you do, so always ask which convention produced a reported number. n ↑ — expect more extreme points, not fewer. About 2,700 in a million normal readings sit beyond 3σ and are perfectly genuine. Finding vs judging One dimension → box plot. Many dimensions → no good general pre-model test; instead fit a model first and look where it is most wrong (the largest residual against a smooth fit). Detection only produces candidates. Then ask what caused it : Error (typo, broken sensor) → correct or remove. Real, different mechanism → split into two models rather than forcing one. Real and still unusable → keep it, but don't let it drive the fit; record the decision. You cannot answer \"which kind is it?\" from inside the dataset."},{"id":"","h":"Cram sheet — Terms","t":"Terms Change detection — deciding, as data arrives, whether the process has shifted, early enough to act and without over-reacting to noise. Why not a hypothesis test — it answers \"did it change?\" once enough data has been collected. Here the answer is needed while the data is still arriving. CUSUM — a cumulative sum of deviations from μ, slack-adjusted, floored at zero. μ the expected value when nothing has changed · C the allowance: how much variation you agree to ignore · T the threshold: how much evidence before you call it. Equations increase: S⁺ₜ = max{ 0 , S⁺ₜ₋₁ + (xₜ − μ − C) } , S₀ = 0 decrease: S⁻ₜ = max{ 0 , S⁻ₜ₋₁ + (μ − xₜ − C) } ≡ max{ 0 , S⁻ₜ₋₁ − (xₜ − μ) − C } alarm when Sₜ ≥ T run both recursions to watch both directions industrial notation: C⁺ᵢ , C⁻ᵢ with μ₀ = μ , K = C , H = T identical; the \"tabular CUSUM\" EWMA: zₜ = λxₜ + (1 − λ)zₜ₋₁ , z₀ = μ ; limits μ ± Lσ√( λ/(2−λ)·[1 − (1−λ)^2t] ) λ ≈ 0.05–0.25, L = 3 What each piece is for running total — carries evidence forward, so a small persistent shift accumulates into a signal. − C — subtracts a little each period so ordinary noise doesn't accumulate. max{0, ·} — throws the total away whenever the evidence turns negative, so noise can't build a head start. Turn the knob C ↓ or T ↓ — changes detected sooner, false alarms more frequent. Right when late detection is the expensive mistake (every chip made before detection is scrapped). C ↑ or T ↑ — false alarms rarer, real changes take longer to surface. Right when interrupting a healthy process is the expensive mistake. Why not C = 0 and raise T instead? With C = 0 the total drifts upward on noise alone — in the example it reached 119 against a 150 threshold with nothing wrong. C keeps the baseline near zero instead. Do not update μ as new data arrives — it would absorb the very shift you are trying to detect. Worked example — μ = 300, +100 added from t = 11 C = 0, T = 450 → alarm at t = 14. Slow, but noise peaked at 119, never close. C = 0, T = 150 → alarm at t = 11. Fast, but noise had already reached 119 — 79% of the threshold. C = 15, T = 150 → alarm at t = 12. One period later, and the total never exceeded 23 beforehand."},{"id":"","h":"Cram sheet — Terms","t":"Terms Time series — the same response recorded period after period. It moves for three reasons: trend , cyclical/seasonal variation, and randomness . Exponential smoothing — today's estimate is a blend of the newest reading and yesterday's belief. S baseline · T trend · C seasonal factor · L cycle length. Holt = trend added. Holt-Winters = trend + seasonality. Stationary — mean, variance and correlation structure don't drift; what ARIMA needs, reached by differencing. Equations — smoothing Sₜ = α·xₜ + (1 − α)·Sₜ₋₁ , 0 < α < 1 , S₁ = x₁ with trend: Sₜ = α·xₜ + (1 − α)(Sₜ₋₁ + Tₜ₋₁) Tₜ = β(Sₜ − Sₜ₋₁) + (1 − β)Tₜ₋₁ , T₁ = 0 seasonal (multiplicative): Sₜ = α(xₜ / Cₜ₋L) + (1 − α)(Sₜ₋₁ + Tₜ₋₁) Cₜ = γ(xₜ / Sₜ) + (1 − γ)Cₜ₋L , first L factors = 1 forecasts: Fₜ₊₁ = Sₜ · Sₜ + Tₜ · (Sₜ + Tₜ)·C₍ₜ₊₁₎₋L unrolled: Sₜ = αxₜ + α(1−α)xₜ₋₁ + α(1−α)²xₜ₋₂ + … weight k periods back = α(1−α)ᵏ error-correction form: Fₜ₊₁ = Fₜ + α(xₜ − Fₜ) new forecast = old + α × last error fitting: min Σₜ (Fₜ − xₜ)² over α, β, γ s.t. 0 ≤ α, β, γ ≤ 1 Equations — ARIMA & GARCH ARIMA(p,d,q): Dₜ = μ + Σᵢ₌₁..p φᵢDₜ₋ᵢ + Σᵢ₌₁..q θᵢεₜ₋ᵢ + εₜ D = the d-th differences, not the raw values GARCH(p,q): σ²ₜ = ω + Σᵢ βᵢσ²ₜ₋ᵢ + Σᵢ γᵢε²ₜ₋ᵢ models the variance, not the value RiskMetrics: σ²ₜ = λσ²ₜ₋₁ + (1 − λ)ε²ₜ₋₁ , λ = 0.94 daily smoothing on squared returns eₜ = Fₜ − xₜ ; MAE = mean|eₜ| ; RMSE = √(mean eₜ²) ; MAPE = mean|eₜ / xₜ| Turn the knob α ↑ — trust the newest reading: short memory, responsive, jumpy. α ↓ — long memory, smooth, slow to react. Large randomness ⇒ small α. α = 1 — believe every reading (that is the naive forecast). α = 0 — never update. β, γ — the same trade-off applied to the trend and to the seasonal factors. d ↑ — more differencing to remove drift; stock prices usually need d = 1. p, q — read off the differenced series: ACF dies away + PACF cuts off at lag p → AR(p); ACF cuts off at lag q + PACF dies away → MA(q); both die away → try small p and q and compare on AIC/BIC. Rules that carry marks Multiplicative seasonality scales with the level; additive is a fixed size. Use C₍ₜ₋L₎, not Cₜ₋₁ — the most recent factor from the same stage of the cycle. If today is Monday, use last Monday's. ARIMA is short-term, wants stable data and roughly 40+ observations; exponential smoothing handles jumpier, spikier series. Familiar models in disguise: naive = ARIMA(0,1,0); white noise = ARIMA(0,0,0). Never use shuffled k-fold on a time series — training on March and July to \"predict\" May leaks the answer. Use walk-forward : fit 1–40 → forecast 41 → record the error → add 41 → refit → repeat → average. Signed errors cancel: +8, −8, +6, −6 gives mean error 0 but MAE 7 and RMSE ≈ 7.07. Beat the benchmarks: naive Fₜ₊₁ = xₜ, seasonal naive Fₜ₊₁ = x₍ₜ₊₁₎₋L. Leftover errors should look like white noise."},{"id":"","h":"Cram sheet — Terms","t":"Terms Regression — estimate a numeric response from predictors . Two uses: how a system works (read the coefficients) and what will happen (read the predictions). Not directly prescriptive. aⱼ — change in predicted y per one-unit rise in xⱼ, others held fixed. Units: response per predictor unit. Residual — yᵢ − ŷᵢ, measured vertically in response units. Likelihood — probability (density) of the observed data given the parameters. Maximum likelihood : the parameters that make it largest, value L*. Correlation — move together. Causation — one drives the other. Outside factor — drives both. Interaction — a product term x₁x₂: the effect of one attribute depends on the other. R² — share of variability accounted for. Adjusted R² — the same, charged for each attribute. Equations ŷ = a₀ + Σⱼ aⱼxⱼ simple linear: ŷ = a₀ + a₁x₁ SSE = Σᵢ ( yᵢ − (a₀ + Σⱼ aⱼxᵢⱼ) )² minimise over a — convex, so set partials to 0 normal iid errors: max L ⇔ min SSE AIC = 2k − 2 ln L* regression: n·ln(SSE/n) + 2k + const AICc = AIC + 2k(k+1)/(n − k − 1) finite-sample correction BIC = k·ln n − 2 ln L* heavier than AIC once n ≥ 8 relative likelihood of worse model = exp( (AIC_low − AIC_high)/2 ) Δ = 4 → 13.5%, Δ = 6 → 5% y = a₀ + a₁x₁ + a₂x₁² · y = a₀ + a₁x₁ + a₂x₂ + a₃x₁x₂ effect of x₁ = a₁ + a₃x₂ Box-Cox: y(λ) = (y^λ − 1)/λ, y(0) = ln y needs y > 0; λ=1 none, ½ root, 0 log t = aⱼ / SE(aⱼ) · 95% CI ≈ aⱼ ± 2·SE(aⱼ) R² = 1 − SSE/SST · adj R² = 1 − (1 − R²)(n − 1)/(n − m − 1) Turn the knob k ↑ — likelihood and R² can only improve; AIC/BIC/adj R² decide if it paid. Under AIC a parameter must raise ln L* by more than 1. n ↑ — BIC penalty per parameter (ln n) grows, AICc correction shrinks toward AIC, and p-values shrink even for trivial effects. p threshold ↑ (0.10) — more attributes kept, more irrelevant ones in. ↓ (0.01) — fewer kept, more relevant ones lost. more tests — more false positives: 100 null attributes at 0.02 → about 2 pass by chance. ΔBIC — >10 very likely · 6–10 likely · 2–6 somewhat · 0–2 slightly better. Rules that carry marks “Linear” = linear in the coefficients . x², ln x, x₁x₂ are new columns; fitting is unchanged. Reverse the regression and the p-value and fit are identical — quality metrics can’t show direction. Causation rules of thumb: cause before effect · makes sense · no outside factor (hardest). Rule of thumb: drop attributes with p > 0.05. Most p-values are clearly tiny (≤ 0.001) or large (≥ 0.3). Significant ≠ important: check coefficient × realistic change against the size of the response. p-value, t and CI are the same test; the estimate is the size. Compare AIC/BIC only across models on the same data. Use BIC only when n ≫ k. R² of 0.3–0.4 can be good for human behaviour. Lecture vs standard: p-value = P(estimate this extreme | true coefficient 0), not P(coefficient = 0)."},{"id":"","h":"Cram sheet — The eight points the instructor kept returning to","t":"The eight points the instructor kept returning to 1 · Wider margin — not aesthetics. A wide gap tolerates measurement error and new points landing slightly off; a narrow one flips classes on tiny perturbations. 2 · Scaling vs standardization — scaling bounds to [0,1] and keeps the shape but is dragged by outliers; standardizing gives mean 0, sd 1, is unbounded, and suits roughly normal data. 3 · KNN or SVM — decided by the shape of the boundary and the size of the data, not by which is \"better\". SVM: a compact rule with coefficients, fast at prediction time, wants a broadly linear separation. KNN: no boundary, handles irregular and local structure and many classes, but stores every point and slows as n grows. 4 · Units weight distance — every distance-based method silently gives the biggest-numbered attribute the most say. Recording dollars instead of thousands changes the answer. Scale first. 5 · Validation bias — every dataset holds real and random patterns, so the model that wins on validation won partly on luck and its error rises on the test set. That is why one set can't both choose and report. 6 · Classification vs clustering — supervised against unsupervised; whether the categories arrived with the data or were discovered. The phrasing test: \"which of these two groups?\" against \"which groups are there?\" 7 · Kinds of outlier — finding one and judging it are different problems. Easy to find and hard to judge is the common case, and the dataset alone can never say whether a point is an error, a rare real event, or the most informative row you have. 8 · C and T — T is how much evidence before you call it; C is how much variation you agree to ignore. Don't set C = 0 and raise T instead — the total then drifts up on noise. And don't update μ as data arrives."},{"id":"","h":"Cram sheet — Eight checkpoints — the recurring exam topics","t":"Eight checkpoints — the recurring exam topics Validation vs test data — validation chooses among models; test estimates the winner's true quality, once. Classification vs clustering — labels given, or groups discovered. Margins in SVM — margin = 2/√(Σⱼ aⱼ²); wider is more robust to new points. Scaling vs standardization — (x − x min)/(x max − x min) against (x − μ)/σ; bounded and outlier-sensitive against unbounded and outlier-tolerant. KNN vs SVM — boundary shape, number of classes, size of the data, cost at prediction time. Why feature scale matters for distance — the attribute with the largest numbers dominates every distance and every margin term. Kinds of outlier — detection produces candidates only; the cause decides what you do about them. CUSUM: C, T and the fixed mean — C trades false alarms against detection delay, T sets the evidence required, μ stays fixed. How to use it Answer, read the reasoning, move on. Nothing is timed and nothing is saved between visits — work through it once your handwritten sheet is drafted, and write down every item you had to look up."}]},{"f":"module-1-introduction.html","t":"Module 1: Foundations","s":[{"id":"l1","h":"What analytics answers","t":"Lesson 1 What analytics answers Analytics answers three kinds of question — what happened, what will happen, and what to do about it — and modeling is the act of turning a situation into math and the math back into a recommendation. The three parts of analytics modeling Across almost any applied project, the critical work breaks into three pieces: Selecting and specifying the right model or models to combine into a solution. Compiling, building and/or forecasting the necessary data sets. Interpreting the model’s output to make suggestions that match the organization’s needs, priorities and structure. Notice that only the middle third is what most people picture as “doing analytics.” The first and last thirds are judgement about the organization, and the whole subject is shaped around that. Frame for the term Which of the three parts does an algorithm actually do for you? Show answer Only part of the middle one. Choosing the model and interpreting its output for a specific organization are both yours — which is why Module 4 later calls this “the art of analytics.” The three kinds of question Click through the diagram, then read on. Four regions are clickable, each with worked examples. Open all four to finish this activity. quick check answered Which of these is prescriptive ? What will this asset be worth a year from now, and how uncertain is that estimate? How should traffic signals be timed to minimize delay during a large event? How much effect did the new checkout layout have on average basket size? quick check answered “Which customers behave most alike, and which attributes drive that similarity?” This counts as descriptive. Why not predictive? Because it would be answered with clustering, and clustering isn’t a predictive method. Because it asks for an explanation of patterns already present in past behaviour, not for a future value. Because you can answer it with certainty, and predictive questions always carry uncertainty. Exam target Given an unfamiliar business question, how do you decide which of the three it is? Show answer Ask what the answer would be. An explanation of something past → descriptive. A value for something not yet observed → predictive. A course of action, usually with an objective to optimize → prescriptive. What “modeling” means here Modeling, in analytics Taking a real-life situation and expressing the key parts of that situation in terms of math — so we can analyze the math, and then turn the mathematical analysis back into a real-life answer, solution or recommendation. Read that as a round trip with three legs: situation → math , then analysis , then math → recommendation . Most teaching covers the middle leg, but the two translations at either end are where analytics work actually succeeds or fails. The word “key” is doing real work too — a model expresses the key parts, not all of them. Class discussion — Lesson 1 click to type; saves automatically Worth memorising Can you state the definition of modeling in one breath? Show answer Expressing the key parts of a real-life situation in terms of math, so the math can be analyzed and the analysis turned back into a real-life answer. It reappears in Lesson 4."},{"id":"l2","h":"The map of methods","t":"Lesson 2 The map of methods A survey course gives you a wide, shallow toolbox on purpose — enough of each method to recognise when it fits, not enough to derive it. Knowing the shape of the toolbox is the point. About this lesson The source lectures cover course logistics here rather than analytics content. That material has been left out, and this lesson is orientation written for these notes instead — a survey of the field so the later modules have somewhere to sit. It is not lecture content, and your syllabus is the authority on what is actually examinable. What a survey course is A survey course trades depth for breadth deliberately. You get enough mathematical intuition about each method to understand what it does, what it assumes, and where it breaks — but not the full theory, and not every advanced variant. You will see the most commonly used version of each model and then move on. That sounds like a limitation. It is actually the skill being taught: most real analytics failures are choosing the wrong method, not implementing the right one badly . Recognising that a question is prescriptive and needs optimization rather than a classifier is worth more than knowing one algorithm perfectly. What breadth buys you The ability to look at an unfamiliar problem and say: this is a classification question, the data is unlabelled so it might be clustering first, the costs are asymmetric so accuracy is the wrong metric, and the attributes are on wildly different scales so it needs standardizing. None of that requires deep theory. All of it requires knowing the map. Set expectations If you finish a course like this and don’t feel expert in any single method, has something gone wrong? Show answer No — that’s the intended outcome. Depth in one method is what specialist study is for. What you should have is the judgement to pick a method and the vocabulary to go and learn it properly. The families of methods Almost everything you’ll meet falls into one of these groups. Click through the map, then check yourself against it. Five regions are clickable. Open all five to finish this activity. quick check answered “Which of our customers behave alike? We have no labels.” Which family fits? Clustering Classification Optimization Time series forecasting quick check answered “Will this applicant repay? We have thousands of past outcomes.” Which family fits? Clustering Classification Regression Optimization quick check answered “Where should we place four depots to minimise driving time?” Which family fits? Clustering Optimization Simulation Dimensionality reduction quick check answered “How many units will we sell each week next quarter?” Which family fits? Classification Time series forecasting Clustering Optimization quick check answered Why is it worth over-learning the cross-cutting concepts relative to any single model? Because they’re simpler, so the effort pays off faster. Because they apply to every method that follows, so the investment keeps paying. Because they’re the parts most likely to appear on an exam. Class discussion — Lesson 2 click to type; saves automatically Orientation Where do the other modules in these notes sit on the map? Show answer Module 2 (classification) is predictive. Module 4 (clustering) is descriptive. Module 3 (validation) is cross-cutting — which is why it applies to both of the others and to everything after them."},{"id":"l3","h":"How a project runs","t":"Lesson 3 How a project runs Fitting the model is one step out of seven, and rarely the one that decides whether the project works. About this lesson Like Lesson 2, this is orientation written for these notes rather than lecture content. The individual pieces — validation, scaling, variable selection — are covered properly in later modules; this is the frame they hang on. The end-to-end shape Practitioners consistently report that acquiring and cleaning the data takes the largest share of the time. Framing and interpretation bookend the technical work and decide whether it gets used. quick check answered Which sequence is the correct end-to-end shape of a project? Get and clean data → frame the question → prepare → fit → validate → test → interpret Frame the question → get and clean data → prepare it → fit candidate models → validate and choose one → estimate its true quality → interpret for the organization Frame the question → get and clean data → fit candidate models → prepare it → validate → interpret → estimate quality Frame the question → get and clean data → prepare it → fit → estimate true quality → validate and choose one → interpret quick check answered What comes immediately after “compare candidates on validation data and pick one”? Translate the output into a recommendation. Estimate the chosen model’s quality on data used for neither fitting nor choosing. Prepare the data: handle missing values and scale attributes. quick check answered The diagram draws an arrow looping back when the result “isn’t good enough.” What does that loop tell you about the workflow? That the project has failed and should be restarted from the question. That preparation and model specification get revisited, rather than the pipeline running once end to end. That you should always collect more data when a model underperforms. Reality check Which stage typically consumes the most time? Show answer Getting and cleaning the data — routinely reported as the majority of the effort on real projects. Coursework hands you tidy datasets, which makes this the biggest gap between studying analytics and doing it. The tools No single tool matters as much as the habit of picking up new ones. Whatever is standard now will have shifted in a few years, and the reason coursework often withholds step-by-step software instructions is that copying steps teaches you only those steps. Tool What it’s for R The traditional statistics environment. Strong for classical modelling, and most course-level analytics packages have a mature R implementation Python General-purpose, dominant for machine learning and for anything that has to run inside a larger system SQL Getting the data out in the first place. Underrated, and the step that precedes everything else Spreadsheets Genuinely fine for small problems, and often what your audience will want the result in Optimization solvers Specialist engines for prescriptive problems, usually driven from R or Python rather than used directly The skill worth building Being able to use unfamiliar software effectively when nobody tells you which steps to take. Assignments that make you work this out are slower on purpose — the transferable skill is learning a package on your own, not the package. quick check answered Which problem does scaling or standardization solve? Attributes on incompatible units A model that flatters itself on its own data Missing values you can’t simply discard Attributes that add noise rather than signal quick check answered Which problem does validation solve? Attributes on incompatible units A model that flatters itself on its own data Missing values you can’t simply discard Attributes that add noise rather than signal quick check answered Which problem does imputation solve? Attributes on incompatible units A model that flatters itself on its own data Missing values you can’t simply discard Attributes that add noise rather than signal quick check answered Which problem does variable selection solve? Attributes on incompatible units A model that flatters itself on its own data Missing values you can’t simply discard Attributes that add noise rather than signal quick check answered A colleague reports that their model is 94% accurate on the data they built it from. What is the first thing to ask? Whether accuracy is the right metric given how the two error types differ in cost. Whether that 94% was measured on data the model had never seen. Whether the dataset was large enough for the figure to be stable. Class discussion — Lesson 3 click to type; saves automatically Practical Why do assignments often refuse to give step-by-step software instructions? Show answer Because copying steps transfers nothing. The skill being built is working out how to use a tool without instructions — which is what you’ll be doing for the rest of your career, on packages that don’t exist yet."},{"id":"l4","h":"What is a model?","t":"Lesson 4 What is a model? “Model” means at least three different things in analytics, often within a single paragraph — so learn to read which one is meant from the level of detail on the table. Three zoom levels, one word Modeling means expressing the key parts of a real-life situation in math; the mathematical expression of that situation is called a model. Confusingly, there are at least three ways the word gets used. Here they are on one worked example — predicting how long a parcel takes to arrive. Each box is called a model. The outer one names only the approach; the inner one is a specific equation with numbers in it. Which sense is meant is almost always readable from how much detail is on the table. quick check answered “We’ll use regression to predict delivery times.” Which sense of “model” is in play? The method being used The method plus its chosen inputs The specific fitted equation quick check answered “Regression on parcel size, weight and shipping distance.” Which sense? The method being used The method plus its chosen inputs The specific fitted equation quick check answered “days ≈ 1.4 + 0.02·size + 0.05·weight + 0.003·distance”. Which sense? The method being used The method plus its chosen inputs The specific fitted equation quick check answered Later you read: “we’ll compare five different SVM models.” Which sense of the word is that, and how can you tell? The method — SVM is named, so it’s level 1. A specification — five SVMs means five different setups, so the variation is below the method. A fitted equation — comparing models means comparing their coefficients. Class discussion — Lesson 4 click to type; saves automatically Why this matters later Module 3 says “pick the best of ten models, then retrain the model on all the data.” How many senses of the word are in that sentence? Show answer Two. The ten you compare are specifications; the thing you retrain is a specification that then produces a fitted equation. Reading it as one sense is exactly how people end up thinking they should deploy one of the fold models."},{"id":"targets","h":"Transfer practice — Foundations","t":"Transfer practice — Foundations Same ideas, unfamiliar settings Why these are here Each prompt below moves the module’s ideas into a situation the lessons never mentioned. If you can only answer a question in the wording it was taught in, what you have memorised is the example rather than the concept — and an exam that changes the setting will find that out. Answer these out loud before opening them. Exam target A hospital wants to know which patients are likely to be readmitted within thirty days. Which of the three kinds of question is that? Show answer Predictive — the answer is a value not yet observed for a particular patient. The same setting supports the other two: what characterises the patients who were readmitted last year is descriptive, and given a fixed number of follow-up calls, whom should we call is prescriptive. The subject matter does not determine the type; the shape of the answer does. Exam target A logistics firm asks how to route sixty deliveries across eight vehicles to minimise total driving time. Which kind, and what is the giveaway? Show answer Prescriptive. The giveaway is the objective and the constraint: there is something to minimise and a limited resource to allocate. The answer is a course of action, not a description or an estimate. Exam target A colleague says the team has “retrained the model.” Which sense of the word is in play? Show answer The second level — a method together with its chosen variables — being used to produce the third, a fitted equation with new numbers in it. The method has not changed and the variables have not changed; only the coefficients have. Saying “we changed the model” would be ambiguous between all three. Exam target A project's model validates well, but six months later nobody in the organisation is using its output. Which of the three legs of modeling failed? Show answer The third: translating the mathematics back into a recommendation someone can act on. Validation only measures the middle leg. A model can be technically correct and still fail because the output arrives in a form, at a time, or with a degree of explanation that the decision cannot use. Exam target Name three concerns that would apply whether the project ends up using regression, a support vector machine, or k-means. Show answer Any three of: scaling, validation, missing data, outliers, variable selection, the choice of distance measure, and how model quality is to be measured. These are the cross-cutting concerns — the reason they are taught separately is that they do not belong to any one method. Exam target You are handed a dataset and asked to “find something interesting in it.” What is the first thing to establish? Show answer What decision the answer is meant to inform, which determines which of the three kinds of question you are answering. Without that, there is no basis for choosing a method, no criterion for whether a finding is interesting, and nothing to validate against."},{"id":"sec-6","h":"How the module fits together","t":"How the module fits together The through-line across the four lessons Lesson 1 Sets the scope: three question types, and modeling as a round trip from situation to math and back. Lesson 2 Lays out the toolbox, grouped by which question type each family answers. Lesson 3 Shows where the modelling actually sits in a project, and what surrounds it. Lesson 4 Clears one piece of vocabulary that would otherwise confuse every later topic. Four things worth noticing Classify the question before reaching for a method. Descriptive, predictive or prescriptive narrows the toolbox immediately. Starting from the method you happen to know is how people end up forcing a classifier onto an optimization problem. The two ends of the round trip are the hard parts. Situation → math and math → recommendation are where the value of an analytics professional sits. The middle leg is what a computer does. The same argument returns in Module 4 as “the art of analytics.” Cross-cutting beats method-specific. Validation, scaling and variable selection get used against every model you ever build. That’s where the compounding return is. The three senses of “model” resolve a real later confusion. “Which model do we deploy after cross-validation?” only sounds paradoxical if you collapse the specification and the fitted equation into one word. Likely exam targets Classify a business question as descriptive, predictive or prescriptive, with a reason. Give the definition of modeling and name the three legs of the round trip. Name the three parts of analytics modeling from Lesson 1. Given a scenario, name a suitable family of method and say why. Distinguish a method family from a cross-cutting concept. Give the three senses of the word “model” with an example of each. Where the questions went The full multiple-choice bank for this material now lives on the Exam Prep page, under Topics 9 to 11. The instructor’s follow-up notes — margin width, scaling versus standardization, KNN against SVM — are on the Discussion page. Module 1 — my own summary click to type; saves automatically Written by an AI model. Not reviewed by any instructor or institution — verify anything you rely on. Lessons 1 and 4 summarise lecture recordings with generic examples substituted for the ones used in class. Lessons 2 and 3 are not lecture content at all — the source lectures covered course logistics there, and general orientation to the field has been written in its place so the later modules have context. Treat those two lessons as background reading, not as examinable material, and check your syllabus for what is actually covered."}]},{"f":"module-2-classification.html","t":"Module 2: Classification","s":[{"id":"l1","h":"What classification is","t":"Lesson 1 What classification is Classification means putting things into categories — and the whole problem reduces to a picture where you draw a line between two colours of dot, then argue about which line. The definition, and it really is that simple Classification Putting things into categories. The term carries exactly its everyday meaning — nothing special is intended by it in analytics. The simplest cases have two categories, and often those are just yes and no: Which loan applicants will fully repay, and which won’t Which email is real and which is spam Which documents are relevant to a case and which are irrelevant Which people passing through a screening point warrant a second look Whether a donor organ is safe to transplant or carries an infectious disease That last one shows why a model is needed at all rather than just a test. If a donor was infected very recently, a laboratory test may not come back positive yet — so an analytical approach has to fill the gap the test leaves. More than two categories works too: sorting voters into supportive, opposed and undecided; or assigning a newly found bone to one of many species. quick check answered Which of these problems does not sort cases into exactly two categories? A campaign sorting voters by their position on a candidate An email filter separating real mail from spam A transplant team judging whether a donor organ is safe A lender deciding which applicants will fully repay Worth noticing Why can’t the transplant question be settled with a lab test alone? Show answer A recent infection may not have become detectable yet, so a negative test isn’t conclusive. The model uses other attributes to estimate risk the test can’t yet see. What the data looks like Each classification question needs data. For lending, that might be income, credit history, age, family size, assets and liabilities. Combine those attributes of previous borrowers with the observed outcome — repaid or not — and you can build a model for future applicants. Graphically, put credit score on the horizontal axis and household income on the vertical. An applicant with a credit score of 730 and an income of $76,000 is one point. Blue if they repaid the whole loan, red if they defaulted. Real problems have more factors — length of relationship with the bank, number of dependents, total assets, liabilities — and each is another dimension. Two dimensions are used throughout this module purely because they can be drawn. The move that sets up everything else Draw a line separating blue above from red below. A new applicant landing in the blue zone gets a loan; one landing in the red zone doesn’t. That works — but there are infinitely many lines that separate these points equally well. Which is best? quick check answered Why is “infinitely many lines separate the points” a problem rather than a convenience? Because searching an infinite set of candidate lines is computationally impossible. Because the lines disagree about how to classify the historical data you already have. Because they all handle the known data identically but give different answers for new applicants. Class discussion — Lesson 1 click to type; saves automatically Exam target What are the two ingredients a classification model needs from historical data? Show answer The attributes of each past case, and the observed outcome for each — which category it actually turned out to belong to. Without the second, this would be clustering, not classification."},{"id":"l2","h":"Choosing a classifier","t":"Lesson 2 Choosing a classifier Prefer the line that is furthest from making a mistake — then bend that rule when one kind of mistake costs far more than the other. Distance from error Two candidate classifiers will agree about most applicants. It’s the ambiguous ones — sitting between the groups — where one says lend and the other says decline. The principle: choose a line that is further from making mistakes . A line passing close to a red point and a blue point has nearly misclassified both. If the line, or the points, were slightly different, it would. And the input data is inexact — a recorded income may be a little off. Small uncertainties in the data shouldn’t flip a decision. A line sitting well clear of every point would need a much bigger data error before it misclassified anything. Lesson 4 formalises this as maximising the margin . Four regions are clickable. Open all four to finish this activity. Vocabulary Hard classifier versus soft classifier — what separates them? Show answer A hard classifier separates the classes perfectly. A soft one accepts some misclassification and aims for the best separation available, which is what you need when no perfect boundary exists. When one mistake costs more So far both errors have been treated as equally bad. Often they aren’t. Lending to someone who won’t repay costs more than turning away someone who would have. Eating a poisonous plant is far worse than passing up an edible one. The rule The more costly one type of mistake is, the further you move the line away from it. If a bad loan is judged twice as costly as a rejected good applicant, shift the line closer to the blue points. Now an ambiguous applicant — nearer the blue group but still in the contested zone — gets declined anyway. With a life at stake you might go further still. Even when the data looks perfectly separable, you might deliberately misclassify some safe points in order to push the boundary further from any dangerous ones. Foragers do exactly this: anything that even resembles a poisonous species gets left alone. The same logic runs through medical screening. Missing a case of an infectious disease is very costly, because someone may unknowingly infect others. So the initial test is set with a deliberately aggressive threshold, and anyone who tests positive takes a second, confirmatory test. SO Sam Okafor Risk analyst, small business lending Sam’s classifier separates repaid from defaulted loans cleanly on five years of history. Finance tells him a default costs roughly four times what a wrongly declined applicant costs in lost margin. A colleague: “The line already gets 100% of the historical cases right. There’s nothing to change.” What should Sam do? Nothing. A perfect score means the boundary is already correct. Shift the line toward the repaid side, since he can move it freely inside the gap. Remove the weakest attribute to make the boundary simpler and more robust. Six months later. New data arrives and the classes now overlap — no line separates them. Sam’s shifted boundary now misclassifies a visible handful of applicants who did repay. Re-centre the line to minimise the total number of misclassified points. Keep the asymmetry and weight the two error types differently in the objective. Exam target Which way do you move the boundary when one error is more costly? Show answer Away from the costly side — so that ambiguous cases fall on the cheap-error side. Moving it toward the costly class would do the opposite of what you want. A free insight from the picture Suppose you assumed two attributes were needed, and the fitted classifier turns out to run almost parallel to the vertical axis. Then only the horizontal attribute is really doing any work — the vertical one barely affects the decision. You’ve discovered that one attribute suffices. This observation returns in Lesson 6 as a method that works in dimensions you can’t draw. Class discussion — Lesson 2 click to type; saves automatically Check A classifier is almost parallel to the horizontal axis. Which attribute is irrelevant? Show answer The horizontal one. The boundary changes with the vertical attribute and barely with the horizontal, so the horizontal attribute isn’t driving the decision. Read the direction the line varies in, not the axis it lies along."},{"id":"l3","h":"Data definitions","t":"Lesson 3 Data definitions A table where rows are data points and columns are attributes — plus one special column, the response, that holds the answer. The mental model Analytics talk almost always assumes data sits in a table. The response column is the one that makes this classification rather than clustering. Take it away and the algorithm has nothing to learn against. Data point A row. One observation — one applicant, or one day of sales Attribute A column. Also called a feature , and depending on use, a covariate or predictor Response The special column holding the answer for each data point. Also called the outcome For loan applicants the response is whether they repaid the full loan — or it could be the fraction repaid, from 0 to 100%. For daily sales it’s the number of sales that day. Vocabulary Four words for a column, and one for the special column. Name them. Show answer Attribute, feature, covariate, predictor. The special one is the response, also called the outcome. Types of data Structured Data that can be described and stored neatly. Credit score, age and number of sales are stored as numbers; a category might be stored as a single letter or one of a few fixed words. Unstructured Data that isn’t easily described and stored. Text is the most common example — free-written incident reports, say, where every author writes differently and the text must be parsed before any trend can be found. Within structured data: Quantitative The numbers mean something. Higher means more, lower means less. Age, sales, temperature, income Categorical The number only says which category. Postcodes are the classic case — moving from one to another doesn’t mean anything increased or decreased Binary A special case of categorical with exactly two values. Repaid or not, holiday or not, on or off. Binary data can sometimes be treated as quantitative even though it is categorical Time series Sometimes data points are unrelated — each loan applicant is a different person. Sometimes they’re related in time: put daily sales in chronological order and reading down the column shows sales day after day. Often recorded at equal intervals (daily sales, a stock price every two seconds, a child’s height each birthday), but not necessarily. quick check answered A dataset has a postcode column stored as an integer. What is the risk of feeding it straight into a distance-based model? The model will read the numeric gaps as distances, when the numbers only label categories. Postcodes are too coarse a location measure to be precise enough for a model. are present.\">Postcodes are often missing, so the model will silently drop those rows. quick check answered Which column is structured but categorical ? Whether the day was a holiday Household income in dollars The free text of an incident report Postcode Class discussion — Lesson 3 click to type; saves automatically Cross-module link Where does the response column reappear later? Show answer In Module 4. Having an observed response is precisely what makes classification supervised; clustering has no response column at all, which is what makes it unsupervised."},{"id":"l4","h":"Support vector machines","t":"Lesson 4 Support vector machines “Find the line furthest from mistakes” becomes: minimise the sum of squared coefficients, subject to every point landing on its correct side. Notation m Number of data points n Number of attributes per point x ij The j th attribute of data point i — so x i1 is person i ’s credit score, x i2 their income y i The response: +1 for a blue point (repaid in full), −1 for a red point (defaulted) a 1 …a n One coefficient per attribute — these define the line’s direction a 0 The intercept — this slides the line without turning it a₁x₁ + a₂x₂ + … + aₙxₙ + a₀ = 0 A line (or in n dimensions, a hyperplane). Parallel lines share a₁…aₙ and differ only in a₀ — which is exactly why the intercept is the thing you slide in Lesson 6. Check What geometrically distinguishes two parallel lines in this notation? Show answer Only the intercept a₀. The coefficients a₁…aₙ set the direction, so keeping them fixed and changing a₀ slides the line without rotating it. Stating “classified correctly” Blue points must sit on one side and red points on the other: blue: Σⱼ aⱼx ij + a₀ ≥ 1 red: Σⱼ aⱼx ij + a₀ ≤ −1 The 1 is there for scaling and could be any number. Because y i was defined as +1 for blue and −1 for red, both conditions collapse into one: ( Σⱼ aⱼx ij + a₀ ) · y i ≥ 1 for every point i This is the reason the response is coded ±1 rather than 1 and 0. Multiplying by −1 flips the inequality for red points, which is exactly what merging the two conditions requires. quick check answered Why is the response coded as +1 and −1 rather than 1 and 0? Because multiplying by −1 flips the red inequality, letting both classes share a single constraint. Because it keeps the two classes symmetric around zero, which makes the maths tidier to write. Because a model needs inputs centred on zero before it can be fitted. Exam target Write the single combined constraint from memory. Show answer y i (Σⱼ aⱼx ij + a₀) ≥ 1, required for every data point i. Turning “widest gap” into an objective From linear algebra, the distance between the two parallel boundary lines is margin = 2 / √( Σⱼ aⱼ² ) The coefficients sit in the denominator — so shrinking them widens the gap. Maximising that margin is therefore the same thing as minimising Σ aⱼ² . Which gives the whole hard-separation problem: minimize a₀…aₙ Σ n j=1 aⱼ² subject to y i ( Σ n j=1 aⱼx ij + a₀ ) ≥ 1 for all i Find coefficients giving the greatest distance between the lines — but only from among coefficients that separate every point correctly. Plenty of software solves this directly. quick check answered Which sequence correctly states the derivation of the hard-separation SVM? small coefficients — maximising the sum would squeeze the gap shut.\">Code the response ±1; merge the conditions; maximise the sum of squared coefficients subject to the constraint Minimise the sum of squared coefficients, then choose a margin formula that agrees with the result Count the misclassified points; minimise that count; then widen the margin if any slack remains Code the response ±1; write the two conditions; multiply by y to merge them; note margin = 2/√(Σaⱼ²); minimise Σaⱼ² subject to that constraint Check Why does making the coefficients smaller widen the margin? Show answer They sit in the denominator of 2/√(Σaⱼ²). Shrinking them shrinks the denominator, which enlarges the fraction — the gap between the two boundary lines. When perfect separation is impossible Measure how wrong each point is. If point i is on the correct side, y i (Σaⱼx ij + a₀) − 1 ≥ 0 and there is no error. If it is on the wrong side that quantity goes negative, and how negative is how big the mistake — the further a misclassified point sits from the line, the worse it is. error i = max { 0 , 1 − y i ( Σⱼ aⱼx ij + a₀ ) } Zero when the point is comfortably right; growing with distance when it is wrong. Now trade the two goals against each other with a parameter λ: minimize Σ i max{ 0, 1 − y i ( Σⱼ aⱼx ij + a₀ ) } + λ · Σⱼ aⱼ² Large λ: a wide margin outweighs avoiding mistakes on known points. λ toward 0: avoiding mistakes outweighs a wide margin. Class discussion — Lesson 4 click to type; saves automatically Exam target What happens to the classifier as λ grows very large? Show answer Margin width dominates, so the model accepts more misclassified training points in exchange for a wider gap. Push λ toward zero and it does the reverse, chasing a perfect fit on the training data — which Module 3 warns is not the same as a good model."},{"id":"l5","h":"Where the name comes from","t":"Lesson 5 Where the name comes from A support vector is a data point the boundary rests on — and the odd part is that the classifier the model returns doesn’t touch any of them. The picture behind the name Take a set of points and connect the dots around the outside. That outline is called the convex hull — a term you don’t need, but it makes the picture concrete. The shape appears to float; a line placed beneath it would hold it up. If the shape balances on that line, the point where they touch is supporting it. That point is a support vector. Why vector rather than point? Because each data point is a vector of attribute values. And support vectors don’t need the line underneath — mathematically one can support from the side, or from above. Depending on which line you choose, there may be more than one. Four regions are clickable. Open all four to finish this activity. Why “machine” Because the model takes the dataset and automatically determines where the two lines should go and which points are the support vectors. Nobody places them by hand. Class discussion — Lesson 5 click to type; saves automatically Worth noticing If you deleted a data point sitting well inside the blue cloud and refitted, what would change? Show answer Nothing. Only the points the boundaries rest against constrain the solution — interior points are already comfortably satisfied. This is why the method is named after them, and it makes SVM robust to changes far from the boundary."},{"id":"l6","h":"Extending SVM","t":"Lesson 6 Extending SVM Four extensions: weight the costly error, scale the data first, read variable importance off the coefficients, and bend the boundary with kernels. Building unequal costs into the model Under hard separation , the classifier you pick depends on the intercept — and it can range anywhere from a₀ − 1 to a₀ + 1 without making a single mistake on the known data. That whole range is free to choose from. if a bad loan costs twice a rejected good one: intercept = ⅔ ( a₀ − 1 ) + ⅓ ( a₀ + 1 ) A weighted blend of the two extremes, pulled toward the costly side in proportion to the cost. Under soft classification , add an extra multiplier for each type of error. The larger the penalty on a given error type, the less willing the model is to make it. Check Why is there a whole range of intercepts to choose from under hard separation? Show answer Because every intercept between a₀ − 1 and a₀ + 1 classifies all the known points correctly. The data can’t distinguish between them, so cost is what should. Scale first, or the margin term breaks Credit scores run from about 300 to 850 — some newer scales from 150 to 950 — so a range under a thousand. Household incomes measured in dollars can range over millions. That means a₁ and a₂ may differ by two or three orders of magnitude. Since the objective adds their squares , a small change in one coefficient can swamp a huge change in the other, and the model won’t work well. The fix is to scale the data beforehand so the orders of magnitude are comparable. Some software does it automatically; some doesn’t. Lesson 7 covers exactly how. Reading variable importance off the coefficients Lesson 2 noticed that a classifier running almost parallel to one axis means that axis’s attribute barely matters. That is easy to spot in a drawing and impossible in twenty dimensions. But the coefficients tell you the same thing: The rule If a coefficient a j is very close to zero, attribute j is probably not relevant for classification, and can be dropped. This only works after scaling. On raw data a coefficient can be tiny purely because its attribute is measured in large units — which is the whole problem above. quick check answered Your fitted SVM has a coefficient of 0.003 on income and 2.1 on credit score. What can you conclude? Credit score is being over-weighted, so the model has been fitted incorrectly. Income is irrelevant and can be dropped from the model. Nothing yet — you need to know whether the data was scaled before fitting. Exam target Name the two things scaling makes possible in SVM. Show answer It stops the sum-of-squares margin term being dominated by whichever attribute has the larger units, and it makes coefficient magnitudes comparable so they can be used for variable selection. Four questions you were probably about to ask quick check answered You want a number like “a 37% chance this applicant defaults” rather than a lend/don't-lend verdict. What does the module point you toward? k-nearest-neighbours Standardization of the attributes A kernel method Logistic regression Class discussion — Lesson 6 click to type; saves automatically Vocabulary What does a kernel method buy you? Show answer Non-linear classifiers. The boundary can curve rather than being a straight line or flat hyperplane, while the rest of the SVM machinery stays the same."},{"id":"l7","h":"Scaling and standardization","t":"Lesson 7 Scaling and standardization Two ways to put attributes on a common footing. Scale when a bounded range matters; standardize when distance from the mean matters. Why mismatched units break the margin Classify borrowers on household income — tens of thousands or more — and credit score, in the hundreds. A plausible set of SVM coefficients might be 5 × income and 700 × credit score . Watch what the sum of squares does. Coefficients Sum of squares Change 5, 700 25 + 490,000 = 490,025 — 5, 701 25 + 491,401 = 491,426 +1,401 37.8, 700 ≈ 1,429 + 490,000 +1,404 Nudging the credit-score coefficient from 700 to 701 is under a 1% change and barely moves the line — yet it shifts the objective by 1,401. To move the objective by the same amount through the income coefficient, you’d have to take it from 5 to about 37.8: a change of more than 600%, and an enormous change to the line. The consequence Because the data are on such different scales, the coefficients are too — so the sum of squared coefficients is far more sensitive to one than the other. The optimizer spends all its effort on the attribute with the large coefficient and effectively ignores the other. The model doesn’t work well. quick check answered In the table above, the two coefficient changes move the objective by roughly the same amount. Why is that a problem rather than a reassurance? Because the two rows aren’t comparable, so the arithmetic in the table is misleading. Because equal movement in the objective corresponds to wildly unequal movement of the actual boundary. Because the sums are large enough that floating-point rounding will dominate the result. Exam target State in one sentence why unscaled data breaks an SVM. Show answer Attributes on different scales produce coefficients on different scales, so the sum of squared coefficients responds far more to one attribute than another and no longer measures the margin fairly. The two fixes Scaling — squeeze into an interval Usually into 0 to 1, the most common choice. For each attribute j , let x min,j and x max,j be its smallest and largest values. x ij scaled = ( x ij − x min,j ) / ( x max,j − x min,j ) For a different range [a, b], take the 0-to-1 value, multiply by (b − a), and add a. Standardization — recentre on the mean Rescale to a mean of 0 and a standard deviation of 1 — the standard normal. Let μ j be the mean of attribute j and σ j its standard deviation. x ij standardized = ( x ij − μ j ) / σ j The result says how many standard deviations from the mean each value sits. Unbounded by construction. Nothing about the data changed — only the units. But the optimizer now sees variation in both directions instead of one. Formulas to know Write both formulas from memory. Show answer Scale: (x − x min ) / (x max − x min ). Standardize: (x − μ) / σ. Both are applied per attribute, using that attribute’s own statistics. Which one, when? It depends — and sometimes it genuinely isn’t clear, in which case you try both and see which works better. Reach for When Scaling A bounded range matters. Neural networks often require inputs between 0 and 1; optimization models may need bounded data to guarantee feasibility. Also when the data type is naturally bounded — batting averages between 0 and 1, RGB intensities 0 to 255, certain test scores 200 to 800 Standardization Some models simply work better with it. Principal component analysis and clustering are the two named examples The point that outlives this lesson Scaling or standardizing the input data is often necessary, and true for most models in this course — classification, clustering, prediction, selection and others. It won’t be repeated every time it applies. Do it anyway. quick check answered You’re feeding attributes into a neural network that requires every input between 0 and 1. Scaling or standardization? Either — they’re interchangeable, so try both and keep whichever scores better. Standardization, because it puts everything on a common statistical footing. Scaling, because only it can guarantee the values stay inside a fixed range. Class discussion — Lesson 7 click to type; saves automatically Trap to avoid Where should the min, max, mean and standard deviation come from when you also have a test set? Show answer The training data only, then apply those same numbers to the validation and test sets. Computing them across everything lets information from the test set leak into training — which Module 3 shows is exactly what invalidates a quality estimate. The lecture doesn’t raise this, but graders often do."},{"id":"l8","h":"k-nearest neighbours","t":"Lesson 8 k-nearest neighbours Skip the boundary entirely — just look at the k closest known points and take a vote. Handles many classes without any extra machinery. The idea Same picture as before: credit score horizontal, household income vertical, blue for repaid and red for defaulted. But instead of drawing a line or any other function to separate them, assume that each new applicant resembles the previous applicants closest to it . Look at the five nearest points to a new applicant. Four are blue, one is red — so the new point is more likely blue, and the recommendation is to lend. There is nothing magical about five. Any number of points can be used, and that number is denoted k — which is where the name comes from. The white square is the new point. Multi-class needs no extra machinery — you count colours among the k closest and take the most common. quick check answered Which sequence describes k-nearest-neighbours correctly? Average the attributes within each class, then assign the new point to the closest class average closest points, not the furthest. Keeping the furthest would systematically pick the least relevant neighbours.\">Measure distance to every known point; keep the k furthest; count classes among them; assign the most common Fit coefficients on the training data, then use them to place each new point on one side of a boundary Choose k and a distance measure; measure distance to every known point; keep the k closest; count classes among them; assign the most common Contrast What does k-nearest-neighbours never build that SVM does? Show answer A boundary. SVM produces a line described by coefficients; k-nearest-neighbours keeps all the training points and answers each query by looking at them directly. Three complications, each handled elsewhere Complication Where it’s dealt with There’s more than one way to measure distance. Straight-line is the most common, but not the only one The distance metrics material — the p-norms in Module 4 Some attributes matter more than others. Weight each dimension’s distance differently: the larger the weight, the greater that dimension’s influence. Finding the right weights is harder and may need regression Regression, later In the extreme, some attributes barely matter at all and can be removed, measuring distance only in the important ones Variable selection How do you find the right k? Validation — try different values and measure how well each works quick check answered How do you choose k? Use the largest k the dataset supports, since more neighbours means more information. Try several values and measure how well each one performs, using validation. Take the square root of the number of data points, rounded to an odd number. Class discussion — Lesson 8 click to type; saves automatically Cross-module link Three different k’s now. What does each count? Show answer k-nearest-neighbours: how many neighbours vote. k-means (Module 4): how many clusters. k-fold cross-validation (Module 3): how many parts the data is split into. You are already doing machine learning Both SVM and k-nearest-neighbours are basic machine learning algorithms. If you were waiting for the course to reach machine learning — it already has. Alongside the two models, this module also delivered several cross-cutting concepts that will keep returning: data terminology and data types, validation, distance metrics, and confusion matrices. Orientation Which parts of this module were models, and which were cross-cutting? Show answer Models: SVM and k-nearest-neighbours. Cross-cutting: the data dictionary, scaling and standardization, distance metrics, and validation — all of which apply to methods far beyond classification."},{"id":"targets","h":"Transfer practice — Classification","t":"Transfer practice — Classification Same ideas, unfamiliar settings Why these are here Each prompt below moves the module’s ideas into a situation the lessons never mentioned. If you can only answer a question in the wording it was taught in, what you have memorised is the example rather than the concept — and an exam that changes the setting will find that out. Answer these out loud before opening them. Exam target An email filter treats a legitimate message routed to spam as far worse than a spam message reaching the inbox. What do you change, and what do you leave alone? Show answer Change the relative weight on the two error types — either by weighting each class's error term in the objective by its cost, or by moving the decision threshold so ambiguous messages land on the cheaper side. Leave the orientation of the boundary alone: the attributes that separate the classes have not changed, only what it costs to get each one wrong. Exam target A report states the fitted coefficients are 0.02 and 340, concluding the second attribute is overwhelmingly more important. What do you ask first? Show answer Whether the attributes were scaled before fitting. On unscaled data the coefficients reflect the units the attributes happened to be recorded in. An attribute measured in dollars and one measured in decades will produce wildly different coefficients for reasons that have nothing to do with importance. Exam target You must sort records into seven categories, and the regions belonging to each are irregular and interleaved. Make the case for k-nearest neighbours over a support vector machine. Show answer KNN handles any number of classes with no extra construction — it counts the classes among the nearest neighbours — whereas a support vector machine is natively a two-class method and needs one-vs-rest or one-vs-one decomposition. KNN also makes no assumption that the classes are separable by a single surface, so interleaved local pockets are exactly the situation it suits. Exam target A soft-margin model has almost no training error and disappointing validation error. Which way do you move the regularisation weight, and what is the reasoning? Show answer Up, so that margin width counts for more relative to errors on the training records. Near-zero training error with poor validation error is the signature of a boundary contorted to accommodate individual records, including the random ones. Widening the margin means accepting more training mistakes in exchange for a boundary that generalises. Exam target Explain why a wider margin is more robust, in terms of measurement error rather than geometry. Show answer Attributes are recorded with error — a reported income, a sensor reading, a self-reported figure. A record sitting close to the boundary can cross it on a small inaccuracy, changing its predicted class. A wide margin means a record has to be wrong by a lot before its classification flips, so the model's verdicts are stable against the imprecision that is present in any real dataset. Exam target Someone proposes handling an imbalanced two-class problem by moving the boundary until accuracy is maximised. What is wrong with that? Show answer On imbalanced data, accuracy is maximised by favouring the majority class, potentially to the point of never predicting the minority one at all. Accuracy treats the two errors as equally costly, which is rarely why the problem was worth solving. The threshold should be set against the costs of the two mistakes, or against recall on the class you actually care about."},{"id":"sec-10","h":"How the module fits together","t":"How the module fits together The through-line across all eight lessons Lesson 1 Poses the problem as a picture, then points out that infinitely many lines solve it. Lesson 2 Argues for the line furthest from mistakes — then complicates it with unequal error costs. Lesson 3 Supplies the vocabulary the rest of the course assumes you have. Lesson 4 Turns “furthest from mistakes” into a solvable optimization problem. Lesson 5 Explains the name, and reveals that only a handful of points determine the answer. Lesson 6 Adds cost weighting, variable selection from coefficients, and curved boundaries. Lesson 7 Fixes the units problem Lesson 6 flagged, in a way that applies to nearly every model. Lesson 8 Abandons boundaries entirely and gets multi-class classification almost for free. Five things worth noticing Lesson 2 is Lesson 4 without the algebra. Every intuition — margin, soft classifiers, cost weighting — reappears as a term in the optimization. If the maths feels opaque, reread Lesson 2 and match the pictures to the symbols. Scaling is load-bearing twice over. It rescues the margin term, and it is what makes coefficient-based variable selection legitimate. Skip it and both fail quietly rather than loudly. The two models fail in opposite directions. SVM compresses the training data into a handful of coefficients; k-nearest-neighbours keeps every point and does all the work at prediction time. That trade decides which is practical at scale. Every parameter is a selection, and every selection has a price. k, λ, the kernel, even which attributes survive — each is chosen by comparing scores, and each comparison biases the winning score upward. The instructor’s notes on that, and on where classification stops and clustering starts, are on the Discussion page. Nearly every complication is deferred. Distance metrics, variable selection, validation, logistic regression — this module names four gaps and fills none of them, which is what a cross-cutting curriculum looks like from the inside. Likely exam targets Explain why a wide margin is preferable to a narrow one, given both classify the training data perfectly. State the hard-separation SVM problem — objective and constraint — from memory. Explain why the response is coded ±1 rather than 1 and 0. Describe the effect of increasing λ in the soft-classification objective. Define a support vector and say why the returned classifier touches none of them. Show how unequal error costs change the intercept under hard separation. Give both the scaling and standardization formulas, and say when each is appropriate. Walk through k-nearest-neighbours and name its three complications. Distinguish quantitative, categorical and binary data with an example of each. Explain why the validation winner’s error usually rises on the test set, and why that is not a defect in the test data — see Discussion, Topic 5 . Distinguish classification from clustering three ways: supervision, where the categories come from, and question phrasing — see Discussion, Topic 6 . Where the rest of this lives This page carries the content and one check per lesson. The full question bank for Module 2 — around ninety multiple-choice and discussion items covering all eight lessons — is Topic 12 on the Exam Prep page. The instructor’s posted notes on validation bias and on classification against clustering, with their own checks, are Topics 5 and 6 on the Discussion page. Module 2 — my own summary click to type; saves automatically Written by an AI model from lecture recordings. Not reviewed by any instructor or institution — verify anything you rely on against the original lectures. Examples here are generic illustrations rather than the ones used in class. The Sam Okafor case, the leakage warning in Lesson 7, and “Five things worth noticing” are this summary’s own additions rather than lecture content. The instructor’s posted notes on validation and on classification against clustering are covered on the Discussion page, and the question bank for this module is on the Exam Prep page."}]},{"f":"module-3-validation.html","t":"Module 3: Validation","s":[{"id":"l1","h":"Introduction to validation","t":"Lesson 1 Introduction to validation Every dataset contains real patterns and random ones. A model fits both, but only the real ones show up again — so scoring a model on its own training data always flatters it. The question you will always be asked Validation means checking how good a model is. When you present a model to a boss or a client, one of the first questions is how good it is. How accurately does it classify loan applicants? How well does it predict future sales? How often does it correctly determine whether a transplanted liver will transmit infectious encephalitis? The tempting answer is to score the model on the points used to build it. In the lecture picture there are 24 data points and only three sit on the wrong side of the classifier, so the model gets 21 out of 24 — 87.5%. Easy calculation. Wrong thing to do. quick check answered You report that 87.5% figure to your client. What is the actual problem with it? 24 data points is too small a sample to draw any conclusion from. The model was fitted to those same points, so the score includes credit for patterns that exist only in that data. Accuracy is the wrong metric; you should report precision and recall instead. Core idea Two kinds of pattern live in any dataset. Can you name both, and say which one survives into new data? Show answer Real effects — genuine relationships between attributes and the response. Random effects — patterns that appear by chance. Only the real effects reappear in new data. The random ones are different every time, which is exactly why measured performance falls. Why training scores are optimistic Any dataset, including the training set, holds two kinds of patterns: Real effects Genuine relationships between the attributes and the response. These are present in any dataset drawn from the same source. Random effects Patterns that appear purely by chance. The data looks like it contains a relationship when it really doesn’t. The trouble is that we can’t tell which is which. Fitting a model to a training set finds something matching both . Use that model on different data and only the real effects are still there — the random effects have changed. So you get fewer correct predictions on new data than on the data you trained with. Watch for Can you retell the random-draw demonstration in three sentences? Show answer Ten numbers were drawn uniformly at random from 1 to 50, and nine happened to land in the top third. A model guessing 42 looks excellent on those ten and terrible on any fresh draw, because the numbers are genuinely spread evenly. A demonstration you can run yourself Draw ten numbers uniformly at random from 1 to 50 and plot them. Often enough, the result looks like the chart above: nine of the ten bunched in the upper third, one stray low value. Anyone shown only this sample would conclude the numbers skew high. A predictive model would guess about 42, right in the middle of where nine of the ten points sit. Which we know is wrong, because we generated the numbers ourselves and they are uniform by construction. The skew is pure randomness. On this sample the model looks excellent — within eight for nine of the ten. On a fresh draw it would be badly off for most of them. That is the entire argument, and the only reason it is obvious here is that we happen to know the truth. Four regions are clickable. Open all four to finish this activity. Exam target Explain in your own words why performance on training data is biased upward, without using the word “overfitting.” Show answer Because the model was shaped to match every pattern in that specific data, including the accidental ones. Scoring it there gives it credit for matching accidents it will never see again. Was that just a lucky sample? Reasonable question: was that particular clustering cherry-picked? In a sense, yes — but it doesn’t matter, because some spurious pattern is always available. It might have been a bunch of low values instead, or a cluster in the middle, or a run of even numbers, or of odd ones, or of multiples of three. The specific pattern is unpredictable. The existence of one is not, and a flexible enough model will find whichever one showed up. quick check answered Why is “that was just an unlucky sample” the wrong takeaway from the demonstration? Because with more draws the same clustering would have appeared even more strongly. Because he didn’t need that particular pattern — any dataset would have handed him some chance pattern to fit. Because the numbers genuinely do skew high, so the model wasn’t wrong. Class discussion — Lesson 1 click to type; saves automatically Looking ahead The lesson ends without answering its own question. What is left open? Show answer If we can’t measure effectiveness on the training data, how do we measure it? Lesson 2 gives the answer: hold out data the model has never seen."},{"id":"l2","h":"Validation and test sets","t":"Lesson 2 Validation and test sets One held-out set gives an honest score for one model. The moment you pick a winner from several, that winner’s score is inflated by luck — so you need a third set that had no part in the choosing. Two sets, for one model The fix is to use two different sets of data: a larger one to fit the model, a smaller one to measure it. In the loan example — classifying applicants into those who fully repay and those who don’t — one set of points builds the classifier and a second set measures it. If the training set had unique random effects the classifier was designed around, those benefits aren’t counted the second time. Validation set The second set of data, held out from fitting, used to measure how the model actually performs. In the lecture’s picture the same line classifies 90% of the training points correctly but only 80% of the validation points. The 90% is probably too optimistic; 80% is the more accurate measure. Numbers to remember What were the two accuracy figures in the loan classifier example, and which one would you quote? Show answer 90% on training, 80% on validation. Quote the 80% — and only if this was the single model you built. The complication: choosing among models Now suppose you’re comparing more than one model. You have a support vector machine and a k-nearest-neighbor model — and to make it harder, five different SVMs and five different KNNs. Ten models, and you want the best one. Measure each on the validation set and pick the winner. In the lecture that’s SVM #4 . Can you expect that level of performance from it? Maybe not. Every performance estimate carries randomness. Sometimes randomness makes a model look worse than it is; sometimes better. And when you pick the highest of ten scores, part of what made that one highest may be luck. The intuition, at its most extreme Suppose all ten models were equally good . Then every difference you see on the validation set is pure randomness. One of them happened to do a little better on this particular set, and that’s the one you pick. Its measured score isn’t its true quality — its true quality is lower. The winner is simultaneously the most likely to genuinely be best and the most likely to have received an above-average random boost. So its validation score can’t be used as an estimate of its quality. quick check answered You compare 10 models on a validation set. The best scores 84%. What is the most defensible thing to say about that 84%? It is a fair estimate of that model’s quality, since the validation data was held out of training. It is meaningless, because selecting on validation data invalidates the comparison entirely. It is good enough to choose with, but likely inflated — you need a fresh set to estimate quality. Why this is hard Note most people find this confusing at first. What is the subtle part? Show answer That the very act of picking the best introduces bias. Each individual score is unbiased; the maximum of many scores is not, because the winner is disproportionately likely to have been lucky. The flowchart Three sets, three jobs. Click through the diagram before reading on. Five areas are clickable — three sets and the two warnings. Open all five to finish this activity. Set Job Training Building models Validation Picking a model Test Estimating the performance of the model you picked If there is only one model, step two is skipped entirely — you go straight from training to test. Two different biases The training warning and the validation warning are not the same problem. What separates them? Show answer Training bias comes from fitting : the model was shaped around that data’s noise. Validation bias comes from selecting : nothing was fitted to it, but choosing the maximum of several noisy scores favours the lucky one. Try it as a case DR Dana Reyes Analytics associate, consumer lending Dana has 40,000 past loan applications with known outcomes. She has built ten candidate classifiers — five SVMs, five KNNs — on a training set, and scored all ten on a held-out validation set. SVM #4 came top at 84%. Her manager: “Great. Put 84% in the deck for Thursday and let’s move on.” What should Dana do? Use the 84%. The validation set was held out of training, so the number is honest. Retrain SVM #4 on all 40,000 rows so it’s as strong as possible, then report its accuracy. Score SVM #4 on a third set that was used for neither fitting nor choosing, and report that. The follow-up question. Dana reports 81% from the test set. Her manager asks whether they can now also try three more model variants and report the best of those on the same test set. “It’s the same test set we already agreed on, so it’s still clean, right?” Yes — the test set doesn’t change, so its scores stay unbiased. No — choosing on it makes it a validation set, and the winner’s score is inflated again. Class discussion — Lesson 2 click to type; saves automatically The trap What turns a test set into a validation set? Show answer Making a choice based on it. The label doesn’t matter — any set you select on stops being an unbiased estimator for whatever you selected."},{"id":"l3","h":"Splitting the data","t":"Lesson 3 Splitting the data There is no correct ratio — training should be large, validation and test merely large enough. How you split matters more than the exact percentages. How much goes where There is no hard and fast rule; different people prefer different amounts. Sets Recommendations in circulation Two (training + test) 70/30 through 90/10 Three (training + validation + test) 50/25/25, or 60/20/20, or 70/15/15 It isn’t clear that any of these is much better than the others. The competing pressures are simple: more training data means the model can fit as many important points as possible, but the test set also needs enough data to cover important points. The rule that actually matters The training set should be large . The validation and test sets should merely be large enough . Exam target If asked “what split should I use?”, what is the right shape of answer? Show answer That no ratio is clearly best, quote a couple of common ones, and give the principle: training large, the other two large enough to cover the important cases. Two ways to split, and a third With 1,000 points and a 60/20/20 target: Random Randomly choose 600 points for training, then randomly choose 200 of the remainder for validation, and the 200 left over are the test set. Rotation Take turns on a fixed cycle. On a five-point rotation: training, validation, training, test, training — then repeat, over and over. Rotation’s advantage is that each part of the data is equally separated. If the data spans ten years, a random split might hand the validation set a higher fraction of years 1–2 and less of years 9–10, while the test set gets the opposite. If conditions differed between the early and late years, that causes inaccuracies in your measurements. Rotation’s danger is creating a different bias. In that five-point rotation, every first, third and fifth point goes to training, every second to validation, every fourth to test. Now suppose this is daily sales data for a retailer open five days a week: The Monday-Wednesday-Friday trap Training would receive all the Monday, Wednesday and Friday data and no Tuesdays or Thursdays. Any pattern the model finds in Friday data would never be checked, because validation and test only hold Tuesdays and Thursdays. That could be a more significant problem than having different fractions of data in each set. The in-between fixes it: randomly put 60% of the Mondays into training, 60% of the Tuesdays, 60% of the Wednesdays, and so on. quick check answered Why is the Monday-Wednesday-Friday problem worse than the uneven-years problem it was meant to solve? Because the rotation split leaves the training set smaller than 60%. Because entire categories are missing from the checking sets, so some of the model’s behaviour is never measured at all. Because rotation is deterministic, and any deterministic split is statistically invalid. The general lesson What property of the data made the five-point rotation dangerous? Show answer A repeating cycle in the data — a five-day business week — that lined up with the length of the rotation. Any time the split’s period matches a period in the data, whole categories get sorted into the same set. What each approach actually gives you quick check answered You split a time-ordered dataset by fixed rotation: point 1 to training, point 2 to validation, point 3 to test, then repeat. What have you bought and what have you risked? Even-handed on average, but the coverage of any one period is left to chance. Equal spread across the data, at the risk of a bias whenever the cycle lines up with a pattern. Every category represented in every set, in the right proportion. quick check answered Sales data where Mondays behave nothing like Saturdays. Which approach keeps all three sets representative? Simple random selection across all the rows. Random selection within each day of the week. Fixed rotation through the three sets. Make the training set large and the other two just large enough. Class discussion — Lesson 3 click to type; saves automatically Looking ahead Lesson 3 ends by pointing at one more use of the data. What is it? Show answer Cross-validation — a way of using the training and validation data repeatedly rather than splitting it once."},{"id":"l4","h":"Cross-validation","t":"Lesson 4 Cross-validation Rotate which slice is held out, train k times, average the k scores. Every point gets used for training — and the model you finally deploy is none of the k you built. The worry it solves Something in the flowchart makes people nervous. What if a couple of important data points only show up in the validation or test set? Then they aren’t in the training set, the model never sees them, and it never fits itself to those important pieces of data. k-fold cross-validation Split the training-and-validation data into k parts. For each part, train on all the others and evaluate on the one held out. Average the k evaluations to estimate the model’s quality. A note on the letter k k-means, k-nearest-neighbor, and now k-fold cross-validation. It refers to something different in each — but in every case it says how many of something we have. Cross-module link What does k count in each of the three methods? Show answer k-means: how many clusters. k-nearest-neighbor: how many neighbours get a vote. k-fold: how many parts the data is split into. How the rotation works Say 20% of the data is reserved for testing and the other 80% is used for training and validation together. Split that 80% into k parts — the lecture uses k = 4 — and instead of training and validating once, do it four times on slightly different datasets: Train on parts 1, 2, 3 and evaluate on 4; then 1, 2, 4 evaluating on 3; then 1, 3, 4 evaluating on 2; then 2, 3, 4 evaluating on 1. No data point is ever left out of training entirely. When comparing models to decide which to choose, take the average of all four evaluations. There is no standard number to use as k, although k = 10 has become fairly common. Numbers to remember In four-fold cross-validation, how many of the four models does each data point help train? Show answer Three. It’s held out exactly once, so it trains in the other three rounds — which is the whole point: no important point is ever excluded. So which model do you actually use? Four-fold cross-validation on a support vector machine gives four slightly different classifiers, one per split. Which do you deploy? None of them And don’t average the coefficients across the four splits either — there’s no guarantee that will work well. Once the model is selected, train it again using all four parts of the data together. quick check answered You ran 10-fold cross-validation and chose an SVM. What goes into production? The fold model that scored highest of the ten. The average of the ten models’ coefficients. A fresh SVM retrained on all ten parts together. quick check answered Which sequence describes cross-validation done properly, start to finish? Split into k parts, train and evaluate k times, average, choose the best model, then carve out a test set to score it. Hold out a test set, split the rest into k parts, train and evaluate k times, average, choose the best model, then score that fold’s model on the test set. Hold out a test set, split the rest into k parts, train and evaluate k times, average, choose, retrain the winner on all k parts, then score once on the test set. Cross-validation helps you make better use of your data, and the averaging gives a better estimate of model quality. Together they help you choose a model more effectively, which is why it has become a commonly used approach in analytics. Class discussion — Lesson 4 click to type; saves automatically Exam target Give the two distinct benefits of cross-validation. Show answer It uses the data better — no point is permanently excluded from training. And averaging k evaluations gives a more stable estimate of quality than a single held-out score."},{"id":"l5","h":"What validation is actually measuring","t":"Lesson 5 What validation is actually measuring Test error splits into three pieces: how wrong the model’s shape is, how much it wobbles when the data changes, and noise nobody can remove. Every choice validation makes is a trade between the first two. Two numbers, and the gap between them Lesson 1 said the training score is inflated. This lesson says by how much, and why — and it does so by naming the quantity validation is trying to estimate. Test error The average error the model would make on a fresh observation drawn from the same source, that played no part in fitting it. It is the only number that answers the client’s question, and it can never be observed directly — only estimated. For a numeric response the usual measure is mean squared error — average of (actual − predicted)². For a classification problem it is the misclassification rate — the fraction predicted wrongly. Everything in this module works identically for both; only the thing being averaged changes. expected test error = variance of the fit + (bias of the fit)² + irreducible error All three terms are non-negative, so the expected test error can never fall below the irreducible term however good the model is. Term What it is What increases it Variance How much the fitted model would change if you handed it a different training set from the same source Flexibility. A very flexible model chases the particular points it was given — Lesson 1’s random effects, seen from the other side Bias The error from forcing a complicated reality into a simpler shape — a straight line through a genuinely curved relationship, say Rigidity. A model too simple to represent the truth is wrong no matter how much data it gets Irreducible error Randomness in the world itself: unmeasured causes, genuine unpredictability, measurement noise in the response Nothing you can do. It is a property of the problem, not the model That third term is why “our model is only 81% accurate” is not automatically a criticism. Two identical applicants can take different decisions. Some ceiling exists, it is below 100%, and no method reaches past it. Core idea Why can a model have zero training error and still have large test error? Show answer Because zero training error is bought with flexibility, and flexibility is variance. The model has fitted this training set’s random effects exactly; on a different sample from the same source those effects are different, so the fit is wrong in a new way each time. The U-shaped curve everything in this course lives on Turn any flexibility dial slowly — the degree of a polynomial, k in nearest-neighbours, λ in an SVM, the depth of a tree — and the two movable terms move in opposite directions. Bias falls. Variance rises. Their sum is a U. Training error would slide downward across this whole picture without ever turning up — which is precisely why it cannot locate the minimum, and why validation exists. What validation is for, stated exactly Validation is a way of estimating the red curve using only data you have. You cannot see the true test error, so you approximate it by holding data out. Every technique in the rest of this module — a single split, k-fold, leave-one-out, the bootstrap — is a different approximation, with different bias and different variance of the estimate itself . That is the subtlety: the estimate has its own bias–variance problem, separate from the model’s. quick check answered A colleague reports that their model’s test error is 0.04 and says the remaining error proves they need a more flexible model. What should you ask? How large the test set was, since a small one gives a noisy estimate. What the irreducible error is likely to be, since error above zero is expected and may already be near the floor. Whether they used cross-validation, since a single split understates error. What the training error was, so the gap between the two can be measured. quick check answered Two models are fitted to the same data. Model A changes dramatically when you refit it on a slightly different sample; Model B barely moves. What do you know? Model B is better, because a stable model generalises better. Model A has higher variance. Nothing yet about which has lower test error, since B may have much higher bias. Model A has higher bias, since it is being pulled around by individual points. Class discussion — Lesson 5 click to type; saves automatically Cross-module link Name four flexibility dials from elsewhere in the course and say which end is high-variance. Show answer k in nearest-neighbours (small k is high variance), λ in the SVM objective (small λ, narrow margin, high variance), γ in a radial kernel (large γ is high variance), and LDA against QDA (QDA is the flexible, higher-variance one). Different letters, one picture."},{"id":"l6","h":"Why one split isn’t enough","t":"Lesson 6 Why one split isn’t enough A single held-out set gives an honest estimate — but a noisy one, and a pessimistic one. Two separate defects, and cross-validation was invented to fix both. The demonstration Take a dataset, split it at random into training and validation halves, fit a series of models of increasing flexibility, and plot validation error against flexibility. You get a curve. Now do the whole thing again with a different random split. You get a different curve. Do it ten times and you get ten curves that agree on the big picture and disagree on the details — including, often, on which model actually wins. All ten curves agree that the most rigid model is inadequate. None of them agrees with the others about which of the middle models is best, and each gives a different number for every model considered. What the picture licenses you to conclude is narrower than it first appears. The big effect — the rigid model is not good enough — is visible in every split and is safe. The small differences among the good models are within the noise, and a single split cannot resolve them. Reporting “model 4 is best, at 18.2” from one split claims a precision the procedure does not have. Drawback one — the estimate is high-variance Which observations happened to land in the validation set moves the number, sometimes a lot. Rerun with a different random seed and the answer changes. Drawback two — the estimate is biased upward The model being scored was trained on only part of the data. Methods generally perform worse with fewer observations, so the held-out score tends to overstate the error of the model you will actually deploy, which gets trained on everything. Split 50/50 and you are measuring a model built from half your data. Notice these push in opposite directions from Lesson 2’s bias Lesson 2’s selection bias makes the winner’s validation score too optimistic . This lesson’s training-size bias makes any held-out score too pessimistic . Both are real, they do not cancel in any reliable way, and confusing them is a standard exam trap. Selection bias is about which model you picked; training-size bias is about how much data the scored model got. quick check answered You change the random seed on your train/validation split and your chosen model changes from a 4th-degree polynomial to a 6th. What is the right conclusion? One of the two seeds produced a bad split, so rerun until the results are stable. The two models are probably indistinguishable at this sample size, and the estimate is too noisy to separate them. The 6th-degree model is genuinely better, since it won on the more recent split. Average the two answers and use a 5th-degree polynomial. quick check answered Which statement about a 50/50 train–validation split is correct? It is the safest choice, since it gives the estimate as much data as the model. It tends to overstate the error of the finally deployed model, and gives a noisier estimate than a larger training share would. It gives an unbiased estimate of the deployed model’s error, just an imprecise one. Class discussion — Lesson 6 click to type; saves automatically Exam target State the two drawbacks of the single-split validation approach, and say which way each one biases the number. Show answer High variance — the estimate depends on which observations landed where, so it moves with the split. And upward bias on error — the scored model was trained on a subset, so it is weaker than the model you will deploy, making the estimated error too high. Cross-validation attacks both."},{"id":"l7","h":"Leave-one-out cross-validation","t":"Lesson 7 Leave-one-out cross-validation Push k all the way to the number of data points. Each model trains on everything but one observation — which removes almost all of the training-size bias and all of the randomness, at a price. The extreme case of k-fold LOOCV Hold out a single observation. Train on the other n − 1. Record the error on that one point. Repeat for every observation in turn, then average the n errors. CV (n) = (1/n) · Σ n i=1 MSE i where MSE i = ( y i − ŷ i )² for classification: CV (n) = (1/n) · Σ n i=1 Err i , Err i = 1 if misclassified, 0 otherwise Each ŷ i comes from a model that never saw observation i. Only the thing being averaged changes between the regression and classification versions. Each individual error is a terrible estimate on its own — it rests on one observation. The average of all n is the useful quantity. Advantage Why Almost no training-size bias Each model is trained on n − 1 observations — essentially the full dataset — so it is about as strong as the model you will deploy. This directly fixes Lesson 6’s second drawback Completely deterministic There is no random split to make. Run it twice and you get the identical number, every time — which fixes the seed-sensitivity of Lesson 6’s first drawback, though not the variance of the estimate itself Uses every observation for evaluation Every point is held out exactly once, so nothing is permanently excluded from either role The price The model has to be fitted n times . With 50,000 observations and a model taking ten seconds to fit, that is close to six days. For most methods LOOCV is simply unaffordable, which is the first and most obvious reason people use k = 5 or k = 10 instead. Exam target Give two advantages of LOOCV over a single train/validation split. Show answer Far less bias, because each fit uses n − 1 observations rather than roughly half. And no randomness — there is no split to choose, so repeated runs give exactly the same answer. The shortcut that makes it free — sometimes For a least-squares linear or polynomial regression there is a remarkable identity: the LOOCV score can be computed from a single fit, with no refitting at all. CV (n) = (1/n) · Σ n i=1 [ ( y i − ŷ i ) / ( 1 − h i ) ]² ŷ i is the ordinary fitted value from the full fit, and h i is the leverage of observation i — between 1/n and 1 — measuring how much that point influences its own fitted value. It is the ordinary mean squared error with each residual divided by 1 − h i . High-leverage points are the ones a model bends toward, so their ordinary residuals understate how badly the model would do without them; dividing by 1 − h i inflates exactly those residuals, by exactly the right amount for the identity to hold. Read the scope carefully This holds for least-squares linear and polynomial regression. It does not hold in general — not for SVMs, not for k-nearest-neighbours, not for trees — and there the model really must be refitted n times. An exam question that offers the shortcut for a logistic regression or an SVM is testing whether you know where the boundary is. quick check answered You run LOOCV twice on the same dataset with the same model and get identical results. Your colleague, running 10-fold, gets slightly different numbers each time. Who has the problem? The colleague — variation between runs means their fold assignment is faulty. Neither. LOOCV makes no random choices; k-fold assigns folds at random, so it varies by design. You — LOOCV giving the same number twice means its variance is zero, which is impossible. Class discussion — Lesson 7 click to type; saves automatically Trap to avoid Does the leverage shortcut apply to a logistic regression? Show answer No. It is an identity for least squares linear and polynomial regression. For anything else — logistic regression, SVMs, nearest-neighbours, trees — the n refits are real and must be paid for."},{"id":"l8","h":"Choosing k, and what the CV number is for","t":"Lesson 8 Choosing k, and what the CV number is for Lesson 4 said k = 10 is common without saying why. The reason is a second bias–variance trade-off — this one in the estimate rather than the model — and it lands on 5 or 10. Bias falls as k rises Each fold model trains on roughly (k − 1)/k of the data. So k = 2 trains on half, k = 5 on 80%, k = 10 on 90%, and LOOCV on all but one point. The larger k is, the closer each fold model is to the model you will actually deploy, and the less the training-size bias from Lesson 6 inflates the error estimate. k Each fit trains on Bias of the estimate Number of fits 2 50% Large — badly pessimistic 2 5 80% Modest 5 10 90% Small 10 n (LOOCV) ≈ 100% Nearly zero n On bias alone, LOOCV wins outright. If that were the whole story everyone would use it whenever they could afford to — and they don’t. Variance rises as k rises Here is the part that surprises people. LOOCV averages n numbers, and averaging usually stabilises things — but only when the things being averaged are independent. The n LOOCV models are trained on training sets that differ by a single observation each, so they are nearly identical to one another, and their errors are therefore highly correlated . The statistical fact underneath The mean of many highly correlated quantities has higher variance than the mean of the same number of weakly correlated ones. Averaging buys you very little when everything you are averaging is essentially the same measurement repeated. With k = 5 or 10 the training sets overlap much less, the fold errors are less correlated, and the average is genuinely more stable. So the two effects oppose each other: raising k lowers bias and raises variance. Empirically, k = 5 and k = 10 sit in the region where neither is bad, which is the actual justification for the convention Lesson 4 mentioned in passing. quick check answered Why does LOOCV tend to give a higher-variance estimate than 10-fold, despite averaging far more numbers? Because each of the n errors is based on a single observation, so each is extremely noisy. Because the n models are trained on nearly identical data, so their errors are highly correlated and averaging them reduces variance very little. Because the random assignment of single points to folds introduces extra randomness. Because LOOCV has the highest bias, and bias and variance always move together. Two different jobs for the same number Job What you need from the CV curve Model selection — which of these candidates should I use? Only the location of the minimum. The curve can be wrong about the level of error throughout and still point at the right model Model assessment — how good is the model I chose? The value itself, and now its bias matters. Worse: if you selected using this number, it carries Lesson 2’s selection bias and is no longer an honest estimate This matters more than it sounds. Cross-validation estimates are often noticeably off in level while still identifying the right level of flexibility — which is good news, because selection is what people mostly use them for. It is also why selecting and reporting from the same cross-validation is the error Lesson 2 warned about, wearing a different hat. The one-standard-error rule Compute the standard error of the cross-validation estimate across the folds. Then choose the simplest model whose score is within one standard error of the best score. The rationale: if several models are statistically indistinguishable, the simplest is the safer bet — it has less variance, is easier to explain, and is less likely to have won by luck. This is the principled answer to Lesson 6’s wobbling curves. Rather than pretending the estimate can separate models it cannot, the rule reads the noise band honestly and breaks the tie on simplicity. quick check answered 10-fold CV gives these mean errors, with the standard error across folds in brackets: 3 predictors 0.248 (0.011), 4 predictors 0.239 (0.010), 6 predictors 0.234 (0.012), 9 predictors 0.236 (0.011). What does the one-standard-error rule select? 6 predictors, since 0.234 is the lowest score. 4 predictors — the smallest model scoring within 0.246. 3 predictors, since simpler is better whenever the curve is flat. 9 predictors, since more predictors reduce bias. Class discussion — Lesson 8 click to type; saves automatically Exam target State the bias–variance trade-off in the choice of k, and say which direction each moves. Show answer Larger k means each fit trains on more data, so the estimate’s bias falls — LOOCV is nearly unbiased. But larger k also means the fold models are trained on more nearly identical data, so their errors are more correlated and the averaged estimate has higher variance. k = 5 or 10 balances the two."},{"id":"l9","h":"Cross-validation for classification, and nesting","t":"Lesson 9 Cross-validation for classification, and nesting Swap the quantity being averaged and everything carries over. The harder question is what to do when you are tuning and assessing with the same procedure. Same machinery, different summand Cross-validation was described above using mean squared error because that is the natural measure for a numeric response. For classification, replace it with the misclassification count: Err i is 1 if the held-out case was predicted wrongly and 0 otherwise, and the fold score is the fraction wrong. Nothing else about the procedure changes. Everything Module 2 said about metrics applies inside each fold. If accuracy is the wrong criterion for your problem — and for an imbalanced one it usually is — then cross-validating accuracy gives you a stable estimate of the wrong thing. Cross-validate the quantity you actually care about: sensitivity, expected cost, or AUC. Stratify when the classes are unbalanced With 3% positives and ten folds, a plain random split can easily hand one fold twice the positive rate of another, and can in principle give a fold none at all. Stratified k-fold assigns folds so that each preserves the overall class proportions. It is the same instinct as Lesson 3’s fix for the weekday problem — split within each category rather than across the whole pile — applied to the response instead of an attribute. When you tune and assess with the same data Here is the situation that trips up real projects. You use 10-fold cross-validation to choose k for a nearest-neighbours model. The winning k scores 0.19. Is 0.19 your estimate of the model’s error? No — for exactly the reason in Lesson 2. You selected on those scores, so the winner’s score carries selection bias. Cross-validation lowers the noise that drives that bias but does not remove it, and if you tried thirty values of k the effect is not small. Nested cross-validation Two loops. The inner loop cross-validates within the training portion to choose the parameter. The outer loop holds out a fold that the inner loop never touched, and scores the whole tuning-plus-fitting procedure on it. The outer number estimates the quality of the procedure , which is what you would actually rerun on new data. The simpler alternative, and the one this module has been describing all along, is to keep a genuine test set out of the cross-validation entirely: cross-validate on the training-and-validation portion to choose, refit the winner on all of it, and score once on the reserved test set. Nesting is the same idea done repeatedly so that every observation gets a turn in the outer role. quick check answered You cross-validate 30 candidate values of λ and report the best fold-averaged score as your model’s expected performance. What is wrong? Nothing, since cross-validation already corrects for selection. The reported number was selected as the maximum of 30 estimates, so it is biased upward and needs an outer test set or an outer CV loop. Thirty candidates is too many to cross-validate reliably; use no more than ten. Averaging across folds hides the fold-to-fold variation, so the mean should not be reported. quick check answered A fraud dataset has 0.9% positives. You run plain 10-fold CV and one fold reports 100% accuracy. What is the most likely explanation? That fold happened to be an easy one, so the model genuinely got everything right. That fold probably contains no positive cases at all, so predicting the majority class scores perfectly. Information has leaked from the training folds into that fold. Class discussion — Lesson 9 click to type; saves automatically Cross-module link Module 2 warned against reporting accuracy on imbalanced data. How does that warning change inside cross-validation? Show answer It doesn’t change at all — it just gets averaged. Cross-validation makes an estimate more stable; it has no opinion about whether the thing being estimated is the right quantity. Cross-validate expected cost or sensitivity if that is what the decision depends on."},{"id":"l10","h":"Where cross-validation quietly breaks","t":"Lesson 10 Where cross-validation quietly breaks Every method in this module assumes the held-out data is genuinely independent of the training data. Four common situations where it isn’t — and in all four, the failure shows up as an excellent score. One: the preprocessing was done first Lesson 2 establishes that any set you make a choice on stops being clean. The same applies to any set that contributed to a calculation applied to the training data — and preprocessing is full of such calculations. Step done on the whole dataset before splitting What leaks Scaling or standardizing using the overall min, max, mean or standard deviation Summary statistics of the held-out rows enter the training pipeline Selecting the attributes most correlated with the response The held-out responses helped decide which attributes the model may use — often the largest leak of the four Imputing missing values from column means Held-out values contribute to the numbers filled into training rows Balancing classes by oversampling, then splitting Copies of the same observation land in both training and held-out folds The rule that covers all of them Every step that learns anything from data belongs inside the cross-validation loop, not before it. Fit the scaler on the training folds, apply it to the held-out fold. Select attributes using the training folds only, and re-select from scratch on each fold. If the choice of attributes changes between folds, that is not a bug — it is the procedure telling you how unstable the selection really is, which is information the leaky version hides completely. The tell is always the same: performance that looks too good and does not survive contact with production. Attribute selection done once on all the data before cross-validating can produce a strong-looking score on data that contains no real signal at all , because the selection step has already found the attributes that happen to correlate with the held-out responses. Two: the data is ordered in time Random folds scatter observations across time, so a model is routinely trained on next month and evaluated on last month. That is not a mistake a deployed model can make, so the estimate is measuring something that will never happen. Any trend, seasonality or drift makes the score optimistic. Rolling origin trains on a stretch of history and evaluates on the period immediately after, repeatedly. It uses less data per fit and gives a more pessimistic number — which is the honest one, because it is the only arrangement a deployed model ever faces. Three: the rows aren’t independent Cross-validation assumes each observation is a separate draw. It often isn’t: forty readings from the same patient, six months of transactions from the same customer, repeated measurements on the same machine. Split those at random and near-duplicates of the same underlying unit end up on both sides. The model can then succeed by recognising the unit rather than learning anything generalisable, and the estimate silently answers “how well does this work on a patient I have already seen?” instead of the question you asked. The fix is grouped splitting: assign whole patients, customers or machines to folds rather than individual rows. Expect the honest score to be worse. Lesson 3’s weekday example is the same principle seen from the other direction — there the worry was categories missing from a set; here it is the same unit appearing in two sets at once. Four: the future isn’t like the past Every estimate in this module assumes new data comes from the same source as the old. When the world shifts — a policy change, a new competitor, a pandemic — a perfectly executed cross-validation still gives an over-confident number, because the assumption it rests on has stopped being true. Nothing in the arithmetic can detect this. It is a judgement about the domain, and it belongs in the memo alongside the number. quick check answered An analyst screens 20,000 attributes down to the 50 most correlated with the response, then runs 10-fold cross-validation on those 50 and reports the result. What is the flaw? Fifty attributes is too many for the model to handle, so it will overfit. The screening step used the held-out responses, so every fold was contaminated before cross-validation began. Correlation only detects linear relationships, so useful attributes were discarded. Ten folds is too few when the number of attributes is this large. TO Tom Oyelaran Data scientist, equipment maintenance Tom predicts which pumps will fail within 30 days. He has 900,000 daily sensor rows from 300 pumps over three years. Random 10-fold cross-validation reports 96% accuracy and an AUC of 0.97. His lead: “That’s the best result we’ve had. Write it up.” What should Tom check first? Rerun with 20 folds to confirm the estimate is stable. Whether rows from the same pump, and from later dates, are appearing in both training and held-out folds. Swap accuracy for expected cost, since failures are rare and accuracy is misleading. He refits properly. Grouping by pump and respecting time order, AUC drops to 0.71. His lead asks whether something has been broken. Go back to the random folds — 0.97 was measured on real data, so it was a real result. Report 0.71 and explain that the earlier figure measured a question the deployed model will never face. Report both numbers and let the business decide which to believe. Class discussion — Lesson 10 click to type; saves automatically Exam target What do all four failure modes in this lesson have in common? Show answer The held-out data is not independent of the training data — through preprocessing, through time, through repeated units, or because the population itself has moved. And all four announce themselves the same way: a better score than the model deserves, which no amount of extra folds will correct."},{"id":"l11","h":"The bootstrap","t":"Lesson 11 The bootstrap Cross-validation answers “how good is this model?”. The bootstrap answers a different question — “how much would this number move if I had collected different data?” — by resampling the data you have, with replacement. The question it answers Any quantity computed from data is itself uncertain: a regression coefficient, a median, a ratio, the optimal allocation between two investments. Ideally you would measure that uncertainty by collecting many fresh datasets from the population and watching the estimate vary. You cannot. The bootstrap emulates that process on a computer using the single dataset you have. The procedure From a dataset of n observations, draw n observations at random with replacement to form a bootstrap sample. Compute your statistic on it. Repeat B times — B is typically in the hundreds or thousands. The spread of those B values estimates the sampling variability of the statistic, and its standard deviation estimates the standard error. Each bootstrap sample is the same size as the original but contains some observations more than once and others not at all. That variation is what stands in for collecting fresh data. The number worth memorising The chance a given observation appears in a particular bootstrap sample is 1 − (1 − 1/n) n , which converges to 1 − 1/e ≈ 63.2% . So each bootstrap sample contains about two-thirds of the distinct observations, and roughly 36.8% are left out — the “out-of-bag” observations, which some methods reuse as a free validation set. Exam target What fraction of the original observations appears in a typical bootstrap sample, and where does the number come from? Show answer About 63.2%. Each draw misses a given observation with probability (1 − 1/n); n independent draws miss it with probability (1 − 1/n) n → 1/e ≈ 0.368, so it appears with probability ≈ 0.632. Bootstrap or cross-validation? Cross-validation Bootstrap Question answered How well does this model predict new data? How uncertain is this estimated quantity? How data is reused Partitioned — each observation is held out exactly once Resampled with replacement — observations repeat and are omitted Typical output An estimated test error A standard error, or a confidence interval Typical use Choosing between models; estimating quality Putting an error bar on a coefficient, a ratio, or any statistic with no clean formula They are complementary rather than competing. A common pairing: cross-validate to choose the model, then bootstrap to attach uncertainty to the quantity you report from it. Why you cannot simply bootstrap the test error It is tempting to treat the out-of-bag observations as a validation set and stop there — but the bootstrap sample and the original dataset overlap heavily by construction, with about two-thirds of observations in common. Naively scoring a bootstrapped model against the full original data therefore gives an optimistic answer, because most of the “held-out” rows were in training. Cross-validation partitions and so has no overlap at all, which is why it remains the right tool for estimating test error. The bootstrap’s real strength is that it needs no formula. Standard errors for a regression coefficient come from textbook theory that assumes the model is correct; bootstrap standard errors make no such assumption, which is why they are often the more trustworthy of the two when the model is shaky. And for statistics with no theory at all — the ratio of two medians, an optimal allocation, the difference between two AUCs — it may be the only option. quick check answered Which task is the bootstrap the right tool for? Estimating how accurately a classifier will perform on future applicants. Choosing between five candidate values of k for a nearest-neighbours model. Putting a standard error on the ratio of two median claim amounts, for which no formula is handy. Deciding whether to use a 70/30 or 60/20/20 split. quick check answered Why must bootstrap sampling be done with replacement? Because sampling n observations from n without replacement just returns the original dataset, so every resample would give the same answer. Because it allows each resample to be larger than the original dataset, improving precision. Because it guarantees that about a third of observations are left out for validation. Class discussion — Lesson 11 click to type; saves automatically Contrast In one sentence each, what do cross-validation and the bootstrap estimate? Show answer Cross-validation estimates how well a modelling procedure will predict data it has not seen. The bootstrap estimates how much a computed quantity would vary if you had drawn a different sample from the same population."},{"id":"l12","h":"Midterm and final-style questions","t":"Exam workout Midterm and final-style questions Validation questions on real papers are rarely definitional. They give you a procedure and ask what is wrong with it, or a table of numbers and ask what you are entitled to conclude. How to use this Write your answer before opening the model one. The marking notes say where the marks usually sit — which on this topic is almost always the direction of a bias and the reason for it, not the name of the method. Section A — Multiple choice, midterm style midterm · calculation answered You tune an SVM over 6 values of λ and 4 values of γ using 10-fold cross-validation, then deploy the winner. How many times is the model fitted in total? 240 241 24 250 midterm · direction of bias answered Which pairing of bias and direction is correct? Training on a subset makes the error estimate too low; selecting the best of many models makes it too high. Selecting the best of many models makes the estimate too optimistic; training on a subset makes it too pessimistic. Both biases make the estimate too optimistic, so they compound. Cross-validation eliminates both biases, so neither direction matters once it is used. midterm · concept answered Which statement about LOOCV versus 10-fold cross-validation is correct? LOOCV has higher bias and lower variance, because it averages more estimates. LOOCV has lower bias and higher variance, because its n training sets are nearly identical to one another. LOOCV has lower variance, since it involves no random fold assignment. They are statistically equivalent; the only difference is computational cost. final · diagnosis answered A demand forecasting model is cross-validated with random 10-fold splits over four years of daily data and reports very low error. It performs far worse in production. Most likely cause? Too few folds, so the estimate was unstable. Random folds let the model train on future observations, which a deployed model can never do. Selection bias from comparing several models on the same folds. high , not too low. It cannot explain an estimate that turned out to be optimistic.\">Training-size bias, since each fold model trained on only 90% of the data. final · scope answered For which model can the LOOCV score be obtained from a single fit using the leverage formula? A support vector machine with a radial kernel A cubic polynomial regression fitted by least squares A logistic regression fitted by maximum likelihood A k-nearest-neighbours classifier final · calculation answered Roughly what proportion of the original distinct observations appears in a single bootstrap sample of size n, for large n? All of them — the sample is the same size as the original. About 50% About 63% About 37% final · critical thinking answered A team reserved a test set properly, cross-validated on the rest, chose a model, refit it, and scored 0.79 on the test set. They now want to try two more ideas. What is the correct procedure? Cross-validate the two new ideas, then score all three on the existing test set and report the best. Cross-validate the new ideas alongside the original candidates, choose there, and obtain fresh test data before reporting a new number. Score the two new ideas on the test set for comparison only, and keep reporting 0.79 for the original. Stop — once a test set has been used, no further model development is permitted. final · synthesis answered Which pairing of situation and validation design is the weakest ? Stratified 10-fold, for a classification problem with 2% positives LOOCV, for a least-squares regression on 200 observations Random 10-fold by row, for repeated daily measurements on 80 patients Rolling-origin validation, for a monthly sales forecast Technique Most validation questions reduce to one diagnostic question. Which? Show answer “Did the held-out data influence anything the model saw?” — through fitting, through selection, through preprocessing, through time, or through a repeated unit. If yes, the score is optimistic. If no, ask instead how much data the scored model got, which tells you whether it is pessimistic. Section B — Short answer and discussion, final style Roughly 4–8 marks each in a typical paper. Attempt each before opening it. B1 · Explain why test error decomposes into three parts, and use the decomposition to explain the U-shaped relationship between flexibility and test error. Model answer Expected test error = variance of the fit + squared bias of the fit + irreducible error. Variance is how much the fitted model would change if trained on a different sample from the same source. Bias is the error from approximating a complicated reality with a simpler form. Irreducible error is randomness in the world — unmeasured causes and measurement noise — and is a property of the problem, not the model. As flexibility rises, bias falls (the model can represent more shapes) and variance rises (the model chases the particular sample). Early on, bias falls faster than variance rises, so total error falls. Beyond some point bias has little left to give while variance keeps climbing, so total error rises again. The sum is therefore U-shaped, with a minimum at the flexibility where the two rates cross over. Since all three terms are non-negative, expected test error can never fall below the irreducible error — there is a floor, and it is above zero. Marking notes. Marks are for the mechanism, not the equation: the crossover of rates is what produces the U. A strong answer adds that training error has no such U — it declines monotonically with flexibility — which is precisely why it cannot be used to locate the minimum, and why validation exists. B2 · State the two drawbacks of the single train/validation split and explain how k-fold cross-validation addresses each. Model answer High variance of the estimate. The number depends on which observations happened to land in the validation set; repeat the split with a different seed and both the estimate and often the chosen model change. k-fold addresses this by averaging k held-out estimates, each using a different portion of the data, so the result depends far less on any one partition. Upward bias on error. The scored model was trained on only part of the data — half, under a 50/50 split — and methods perform worse with fewer observations, so the estimate overstates the error of the model that will actually be deployed on everything. k-fold addresses this by training each fold model on (k − 1)/k of the data: 90% at k = 10, much closer to the full dataset. Marking notes. Both drawbacks must be named with direction — “biased” without saying which way typically scores half. The strongest answers note that k-fold reduces rather than eliminates both, and that raising k to reduce bias raises the variance of the estimate for a separate reason, which is the subject of the next question. B3 · Explain the bias–variance trade-off in the choice of k, and justify the common recommendation of k = 5 or 10. Model answer Each fold model trains on about (k − 1)/k of the data, so larger k means more training data per fit and less upward bias in the error estimate. At k = n (LOOCV) each fit uses n − 1 observations and the estimate is nearly unbiased. But larger k also means the k training sets overlap more heavily. At k = n they differ by a single observation, so the n fitted models are near-copies and their errors are highly correlated. The mean of many highly correlated quantities has higher variance than the mean of the same number of weakly correlated ones, so LOOCV’s average is less stable than a 10-fold average despite averaging more terms. k = 5 or 10 sits where neither effect is severe: enough training data per fold to keep bias modest, enough independence between folds to keep variance modest, and a computational cost of 5 or 10 fits instead of n. Marking notes. The correlation argument is the whole question. Answers that say “LOOCV has higher variance because each error is based on one point” have named a real fact that is not the reason — the averaging is meant to absorb it, and the reason it absorbs it poorly is correlation between the fits. B4 · Describe k-fold cross-validation end to end for a project with a reserved test set, and state precisely what goes into production. Model answer First, before anything else touches the data, set aside a test set. Split the remaining training-and-validation data into k roughly equal folds. For each fold in turn: train each candidate model on the other k − 1 folds and score it on the held-out fold. Average each candidate’s k scores. Compare the averages and choose a candidate. Then refit the chosen candidate on all k folds together — the deliverable is none of the k fold models, each of which saw only (k − 1)/k of the available data. Averaging their coefficients is also not an option; there is no guarantee that the average of several good models is a good model. Finally, score the refitted model once on the reserved test set and report that number. Marking notes. The two ends are where marks are lost: the test set must be reserved before the folds are made, and the winner must be refitted on everything after the choice. The middle of the procedure is what everyone remembers and is usually worth the fewest marks. B5 · Distinguish selection bias from training-size bias. Give the direction of each and an example where both are present. Model answer Selection bias comes from choosing. Each candidate’s held-out score is unbiased on its own, but the maximum of several noisy scores favours whichever candidate got a favourable draw, so the winner’s score is optimistic. It grows with the number of candidates compared. Training-size bias comes from data volume. The scored model was fitted on less data than the deployed model will be, so it is weaker, and its error estimate is pessimistic. Both are present in a routine tuning exercise: 10-fold cross-validating twenty values of a parameter. Each fold model trains on 90% of the data, so every candidate’s average is slightly pessimistic; and the winning average is then selected as the best of twenty, which is optimistic. The two do not cancel in any predictable way — their sizes are unrelated — which is why the honest figure still has to come from a set that took part in neither the fitting nor the choosing. Marking notes. Naming both directions correctly is most of the marks. Explicitly rejecting the “they cancel out” intuition is worth an extra one, because it is a plausible-sounding conclusion that examiners plant in distractors. B6 · An analyst standardizes all the data, then selects the 100 attributes most correlated with the response, then runs 10-fold cross-validation on those 100. Identify what is wrong and state the general rule. Model answer Both preprocessing steps used the whole dataset, including every fold that will later be held out. The standardization means each fold’s own values contributed to the mean and standard deviation applied to the training data. The attribute screening is worse: it used the held-out responses to decide which attributes the model is allowed to use, so every fold model has already been given information about the observations it will be scored on. With a large candidate pool, this can produce a respectable cross-validated score on data containing no real signal whatsoever. The general rule: any step that learns something from the data must sit inside the cross-validation loop. Fit the scaler on the training folds and apply it to the held-out fold; re-run the attribute screening from scratch within each fold. If the selected attributes differ between folds, that instability is real information about how reliable the selection is — information the leaky version conceals. Marking notes. Both leaks should be named, and the attribute-selection one identified as the more serious. The best answers point out the diagnostic symptom: a good cross-validated score that collapses in production, with nothing in the validation arithmetic able to detect the problem. B7 · Why is random k-fold inappropriate for time-ordered data, and what should be used instead? Model answer Random folds place observations from after the held-out period into the training set, so the model is evaluated on the past having been trained on the future. A deployed model can never do this. Where there is trend, seasonality or drift, knowing the surrounding period makes the held-out points far easier than they will be in reality, so the estimate is optimistic — and the closer the correlation between adjacent periods, the larger the effect. Use rolling-origin validation instead: train on an initial stretch of history, evaluate on the period immediately following, then extend the training window forward and repeat. Each round uses only information that would have been available at the time. It uses less data per fit and yields a worse number than random folds — and that worse number is the honest one, because it is the only arrangement a deployed model ever faces. Marking notes. The phrase that earns marks is training on the future. Describing rolling origin concretely — the window extends, the evaluation period is always the next one — is worth more than naming it. Noting that the estimate legitimately gets worse, and that this is not a regression in quality, is a good closing point. B8 · Define LOOCV, give two advantages and one cost, and state exactly when the computational shortcut applies. Model answer LOOCV holds out a single observation, trains on the remaining n − 1, records the error on that one point, and repeats for every observation, averaging the n errors: CV (n) = (1/n)Σ MSE i , or the same with misclassification indicators for a classification problem. Advantages: almost no training-size bias, since each fit uses n − 1 observations; and complete determinism, since there is no random split — repeated runs give identical results. Cost: n model fits, which is prohibitive for large n or slow methods. The shortcut: for least-squares linear and polynomial regression, CV (n) = (1/n)Σ[(y i − ŷ i )/(1 − h i )]², computable from a single fit, where h i is the leverage. It does not generalise — logistic regression, SVMs, nearest-neighbours and trees all require the n refits. Marking notes. The scope restriction on the shortcut is a reliable mark and a reliable trap. An answer that adds why dividing by 1 − h i is the right correction — high-leverage points pull the fit toward themselves, so their ordinary residuals understate the error the model would make without them — shows understanding rather than recall. B9 · State the one-standard-error rule and explain the reasoning behind it. Why not simply take the minimum? Model answer Compute the standard error of the cross-validation estimate across folds. Identify the best score, then choose the simplest model whose score falls within one standard error of it. The reason not to take the minimum is that the cross-validation curve is itself an estimate with noise. When several models score within the noise band, the one that happens to be lowest is partly the luckiest, and re-running with different folds would likely promote a different one. Taking the minimum therefore commits to a distinction the data cannot support — and systematically favours the more flexible model, since flexibility adds variance and variance is what produces lucky low scores. Choosing the simplest model in the band is defensible on several grounds at once: lower variance, easier to explain, cheaper to run, and less likely to have won by accident. Marking notes. Watch what “simplest” means for the method in question. For subset selection it is the fewest predictors; for nearest-neighbours it is the largest k, since large k is the rigid end. Candidates who reflexively pick the smallest number lose marks here, and examiners know it. B10 · Describe the bootstrap, explain where the figure of roughly 63% comes from, and say what it is for that cross-validation is not. Model answer Draw n observations from your dataset of n with replacement to form a bootstrap sample; compute the statistic of interest on it; repeat B times. The spread of the B values estimates the sampling variability of the statistic, and their standard deviation estimates its standard error. Sampling must be with replacement — without it, drawing n from n simply returns the original dataset every time and there would be no variation at all. A given observation is missed by a single draw with probability (1 − 1/n), so it is missed by all n draws with probability (1 − 1/n) n , which tends to 1/e ≈ 0.368. It therefore appears with probability ≈ 0.632, leaving about 37% out-of-bag. Cross-validation estimates how well a modelling procedure predicts unseen data. The bootstrap estimates the uncertainty of a computed quantity — a coefficient, a ratio, a median, an allocation — particularly where no convenient standard-error formula exists, and without assuming the model is correctly specified. It is a poor tool for estimating test error, because bootstrap samples share about two-thirds of their observations with the original data, so “held-out” evaluation against the full dataset is heavily contaminated. Marking notes. The derivation of 0.632 is usually worth a mark on its own and is easy to produce under pressure. The clean statement of what each method estimates is the part that separates answers; the two are complementary, not competing. B11 · Compare random, rotation and stratified splitting. Give the situation where each is the right choice and the failure mode of each. Model answer Random: each observation is assigned at random. Unbiased on average and always defensible. Failure mode: nothing guarantees even coverage, so one set may end up heavy on a particular period or category purely by chance — a real risk when the dataset is small. Rotation: assign on a fixed cycle. Guarantees each part of the data is equally represented across sets, which helps when conditions drift over time. Failure mode: if the cycle length lines up with a period in the data, whole categories land in one set. A five-point rotation on five-day-a-week sales data sends every Monday, Wednesday and Friday to training and never checks them. Stratified: split randomly within each category — 60% of the Mondays, 60% of the Tuesdays, and so on, or 60% of each class. Gets the guaranteed coverage without the periodicity trap. It is the default for imbalanced classification, where a plain random fold can contain very few positives or none. Failure mode: you have to know which variable matters enough to stratify on, and stratifying on too many at once leaves strata too small to split sensibly. Marking notes. The weekday example is the one the examiner is looking for and should be given concretely rather than gestured at. A strong answer generalises it: the danger arises whenever the split’s period matches a period in the data, which is a statement about any cyclical structure, not about weekdays specifically. B12 · How does cross-validation change for a classification problem, and what should you be careful to cross-validate when one class is rare? Model answer The procedure is unchanged; only the quantity averaged differs. Instead of mean squared error, each fold contributes its misclassification rate — the fraction of held-out cases predicted wrongly — and the fold scores are averaged as before. With a rare class, two cautions. First, use stratified folds so each preserves the overall class proportions; plain random folds can vary substantially in positive rate and, with very rare positives and small folds, may contain none at all, in which case a model predicting the majority class scores perfectly on that fold while detecting nothing. Second, cross-validate the right quantity : accuracy is dominated by the majority class, so a stable estimate of accuracy is a stable estimate of something that does not distinguish a useful model from a useless one. Cross-validate sensitivity, expected cost, or AUC — whichever the decision actually depends on. Marking notes. The second point is where the marks are. Cross-validation improves the precision of an estimate and has no opinion about whether the estimated quantity is the right one — stating that explicitly connects this module to the metrics material and usually reads as a strong answer. Where the marks hide One habit earns marks on nearly every question in this section. Which? Show answer Stating the direction of every bias you name, and why it runs that way. “The estimate is biased” is worth almost nothing; “biased upward, because the winner was selected as the maximum of several noisy scores” is worth the question. Section C — Multi-part cases, final style Each part builds on the last. Work them in order. C1 · Audit the pipeline. A colleague describes their workflow. Identify every error, say which direction each pushes the reported number, and give the corrected procedure. 1 Loaded all 12,000 rows and standardized every attribute using the overall mean and standard deviation. 2 Dropped the attributes whose correlation with the response was below 0.05. 3 Split 80/20 into training and test. 4 On the training data, ran 10-fold cross-validation over 25 parameter settings and picked the best. 5 Reported the winning cross-validated score as the model’s expected performance, and separately checked it on the test set, which came out lower — concluding the test set was unrepresentative. Model answer Step 1 — leakage, optimistic. The mean and standard deviation were computed over rows that later became the test set, so test information entered the training pipeline. Compute them on the training data only and apply those same numbers to test. Step 2 — leakage, optimistic, and the more serious of the two. Attribute selection used every response, including the test responses, to decide what the model may use. This must be redone using only training data, and ideally re-run inside each cross-validation fold so its instability is visible. Step 3 — correct, but in the wrong place. The split should be the first operation, before any step that learns from data. Step 4 — correct in itself. Cross-validating 25 settings on the training portion is exactly right. Step 5 — two errors. First, the winning cross-validated score was selected as the best of 25 and so is optimistic; it is a basis for choosing, not a performance estimate. Second, the conclusion is backwards: the test score being lower is what the theory predicts, and blaming the test set inverts the diagnosis. The test set is the honest number. Corrected order: split off the test set first → within the training portion, run 10-fold cross-validation, performing standardization and attribute selection inside each fold → choose the setting → refit the winner, with its preprocessing, on the whole training portion → score once on the test set → report that. Marking notes. Four errors, and full marks require all four with directions. Step 5’s reversed conclusion is the highest-value item: it is the same misunderstanding that makes people “fix” a disappointing test result by looking for a friendlier test set, and examiners include it deliberately. C2 · Reading a cross-validation table. 10-fold CV of a nearest-neighbours classifier over five values of k, on 4,000 training-and-validation observations. Misclassification rate, with the standard error across folds in brackets: k 1 5 10 20 50 CV error 0.312 (0.018) 0.268 (0.015) 0.251 (0.014) 0.247 (0.016) 0.259 (0.013) (a) How many model fits has this table cost, and how many more before deployment? (b) Which k does the raw minimum select, and which does the one-standard-error rule select? (c) Why is k = 1 the worst row, and what would its training error have been? (d) Can 0.247 be reported to the client as the model’s expected error rate? (e) The client asks for a single accuracy figure. Describe what you would do to produce one. Model answer (a) 5 candidates × 10 folds = 50 fits . One more — the winner refitted on all 4,000 training-and-validation observations — so 51 in total. (Strictly, nearest-neighbours “fits” nothing; the cost is in the 50 rounds of neighbour searching, which is the same accounting.) (b) Raw minimum: k = 20 at 0.247. One-standard-error rule: the band is 0.247 + 0.016 = 0.263 , and the qualifying models are k = 10 (0.251), k = 20 (0.247) and k = 50 (0.259). The simplest of these is the least flexible , and for nearest-neighbours that is the largest k — so the rule selects k = 50 . Anyone who reads “simplest” as “smallest number” selects k = 10 and has the flexibility direction backwards. (c) k = 1 is maximally flexible: each new point copies its single nearest neighbour, so the boundary wraps around individual observations and the fit is dominated by variance. Its training error would be exactly zero , since every training point is its own nearest neighbour — which is why training error cannot be used to choose k at all. (d) No. 0.247 is the minimum of five noisy estimates and so is optimistically biased by selection; it is also the score of fold models trained on 90% of the data rather than the deployed model trained on 100%, which pushes the other way. The two do not cancel predictably. Report the score from a test set that took no part in either the fitting or the choosing. (e) Refit the chosen k on all 4,000 observations, score once on the reserved test set, and report that figure — with the caveat that a single number hides which errors are being made. If the classes are imbalanced or the errors have different costs, give expected cost or sensitivity alongside it, and state the null-classifier baseline so the number has something to be compared against. Marking notes. Part (b) is the discriminating one — the flexibility direction for k is the planted trap. In (a), forgetting the final refit is both an arithmetic slip and a conceptual one, and is usually marked as the latter. In (d), naming both biases and their directions scores higher than naming either alone. C3 · Wearable sensors. A team predicts falls in elderly patients from accelerometer data: 240 patients, each contributing about 90 days of readings, roughly 21,600 rows. Falls occur on 1.2% of patient-days. Random 10-fold cross-validation gives 98.9% accuracy and AUC 0.94. (a) What does 98.9% accuracy tell you here? (b) Name two structural reasons the AUC of 0.94 is likely to be optimistic. (c) Design a validation scheme that fixes both. (d) After the fix, AUC falls to 0.72 and the clinical lead asks whether the project has failed. Answer them. (e) The team also wants a confidence interval on the difference in AUC between their model and a simpler baseline. Which technique from this module applies, and why not cross-validation? Model answer (a) Almost nothing. Predicting “no fall” on every patient-day scores 98.8%, so the model is one-tenth of a point above a classifier that detects nothing. Accuracy on a 1.2% base rate cannot distinguish a useful model from a useless one, and the null baseline should be quoted every time this figure is. (b) First, grouped data : with 90 rows per patient and random folds, readings from the same patient sit in both training and held-out folds. The model can succeed by recognising individual patients’ movement signatures rather than learning what precedes a fall. Second, time ordering : within a patient, random folds train on days after the held-out day, so the model sees the future of the very patient it is being scored on. (c) Fold by patient , not by row, so every reading from a patient sits entirely in one fold; stratify the patient-level folds by whether the patient ever falls, so each fold contains some positive patients; and within each fold respect time order — train on each patient’s earlier days and evaluate on later ones, or hold out whole patients and evaluate their full series, which is closer to the deployment question of “a new patient arrives”. Reserve a set of patients as a test set before any of this begins. (d) No. The 0.72 is the first figure that answers the question the deployment poses — a new patient, going forward — and the 0.94 answered a question nobody will ever ask. What the project has avoided is promising 0.94 and delivering 0.72 in a clinical setting. Whether 0.72 is good enough is a separate question and a cost question: at a 1.2% base rate, what matters is how many falls are caught at a workable alert volume, and how the cost of a missed fall compares with the cost of a check that finds nothing. That calculation, not the AUC, should decide deployment. (e) The bootstrap . The difference between two AUCs has no convenient standard-error formula, and the bootstrap needs none: resample — at the patient level, to respect the grouping — recompute both AUCs and their difference on each resample, and read the interval off the spread of the B differences. Cross-validation is the wrong tool because it estimates predictive error, not the uncertainty of a computed statistic; it can tell you each model’s expected performance but not how confident you should be that one exceeds the other. Marking notes. Part (b) must name both violations to score well — most candidates find the grouping and miss the time ordering. Part (d) rewards refusing the framing: restating what each number measures, rather than defending or apologising for the drop. In (e), resampling at the patient level rather than the row level is the detail that shows the grouping point was properly understood. Exam workout — my working click to type; saves automatically Technique Across all three cases, what is the single most reliable opening move? Show answer Ask what the reported number would be for a model that does nothing, and ask what the held-out data was allowed to touch. Between them those two questions catch the majority of validation errors that appear on exams and in practice."},{"id":"targets","h":"Transfer practice — Validation","t":"Transfer practice — Validation Same ideas, unfamiliar settings Why these are here Each prompt below moves the module’s ideas into a situation the lessons never mentioned. If you can only answer a question in the wording it was taught in, what you have memorised is the example rather than the concept — and an exam that changes the setting will find that out. Answer these out loud before opening them. Exam target A pipeline standardises the full dataset, then splits it into ten folds and cross-validates. Name the problem and its consequence. Show answer Leakage. The mean and standard deviation were computed using the held-out records, so each fold was not genuinely held out and every fold score is optimistic. The same applies to imputation values, variable selection and any encoding learned from the data. All of it belongs inside the fold, computed from the training part only. Exam target Thirty model configurations were compared on the same validation set. None of them saw validation data during fitting. Why is the winner's score still optimistic? Show answer Because the selection step used it. Each configuration's validation score contains a component of luck — how well it happens to fit that set's random patterns — and taking the maximum over thirty of them preferentially selects a large lucky component. The fitting was clean; the choosing was not. An untouched test set is what restores an unbiased estimate. Exam target With three hundred records, argue for ten folds over two, and over leave-one-out. Show answer Two folds train each model on 150 records, so the estimate describes a weaker model than the one you will ship — pessimistic. Leave-one-out requires 300 fits whose models differ by a single record, so their errors are highly correlated and averaging them stabilises little, giving a high-variance estimate at high cost. Ten folds trains on 270, leaving modest bias, and averages ten reasonably distinct results. Exam target One class makes up 5% of the records. What changes about how the folds are built? Show answer Use stratified folds, which preserve each class's proportion in every fold. With random assignment and a rare class, some folds may contain very few positive records or none at all, making their scores erratic and, in the extreme, undefined for metrics that depend on the rare class. Exam target Cross-validation has selected a model form. What exactly goes into production? Show answer That model form refitted on all the data available — training and validation together. Not the best-performing fold model, which saw less data than necessary, and not an average of the fold coefficients, which corresponds to no fitted model at all. The folds were a measurement procedure, not a set of candidate deliverables. Exam target Records are hourly readings from one sensor over two years. Why would standard k-fold mislead, and what replaces it? Show answer The records are ordered in time and adjacent readings are related, so random folds let the model train on the hours either side of a held-out reading — interpolation rather than prediction. Validate forward instead: fit on an initial stretch, predict the next period, add it to the training data, refit, repeat."},{"id":"sec-14","h":"How the module fits together","t":"How the module fits together The through-line across all four lessons Lesson 1 Establishes the disease: models absorb random effects, so training scores are inflated. Lesson 2 Prescribes the cure — and then finds a second inflation, caused by choosing rather than fitting, needing a third set. Lesson 3 Gets practical about carving the data up, and shows how a tidy-looking split can quietly ruin the sets. Lesson 4 Reuses the data instead of dividing it once, so nothing important is left out of training. Lesson 5 Names the quantity being estimated and splits it into bias, variance and a floor nobody gets under. Lesson 6 Shows a single split is both noisy and pessimistic — the two defects cross-validation was built to fix. Lesson 7 Takes k to its maximum, removing the bias and the randomness, and paying n fits for it. Lesson 8 Finds a second bias–variance trade-off inside the estimate itself, and lands on 5 or 10. Lesson 9 Carries it all to classification, and handles tuning and assessing with the same data. Lesson 10 Four ways a correctly executed cross-validation still lies, all of them announced by a flattering score. Lesson 11 Switches question entirely: not how good the model is, but how uncertain a computed number is. Six things worth noticing Two different biases, often confused. Training bias comes from fitting; validation bias comes from selecting. Cross-validation reduces the noise behind the second one but does not remove it — you still need a test set. The test set is single-use. Nothing in the lecture says this outright, but it follows directly from Lesson 2: any set you make a choice on becomes a validation set. The estimate has its own bias–variance problem. Lesson 5’s trade-off is about the model; Lesson 8’s is about the number you use to judge it. Larger k gives the estimate less bias and more variance, entirely independently of whatever the model is doing. Keeping the two trade-offs apart is most of what this module asks of you. Every failure mode announces itself the same way. Leakage, random folds on time-ordered data, repeated units across folds, selecting and reporting from one set — all four produce a better score than the model deserves. A result that looks too good is a diagnosis, not a celebration. Cross-validation improves precision, not relevance. It has no opinion about whether the quantity being averaged is the right one. Cross-validated accuracy on a 1% base rate is a very stable estimate of something useless. k appears three times in this course. k-means, k-nearest-neighbor, k-fold. The source material flags this. Expect a question that tests whether you know which is which. Likely exam targets Explain why training-set performance is optimistic, using real vs. random effects. Give a small-sample example of a spurious pattern and say what it proves. State the job of each of the three sets, and when the validation step is skipped. Explain why the best validation score among many models is biased upward. Give common split ratios and the principle behind them. Contrast random and rotation splitting, with the weekday example. Describe k-fold cross-validation and say what gets deployed at the end. Give the three-part decomposition of expected test error and use it to explain the U-shaped flexibility curve. Explain why expected test error can never reach zero. State the two drawbacks of the single-split approach, with the direction of each. Distinguish selection bias from training-size bias and give the direction of both. Define LOOCV, give two advantages and one cost, and say exactly where the leverage shortcut applies. Explain why LOOCV has higher variance than 10-fold despite averaging more terms. State the one-standard-error rule, justify it, and apply it to a table — including working out which direction “simplest” runs for the method in question. Explain why every data-dependent preprocessing step belongs inside the cross-validation loop. Explain why random folds are wrong for time-ordered data and describe rolling-origin validation. Explain why repeated measurements on the same unit require grouped folds. Describe the bootstrap, derive the 63.2% figure, and contrast what it estimates with what cross-validation estimates. Say how cross-validation changes for classification, and what to stratify and to cross-validate when a class is rare. Everything above is class material. Additional resources follow — the resampling schemes for grouped and time-ordered data, hyperparameter search, the model-comparison tests, metrics and calibration, practice problems and reading — none of it taught in this module. Module 3 — my own summary click to type; saves automatically"},{"id":"extra","h":"Additional resources","t":"Additional resources Everything below this line is not class material Read this first The lessons above are the module as taught, plus the extended treatment already flagged in the footer. Everything from here on was assembled from outside sources — textbooks, method papers and the documentation of the standard libraries. None of it is examinable unless your instructor says so, and where a convention differs from the lectures, the lectures win . Lessons 4 and 10 establish that plain k-fold assumes rows are independent and interchangeable, and that when they are not it silently fails. The lessons stop at naming the failures. Extra 1 gives the resampling schemes that fix each one. Extra 2 covers the machinery that sits around validation — how to search a hyperparameter space, how to tell whether two models actually differ, and what to do when accuracy is the wrong number. Extra 3 is practice and reading. Extra 1 Every resampling scheme, and the failure each one exists to fix. Extra 2 Searching, comparing and scoring — the apparatus around the validation loop. Extra 3 Practice problems and where to read further."},{"id":"x1","h":"The resampling schemes","t":"Extra 1 · not class material The resampling schemes Plain k-fold makes one assumption: that shuffling the rows is harmless. Every scheme below exists because that assumption fails in a specific, common way. The question that picks the scheme Lesson 10 lists four ways cross-validation quietly breaks. Three of them are structural properties of the data, and each has a standard splitter attached. The question to ask before choosing any of them is: what would have to be true of a new observation for my estimate to be honest? If a new observation… Then the held-out fold must… Use is just another draw from the same population be a random subset KFold is another draw, but a class is rare preserve the class proportions StratifiedKFold comes from a patient, site, user or device the model has never seen contain whole groups, never split one GroupKFold happens after everything you trained on lie strictly in the future TimeSeriesSplit is separated in time from the training data by a lag be time-ordered and gapped Purged / embargoed CV The catalogue Scheme How it splits The failure it fixes KFold Partition into k equal parts; hold each out in turn. Lesson 4's method. — StratifiedKFold As KFold, but each fold keeps the overall class proportions. Lesson 9's point. With 2% positives and k = 10, plain KFold will sometimes produce a fold with no positives at all, making metrics undefined and the fold-to-fold variance enormous. The default for classification , and there is essentially never a reason not to use it. RepeatedKFold RepeatedStratifiedKFold Run the whole k-fold procedure several times with different random partitions, and average everything. Lesson 6's drawback, one level up. The k-fold estimate is itself a random variable depending on how the folds fell; repeating and averaging shrinks that. Cheap insurance when the dataset is small — 5-fold repeated 10 times gives 50 fits and a far more stable number than one 50-fold run, which is not the same thing at all. GroupKFold StratifiedGroupKFold LeaveOneGroupOut Folds are built from whole groups, so every row sharing a group label lands on the same side. Lesson 10's non-independent rows. Multiple scans per patient, multiple sessions per user, multiple photographs of the same object: split them randomly and the model memorises the individual rather than the phenomenon, and the score can be wildly optimistic. This is the most damaging and most frequently missed failure of the four. ShuffleSplit StratifiedShuffleSplit Draw a random train/test split, independently, n times. Folds may overlap and need not cover the data. Decouples the number of repetitions from the size of the held-out set — k-fold ties them together, so k = 10 forces a 10% test fold. Also called Monte Carlo cross-validation. Use when you want, say, 30 estimates at a 20% holdout. LeaveOneOut LeavePOut Hold out one observation (or p) at a time. Lesson 7's method. LeavePOut grows combinatorially and is almost never practical. TimeSeriesSplit Expanding window: train on the first i folds, validate on fold i + 1, repeat. Training set grows; the validation set is always later in time. Lesson 10's ordered data. Random folds train on the future to predict the past, which is not a task anyone will ever ask the model to perform, and the resulting estimate is meaningless rather than merely optimistic. Rolling / sliding window As above, but the training window has a fixed length and slides forward. The regime changes over time, so old data is not merely less relevant but actively misleading. Lesson 10's fourth failure — the future not resembling the past. Purged and embargoed CV Time-ordered folds, with a gap removed between train and validation, and a further period after the validation fold excluded from training. Financial and other data where the label for time t depends on outcomes over the following days. Without a gap, the training rows immediately before the validation fold overlap it in information even though they precede it in time. Standard in quantitative finance; worth knowing whenever a label has a forward-looking horizon. Blocked CV Contiguous blocks held out rather than scattered rows. Any autocorrelated data — spatial as well as temporal. A scattered holdout is surrounded by its own near-duplicates. Nested CV An inner loop tunes, an outer loop assesses. Lesson 9's method. Worth restating in this list because the splitter in each loop must independently respect whichever structural constraint applies — a nested scheme on patient data needs GroupKFold in both loops, not just the outer one. Constraints combine, and they combine in one direction Grouped time-series data with rare classes is not exotic — it is what most production datasets look like. The rule is that each structural constraint removes permissible splits, and you must satisfy all of them at once: time ordering dominates, grouping is next, stratification is applied within whatever freedom remains. If you cannot satisfy them all, the honest move is to satisfy the ones that would otherwise inflate the score and accept a noisier estimate, rather than dropping a constraint because the splitter is awkward to write. quick check answered You have 3,000 MRI scans from 300 patients, roughly ten per patient, and you are predicting a diagnosis. Which splitter? StratifiedKFold, so each fold has the right proportion of positive diagnoses. StratifiedGroupKFold, grouping by patient. LeaveOneOut, since 3,000 fits is affordable and it has the lowest bias. Bootstrap variants, beyond Lesson 11 Lesson 11 covers the bootstrap for estimating uncertainty and notes why the naive version cannot be used for test error: about 63.2% of the original rows appear in any given resample, so roughly 36.8% are out-of-bag — but the training set is still contaminated by duplicates of the very rows you are scoring on. The standard repairs: Estimator What it does Note Out-of-bag (OOB) error Score each observation using only the resamples that excluded it, and average. Clean, and free if you are already bagging. This is why a random forest reports an OOB score without needing cross-validation at all. Biased slightly upward, since each row is scored by an ensemble of about 63% the full size. .632 estimator 0.368 × training error + 0.632 × OOB error. Blends an optimistic number with a pessimistic one in the proportions the resampling implies. Breaks down for models that can memorise, where training error is near zero and the blend is dragged far too low. .632+ estimator The same, with the weight adjusted by how much overfitting is actually occurring. The repair for that failure. Efron and Tibshirani's recommended version, and competitive with cross-validation on small samples — which is where it earns its keep. The practical position: use cross-validation to estimate error, use the bootstrap to put an interval around a statistic. The .632 family exists for small-sample problems where cross-validation's estimate is itself too noisy to be useful, and it is worth knowing the names mainly so that you recognise them when a paper reports one. quick check answered Why does purged cross-validation remove a gap between the training and validation folds? To shrink the training set, so that the estimate is conservative rather than optimistic. Because a label computed over a forward window can overlap the validation period even when the row precedes it. Because adjacent observations in time are autocorrelated and therefore near-duplicates. Which scheme my data needs click to type; saves automatically Link to Lesson 10 What single habit prevents most of these failures? Show answer Before writing any splitter, describe in one sentence what a genuinely new observation looks like — a new patient, next week, a customer from a region not yet seen. Then check that your held-out fold matches that description. Almost every failure in this list is a case where the held-out data was new in some trivial sense and familiar in the sense that mattered."},{"id":"x2","h":"Searching, comparing and scoring","t":"Extra 2 · not class material Searching, comparing and scoring Validation produces a number. Three questions sit around it that the lessons do not answer: how you generated the candidates, whether the difference between two numbers is real, and whether the number was the right one to compute. How to generate the candidates Lesson 9's nested cross-validation assumes a set of candidate models to choose among. Where that set comes from matters more than it looks, because the search strategy determines how much of the hyperparameter space you actually explore per unit of compute. Strategy How it works When to use it Grid search Every combination of a specified list of values per hyperparameter. Two or three hyperparameters with a small number of sensible values each. Cost multiplies: 6 × 4 × 5 values is 120 configurations before cross-validation multiplies it again. Random search Bergstra & Bengio, 2012 Sample configurations at random from distributions over each hyperparameter, for a fixed budget of trials. Almost always better than grid search at the same cost , and the reason is worth understanding: in practice only a few hyperparameters matter much, and a grid wastes most of its trials re-testing the same values of the important one while varying unimportant ones. With 9 grid points arranged 3 × 3, only 3 distinct values of the important parameter are ever tried; 9 random draws try 9. The advantage grows with the number of hyperparameters. Successive halving / Hyperband Evaluate many configurations cheaply — few epochs, a subsample of the data — keep the best fraction, give them more resources, repeat. Training is expensive and partial results are informative. A tournament rather than a full evaluation of everyone. Available as HalvingGridSearchCV and HalvingRandomSearchCV . Bayesian optimisation Fit a surrogate model of the validation score as a function of the hyperparameters, and use it to choose where to look next, balancing promising regions against unexplored ones. Each evaluation is genuinely expensive — hours of GPU time — so it is worth spending computation deciding what to try. Overkill when a fit takes seconds. Every one of these is model selection, and the selection effect is the same Lesson 2's argument — that a set becomes a validation set the moment you select on it — does not care how the candidates were generated. Searching 5,000 random configurations produces a winner whose validation score is more optimistically biased than the winner of a 10-configuration grid, because more selection happened. A bigger search therefore increases the need for a clean outer assessment, which is Lesson 9's nested cross-validation. The practical error is to conclude that a thorough search makes the resulting score more trustworthy; it makes it less so. Is the difference between two models real? Model A averages 0.847 across ten folds and model B averages 0.839. Lesson 8's one-standard-error rule gives one way to think about this; the formal question is whether a paired test can be run on the fold scores. The answer is that the obvious test is wrong, and this has been known since 1998. Why the paired t-test on fold scores is invalid A paired t-test assumes the paired differences are independent. In k-fold cross-validation the training sets overlap heavily — with k = 10, any two training sets share about 89% of their rows — so the fold results are strongly positively correlated. The test's variance estimate is therefore far too small, and it declares differences significant that are pure noise. Dietterich (1998) measured the type I error rate and concluded the test should never be used in this form. Test Setting Verdict Paired t-test on k-fold scores The obvious thing to do. Invalid. Overlapping training sets break independence; false positives are common. Difference of two proportions Comparing accuracies as proportions. Invalid. Same conclusion, high type I error, and it ignores that both models were scored on the same instances. McNemar's test Two models, one test set, each can be trained only once. Recommended when training is expensive. Build a 2 × 2 table of which instances each model got right; the test uses only the two disagreement cells, ignoring instances both got right or both got wrong. Low type I error. 5×2cv paired t-test Two models you can afford to train ten times. Recommended by Dietterich. Five replications of a 2-fold split, so train and test halves swap and no two training sets overlap within a replication. Slightly more powerful than McNemar's and it measures variation due to the choice of training set, which McNemar's cannot. Corrected resampled t-test Nadeau & Bengio Repeated k-fold, where you want to use all the fold scores. Inflates the variance estimate by a correction factor that accounts for the training-set overlap. The practical compromise when you already have repeated cross-validation results and do not want to rerun everything as 5×2. The honest summary for most coursework and much practice: a gap of a fraction of a percent between two models, on one cross-validation run, is not evidence of anything. Lesson 6 makes the point about the estimate's variance; this is the same point with a decision procedure attached. If the choice matters, use 5×2cv or McNemar's; if it does not, take the simpler model, which is Lesson 8's one-standard-error rule doing the same job informally. quick check answered Why is a paired t-test on ten fold scores not a valid way to compare two models? Because ten folds is too small a sample for a t-test to apply. Because the training sets overlap heavily, so the fold results are not independent and the variance is underestimated. Because accuracy scores are bounded in [0, 1] and therefore not normally distributed. Was the number the right number? Everything in the module treats \"error\" as a single quantity. Which quantity is a modelling decision, and on unbalanced or asymmetric problems it is the decision that matters most. Metric Answers Watch out for Accuracy What fraction did it get right? Meaningless under imbalance. With 0.2% positives, \"always negative\" scores 99.8%. Always compare against that do-nothing baseline before quoting an accuracy. Precision / recall / F1 Of those flagged, how many were real? Of the real ones, how many did we flag? The harmonic mean of the two. F1 hides the trade-off it averages. Quote precision and recall separately when the two errors have different costs, which is usually. ROC-AUC How well does the model rank positives above negatives, across all thresholds? Threshold-free and popular, but optimistic under heavy imbalance because the false-positive rate has a huge denominator. A model can look excellent and still flag mostly false alarms. PR-AUC / average precision The same, from the precision–recall curve. The right ranking metric when positives are rare. Its baseline is the positive rate, so it cannot flatter a model the way ROC-AUC can. Log loss / Brier score How good are the predicted probabilities ? Use when the probability itself is the output — a risk score, an expected value. Both punish confident mistakes heavily. MAE / RMSE / MAPE Regression error, in absolute, squared and percentage terms. RMSE punishes large errors disproportionately, which is right when one big miss is worse than several small ones, and wrong when it is not. MAPE is undefined at zero and asymmetric — it penalises over-prediction and under-prediction differently. Calibration When it says 70%, does it happen 70% of the time? Not a metric you optimise but a property you check, with a reliability diagram. A well-ranked model can be badly calibrated — which matters enormously if anyone downstream multiplies the probability by a cost. The metric belongs inside the loop, not after it If you tune on accuracy and report F1, the model you selected was the one best at accuracy. Cross-validation selects on whatever you score with, so the metric has to be the one you actually care about from the start. The same applies to the decision threshold: choosing it after seeing the test set is selection on the test set, and Lesson 2's warning applies without modification. Two things worth knowing that complicate the lessons Learning curves answer a question the validation score cannot. Plot training and validation error against the number of training observations. If both have flattened and sit close together, the model is limited by its form and more data will not help — get a better model. If they are far apart and the validation curve is still falling, the model is limited by data and more of it will help. This is the practical diagnostic that turns Lesson 5's bias–variance decomposition into a decision about what to do next, and it costs only a handful of extra fits. The U-shaped curve is not the whole story any more. Lesson 5's U-shape — error falls, then rises as flexibility increases — is correct for the classical regime and is what the exam expects. For very heavily over-parameterised models, empirical work since about 2018 has documented double descent : test error rises to a peak near the point where the model can just interpolate the training data, and then falls again as capacity grows further. It does not overturn the bias–variance decomposition, which still holds as an identity, but it does mean \"more flexible is now worse\" is not a universal law. Worth knowing about; not worth putting in an exam answer unless asked. quick check answered A fraud model has ROC-AUC 0.94 on a dataset with 0.3% positives. What should you check before celebrating? The precision–recall curve, since ROC-AUC flatters models on heavily imbalanced data. Whether the folds were stratified, since otherwise some may contain no fraud at all. The accuracy, to confirm it is above 99%. Notes on search, comparison and metrics click to type; saves automatically Link to Lesson 2 Does a bigger hyperparameter search give you a more trustworthy score? Show answer The opposite. More candidates means more selection, and selection is precisely what inflates a validation score — the winner of 5,000 trials is more likely to have won on luck than the winner of 10. A large search may well find a better model, but its validation score is a worse estimate of that model's performance, so the outer assessment matters more, not less."},{"id":"x3","h":"Practice, reading and tools","t":"Extra 3 · not class material Practice, reading and tools Problems on the material in Extras 1 and 2 — the resampling schemes, the search, the comparison tests and the metrics. Lesson 12 covers the examinable material; this covers what sits around it. Part A — choosing the scheme Problem 1 — five datasets, five splitters Name the resampling scheme for each and say what goes wrong with plain KFold. (a) 50,000 independent loan applications, 4% default rate. (b) Daily electricity demand for six years; you will forecast next month. (c) 800 speech recordings from 40 speakers; you will deploy to new speakers. (d) 300 rows total, one model, and the estimate is very noisy. (e) Trades labelled by the return over the following ten days. Show worked solution (a) StratifiedKFold. Plain KFold will occasionally produce folds with very few defaults, making the fold-to-fold variance large and some metrics unstable. The rows are independent, so nothing stronger is needed. (b) TimeSeriesSplit , or a rolling window if the demand regime has changed. Plain KFold trains on 2025 to predict 2021, which is not a task anyone will ask of the model — the estimate does not merely flatter, it measures the wrong thing. (c) GroupKFold by speaker. Plain KFold puts some of a speaker's recordings in training and others in validation, so the model can score well by recognising voices rather than content. The deployment condition is a new speaker, so the folds must reproduce it. (d) RepeatedStratifiedKFold. The problem is the variance of the estimate itself, which is Lesson 6's drawback; repeating the whole k-fold procedure with different partitions and averaging shrinks it. Note that this is not the same as raising k — raising k reduces bias and increases the variance of the estimate, which is the opposite of what is wanted here. (e) Purged and embargoed time-series CV. Time ordering alone is not enough: a training row from nine days before the validation fold has a label determined by returns inside that fold. Purge the overlapping rows and embargo a period afterwards. Problem 2 — combining constraints Customer-service transcripts: 40,000 conversations from 5,000 customers over three years, labelled for escalation (7% positive). The model will be deployed next quarter on customers both old and new. Which constraints bind, and in what order? Show worked solution Time ordering binds hardest , because deployment is on the future: validation folds must be later than training folds, or the estimate answers a question nobody asked. Grouping by customer binds partly — the model will see some familiar customers and some new ones, so the honest thing is to report both numbers rather than pick one: a grouped split measures performance on genuinely new customers, an ungrouped time split measures performance on the realistic mixture. Stratification applies within whatever freedom is left , and with 7% positives in folds of thousands it is unlikely to bind at all. The general lesson is the one in Extra 1: constraints only remove permissible splits, and when they conflict the resolution is to report more than one number rather than to silently drop the inconvenient constraint. Two honest estimates bracketing the truth beat one estimate that quietly answers the wrong question. Part B — search and comparison Problem 3 — counting fits You search 8 values of one hyperparameter and 5 of another using 5-fold inner cross-validation, assessed by 5-fold outer cross-validation, and then refit the winner on everything. How many fits? Show worked solution Configurations: 8 × 5 = 40. Inner loop per outer fold: 40 × 5 = 200 fits, plus one refit of the winner on that outer fold's training portion = 201. Across 5 outer folds: 5 × 201 = 1,005 . Final refit on all the data: 1,006 total . The point of the arithmetic is the cost of honesty. A single non-nested search would be 40 × 5 + 1 = 201 fits and would give you a tuned model plus an optimistic score. Nested cross-validation costs five times that and gives you the same tuned model plus an honest score. What you are buying is the estimate, not the model — which is why Lesson 9 insists the two loops are doing different jobs. Problem 4 — grid or random? You have four hyperparameters and budget for 64 fits. A grid gives you 2, 2, 4 and 4 values. Why is random search likely to do better? Show worked solution Because in practice only one or two of the four usually matter much, and the grid has decided in advance how finely to explore each — before you know which is which. If the important one is a 2-value axis, the grid tries exactly two values of it, and spends the other 62 fits varying parameters that barely move the score. Random search with 64 draws tries up to 64 distinct values of every parameter, so the important axis is explored 32 times more thoroughly at identical cost. This is Bergstra and Bengio's result. The advantage grows with the number of hyperparameters and with how unequal their importance is, which is why random search is the sensible default once you are past two or three parameters. Successive halving does better still when partial training results are informative. Problem 5 — is the difference real? Model A: 0.8471 mean accuracy over 10 folds, standard deviation 0.019. Model B: 0.8392, standard deviation 0.022. A colleague runs a paired t-test on the fold scores, gets p = 0.03, and declares A the winner. What is wrong, and what should be done? Show worked solution The test is invalid. With k = 10 any two training sets share about 89% of their rows, so the fold scores are strongly correlated and the paired t-test's independence assumption fails. Its variance estimate is far too small, so it produces small p-values for differences that are noise. Dietterich's 1998 paper measured this and recommends the test never be used in this form. What to do instead. If the models can be trained ten times, run the 5×2cv paired t-test, which avoids overlapping training sets within each replication. If training is expensive and you have one test set, use McNemar's test on the instance-level agreement table. If you already have repeated k-fold results and do not want to rerun, apply the Nadeau–Bengio corrected resampled t-test, which inflates the variance estimate to account for the overlap. And a sanity check. The gap is 0.0079 against fold standard deviations of about 0.02. Before any test, that is a difference well inside the noise of the estimate — Lesson 8's one-standard-error rule would say take the simpler model and stop. Problem 6 — the metric A hospital readmission model reports 91% accuracy on data where 11% of patients are readmitted. The team is pleased. Walk through what you would ask. Show worked solution First: what does the do-nothing model score? Predicting \"no readmission\" for everyone gives 89%. The model's 91% is two points above a model that has learned nothing, which reframes the result entirely. Second: recall. The clinical purpose is presumably to intervene before readmission, so what matters is the fraction of actual readmissions caught. A model can reach 91% accuracy while catching almost none of them. Third: precision, and the cost ratio. Each flagged patient consumes follow-up resource. The precision–recall curve, not accuracy and not ROC-AUC, is where the usable trade-off lives at an 11% base rate. Fourth: calibration. If the output is a risk score that clinicians will act on at different thresholds, it needs to mean what it says — a reliability diagram, not a single number. Fifth, and easy to miss: was accuracy what the search optimised? If so, the selected model is the one best at accuracy, and re-scoring it on recall afterwards does not undo that. The metric has to be inside the loop. Part C — self-test self-test answered What is the difference between RepeatedKFold with k = 5, 10 repeats, and plain KFold with k = 50? Both do 50 fits, but repeated 5-fold reduces the estimate's variance while k = 50 reduces its bias and raises its variance. They are equivalent — both produce 50 held-out estimates, so they give the same answer. Repeated 5-fold is more expensive but produces a lower-bias estimate. self-test answered Why does a random forest report an out-of-bag score without needing cross-validation? Because its training error is already unbiased, so no held-out data is needed. Because each tree omits about 36.8% of the rows, so every observation has trees that never saw it. Because it has no hyperparameters to tune, so there is nothing to validate. self-test answered Training error is 0.04 and validation error is 0.31, and the validation curve is still falling as you add data. What does the learning curve say to do? Use a more flexible model — the wide gap means it is underfitting. Get more data, or reduce variance — the gap is large and the curve has not flattened. Suspect leakage — the gap is too large to be genuine overfitting. self-test answered Which comparison test is recommended when each model can be trained only once? The 5×2cv paired t-test. McNemar's test. The paired t-test on cross-validation fold scores. Part D — written questions These follow the Lesson 12 format but cover the appendix material. Write the answer before opening it. X1 · Explain why grouped data breaks k-fold cross-validation, and describe the fix and what it costs. Model answer k-fold assumes rows are exchangeable, so that a randomly held-out row is a fair stand-in for a future observation. When several rows share a source — ten scans per patient, many sessions per user — that assumption fails: split them randomly and the model can score well by recognising the source rather than learning the phenomenon, because the validation fold contains rows whose siblings are in training. The reported error is then an estimate of performance on already-seen sources, which is not the deployment condition if new patients or users will arrive. The fix is GroupKFold, or StratifiedGroupKFold when classes are also unbalanced: folds are assembled from whole groups, so every row sharing a label lands on the same side of the split. The cost is a noisier estimate — with only 40 speakers, say, the fold-to-fold variance is driven by which speakers land where, not by which recordings — and coarser control of fold sizes, since groups differ in size. Both are worth paying, because the alternative is a number that is confidently wrong. Marking notes. The marks are for identifying the deployment condition as the thing the folds must reproduce, not for naming the splitter. A strong answer notes that the failure is silent — the score goes up , so nothing looks broken — and that the same reasoning covers time ordering, where the deployment condition is \"later than everything I trained on\". X2 · A colleague reports that a 5,000-configuration random search found a model scoring 0.91 in cross-validation, up from 0.88 for their previous 20-configuration grid. Explain what you can and cannot conclude. Model answer You can conclude that a wider search found a configuration with a higher cross-validation score, and quite possibly that it is a genuinely better model — more thorough searching does tend to find better configurations. You cannot conclude that the model performs at 0.91. The cross-validation score of the winner of a search is biased upward, and the bias grows with the number of candidates, because the winner is selected partly on the noise in its estimate. With 5,000 candidates, the maximum over 5,000 noisy estimates sits well above the true performance of whichever configuration is actually best. The 0.88 from 20 candidates was less inflated; comparing the two numbers directly therefore overstates the improvement, and some of the apparent three-point gain is selection, not skill. This is Lesson 2's argument — a set becomes a validation set once you select on it — with the selection intensity varied. The remedy is Lesson 9's: an outer loop, or a genuinely untouched test set, to assess the winner. Note the uncomfortable implication, which is worth stating: a more thorough search makes the resulting score less trustworthy, so it increases rather than reduces the need for a clean assessment. Marking notes. The mark is for connecting search intensity to the size of the selection bias. Answers that say only \"the winner's score is optimistic\" get partial credit; the full answer explains why 5,000 candidates is worse than 20 and what to do about it. X3 · Explain why the paired t-test on cross-validation fold scores is invalid, and name the appropriate alternatives for two different situations. Model answer The paired t-test assumes the paired differences are independent draws. In k-fold cross-validation the training sets overlap heavily — at k = 10, any two of them share about 89% of their rows — so the fold results are strongly positively correlated. The test estimates the variance of the mean difference as though the folds were independent, which makes that estimate far too small, the t-statistic too large, and the p-value too small. Dietterich (1998) measured the type I error rate empirically and concluded the test should not be used in this form; the difference-of-two-proportions test fails for the same reason. If each model can be trained ten times: the 5×2cv paired t-test. Five replications of a two-fold split, with train and test halves swapped, so no two training sets within a replication overlap. It is slightly more powerful than the alternative below and it measures variation arising from the choice of training set. If training is expensive and you have one test set: McNemar's test, built from the 2 × 2 table of which instances each model got right and using only the two disagreement cells. Low type I error, one training run each. A third option, if repeated k-fold results already exist, is the Nadeau–Bengio corrected resampled t-test, which inflates the variance estimate by a factor accounting for the overlap. Marking notes. Marks are for the mechanism — overlapping training sets, correlated folds, underestimated variance, inflated significance — not for the citation. A strong answer adds the practical point that a difference smaller than the fold-to-fold standard deviation needs no test at all, since the one-standard-error rule already says to take the simpler model. Sources and further reading Source What it gives you James, Witten, Hastie & Tibshirani, An Introduction to Statistical Learning , ch. 5 The lessons' own backbone, and still the clearest treatment of the validation set approach, k-fold, LOOCV and the bootstrap. Chapter 6 has the one-standard-error rule. Free PDF. Hastie, Tibshirani & Friedman, The Elements of Statistical Learning , ch. 7 The same ground at research level: the full bias–variance–covariance treatment, the .632 and .632+ estimators, AIC/BIC/Cp, and the \"one-standard-error\" justification. Free PDF. scikit-learn User Guide §3.1 and §3.2 scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/cross_validation.html Every splitter in Extra 1 with a picture of how it partitions the data, plus the grid/random/halving search machinery and the pipeline objects that keep preprocessing out of the leakage trap. The visual comparison of CV iterators is the single most useful page. Raschka (2018), \"Model Evaluation, Model Selection, and Algorithm Selection in Machine Learning\" arXiv:1811.12808 Free, long, and the best single document on exactly the Extra 2 material — nested CV, the comparison tests, confidence intervals on performance. If you read one thing from this list, read this. Kuhn & Johnson, Applied Predictive Modeling , ch. 4 Resampling from a practitioner's angle, with useful judgement about when the differences between schemes actually matter. Dietterich (1998), \"Approximate Statistical Tests for Comparing Supervised Classification Learning Algorithms\". The source of the warning about the paired t-test and of the 5×2cv procedure. Nadeau & Bengio (2003), \"Inference for the Generalization Error\". The corrected resampled t-test. Bergstra & Bengio (2012), \"Random Search for Hyper-Parameter Optimization\". Short, and the argument is more interesting than the result. Li et al. (2018), \"Hyperband\". The bandit-based approach to search under a resource budget. Efron & Tibshirani, An Introduction to the Bootstrap . Where the .632 and .632+ estimators come from. Varma & Simon (2006), \"Bias in error estimation when using cross-validation for model selection\". The empirical demonstration of why Lesson 9's nesting is not optional — the non-nested estimate can be optimistic by a startling margin on small samples. Cawley & Talbot (2010), \"On Over-fitting in Model Selection and Subsequent Selection Bias in Performance Evaluation\". The long version of the same point, and the paper to cite when someone insists their hyperparameter search was harmless. Tool Language For scikit-learn sklearn.model_selection Python Every splitter named in Extra 1; cross_validate , GridSearchCV , RandomizedSearchCV , HalvingRandomSearchCV ; learning_curve and validation_curve ; and Pipeline , which is the mechanism that keeps preprocessing inside the fold. mlxtend Python paired_ttest_5x2cv , mcnemar , paired_ttest_kfold_cv and the corrected variants — the comparison tests from Extra 2, implemented and documented with the caveats attached. Optuna , scikit-optimize , Ray Tune Python Bayesian and bandit-based hyperparameter search when random search is not enough. tidymodels ( rsample , tune , yardstick ) R Resampling schemes including grouped, time-series and nested; tuning; and a large metric library. rsample has the cleanest API for rolling-origin resampling of anything listed here. caret R The older workhorse; still the fastest route to a repeated-CV comparison of several model types at once. A twenty-minute exercise worth doing Take any dataset with a group column. Cross-validate the same model twice — once with KFold and once with GroupKFold — and look at the gap. On grouped data it is routinely several percentage points, sometimes far more, and the plain-KFold number is the flattering one. Nothing in this module lands as firmly as watching your own score fall by six points when you fix the splitter. Reading log click to type; saves automatically Caveat How much of this should you take on trust? Show answer The splitter descriptions and the comparison-test recommendations are standard and consistent across the sources listed. The 63.2% and 36.8% bootstrap figures are asymptotic limits, exact only as n grows. Defaults such as \"random search beats grid search\" are empirical regularities with real exceptions, not theorems. Treat the lectures as authoritative wherever the two differ."},{"id":"sec-19","h":"","t":"Written by an AI model from lecture recordings. Not reviewed by any instructor or institution — verify anything you rely on against the original lectures. Examples here are generic illustrations rather than the ones used in class. The Dana Reyes and Tom Oyelaran cases, the point that a test set becomes a validation set once you select on it, “Six things worth noticing”, and Lessons 5 to 12 in their entirety are this summary’s own additions rather than lecture content — they draw on standard treatments of resampling, in particular An Introduction to Statistical Learning (James, Witten, Hastie & Tibshirani), chapters 2 and 5, with the one-standard-error rule from chapter 6. All worked numbers in those lessons are invented for illustration, and notation follows the lecture material where the two differ. Everything under “Additional resources” is outside material. Extras 1 to 3 — the catalogue of resampling schemes, the bootstrap variants, the hyperparameter search strategies, the statistical comparison tests, the metric and calibration material, the learning-curve and double-descent notes, the practice problems and the reading list — were assembled from published sources, principally The Elements of Statistical Learning (ch. 7), the scikit-learn user guide (§3.1 and §3.2), Raschka's Model Evaluation, Model Selection, and Algorithm Selection in Machine Learning , and the original method papers cited in Extra 3. None of it was taught in this module. All worked numbers there are computed for illustration on invented data. Check quantitative claims against a primary source before relying on them, and treat the lectures as authoritative wherever the two differ."}]},{"f":"module-4-clustering.html","t":"Module 4: Clustering","s":[{"id":"l1","h":"Introduction to clustering","t":"Lesson 1 Introduction to clustering Clustering groups points that are similar — and it finds groups you didn’t know were there, not just the ones you went looking for. The definition Clustering Taking a set of data points and dividing them into groups so that each group contains points that are close to each other, or similar. Learning type Unsupervised — nothing is being predicted (formalised in Lesson 6) Goal Points inside a cluster are near each other Output Group assignments, not a numeric prediction Grouping basis Distance between data points Clustering has two payoffs, not one. It segments data into groups you expected, and it can also reveal groups you didn’t know were in the dataset. Check 1 Why is clustering called unsupervised learning, when regression is not? Show answer There is no response variable being predicted and no correct answer to train against. The algorithm only sees the attributes and groups points by how close they are to each other. Where it gets used Targeted marketing. Segment potential customers and give each group a different message, because different people respond to different message types. For a consumer durable — say a family car — the four angles might be capacity , price , versatility and status . Data produces the clusters; marketing experts then decide what material each cluster sees. The discovery payoff shows up here: inspecting a cluster might reveal a segment you never planned for, such as buyers who care about gas mileage . Personalized medicine. An ideal cluster is a set of people who would react similarly to a treatment, while different clusters react differently. Facility location. Here closeness is literal physical distance: cluster people by where they live, then put one facility in each cluster. Civic examples are police stations, fire stations, medical clinics and libraries; the same approach works for gas stations and fast food restaurants. Image analysis. Re-coloring pictures, recognizing faces and other objects. This is why handwritten CAPTCHAs keep getting more ambiguous — clustering algorithms keep getting better at reading them. Initial data investigation. Find obvious clusters, then analyze each one separately in case they have different properties. Predicting how much a buyer will spend might need a different model per cluster. quick check answered Your clustering of car buyers produces a group you didn’t anticipate — people who mostly care about running costs. What does that tell you about the method? The clustering failed, because it didn’t recover the four segments the marketing team defined. It’s working as intended — discovering unexpected segments is one of the two things clustering is for. You should re-run with k set to five so the new segment gets its own cluster. Check 2 Name the five application areas, and say which one uses distance in the literal physical sense. Show answer Targeted marketing, personalized medicine, facility location, image analysis, initial data investigation. Facility location is the literal one. Elsewhere “distance” is similarity across attributes, not geography. The picture The worked graph puts age on the horizontal axis and miles driven per year on the vertical axis, with each point a potential buyer. Two dimensions only because it is easy to see and easy to draw. The same point cloud is split by eye into two clusters, then three, then four. The same dataset supports multiple valid clusterings. The number of clusters is a choice you make, not something the data hands you. Class discussion — Lesson 1 click to type; saves automatically Check 3 Is a 4-cluster solution better than a 2-cluster solution on the same scatter? What would you need to know? Show answer You can’t say from the picture. More clusters always reduce total distance, so “tighter” isn’t the test. What decides it is the use. Lesson 4 gives the quantitative half of this answer."},{"id":"l2","h":"Distance and norms","t":"Lesson 2 Distance and norms Euclidean, Manhattan and infinity distance are one formula with three values of p — and the right p comes from how the real process works. The same two points, three answers Take X = (0, 0) and Y = (3, 4) . Every metric below measures the same gap and gets a different number: One gap, three measurements. The ∞-norm ignores the shorter leg entirely — that is the whole idea, and it is why the answer is always the largest single-dimension difference. d = √( (x₁ − y₁)² + (x₂ − y₂)² ) Straight-line, or Euclidean, distance. d = |x₁ − y₁| + |x₂ − y₂| Rectilinear distance. Used for driving on a city street grid, which is why it is also called Manhattan distance. Check 4 Why can the rectilinear distance never be smaller than the Euclidean distance? Show answer A straight line is the shortest path, so any route made of axis-aligned legs can only be longer or equal. They are equal only when the points already line up on one axis. Generalizing: the p-norm d = ( Σⁿᵢ₌₁ |xᵢ − yᵢ|ᵖ ) 1/p The p-norm distance, also called the Minkowski distance of order p. Written for n dimensions. Any value of p is available in theory — 3, 10, 1000. In practice the third most common choice is p = ∞ . Why the infinity norm is just “the biggest” Raise every coordinate difference to the infinity power. The largest term dominates completely. 8 ∞ swamps 7 ∞ , and their sum is so close to 8 ∞ that the second term is irrelevant. So the sum is essentially (largest difference) ∞ . You then take the ∞th root of an ∞ power. Root and power cancel. What’s left is the largest difference. Infinity norm The largest of a set of absolute differences. Nothing more. The treatment here is deliberately informal with the infinities here because the intuition comes across better; a formal treatment would use the limit as p goes to infinity. Check 5 Two points differ by 2 in one dimension and 9 in another. Give the 1-, 2- and ∞-norm distances. Show answer 1-norm: 11. 2-norm: √85 ≈ 9.22. ∞-norm: 9. Note the ordering ∞ ≤ 2 ≤ 1. The larger p gets, the more the single biggest gap dominates. When you’d actually want it: the warehouse A warehouse has an automated storage and retrieval system. In each aisle a machine moves horizontally along the aisle, and also stretches vertically to reach the right height. If it moves, stops, then stretches, the total is the sum of the two times — a 1-norm. If it stretches while it moves, the total is whichever takes longer — an ∞-norm. quick check answered The warehouse machine stretches vertically while it moves along the aisle. Which norm gives the retrieval time? 1-norm — the two legs add up. 2-norm — the straight-line distance. ∞-norm — whichever of the two motions is slower. quick check answered Driving to a shop across a city laid out in a grid, versus a drone flying to the same shop. Which pairing is right? Both are 2-norm — distance is distance, whatever is travelling. Grid driving is a 1-norm; the drone is a 2-norm. Grid driving is an ∞-norm; the drone is a 1-norm. Class discussion — Lesson 2 click to type; saves automatically Check 6 The machine needs 6 seconds of aisle travel and 4 seconds of stretch. How long until it can pick, under each design? Show answer Sequential: 10 s. Simultaneous: 6 s. Operations in parallel call for the ∞-norm; operations one after another call for the 1-norm."},{"id":"l3","h":"The k-means algorithm","t":"Lesson 3 The k-means algorithm Alternate two cheap steps — reassign points, recompute centers — until nothing moves. It skips an optimization problem that would be far too slow to solve exactly. Setup and notation The running example is car buyers over the past eight years, with age horizontal and average daily temperature where they live vertical. With n data points and m attributes: x ij Value of data point i , attribute j — the data itself y ik 1 if data point i is in cluster k , 0 if not — cluster membership z kj The j th dimension coordinate of cluster center k minimize Σᵢ Σₖ y ik · d( x i , z k ) subject to Σₖ y ik = 1 for every point i The first part adds up distances from points to centers, but only where the point is in that cluster — the job the 0/1 y term does. The constraint forces every point into exactly one cluster. This is a hard optimization problem, covered properly in the optimization topic later in the course. So instead of solving it, we use k-means. Check 7 Which of x, y, z is given to you, and which two are you solving for? Show answer x is observed and fixed. Both y (membership) and z (center locations) are unknowns — and they depend on each other, which is why k-means alternates between them. The algorithm as a loop The two boxes are the expectation and maximization halves of the name. You leave the loop from the assignment step, because that is where “nothing changed” can first be observed. quick check answered Which description of k-means is correct? Choose k, pick k starting centers, assign each point to its nearest center, replace each center with the centroid of its points, and repeat those two steps until no point changes cluster. Choose k, assign every point to a cluster at random, compute the centroids, and stop after a fixed number of rounds. Choose k, pick k starting centers, recompute each center from the points nearest it, then assign points, exiting from the recompute step. Check 8 Why is “no point changed clusters” a safe place to stop? Show answer Centers are computed purely from membership. If membership didn’t change, the centroids are identical to last round, so the next assignment reproduces the same result. The algorithm is at a fixed point. Jargon break Heuristic An algorithm not guaranteed to find the absolute best solution, but which in many cases — including this one — usually gets very close and gets there quickly. Expectation-maximization (EM) Calculating cluster centers takes the mean of the points in a cluster, similar to an expectation. Reassigning points strictly minimizes distance, but you can view it as maximizing the negative of the distance. k-means alternates between the two. quick check answered Which step of k-means is the “expectation” step, and why? Assigning each point to its nearest center, because you expect points to belong to the closest group. Recomputing the centers, because taking the mean of a cluster’s points is a form of expectation. Choosing the initial centers, because you expect them to land near real groups. Class discussion — Lesson 3 click to type; saves automatically Check 9 If k-means is a heuristic, what exactly is it not promising you? Show answer That its clustering has the lowest possible total distance. It converges to a solution no point wants to leave, but a different start could converge somewhere better."},{"id":"l4","h":"k-means in practice","t":"Lesson 4 k-means in practice Outliers, restarts and the choice of k each get a quantitative default and a qualitative override — and the qualitative one comes first every time. Outliers An outlier here means a point that isn’t close to any cluster center. k-means has no concept of “not in a cluster” — it assigns the outlier to whichever center is nearest, even though the point may not belong there at all. The default move: remove the point and rerun, so it doesn’t drag its cluster center artificially to one side. The lecturer’s position: that’s not always right. Better to ask what the outlier is , what makes its attributes so different, and what the implication is of putting a point like this in the nearest cluster. The soapbox — it recurs all course Answering those questions takes time and effort, and it is part of what separates a top-notch analytics professional from someone who blindly runs algorithms. In many situations the algorithm is really just a guide. As much as this gets called data science, the best professionals also practise the art of analytics : looking at the combination of the data, the algorithm and the real situation to find the right answer. MO Marcus Obi Network planner, county emergency services Marcus is clustering household locations to site new fire stations. His k-means run puts one household far from every center — a property twelve miles up a private road, well outside the built-up area. A colleague: “That one’s wrecking the north cluster’s center. Drop it and re-run.” What should Marcus do first? Drop the point and re-run. It’s one household out of thousands and it’s distorting the centroid. Leave it in. Removing real data is never defensible, and the centroid should reflect everything. Find out what the point actually is before deciding anything. Now what? Marcus knows the outlier is a high-risk farm that no station will cover well. Increase k until the farm gets its own cluster and site a station there. Exclude it from the centroids, but flag it in the report as needing a separate solution. Check 10 Why does an outlier distort a k-means solution rather than sitting harmlessly in the nearest cluster? Show answer The center is the centroid — the mean of its members. A far-off point pulls that mean toward itself, and the shifted center can then change which cluster other borderline points get assigned to. Restarts, and choosing k k-means is fast, and that speed is a resource. Run it several times from different initial centers and keep the best result. Run it across a range of different k rather than guessing. But there’s a trap: Why total distance alone can’t choose k Start with a k-cluster solution and add one more cluster — even one holding a single data point — and the total distance to centers gets smaller. So the clustering always appears better with more clusters. At the extreme, the best-looking solution is one cluster per data point. Qualitative answer. The best solution is the one that fits the situation. If your city only has budget for four new fire stations, a solution giving six new areas isn’t much help. Quantitative answer — the elbow diagram. Look for the kink — where the marginal benefit of another cluster starts to be small. Its real value is that it works even when you can’t see the clusters, because your data has too many attributes to plot. Class discussion — Lesson 4 click to type; saves automatically Check 11 A classmate picks k by finding the value with the lowest total distance to cluster centers. What goes wrong? Show answer Total distance falls monotonically as k rises, so that rule always picks the largest k tested — in the limit, one cluster per point. The elbow looks at where the rate of improvement drops off. And a practical constraint can override it entirely."},{"id":"l5","h":"Clustering for prediction","t":"Lesson 5 Clustering for prediction Send a new point to the nearest center. Do that for every possible location and the whole space divides into regions — a Voronoi diagram, an idea centuries older than analytics. Predicting the cluster of a new point Take a finished solution — five clusters, triangles marking the centers. If a new point lands inside a cluster, the answer is easy. If it doesn’t land inside any cluster, choose whichever center is closest ; that’s as reasonable a choice as any. Do this for every possible location and you get regions. Four areas are clickable. Open all four to finish this activity. Voronoi diagram The partition of a space into one region per center, where each region holds every location nearer that center than any other. Named for a mathematician who lived more than 100 years ago. More than 150 years ago A diagram like it was used to analyze the source of a cholera outbreak in London Early-to-mid 1600s René Descartes used something like it in his work The point: not all the good ideas in analytics are new and cutting-edge. Sometimes the best ideas have been around a long time and just need dusting off. Class discussion — Lesson 5 click to type; saves automatically Check 12 Assigning a new point to the nearest cluster center looks a lot like KNN. What is different? Show answer KNN compares the new point to actual labelled training points and votes among the k nearest. Here you compare to k computed centers, and there are no labels anywhere — the groups were invented by the algorithm. The k also means different things: number of clusters vs. number of neighbours consulted."},{"id":"l6","h":"Clustering vs. classification","t":"Lesson 6 Clustering vs. classification Both split points into groups. The only difference is whether you already knew the right answer going in — and that difference has a name. Identical point positions in both panels. The only thing that changes is what the model was handed. The difference is what we know about the data points Not the shape of the output. Both produce groups. What differs is whether you already had the right answer going in. Classification Clustering Attributes known? Yes Yes Correct group known? Yes — response already observed No Model uses Attributes and responses Attributes only Jargon Supervised learning Unsupervised learning The loan example. We already know each point’s classification: blue repaid in full, red did not. That known response helps us classify a new applicant. In clustering no such labels exist. quick check answered You cluster unlabelled customers, a colleague hand-labels each cluster “high value” or “low value,” and you train a model on those labels. What did you just do? Unsupervised learning throughout, since the original data had no labels. Supervised learning throughout, since the final model was trained on labels. Both, in sequence — and the labels came from judgement, not from observed outcomes. Class discussion — Lesson 6 click to type; saves automatically Check 13 Define supervised and unsupervised learning. Show answer Supervised: we have observed the right answer for each data point, and use that in addition to attributes. Classification is the example. Unsupervised: we have not observed the response, and use only attribute information. Clustering is the example."},{"id":"targets","h":"Transfer practice — Clustering","t":"Transfer practice — Clustering Same ideas, unfamiliar settings Why these are here Each prompt below moves the module’s ideas into a situation the lessons never mentioned. If you can only answer a question in the wording it was taught in, what you have memorised is the example rather than the concept — and an exam that changes the setting will find that out. Answer these out loud before opening them. Exam target Two runs of k-means on the same data with the same k produce different groupings. Is something broken? Show answer No. k-means converges to an arrangement no record wants to leave, which is not necessarily the arrangement with the lowest total distance overall, and where it lands depends on the starting centres. The standard response is several restarts keeping the best result, and a spread-out initialisation so a poor start is less likely. Exam target A colleague selects k by trying every value and keeping whichever gives the lowest total distance from records to their centres. What will that produce? Show answer k equal to the number of records, each its own cluster at distance zero. Total distance falls monotonically as k rises, so it can compare solutions at a fixed k but cannot choose k. Use the elbow, an average silhouette, or a constraint from the decision the clustering serves. Exam target Customers are described by annual spend in dollars and number of visits per year. What must happen before clustering, and what goes wrong otherwise? Show answer Scale or standardise. Untransformed, spend runs in the thousands and visits in the tens, so squared differences in spend swamp the calculation entirely and the clustering is effectively on spend alone. Visits would contribute nothing, and nobody reading the output would be able to tell. Exam target Two records have the same proportional spending across categories, but one spends ten times as much in total. Which distance measure calls them similar? Show answer Cosine distance, which compares direction and ignores magnitude, treats them as nearly identical profiles. Euclidean distance places them far apart. Neither is right in the abstract: segmenting by taste wants the first, segmenting by customer value wants the second. Exam target In a scatter plot the true groups are two long curved bands, and k-means with k = 2 cuts across both. What is the diagnosis? Show answer Assigning each record to its nearest centre divides the space into straight-edged convex regions, which cannot follow a curved band. This is a limitation of the method rather than a tuning problem — no number of restarts fixes it. A different algorithm, or a transformation of the space, is what the situation calls for. Exam target One record has a silhouette value of −0.4. What does that indicate, and what would you do? Show answer On average it is closer to the records of a neighbouring cluster than to those of its own, so it is probably assigned to the wrong group. A few such records are normal at boundaries; many of them suggest that k is wrong or that the groups genuinely overlap. Check whether they concentrate between one particular pair of clusters, which would argue those two should be merged. Exam target A single record sits far from all others. Trace its effect on k-means. Show answer A centre is a mean, and a mean is pulled by extreme values, so the cluster claiming the outlier has its centre dragged away from the bulk of its own records. That shifts the boundary and can change memberships elsewhere. With a large enough k it may take a centre entirely to itself, spending one of your groups on one record."},{"id":"sec-8","h":"How the module fits together","t":"How the module fits together The through-line across all six lessons Lesson 1 Defines clustering as grouping points that are close — and leaves “close” undefined. Lesson 2 Supplies the definition. Closeness is a p-norm, and which p you pick should follow from how the real process behaves. Lesson 3 Puts it to work. Every time k-means asks “which center is this closest to,” it calls a metric from Lesson 2. Lesson 4 Handles what the clean version ignored: outliers, unlucky starting points, and how many clusters to ask for. Lesson 5 Extends the finished solution to new data, and carves the whole space into nearest-center regions. Lesson 6 Names what has been true all along — no responses were ever observed, so this was unsupervised learning. Four things worth noticing The axes change between lessons. Lesson 1 plots age against miles driven per year; Lesson 3 plots age against average daily temperature. Same buyer story, different attributes. The qualitative answer comes first, twice. On outliers and on choosing k, the material leads with “understand the situation” and only then gives the technique. Speed is the resource being spent. Restarts and the elbow diagram are both purchased with the same property: k-means is fast enough to run many times. k-means pairs naturally with the 2-norm. The centroid minimizes total squared Euclidean distance, which is one reason variants like k-medians exist. Likely exam targets Compute 1-, 2- and ∞-norm distances between two points. Explain why the infinity norm reduces to the maximum difference. Walk through the k-means loop and state its stopping condition. Define heuristic; identify the E step and the M step. Explain why total distance alone can’t choose k, and what the elbow diagram does instead. Give the two ways to handle an outlier and say when each applies. Distinguish supervised from unsupervised learning, with an example of each. Describe a Voronoi diagram and what its boundaries mean. Everything above is class material. Additional resources follow — the other clustering algorithms, distance measures beyond the p-norms, the cluster-count and validation criteria, practice problems and reading — none of it taught in this module. Module 4 — my own summary click to type; saves automatically"},{"id":"extra","h":"Additional resources","t":"Additional resources Everything below this line is not class material Read this first The six lessons above are the module as taught: what a cluster is, what “close” means, how k-means finds groups, what goes wrong, and how the whole thing differs from classification. Everything from here on was assembled from outside sources — textbooks, method papers and the documentation of the standard libraries. None of it is examinable unless your instructor says so, and where a convention differs from the lectures, the lectures win . The lectures present k-means as a clustering algorithm and the p-norm as a way to measure distance, without surveying the alternatives. The appendix fills in both surveys, adds the standard ways of choosing k and judging a clustering that the elbow diagram only gestures at, and provides practice. Extra 1 Every major family of clustering algorithm, and what each is actually for. Extra 2 Distance measures beyond the p-norms — including the ones for text, categories and mixed data. Extra 3 Choosing k properly, and how to tell whether a clustering means anything. Extra 4 Practice problems, and where to read further."},{"id":"x1","h":"The other clustering algorithms","t":"Extra 1 · not class material The other clustering algorithms k-means answers one specific question — split the data into k groups so that total distance to the group centre is small. Every algorithm below answers a slightly different question, and the difference shows up as a different shape of cluster it can find. What k-means assumes, and therefore what it cannot find Before the alternatives, it is worth being explicit about the assumptions baked into Lesson 3's loop, because each alternative exists to relax one of them. Assumption Consequence when it fails You know k in advance. You have to supply it, and Lesson 4's elbow diagram is a workaround rather than an answer. Clusters are roughly round and similarly sized. Minimising distance to a single centre point can only carve out convex, blob-shaped regions. A crescent, a ring or a long thin band gets sliced in half. Every point belongs to some cluster. There is no “none of the above” category, so noise and outliers are forced into a group and drag its centre with them — the problem Lesson 4 addresses by hand. Assignments are hard. A point halfway between two centres is assigned to one of them with no record that it was borderline. The mean is meaningful. Categorical attributes have no mean, so k-means cannot be applied to them directly at all. k-means is not making a mistake here — it is correctly minimising total distance to two centres, and the left/right split genuinely does that better than inner/outer would. The ring structure is simply not the kind of structure the objective function can express. No number of restarts and no choice of k fixes this; it needs a different algorithm. Family 1 — Partitioning methods (k-means and its relatives) Same loop, different notion of a cluster's representative point, which changes what the method is robust to and what data it can handle. Method Centre is… Use when k-means the mean The default. Fast, scales to millions of points, minimises within-cluster squared Euclidean distance. k-medians the component-wise median You want the 1-norm rather than the 2-norm. Far less sensitive to the outliers Lesson 4 worries about, because the median ignores how far away an extreme point is. k-medoids / PAM an actual data point The centre must be a real member (a real store to site a depot at, a real patient as the archetype), or your distance measure has no meaningful average. Works with any distance matrix at all. Much slower. k-modes / k-prototypes the most common category / a mix The data is categorical, or mixed numeric and categorical, where a mean does not exist. Fuzzy c-means the mean, weighted by membership You want soft assignments — each point gets a degree of membership in every cluster rather than one label. Useful when boundary cases matter. Bisecting k-means the mean You want a hierarchy as a by-product: repeatedly split the worst cluster in two. Often better than plain k-means on document data. k-means++ — a better answer to Lesson 4's restart problem Lesson 4's advice is to run k-means many times from random starting points and keep the best result, because the algorithm is a heuristic that can settle into a poor local optimum. The standard improvement is to be cleverer about where the starting points go. k-means++ (Arthur & Vassilvitskii, 2007) picks the first centre uniformly at random, then picks each subsequent centre with probability proportional to the squared distance from the nearest centre already chosen — so new centres tend to land far from old ones, and the initial k centres are spread across the data rather than clumped. Two things are worth knowing. First, it comes with a guarantee: the expected cost of the initialisation alone is within a factor of 8(ln k + 2) of the optimum, which is the only bound of its kind for a method this cheap. Second, finding the true k-means optimum is NP-hard, so no practical algorithm removes the restart problem entirely — k-means++ makes each restart much more likely to be good, which means you need fewer of them. It is the default initialisation in scikit-learn and in most other implementations, which is why modern k-means feels more stable than the lecture's description suggests. Family 2 — Hierarchical clustering Instead of committing to k, build the whole nested family of clusterings at once and choose where to cut afterwards. Agglomerative (bottom-up). Start with every point as its own cluster; repeatedly merge the two closest clusters until one remains. The merge history draws a dendrogram , a tree whose height records how far apart the things being joined were. Cut the tree at a height, and the number of branches you cross is your k — so k becomes a decision you make after seeing the structure rather than before. Divisive (top-down). The reverse: start with everything together and split repeatedly. Rarer, because there are many more ways to split than to merge. The crucial parameter is not k but the linkage — how the distance between two clusters is computed from distances between points. Different linkages produce genuinely different shapes. Linkage Cluster distance is… Tends to produce Single the smallest distance between any two members Long straggly chains. Can follow a curved band that k-means would cut, but a single bridging point can merge two real clusters — \"chaining\". Complete the largest distance between any two members Compact, roughly equal-diameter clusters. Sensitive to outliers, since one far member sets the whole distance. Average the mean over all cross pairs A compromise between the two; the common default. Ward the increase in total within-cluster variance caused by merging Round, similar-sized clusters — essentially the hierarchical cousin of k-means, and the one that most often agrees with it. Hierarchical clustering is O(n²) in memory and worse in time, so it stops being practical somewhere in the tens of thousands of points. Its payoff is the dendrogram: for taxonomy-style questions — which customer types sit inside which broader types — a picture of the nesting is the answer, and no partition method gives you one. quick check answered You do not know how many clusters there are, and you want to see how groups nest inside each other. Which family? k-means, run across a range of k and compared with an elbow diagram. Agglomerative hierarchical clustering, reading the dendrogram. DBSCAN, since it works out the number of clusters by itself. Family 3 — Density-based clustering A completely different definition of a cluster: not \"points near a centre\" but \"a connected region where points are packed densely, surrounded by sparser space\". Because there is no centre, there is no requirement that the region be round — which is how these methods find the rings in the figure above. Method How it works Notes DBSCAN Ester et al., 1996 Two parameters: a radius ε and a count minPts . A point with at least minPts neighbours within ε is a core point ; core points that are within ε of each other join the same cluster; points near a cluster but not dense themselves are border points; everything else is noise . Finds arbitrary shapes, works out the number of clusters itself, and has an explicit noise label — the \"none of the above\" that k-means lacks. Struggles when different clusters have very different densities, because one ε cannot suit both. OPTICS Generalises DBSCAN by not fixing ε: it orders points by reachability and produces a plot from which clusters at many density levels can be read. Removes the hardest parameter choice, at the cost of a less direct output. HDBSCAN Runs DBSCAN across all values of ε and extracts the clusters that persist most stably. The current practical default for density clustering. Handles varying density, needs essentially one intuitive parameter (minimum cluster size), and returns a hierarchy. Mean shift Slide every point uphill along the estimated density until it reaches a peak; points arriving at the same peak form a cluster. Discovers the number of clusters, but the bandwidth parameter is doing the same job k did, and it is slow. The trade you are making Density methods swap one hard question for another. k-means asks \"how many clusters?\"; DBSCAN asks \"how dense does dense mean?\". Neither question has a data-driven answer, and both are ultimately settled by what the clusters will be used for. What changes is which question you find easier to reason about in your domain — and for spatial data, fraud, or anything with genuine noise, \"how close do two events have to be to count as the same incident\" is often much easier to answer than \"how many kinds of incident are there\". Family 4 — Model-based clustering Gaussian mixture models assume the data was generated by drawing from one of k Gaussian distributions, each with its own mean and its own covariance , and fit those parameters by expectation-maximisation. Lesson 3 already names EM as the general procedure k-means is a special case of — this is the general case made concrete, and the relationship is exact: a GMM with spherical, equal-sized covariances and hard assignments is k-means. Relaxing that gives two things k-means cannot do. Clusters can be elliptical and differently sized, oriented along whatever directions the data actually varies in. And assignments are soft : each point gets a probability of belonging to each cluster, so a point sitting between two groups is reported as ambiguous rather than silently rounded off. Because a GMM is a probability model, you also get a likelihood, which means AIC and BIC can choose k for you — a principled alternative to reading an elbow off a chart. Family 5 — Graph-based clustering Spectral clustering builds a graph in which nearby points are connected, computes the eigenvectors of that graph's Laplacian matrix, and runs k-means in the resulting low-dimensional space. The effect is to unbend the geometry: structures that are tangled in the original space — the two rings again — become linearly separable in the embedded space, where k-means then works fine. It handles arbitrary shapes well but scales poorly (building an n × n affinity matrix), needs k as an input, and unlike the density methods has no concept of noise, so every point is assigned to something. Affinity propagation and BIRCH round out the list — the first passes messages between points until a set of exemplars emerges (no k required, but slow), the second builds a compact summary tree in one pass and is designed for data too large to hold in memory. The whole landscape in one table Method Needs k? Cluster shapes Noise label? Scales to k-means Yes Round, convex No Millions k-medoids Yes Round, any distance No Thousands Hierarchical No — cut later Depends on linkage No ~10,000 DBSCAN No Arbitrary Yes Hundreds of thousands HDBSCAN No Arbitrary, varying density Yes Hundreds of thousands GMM Yes (or by BIC) Elliptical No — but soft Hundreds of thousands Spectral Yes Arbitrary No ~10,000 Mean shift No Arbitrary-ish Yes Thousands quick check answered Lesson 4 says an outlier can be removed or given its own cluster. Which algorithm makes that a built-in decision rather than a manual one? DBSCAN, because points in sparse regions are labelled as noise rather than assigned. k-medians, because the median is unaffected by extreme values. A Gaussian mixture model, because soft assignment gives the outlier low probability everywhere. Algorithms to try on my data click to type; saves automatically Link to Lesson 3 Lesson 3 calls k-means a special case of EM. What exactly is the general case? Show answer A Gaussian mixture model. Fit one with spherical covariances all of the same size, and replace the soft membership probabilities with hard \"assign to the most likely\" decisions, and the EM steps become exactly Lesson 3's assign-then-recompute loop. Every extra thing a GMM can do — elliptical clusters, differently sized clusters, ambiguous points — comes from one of those two restrictions being lifted."},{"id":"x2","h":"Distance beyond the p-norms","t":"Extra 2 · not class material Distance beyond the p-norms Lesson 2 gives the p-norm family, which covers numeric attributes measured in comparable units. Real data is often none of those things, and each departure has a standard answer. First, the thing that breaks every distance measure Scale decides the answer before the metric does Suppose you cluster customers on age (18–80) and annual spend (£200–£90,000). Every distance is now essentially the difference in spend: a 40-year age gap contributes 40 to the 1-norm while a modest spend difference contributes thousands. The clustering is on spend, and age is decoration — not because you decided that, but because of the units the data happened to arrive in. Change spend to thousands of pounds and you get a different clustering from the same data. So numeric attributes are normally standardised before any distance is computed. That is Module 5's material, and the point here is only that it is not optional for clustering . Unlike a regression, which can absorb scale differences into its coefficients, a distance-based method has no way to compensate. Lesson 2's choice of p matters much less than whether you scaled first. When the attributes are correlated: Mahalanobis distance Euclidean distance treats every direction as equally surprising. If height and weight are strongly correlated, a person who is tall and heavy is unremarkable, while a person equally far from the mean in the tall-and-light direction is genuinely unusual — and the 2-norm gives them the same distance. Mahalanobis distance divides out the covariance, so distance is measured in \"how surprising is this, given how the data actually varies\". Geometrically, it replaces circular contours of equal distance with ellipses aligned to the data's own spread. It is what makes a GMM's elliptical clusters possible, and it is also the standard multivariate outlier measure in Module 5. When only direction matters: cosine distance For text, recommender data, or anything represented as counts, magnitude is often an artefact. A 400-word article and a 4,000-word article on the same subject have wildly different word counts but the same profile . Cosine similarity measures the angle between two vectors and ignores their lengths entirely; cosine distance is one minus that. cos(u, v) = (u · v) / ( ‖u‖ ‖v‖ ) cosine distance = 1 − cos(u, v) Worked contrast: u = (3, 4) and v = (6, 8). Euclidean distance is 5 — they look well separated. Cosine distance is 0, because v is just u doubled: identical direction, identical profile. Which answer is right depends entirely on whether magnitude carries meaning in your data. When the attributes are not numeric Data type Measure What it counts Binary presence/absence, sparse sets tags, baskets, viewed items Jaccard distance 1 − (size of intersection ÷ size of union). Two shopping baskets are close if they share items relative to how many items they have in total. Ignores the vast number of things neither contains, which is what makes it right for sparse data. Categorical of equal length genetic sequences, codes Hamming distance The number of positions where the two differ. The natural metric when every attribute is a category with no ordering. Mixed numeric and categorical most real business data Gower distance Computes a per-attribute distance in [0, 1] — scaled absolute difference for numeric, match/no-match for categorical, a rank-based measure for ordinal — then averages them. The standard answer to \"half my columns are text\". Pairs with k-medoids or hierarchical clustering, since neither needs a mean. Sequences of different lengths sensor traces, trajectories Dynamic time warping Aligns two series by stretching the time axis, so two shapes that match but occur at different speeds count as close. Standard in time-series clustering. Strings names, addresses, product titles Edit (Levenshtein) distance The number of insertions, deletions and substitutions needed to turn one into the other. The basis of most record-matching work. Only k-medoids and hierarchical clustering work directly with all of these, because both need nothing but a table of pairwise distances. k-means needs an average , which is why it is confined to numeric data — a point the lectures make implicitly by defining the centroid as a mean. The problem that gets worse with every extra column The curse of dimensionality As the number of attributes grows, the distances between points become increasingly similar to one another: the ratio between the nearest and farthest neighbour approaches 1. \"Nearest\" stops meaning much, and every distance-based method degrades — clustering, k-nearest neighbours, outlier detection alike. Three standard responses, in increasing order of effort: drop irrelevant attributes , since noise dimensions do nothing but dilute the signal; reduce dimensions first with PCA or a similar method and cluster in the reduced space (this is essentially what spectral clustering does internally); or use a method built for it , such as subspace clustering, which looks for clusters that exist in some subset of the attributes rather than all of them at once. The practical warning is simply that a clustering on 200 columns is often reporting noise, and the fix is upstream of the algorithm. quick check answered You are clustering customers on twelve numeric columns and four categorical ones (region, plan type, channel, segment). What is the standard route? Convert the categories to numbers and run k-means on all sixteen columns. Compute Gower distances and use k-medoids or hierarchical clustering. Drop the categorical columns, since distance is only defined for numbers. Link to Lesson 2 Does any of this change the p-norm material? Show answer No — it extends it. The p-norms are still the right family for numeric attributes in comparable units, and Lesson 2's point that you should choose p from how the process behaves is exactly the argument being made again here for cosine, Jaccard and the rest. The metric is a modelling decision either way."},{"id":"x3","h":"Choosing k, and judging the result","t":"Extra 3 · not class material Choosing k, and judging the result The elbow diagram is the first of about six standard methods, and it is the weakest of them. The rest give a number rather than a shape to squint at. Why the elbow is unsatisfying, stated precisely Lesson 4 makes the essential point: total within-cluster distance always falls as k rises, reaching zero when every point is its own cluster, so it cannot be minimised. The elbow heuristic looks instead for the k where the rate of improvement drops off. The trouble is that on real data there is very often no visible elbow at all — the curve bends smoothly — and where there is one, different people read it at different k. It is a diagnostic, not a decision rule. The silhouette — the one worth learning properly Silhouette coefficient For each point i : let a(i) be its mean distance to the other points in its own cluster, and b(i) its mean distance to the points of the nearest other cluster. Then s(i) = ( b(i) − a(i) ) / max{ a(i), b(i) } which runs from −1 to +1. Near +1 means the point sits comfortably inside its cluster; near 0 means it is on a boundary; negative means it is closer on average to a different cluster than to its own — it is probably in the wrong group. Average s over all points to score the clustering; compare that average across values of k and take the best. Unlike total distance, the silhouette does not improve automatically with k, because splitting a genuine cluster in two lowers b as much as it lowers a . That is what makes it usable as a criterion instead of a curve to interpret. Worked example — six points, two candidate clusterings Points A(1,1), B(2,1), C(1.5,2) sit together; D(8,8), E(9,8), F(8.5,9) sit together far away. Compare the obvious clustering {A,B,C} | {D,E,F} against a wrong one, {A,B} | {C,D,E,F}. Point Correct: {A,B,C} | {D,E,F} Wrong: {A,B} | {C,D,E,F} a b s a b s A 1.06 10.50 0.90 1.00 8.15 0.88 B 1.06 9.81 0.89 1.00 7.64 0.87 C 1.12 9.45 0.88 9.45 1.12 −0.88 D 1.06 9.32 0.89 3.65 9.56 0.62 E 1.06 10.05 0.90 3.91 10.26 0.62 F 1.12 10.39 0.89 4.05 10.64 0.62 Mean 0.89 0.45 Two things to take from the right-hand columns. The average of 0.45 correctly ranks the wrong clustering below the right one. But more useful than the average is C's score of −0.88 , which points straight at the specific mistake: C is nine units from its own cluster-mates and one unit from the other cluster. A plot of individual silhouette values, sorted within each cluster, is the standard diagnostic for exactly this reason — the average tells you a clustering is poor, the per-point values tell you where. The rest of the toolbox Method Idea Read it as Elbow Plot total within-cluster distance against k. Look for a bend. Often absent. Diagnostic only. Silhouette Cohesion versus separation, per point. Maximise. Also gives a per-point diagnostic. Gap statistic Tibshirani et al., 2001 Compare your within-cluster distance at each k against the distance you would get on uniformly random data over the same range. The \"gap\" is how much better than structureless noise your clustering is. Maximise (with a standard-error rule for choosing the smallest adequate k). The only common method that can return k = 1, i.e. \"there is no cluster structure here\" — which is sometimes the true answer and which every other method is incapable of saying. Calinski–Harabasz Ratio of between-cluster to within-cluster dispersion, adjusted for k. Maximise. Very cheap. Biased toward round clusters, as it is built from the same sums of squares k-means optimises. Davies–Bouldin Average, over clusters, of the worst-case similarity to any other cluster. Minimise. Note the reversed direction — a common slip. BIC / AIC Only available if you fit a probability model such as a GMM: penalised likelihood. Minimise BIC. The most principled option, and the reason to reach for a GMM when k is genuinely unknown. Stability Cluster many bootstrap resamples and measure how often pairs of points end up together. A clustering that dissolves under resampling is not a finding. Slow but persuasive. Expect them to disagree — that is the finding Run all six on the same data and it is entirely normal to get, say, gap saying 2, silhouette saying 9, Calinski–Harabasz saying 8 and the elbow showing nothing. Published comparisons find exactly this. Disagreement means the data does not have one obvious cluster count — often because it has structure at several scales, or none. The right response is to look at the candidate clusterings and ask which one is useful for the decision you are making, which is where Lesson 4's \"understand the situation first\" advice comes back in. Judging a clustering when you do have labels Everything above is an internal measure — computed from the data and the clustering alone, because in genuine unsupervised work there is nothing else. Occasionally you do have a known grouping to compare against (a benchmark dataset, or labels withheld deliberately to test the method), and then external measures apply. Adjusted Rand index (ARI). Counts pairs of points that the two groupings agree about, corrected so that random labelling scores 0 and perfect agreement scores 1. The correction matters: the uncorrected Rand index rises with k for no good reason. Normalised mutual information (NMI). How much knowing the cluster label tells you about the true label, scaled to [0, 1]. Purity / homogeneity / completeness. Simpler and more interpretable, but purity increases with k automatically — the same defect as raw total distance, and the reason ARI exists. None of these is available in the situation Lesson 6 describes, which is the whole point of that lesson: no responses were observed, so there is nothing to score against. That is precisely why choosing k is hard in a way that choosing a model in Module 3 is not — there is no held-out truth to validate against. quick check answered A point has silhouette −0.6. What does that mean? It is an outlier and should be removed before reclustering. On average it is closer to a different cluster than to its own — it is in the wrong group. It sits exactly on a boundary between two clusters, which is unavoidable. quick check answered Why can the gap statistic return k = 1 when silhouette and Davies–Bouldin cannot? It compares the result against random data, so \"no better than noise\" is an answer it can express. It is computationally cheaper, so it can afford to test k = 1 as well. It does not assume clusters are spherical, so a single irregular cluster is representable. Notes on choosing k click to type; saves automatically Link to Lesson 4 Does any of this replace \"understand the situation first\"? Show answer No, and the disagreement between criteria is the reason. These methods narrow a continuum down to two or three defensible candidates; which of those you pick still depends on what the clusters are for. Five marketing segments and fifty are both statistically defensible answers, and only the business question settles it."},{"id":"x4","h":"Practice, reading and tools","t":"Extra 4 · not class material Practice, reading and tools Seven problems with worked solutions, a self-test, and where to go next. Part A — distance arithmetic Problem 1 — the three norms Points P = (3, 1, 7) and Q = (8, 4, 5). Compute the 1-norm, 2-norm and ∞-norm distances. Show worked solution Component differences: |3 − 8| = 5, |1 − 4| = 3, |7 − 5| = 2. 1-norm: 5 + 3 + 2 = 10 . 2-norm: √(25 + 9 + 4) = √38 ≈ 6.16 . ∞-norm: max{5, 3, 2} = 5 . The ordering 1-norm ≥ 2-norm ≥ ∞-norm always holds, which is a useful arithmetic check. The reason is Lesson 2's: as p grows, the largest component increasingly dominates the sum until only it survives. Problem 2 — when the metric changes the answer Centre A = (0, 0), centre B = (10, 0). Point X = (6, 6). Which centre is X assigned to under the 1-norm, and under the ∞-norm? Show worked solution 1-norm: to A, 6 + 6 = 12; to B, 4 + 6 = 10. X goes to B . ∞-norm: to A, max{6, 6} = 6; to B, max{4, 6} = 6. A tie — and note that under the 2-norm, to A is √72 ≈ 8.49 and to B is √52 ≈ 7.21, so B again. The lesson is that Lesson 2's choice of p is not cosmetic: it changes cluster membership, and therefore the centroids, and therefore the next iteration. The ∞-norm's tie here is characteristic — it discards all information except the single worst coordinate, so ties are common. Problem 3 — scale You cluster on age (years, range 20–70) and income (pounds, range 20,000–200,000). Without scaling, roughly what fraction of a typical Euclidean distance comes from income? Show worked solution Essentially all of it. A large age difference is ~50; a large income difference is ~180,000 — about 3,600 times bigger, and squared before summing, so income contributes on the order of ten million times more to the squared distance. Age is not being down-weighted, it is being erased . After standardising both to mean 0 and standard deviation 1, each contributes according to how much it actually varies relative to its own spread, which is what you almost always want. The exception is when the units are already comparable and meaningful — three sensor readings all in millivolts, say — where standardising would destroy real information about which sensor moves more. Part B — algorithm behaviour Problem 4 — one iteration of k-means by hand Points: (1,1), (2,1), (1,2), (8,8), (9,9). Initial centres c₁ = (1,1) and c₂ = (2,1). Run one full iteration of the Lesson 3 loop using Euclidean distance, then say what happens next. Show worked solution Assign step. (1,1) → c₁ (distance 0 vs 1). (2,1) → c₂ (0 vs 1). (1,2) → c₁ (1 vs √2 ≈ 1.41). (8,8) → c₂ (√98 ≈ 9.90 vs √85 ≈ 9.22). (9,9) → c₂ (√128 ≈ 11.31 vs √113 ≈ 10.63). So cluster 1 = {(1,1), (1,2)}; cluster 2 = {(2,1), (8,8), (9,9)}. Update step. c₁ = (1, 1.5). c₂ = ((2+8+9)/3, (1+8+9)/3) = (6.33, 6). What happens next. On the second assign step, (2,1) is now 1.12 from c₁ and 7.03 from c₂, so it switches back to cluster 1. The iteration after that gives c₁ = (1.33, 1.33) and c₂ = (8.5, 8.5), assignments stop changing, and the algorithm stops. The point of working through this is the intermediate state: after one iteration the clustering is visibly wrong, with (2,1) grouped with two points forty units away. k-means does not converge in one pass, and a bad start costs iterations rather than causing failure — unless it costs correctness, which is the local-optimum problem Lesson 4 raises. Problem 5 — diagnosing a bad result You run k-means with k = 4 on customer data. One cluster contains 9,800 of the 10,000 points; the other three contain 150, 40 and 10. What are the two most likely explanations, and what would you check first? Show worked solution One: unscaled attributes. If one column has a far larger range than the others, the clustering is effectively one-dimensional on that column, and a few extreme values on it get picked off into tiny clusters while everything else stays together. Check first by printing the standard deviation of each column — a spread of several orders of magnitude is the tell. Two: outliers. A handful of extreme points can capture centres entirely, which is Lesson 4's problem. The tiny clusters of 40 and 10 look exactly like this. Both diagnoses point the same way and are not mutually exclusive. The standard workflow is: standardise, look for and deal with outliers (Module 5), rerun with k-means++ and several restarts. If the imbalance survives all of that, it may be real — some populations genuinely have one dominant group and a few small ones, in which case a density method with an explicit noise label often describes the data better than any k. Problem 6 — choosing the method For each, name a suitable algorithm and say why. (a) Geo-tagged emergency call locations; you want to find hotspots and expect most calls to be scattered background. (b) 400 patient records with 6 numeric measurements and 3 categorical fields; you want a nested picture of patient types. (c) 5 million web sessions on 12 scaled numeric features; you want 8 segments for a marketing campaign. (d) Overlapping customer groups where you need to know which customers are ambiguous. Show worked solution (a) DBSCAN or HDBSCAN. Hotspots are dense regions of arbitrary shape, the background scatter needs a noise label rather than forced membership, and you do not know how many hotspots there are. Every one of those rules out k-means. (b) Gower distance with agglomerative hierarchical clustering. Mixed types rule out k-means (no mean for the categorical fields), 400 rows is comfortably within hierarchical clustering's limits, and \"nested picture\" asks for a dendrogram specifically. (c) k-means with k-means++. Five million points rules out everything except k-means and BIRCH; k is given; the features are already scaled. This is the case k-means was built for. (d) A Gaussian mixture model. Soft assignment is precisely the requirement — a customer with 0.55 and 0.45 membership is reported as ambiguous rather than silently rounded into one segment. Problem 7 — computing a silhouette Three points in cluster 1: X, Y, Z, with d(X,Y) = 2 and d(X,Z) = 4. The nearest other cluster has two points P and Q, with d(X,P) = 5 and d(X,Q) = 7. Compute s(X) and interpret it. Show worked solution a(X) = (2 + 4) / 2 = 3. b(X) = (5 + 7) / 2 = 6. s(X) = (6 − 3) / max{3, 6} = 3 / 6 = 0.5 . Positive, so X is in the right cluster, but only moderately well placed — the other cluster is just twice as far as its own, which is not a wide margin. Values above about 0.7 are usually read as strong structure, 0.5 to 0.7 as reasonable, below 0.25 as essentially no structure, and anything negative as misassigned. These bands are conventions rather than tests, so treat them as rough guidance. Part C — self-test self-test answered Which statement about k-means is false? It always terminates. It always finds the partition that minimises total within-cluster distance. Different starting points can give different final clusters. self-test answered In DBSCAN, what is a border point? A point that sits equally far from two clusters. A point within ε of a core point, but without enough neighbours to be a core point itself. A point with no neighbours within ε of it at all. self-test answered Why can total within-cluster distance not be used to choose k, while the silhouette can? Total distance always falls as k rises, so its optimum is degenerate; the silhouette balances cohesion against separation. Total distance requires knowing the true labels, whereas the silhouette does not. Total distance only works for spherical clusters, whereas the silhouette works for any shape. self-test answered Lesson 6 says clustering is unsupervised. What does that cost you that Module 3 took for granted? You cannot resample the data, so cross-validation is unavailable. There is no held-out answer to validate against, so every quality measure is internal. You cannot make predictions about new data points. Part D — written questions Four to write out 1. Explain why k-means cannot find the two rings in the figure in Extra 1, and name two algorithms that can. Show a model answer k-means minimises total distance to k centre points. The set of points closer to one centre than to any other is always a convex region — that is what a Voronoi cell is — so every cluster k-means can produce is convex. A ring is not convex: its own centre region belongs to the other ring. No value of k and no number of restarts changes this, because the limitation is in the objective function rather than the search. DBSCAN finds the rings by treating a cluster as a dense connected region, which follows a curve without difficulty; spectral clustering finds them by embedding the data in a space where the rings become linearly separable and then running k-means there. 2. State the k-means++ initialisation rule and explain what problem it addresses. Show a model answer Pick the first centre uniformly at random from the data. Then pick each remaining centre by sampling a data point with probability proportional to the squared distance from the nearest already-chosen centre, until k are chosen; then run the ordinary loop. It addresses the local-optimum problem: with uniform random starts, several centres can land in the same dense region, leaving other regions to share a centre, and the loop cannot recover. Distance-proportional sampling spreads the initial centres out, so each run is far more likely to land in a good basin — reducing how many restarts are needed rather than removing the need for them, since the underlying problem is NP-hard. 3. Give the silhouette formula, and explain why its maximum over k is meaningful while the minimum of total within-cluster distance is not. Show a model answer s(i) = (b(i) − a(i)) / max{a(i), b(i)}, where a(i) is the mean distance from i to the other members of its own cluster and b(i) the mean distance to the members of the nearest other cluster. Total within-cluster distance falls monotonically as k rises and hits zero when every point is its own cluster, so minimising it always selects the degenerate answer — the elbow heuristic exists only to work around that. The silhouette rewards separation as well as cohesion: splitting a real cluster in two reduces a but reduces b by a comparable amount, so the score does not improve, and the maximum lands on a genuine structure rather than on k = n. 4. Your data has eight numeric and three categorical columns. Explain what must be done before any distance-based method will give a sensible answer. Show a model answer Two things. The numeric columns must be put on a common scale, because otherwise the column with the largest range dominates every distance and the clustering is effectively on that column alone — the metric choice from Lesson 2 has far less influence on the result than this does. And the categorical columns need a distance definition of their own, since they have no mean and no ordering; the standard route is Gower distance, which computes a [0,1] distance per attribute in the way appropriate to its type and averages them. Gower produces a distance matrix rather than coordinates, so it pairs with k-medoids or hierarchical clustering, both of which need only pairwise distances. Encoding the categories as integers and running k-means invents an ordering and a spacing that do not exist. Sources and further reading Source What it gives you scikit-learn User Guide §2.3, Clustering scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/clustering.html The best free overview in existence. Covers every algorithm named above with a side-by-side picture of how each behaves on the same awkward datasets — rings, moons, anisotropic blobs — plus the full set of evaluation metrics. Start here. James, Witten, Hastie & Tibshirani, An Introduction to Statistical Learning , ch. 12 k-means and hierarchical clustering treated properly and readably, with the linkage comparison and a careful discussion of why clustering results need sanity-checking. Free PDF. Hastie, Tibshirani & Friedman, The Elements of Statistical Learning , ch. 14 The same ground at research level, including k-medoids, self-organising maps and spectral clustering. Free PDF. Aggarwal & Reddy, Data Clustering: Algorithms and Applications Reference-grade coverage of everything this appendix only names. Arthur & Vassilvitskii (2007), \"k-means++: The Advantages of Careful Seeding\". Short and readable; the source of the initialisation rule and its guarantee. Ester, Kriegel, Sander & Xu (1996), \"A Density-Based Algorithm for Discovering Clusters…\". The DBSCAN paper. Tibshirani, Walther & Hastie (2001), \"Estimating the number of clusters in a data set via the gap statistic\". Rousseeuw (1987), \"Silhouettes: a graphical aid to the interpretation and validation of cluster analysis\". The original, and still the clearest account of the per-point plot. Campello, Moulavi & Sander (2013) and McInnes, Healy & Astels (2017). HDBSCAN. Tool Language For scikit-learn sklearn.cluster Python KMeans, MiniBatchKMeans, BisectingKMeans, AgglomerativeClustering, DBSCAN, OPTICS, HDBSCAN, SpectralClustering, MeanShift, Birch, AffinityPropagation — plus sklearn.metrics for silhouette, Calinski–Harabasz, Davies–Bouldin, ARI and NMI. scipy.cluster.hierarchy Python Linkage computation and dendrogram plotting, which scikit-learn does not draw for you. gower , kmodes , tslearn Python Gower distances; k-modes and k-prototypes for categorical and mixed data; DTW-based clustering for time series. cluster , factoextra , NbClust , dbscan , mclust R PAM and Gower ( daisy ); dendrogram and elbow/silhouette plotting; NbClust runs about thirty cluster-count criteria at once and reports the majority vote; density methods; model-based clustering with BIC selection. A twenty-minute exercise worth doing Generate three datasets — round blobs, two concentric rings, and two crescents — and run k-means, agglomerative clustering with Ward and with single linkage, DBSCAN and spectral clustering on each. Five algorithms, three datasets, one picture. It takes about fifteen lines of scikit-learn and teaches the shape limitation of each method more durably than any table can, including this one's. Reading log click to type; saves automatically Caveat How much of this should you take on trust? Show answer The algorithm descriptions and the silhouette arithmetic are standard and consistent across the sources listed. The scaling limits in the comparison table are rough orders of magnitude that depend heavily on implementation and hardware, and the silhouette interpretation bands are conventions rather than tests. Treat the lectures as authoritative wherever the two differ."},{"id":"sec-14","h":"","t":"Written by an AI model from lecture recordings. Not reviewed by any instructor or institution — verify anything you rely on against the original lectures. Examples here are generic illustrations rather than the ones used in class. The Marcus Obi case, the comparison with k-nearest neighbours, the note that k-means pairs naturally with the 2-norm, and “Four things worth noticing” are this summary’s own additions rather than lecture content. Everything under “Additional resources” is outside material. Extras 1 to 4 — the survey of other clustering algorithms, the distance measures beyond the p-norms, the cluster-count and validation criteria, the concentric-rings figure, the worked silhouette numbers, the practice problems and the reading list — were assembled from published sources, principally the scikit-learn user guide, An Introduction to Statistical Learning (ch. 12), The Elements of Statistical Learning (ch. 14), and the original method papers cited in Extra 4. None of it was taught in this module. All worked numbers there are computed for illustration on invented data. Check quantitative claims against a primary source before relying on them, and treat the lectures as authoritative wherever the two differ."}]},{"f":"module-5-preparing-data.html","t":"Module 5: Preparing Data","s":[{"id":"l1","h":"Preparing data","t":"Lesson 1 Preparing data Three things can quietly wreck a model before it is even fitted — mismatched scales, a handful of extreme points, and columns carrying no information — and all three are fixable in advance. What this lesson is, and isn’t The source lecture for this lesson is a short preview: it names the three problems, says each can be dealt with before modelling, and hands off to the lessons that follow. It contains no procedures. Everything below that goes past naming the three problems — the worked scale comparison, the pipeline diagram, and the pointers to where each problem is actually solved — is orientation material written for these notes, not lecture content. The margin questions and both activities in this lesson are likewise written here rather than drawn from the recording. The gap between how we talk and what we do Throughout this course, data is described as though it went straight into the model: a predictor in a regression, a factor in a classification model. Credit score. Average daily temperature. Units sold at each event. The value of a share at five-second intervals. The concentration of a chemical in drinking water. The notation encourages this. Writing x ij for attribute j of data point i makes it sound as though x ij is simply the number that was measured — the exact count of units sold, the exact share price. Data preparation Everything done to a dataset between collecting it and handing it to a model. When a course says “assume the data is given”, this is the work it is assuming you already did. Three problems make that assumption unsafe. Three boxes are clickable. Open all three to finish this activity. Orientation Which of the three problems is actually taught inside this module? Show answer Only the second. Scaling belongs to Module 2, Lesson 7; variable selection and dimensionality reduction are named but not covered anywhere in these recordings. Lessons 2 and 3 here handle extreme points. Module 6 then changes the question entirely, from one odd point to a shift in the whole process. Why scale is a problem and not just an inconvenience Put average household income and credit score side by side. Income runs into the hundreds of thousands; a credit score runs to a few hundred. Nothing about that is wrong — the two quantities simply measure different things in different units. The trouble is that many algorithms add up contributions from every attribute, and an attribute measured in larger numbers contributes larger numbers. The optimizer then spends its effort where the numbers are big, which is a fact about the units rather than about the world. That is what “leading our algorithms down the wrong path” means concretely. A cross-reference, not a repeat Module 2, Lesson 7 works this through with an SVM: coefficients of 5 and 700 on two attributes, and a 1% nudge to the larger one moving the objective as much as a 600% change to the smaller. Rather than restate it here, treat that lesson as the answer to the first box in the diagram above. quick check answered What makes data preparation a distinct stage rather than part of modelling? It always happens before any model is fitted, so it is chronologically separate. The same problems and the same fixes apply across every model, so they are dealt with once rather than per method. It is about correcting errors in the data, whereas modelling is about the data that is correct. Class discussion — Lesson 1 click to type; saves automatically Worth noticing Two of the three problems are about values, and one isn’t. Which, and why does that matter? Show answer Scale and extreme points are properties of the numbers in a column. Extraneous information is a property of which columns you kept at all — so it is fixed by removing or combining columns rather than by transforming values. That is why it needs a different family of methods."},{"id":"l2","h":"Finding outliers","t":"Lesson 2 Finding outliers An outlier is a point that doesn’t fit its context — which is why there are several kinds of them, and no single test that finds them all. The definition, and immediately a complication Outlier A data point that is very different from the rest of the dataset. The obvious case is a single value far from everything else. That is what most people picture when they hear the word, and it has a name: a point outlier . But suppose that alongside one far-flung value there is also a small huddle of two or three points, well away from the mass of the data though not as extreme as the single one. Are those outliers too? There are only a few of them, and they are clearly different from the overwhelming majority — which is the definition. Yet they might have a perfectly good reason for being there. Why the answer is “it depends”, and more so here than usual Outliers carry philosophical questions as well as statistical ones. Deciding that a point “doesn’t belong” is a claim about what the dataset is supposed to represent, and no amount of arithmetic settles that. Lesson 3 is entirely about this problem; the rest of this lesson only covers spotting candidates. Two further kinds show up once the data has a time attribute. Three panels are clickable. Open all three to finish this activity. The middle panel is the one to hold on to: the January value is drawn at exactly the height of the July value, so the picture shows a point that is only an outlier because of where it sits in time. Vocabulary Why is a contextual outlier called contextual? Show answer Because the surrounding points supply the context that makes it anomalous. Its own value is unremarkable; only its position relative to the rest of the series — the time at which it occurs — makes it stand out. quick check answered A mid-winter temperature reading equal to a perfectly ordinary mid-summer one. What kind of outlier is it? A point outlier. A contextual outlier. A collective outlier. quick check answered A stretch of heartbeat data where an expected beat simply never appears. Which kind is this, and why does it matter for detection? A point outlier — the missing beat is a value far from the others. A contextual outlier — an ordinary value in the wrong place. A collective outlier — an anomaly by omission, visible only across the group. Finding them automatically: one dimension Eyeballing pictures is the easy part. For a single quantitative attribute there is a standard tool. Box and whisker plot The top and bottom of the box are the 75th and 25th percentiles; the line through the box is the median, the 50th. The whiskers run out to whatever you consider a reasonable range — the 10th and 90th percentiles, say, or the 5th and 95th. Every value beyond the whiskers is plotted individually, and those points are the possible outliers. Twenty order-fulfilment times, ranging from 38 to 96 minutes. With whiskers set at the 10th and 90th percentiles, four values fall outside and get plotted as individual dots. Where you put the whiskers decides how many points you are asked to think about — the plot does not decide it for you. A convention this lesson doesn’t mention Most statistical software does not use percentile whiskers by default. It extends them to the furthest point within 1.5 × IQR of the box, where IQR is the 75th minus the 25th percentile. For the data above that gives 44.75 − 1.5 × 8.75 = 31.6 and 53.5 + 1.5 × 8.75 = 66.6, so only 71 and 96 get flagged — half as many points as the percentile rule. Same data, same plot type, different answer. Know which convention your software used before you report a count of outliers. quick check answered A colleague reports that a box plot “found four outliers” in the order-time data. What is the first thing to ask? How many data points there were in total. Where the whiskers were set, since that alone changes the count. Whether the four points were errors or real values. Exam target Name every element of a box and whisker plot and say what each shows. Show answer Box edges: the 25th and 75th percentiles. Line inside the box: the median, the 50th percentile. Whiskers: out to a chosen reasonable range, such as the 10th and 90th or 5th and 95th percentiles. Individual dots beyond the whiskers: the possible outliers. Finding them automatically: more than one dimension There is no good all-purpose method for detecting multi-dimensional outliers, or the other types, before modelling. That is a genuine gap rather than an omission from the lesson. What you can do is turn the problem around: fit a model first, then look at where it is most wrong . Fit an exponential smoothing model to a time series, and in most periods the difference between the actual value and the model’s estimate will be small, because the fitted function is smooth. At an outlier it will not be. The model expects a point sitting on the smooth curve, and the actual value is nowhere near it. Every filled dot sits on the fitted curve, so the model’s error there is near zero. One observation sits far below it, and that error is what flags the point — no threshold on the raw values would have found it, since the value itself falls inside the range the series visits elsewhere. Only half the battle Everything in this lesson produces candidates. Not one of these methods tells you whether a flagged point is a mistake, a rare but genuine event, or the most informative observation in the dataset. That question is Lesson 3’s, and it cannot be answered from inside the dataset. Class discussion — Lesson 2 click to type; saves automatically Worth noticing Why does the model-error approach catch contextual outliers when a box plot can’t? Show answer A box plot sees only the distribution of values, so a mid-winter reading equal to a normal summer one sits comfortably inside the box. A fitted time-series model expects a particular value at that time , so the same reading produces a large error. The model supplies exactly the context the word “contextual” refers to."},{"id":"l3","h":"Dealing with outliers","t":"Lesson 3 Dealing with outliers Finding an outlier is the easy half — deciding whether it is an error, a real rarity worth modelling, or a real event you can’t model, takes investigation outside the dataset. First question: what caused it? Everything else follows from this. Often an outlier is simply bad data: A sensor malfunctioned A wireless transmission between sensor and receiver failed An experiment was contaminated — bacteria accidentally introduced into a medical test, say A person entered the wrong figure, or copied the wrong value across But sometimes an outlier reflects something real in the system. Then you need to find out what caused it and decide whether the model should account for it. The uncomfortable part Answering either question — is it real or an error, and if real what to do — usually requires investigation. Where did the data come from? How was it compiled? What was unusual about the circumstances of that particular point? This means looking beyond a dataset and a model. It is also a fast way to make your results actually worth something to whoever asked for them. Flag the candidate, by any of the methods in Lesson 2. Trace it: where the value came from, how it was recorded, and what was going on at the time. Decide whether it is an error or real. This step happens outside the dataset. If it is an error, omit the point — or, if you need a value in that slot, impute one. If it is real, ask whether the thing it represents can be predicted at all. If it can, decide whether your model should represent it, or whether a second model should. If it can’t, you may still have to remove it — but now you know what you are removing. quick check answered A detection method flags a point as an outlier. What is the next step? Remove it — that is what a detection method flagging it is telling you. Impute a replacement from the neighbouring values so the series stays complete. Trace where the value came from and what happened at the time. quick check answered You establish the flagged point is a real observation, not an error. What question comes next? Whether the event behind it is predictable from the data you have. Whether it is far enough from the median to fail the 1.5×IQR rule. Whether removing it improves the model’s fit. Exam target Give four causes of an outlier that is simply bad data. Show answer A malfunctioning sensor; a failed transmission between sensor and receiver; a contaminated experiment; and human error in entering or copying a value. If it is real: expect some of them Some outliers are nothing but true randomness. With enough data, extreme values are not merely possible — they are guaranteed. normally distributed noise: P( |x − μ| > 2σ ) = 4.55% P( |x − μ| > 3σ ) = 0.270% → about 2,700 points in a million Both figures are two-sided — they count values that are extreme in either direction. Rounding the second to “about 2,000” undercounts it; if you want the one-sided figure, values more than 3σ above the mean, that is roughly 1,350 in a million. So in a dataset of a million points, thousands of them being far from expectation is not evidence of anything wrong. It is what the distribution says should happen. The question then is not whether these points are legitimate but whether they matter to the model. The test to apply If the magnitude of your model’s error is part of how its value is judged, then rare large values may belong in the dataset. Throw them out and the model becomes too optimistic, and reality will supply some bad surprises. Take a supply chain moving perishable medicine from a manufacturing site to clinics in a remote region. Most shipments take a predictable time. Occasionally severe weather or a border disruption makes one take far longer. Those points are real, they recur, and the cost of underestimating them lands on patients. Deleting them would produce a model that is right about the ordinary case and silent about the case that matters. Splitting one model into two You do not have to choose between including outliers and discarding them. A common alternative is to model the two regimes separately: Build one model — logistic regression, for example — estimating the probability that an outlying event occurs under given conditions. Build a second model estimating the outcome under normal conditions, fitted to data with the outliers removed. Combine them when forecasting: how likely is the disruption, and how long does each case take. Why this often beats a single model A single model fitted to both regimes is pulled between them and describes neither well. Splitting lets each model see a coherent population. The price is that you now have two models to validate, and a probability estimate that is itself uncertain. Check In a million normally distributed observations, roughly how many sit more than three standard deviations from the mean? Show answer About 2,700, counting both tails — 0.27% of the data. Roughly 1,350 of those are on the high side. Either way, the point is that thousands of extreme values in a large dataset are expected rather than suspicious. Real, and still unusable The last case is the awkward one: an outlier that is genuine data but describes something that will not happen again in any predictable way. Sometimes such points have to be removed even though they are correct. What makes this case dangerous is that it looks exactly like bad data from the outside. The scenario below is one version of how that goes wrong. PR Priya Raman Demand analyst, regional grocery chain Priya is fitting a weekly sales model for a single product line. Three years of data look clean except for one week, where sales spike to roughly four times anything else in the series. Every other column that week looks normal. A teammate: “That’s obviously a data error — probably a duplicated import. Drop it and refit; we’re already behind.” What should Priya do? Drop it. A fourfold spike with no supporting change in any other column is almost certainly an import error. Ask someone in the business what happened that week, before touching anything. Cap the value at the 99th percentile so it stops dominating the fit, and move on. The point is confirmed as real. It was driven by a one-off news cycle. Nothing in Priya’s available data — price, promotion, season, store count — would have predicted it, and nobody expects it to recur. Now what? Keep it in. It is real data, and removing real data biases the model. Remove it, and document that it was a verified one-off rather than an error. Build a second model for spike weeks, as the perishable-medicine example did. The lesson underneath the story Even when you are confident you know what an odd data point is, it is worth asking. The cost of one conversation is small; the cost of deleting the most informative row in a dataset is not. And note that checking changed the reason for removing the point without changing the decision — which is still a better position to be in. Class discussion — Lesson 3 click to type; saves automatically Worth noticing Two of the three destinations for an outlier end in removing it. What distinguishes them? Show answer The reason, and therefore what you write down. An erroneous point is removed because it never happened. A verified one-off is removed because it happened but cannot be predicted from the data you have. Only the second obliges you to record the event somewhere, in case it stops being a one-off."},{"id":"targets","h":"Transfer practice — Data preparation","t":"Transfer practice — Data preparation Same ideas, unfamiliar settings Why these are here Each prompt below moves the module’s ideas into a situation the lessons never mentioned. If you can only answer a question in the wording it was taught in, what you have memorised is the example rather than the concept — and an exam that changes the setting will find that out. Answer these out loud before opening them. Exam target A sensor logs the value 999 whenever it fails. What kind of problem is this, and what does detection alone tell you? Show answer A recording error presenting as an extreme value. Detection tells you only that the value is unlike the others; it cannot tell you that 999 is a failure code rather than a genuine reading. That comes from knowing how the data was produced — which is why detection yields candidates and the cause determines the action. Exam target One record shows an unremarkable height and an unremarkable weight, but the combination is impossible. Would a box plot find it? Show answer No. A box plot examines one attribute at a time, and neither value is extreme on its own. Finding it requires looking at the attributes jointly — in practice, fitting a model relating them and inspecting the largest residuals, since that identifies records the rest of the data does not account for. Exam target In a million records, about 2,700 fall beyond three standard deviations from the mean. What should you conclude? Show answer Nothing yet — that is precisely what normally distributed noise produces, since P(|x − μ| > 3σ) = 0.270%. Being extreme is not evidence of error. In a large dataset, extreme values are expected in quantity, and treating all of them as problems removes real data. Exam target Two teams report different outlier counts from the same column. What is the first question? Show answer Which whisker convention each used. The common default extends whiskers to the furthest record within 1.5 × IQR of the box; percentile conventions such as 10th/90th or 5th/95th are also used and flag a different number of points on identical data. Neither is wrong, but a count is meaningless without the convention attached. Exam target A column is 40% missing. What are the options and what does each cost? Show answer Drop the column, losing whatever signal it carries; drop the affected records, losing 40% of the dataset and biasing what remains if the missingness relates to the outcome; or impute and add an indicator column recording that the value was absent. The decision turns on why the values are missing, since missingness that relates to the outcome is itself information and cannot be filled in neutrally. Exam target A text column holds city names and you intend to cluster. How do you encode it? Show answer As indicator columns, one per city, each 0 or 1 — which leaves every pair of cities equidistant. Assigning the numbers 1, 2, 3 instead would tell the distance calculation that the cities are ordered and that some pairs are three times further apart than others, neither of which is true."},{"id":"sec-5","h":"How the module fits together","t":"How the module fits together The through-line across the three lessons Lesson 1 Names the three things that break a model before it is fitted, and hands two of them to other topics. Lesson 2 Shows that “doesn’t fit” has several meanings, and that detection produces candidates rather than verdicts. Lesson 3 Turns the candidates into decisions, using information that isn’t in the dataset. Things worth noticing The module narrows as it goes. Lesson 1 names three problems, Lessons 2 and 3 pursue exactly one of them, and by the end you are looking at a single data point and asking a question no algorithm can settle. That funnel is the shape of the topic. Detection and judgement are strictly separate. Nothing in Lesson 2 tells you whether a flagged point is an error. Nothing in Lesson 3 helps you find one. Confusing the two is how people end up deleting real data because software drew a dot outside a whisker. Every threshold here is chosen, not derived. Where the whiskers go, what counts as “a lot of error” in a fitted model — both are settings. Module 3’s validation material is what turns such choices into defensible ones, and no lesson here says so out loud. Lesson 2’s model-error trick reappears in Module 6. Fitting a model of what the data should look like and then watching the residual is exactly what CUSUM does, except that CUSUM accumulates the residual instead of examining each one alone. Likely exam targets Define point, contextual and collective outliers, with an example of each. Explain why a contextual outlier can be invisible to a box plot but obvious to a fitted time-series model. Name every element of a box and whisker plot and what it represents. List four causes of an outlier that is bad data, and say what to do in each case. Explain when a real outlier should be kept, when it should be split into a second model, and when it should be removed anyway. Say roughly what fraction of normally distributed data falls beyond two and three standard deviations, and why that matters for large datasets. Name the three data-preparation problems and say where each is dealt with. Everything above is class material. Additional resources follow — the rest of the detection toolbox, scaling and missing-data methods, the leakage rule, practice problems and reading — none of it taught in this module. Module 5 — my own summary click to type; saves automatically"},{"id":"extra","h":"Additional resources","t":"Additional resources Everything below this line is not class material Read this first The three lessons above are the module as taught: what preparing data means, what an outlier is and how a box plot finds one, and how to decide what to do once you have found one. Everything from here on was assembled from outside sources — textbooks, the NIST engineering statistics handbook, method papers and library documentation. None of it is examinable unless your instructor says so, and where a convention differs from the lectures, the lectures win . Lesson 1 names three data-preparation problems and hands two of them to other topics; Lesson 2 gives one detection method and says there are others. The appendix supplies both missing halves — the full detection toolbox, and the parts of data preparation the lectures deliberately set aside — and adds practice. Extra 1 Every standard way of detecting an outlier, from the IQR rule to isolation forests. Extra 2 The rest of data preparation: scaling, missing values, categories, imbalance — and the leakage rule that governs all of them. Extra 3 Practice problems with worked arithmetic, a self-test, and where to read further."},{"id":"x1","h":"The full detection toolbox","t":"Extra 1 · not class material The full detection toolbox Lesson 2's box plot is one of about a dozen standard methods. They disagree with each other routinely, and knowing why is more useful than knowing any one of them. Two failure modes every method has to handle Before the catalogue, the two problems that separate the good rules from the naive ones. Both arise because most rules estimate \"normal\" from the same data that contains the abnormality. Masking Outliers inflate the estimate of spread, which makes them look less extreme, which can push them back inside the threshold. Two outliers hide each other; one large one can hide a smaller one. This is why a rule built on the mean and standard deviation is fragile. Swamping The reverse: an extreme point drags the centre toward itself, so perfectly ordinary points on the opposite side start to look far from the mean and get flagged. The method reports outliers that are artefacts of the outlier it should have found. Worked demonstration — the same twenty values, three rules Order-fulfilment times, in hours: 12, 14, 14, 15, 15, 16, 16, 17, 18, 19, 20, 21, 22, 23, 25, 26, 28, 31, 88 , 96 . Two values are clearly wrong. Mean = 26.80, standard deviation = 22.90, median = 19.5, MAD = 4.5, Q1 = 15.5, Q3 = 25.5, IQR = 10. Value Classical z (x − mean) / sd Modified z 0.6745 (x − median) / MAD Tukey beyond Q3 + 1.5 × IQR = 40.5 31 (ordinary) 0.18 1.72 no 88 2.67 — passes 10.27 — flagged flagged 96 3.02 — just flagged 11.47 — flagged flagged Under the usual \"|z| > 3\" rule, 88 goes undetected — not because it is borderline but because 96 inflated the standard deviation to 22.9, making everything look less extreme. That is masking, in a dataset small enough to eyeball. The two robust rules, built on the median rather than the mean, are unaffected and flag both at enormous margins. Family 1 — Univariate rules of thumb Rule Flags a point when Notes Tukey / IQR fences the box plot's whiskers It falls beyond Q1 − 1.5 × IQR or Q3 + 1.5 × IQR. Some software also draws \"far out\" fences at 3 × IQR. Lesson 2's method. Distribution-free, robust, and visual. The 1.5 is a convention, not a test: on normally distributed data it flags about 0.7% of points by chance, so a clean sample of 1,000 produces roughly seven \"outliers\" that are nothing of the sort. Classical z-score |x − mean| / sd exceeds 2 or 3. Easy, familiar, and the most fragile thing here. Masking and swamping both hit it directly, as above. Use it only when you already know the data is clean, which is a strange condition for an outlier rule. Modified z-score Iglewicz & Hoaglin 0.6745 × |x − median| / MAD exceeds about 3.5. The same idea with the median for centre and the median absolute deviation for spread, so neither estimate can be dragged by the points being tested. The 0.6745 factor makes MAD comparable to a standard deviation under normality. The drop-in replacement for the z-score, and almost always the better default. Hampel filter The same rule applied in a sliding window. The version for time series, where \"normal\" drifts. Related to Lesson 2's point that a contextual outlier needs a local notion of expectation. Percentile / winsorising bounds It falls outside, say, the 1st or 99th percentile. Crude but predictable, and it names in advance how much data you will treat as extreme. Often paired with capping rather than deletion. Family 2 — Formal statistical tests These give a p-value rather than a flag, which matters when you have to justify the decision to someone. All of them assume the data is approximately normal apart from the outliers, and all of them are misleading if it is not — a skewed distribution produces \"significant\" outliers in its long tail forever. Test Designed for Caveat Grubbs' test Exactly one outlier, in roughly normal data, n > 25 or so. The statistic is simply the largest |z| in the sample, compared against a critical value that depends on n. Applying it repeatedly to find several outliers is wrong — masking means the second one may never reach significance, and the significance level is not maintained across repeated tests. Dixon's Q One outlier in a very small sample, roughly n < 25. Based on the gap to the nearest neighbour relative to the total range. Low power; really a laboratory convention for triplicate measurements. Generalised ESD (Rosner) Up to a stated number of outliers. Removes the most extreme point, re-tests, and adjusts the critical values for the sequence of tests. The right answer when you suspect more than one. You must specify an upper bound on how many; overstating it is safer than understating it. Tietjen–Moore An exact number of outliers, known in advance. Rarely the situation you are actually in, which is why ESD is more used. On the fulfilment data above Grubbs computes the largest z, which is 3.02 for 96 against a 5% critical value of about 2.71 at n = 20 — significant, so 96 is declared an outlier. Then you stop, because Grubbs tests one point. Iterating it naively would next test 88 against a recomputed mean and standard deviation, and while here it would in fact be caught, the significance level of that second test is not the one you think it is. If you suspect two, the correct tool is generalised ESD with an upper bound of two or three, which handles the sequencing properly. Family 3 — More than one dimension at a time Lesson 2 raises this: a point can be entirely ordinary on every attribute separately and absurd in combination — a 22-year-old with 40 years of driving experience. Column-by-column rules cannot see it. Mahalanobis distance. Distance from the centre measured in units of the data's own covariance, so it accounts for how attributes vary together. Under normality its square follows a chi-squared distribution with as many degrees of freedom as there are attributes, which gives a threshold with a real significance level attached. The standard multivariate rule. Robust Mahalanobis (MCD). Same idea, but the centre and covariance are estimated from the most tightly packed subset of the data — the minimum covariance determinant — so the outliers cannot inflate the covariance that is meant to catch them. This is the masking fix, applied in many dimensions, and it matters more here than in one dimension because a few bad points can distort a covariance matrix badly. Regression influence measures. When there is a fitted model, \"outlier\" splits into three distinguishable things: a large residual (the model predicts this point badly), high leverage (the point sits far out in the space of the inputs, so it has the opportunity to move the fit), and high influence (it actually does move the fit). Cook's distance measures the third by refitting without the point and seeing how much the predictions change; DFFITS and DFBETAS do the same per-prediction and per-coefficient. A high-leverage point that happens to sit on the line is harmless; a moderate residual with high leverage can be the most damaging point in the dataset. This is Lesson 2's \"fit a model and look at the error\" made precise. Family 4 — Machine-learning detectors For many attributes, non-normal data, or where the notion of \"unusual\" is about local structure rather than distance from a single centre. Method Idea Best for Isolation Forest Liu, Ting & Zhou, 2008 Build random trees that split on random attributes at random values. Anomalies get isolated in few splits because they sit apart; normal points need many. The anomaly score is the average path length. The practical default for tabular data. Fast, scales well, needs little tuning, handles many attributes. Note the inversion: it models what is easy to separate rather than what is normal. Local Outlier Factor Breunig et al., 2000 Compare each point's local density with the density of its neighbours. A point in a sparse region surrounded by sparse regions is fine; a point in a sparse region next to a dense one is not. Data with clusters of differing density , where a global threshold is meaningless. A point that is unremarkable in a sparse cluster would be extreme inside a tight one, and only a local method sees that. One-class SVM Learn a boundary enclosing the normal data; anything outside is an anomaly. Novelty detection, where you have a clean training set and want to flag genuinely new things. Sensitive to its kernel parameters. Elliptic envelope Fit a robust Gaussian and flag points outside a confidence ellipse. Essentially robust Mahalanobis in a scikit-learn wrapper. Good when the data really is roughly elliptical. DBSCAN Points that fall in no dense region are labelled noise. A free by-product if you are clustering anyway (Module 4). The noise label is an outlier flag. Autoencoder reconstruction error Train a network to compress and rebuild the data; points it rebuilds badly are unlike what it learned. Images, sensor arrays, high-dimensional data. Heavy machinery for a tabular problem. Family 5 — Outliers in time series Lesson 2's contextual outlier lives here, and the general recipe is the one the lesson describes: model what the value should have been, then examine the residual. What varies is the model. Seasonal decomposition (STL) residuals. Strip out trend and seasonality, then apply any of the univariate rules above to what is left. A 35°C reading in July is only an outlier once July has been accounted for. Forecast residuals. Fit an ARIMA or exponential-smoothing model and flag points far from their one-step-ahead prediction. Module 6's CUSUM is the same trick with the residuals accumulated rather than judged one at a time — which is precisely the difference between an outlier and a change. Hampel filter. Rolling median and rolling MAD, so \"normal\" is defined locally and can drift. Choosing Situation Start with One column, quick screen, want to see the data Box plot / Tukey fences — Lesson 2's method One column, need a defensible number Modified z-score; generalised ESD if you need a p-value One column, several suspected outliers Modified z-score or generalised ESD — never iterated Grubbs Several correlated columns Robust Mahalanobis (MCD) A fitted model exists Cook's distance, leverage, standardised residuals Many columns, mixed types, no distributional assumption Isolation Forest Clusters of differing density Local Outlier Factor Time series Decompose or forecast first, then any rule on the residuals When two methods disagree, that is the result Running three detectors and getting three different sets of flagged points is the normal outcome, not a sign that something is broken. Each encodes a different definition of \"doesn't fit\", which is Lesson 2's opening complication restated: the definition was never settled in the first place. The productive response is to treat every disagreement as a point worth looking at by hand — and none of it changes Lesson 3's verdict, which still comes from knowing where the data came from rather than from any statistic. quick check answered Why did the value 88 escape the |z| > 3 rule in the worked example? Because 88 is genuinely close to the rest of the data, and the other rules over-flagged it. Because 96 inflated the standard deviation, shrinking every z-score including 88's. Because the sample is too small for a z-score to be meaningful at n = 20. quick check answered A data point has high leverage but a small residual. Should you worry? Yes — high leverage always means the point is distorting the model. Not much — leverage is the opportunity to move the fit, and a small residual says it is not taking it. No — leverage only matters for classification, not regression. Detection methods for my data click to type; saves automatically Link to Lesson 2 Does any of this change the box plot's status? Show answer No. The box plot remains the right first move on a single column, because it flags candidates and shows you the shape of the data at the same time — which no numeric rule does. What the rest of the toolbox adds is what to reach for once one column is not enough: robustness when the data is dirty, a p-value when you need to justify a decision, and multivariate methods when the problem is a combination rather than a value."},{"id":"x2","h":"The rest of data preparation","t":"Extra 2 · not class material The rest of data preparation Lesson 1 names three problems and pursues one. Here are the other two, plus the ones it does not name — and the single rule that governs how all of them must be carried out. The rule that comes before every technique on this page Fit every transformation on training data only A scaler needs a mean and a standard deviation. An imputer needs a median. A target encoder needs group averages. Every one of those is a parameter estimated from data, and if you estimate it from the whole dataset before splitting, information from the validation and test sets has leaked into the training procedure. The validation score then overstates how well the model will do, in exactly the way Module 3 spends twelve lessons warning about. The correct order is: split first, compute the statistic on the training portion, apply it unchanged to the held-out portion. Inside cross-validation this has to happen within each fold , refitting the transformation k times, which is why every serious library has a pipeline object — it exists precisely so this cannot be got wrong by accident. Standardising the full dataset and then calling train_test_split is the single most common leakage bug in practice, and it is silent: nothing errors, the score is just quietly too good. Problem 1 — Scale, in more detail than Lesson 1 gives it Lesson 1 makes the case that attributes on wildly different scales break a model. Which fix to use depends on the model and on what the data looks like. Transformation Formula Use when Standardisation (z-score) (x − mean) / sd → mean 0, sd 1 The general default. Required by anything distance-based (k-means, k-NN, SVM) or regularised (ridge, lasso), since a penalty on coefficient size is meaningless if the coefficients are in different units. Does not bound the range, and is itself dragged by outliers. Min–max normalisation (x − min) / (max − min) → [0, 1] You need a bounded range — neural network inputs, image pixels, anything fed to a bounded activation. Extremely sensitive to outliers , since a single extreme value sets the denominator and squashes everything else into a sliver of the range. Robust scaling (x − median) / IQR Outliers are present and you are keeping them. Same shape as standardisation, computed from quantities they cannot move — the Extra 1 lesson applied to scaling. Max-abs scaling x / max|x| → [−1, 1] Sparse data, because it does not shift values and so does not destroy the zeros. Subtracting a mean from a sparse matrix makes it dense and can blow up memory. Unit-norm (L2) scaling each row divided by its own length Only direction matters — text vectors, profiles. Note this scales rows, not columns, unlike everything else here. Log transform log(x) or log(1 + x) Strongly right-skewed positive data: income, counts, durations, prices. Often the single most useful transformation there is, because it turns multiplicative structure into additive structure — and it makes extreme values much less extreme without deleting anything, which is sometimes the better answer to a Lesson 3 dilemma. Box–Cox a power transform with the exponent fitted You want the log idea generalised, with the data choosing the power. Strictly positive values only. Yeo–Johnson the same, extended As Box–Cox but handles zero and negative values. The safer default of the two. Quantile transform map to ranks, then to a uniform or normal distribution Nothing else works and the model needs well-behaved inputs. It forces any distribution into the target shape — but it collapses outliers onto the boundary, making them indistinguishable from ordinary extreme points, which destroys exactly the information Lesson 3 cares about. Powerful and blunt. Two practical notes. Tree-based models need none of this — decision trees, random forests and gradient boosting split on thresholds within each column independently, so any monotonic rescaling gives an identical tree. If you are wondering whether to scale, the first question is which model. And scaling does not remove outliers : a point three standard deviations out is still three standard deviations out afterwards. Scaling fixes the comparability problem, not the extremity problem. quick check answered Your data has a few extreme values you have decided to keep, and you need to scale before running k-means. Which scaler? Min–max normalisation, so everything sits neatly in [0, 1]. Robust scaling, using the median and IQR. Standardisation, since it is the general default. Problem 2 — Missing values Lesson 1 names this and passes it on. The essential first step is not a technique but a question: why is it missing? The answer determines whether any fix is safe. Mechanism Meaning Consequence MCAR missing completely at random Whether a value is missing has nothing to do with anything — a form was lost, a sensor dropped a packet. The easy case. Deleting the rows loses power but introduces no bias. MAR missing at random Missingness depends on observed data — older respondents skip a question more often, and you have their age. Fixable. Imputation that uses the other columns can recover an unbiased picture. MNAR missing not at random Missingness depends on the unobserved value itself — high earners decline to state income; a sensor fails precisely when readings go out of range. The dangerous case. No imputation method fixes it, because the information needed is exactly what is absent. The fact of missingness is itself data and should be recorded as an indicator column. The techniques, in rough order of sophistication: Deletion. Drop rows (listwise) or columns. Safe under MCAR, biased otherwise, and it can throw away most of a dataset when missingness is scattered across many columns. Mean / median / mode imputation. One number for every gap. Fast, and it systematically shrinks the variance of that column and weakens its correlations with everything else — the imputed points all sit exactly at the centre. Acceptable for a small percentage, misleading beyond that. Use the median for skewed columns. Indicator column. Add a binary \"was missing\" flag alongside whatever you impute. Cheap, and it lets the model learn from the missingness itself — essential under MNAR. Regression / k-NN imputation. Predict the missing value from the other columns. Preserves relationships far better; understates uncertainty, because the imputed value is a point estimate treated as though it were observed. Multiple imputation (MICE). Generate several complete datasets with different plausible fills, analyse each, and combine the results so the extra uncertainty appears in the standard errors. The statistically correct answer under MAR, and the standard in fields where inference matters more than prediction. Problem 3 — Categorical attributes Not mentioned in Lesson 1, but it sits in the same slot: something the model cannot consume as it stands. One-hot encoding. One binary column per category. The safe default; explodes in width when a column has thousands of levels. Ordinal encoding. Map categories to integers. Correct only when there genuinely is an ordering (small < medium < large). Applied to unordered categories it invents a false ordering and false spacing — the same error as numbering regions and computing a distance in Module 4. Target encoding. Replace each category with the mean response for that category. Handles high-cardinality columns compactly and leaks badly if computed on the full data, since each row's own response contributes to its own feature. Must be done inside the cross-validation folds, with smoothing for rare categories. Frequency encoding, hashing, embeddings. Progressively more compact routes for very high cardinality. A fourth problem: unbalanced classes When 0.2% of transactions are fraudulent, a model that predicts \"not fraud\" every time is 99.8% accurate and completely useless. The standard responses are resampling the training set (oversampling the minority, undersampling the majority, or generating synthetic minority examples with SMOTE), weighting the classes in the loss function, or leaving the data alone and changing the metric — precision, recall, F1 or the area under the precision–recall curve instead of accuracy. Two cautions worth carrying: resampling belongs inside the cross-validation loop, never before the split, for the leakage reason above; and it is applied to the training folds only, never to the held-out fold, which must keep the real class balance or the estimate is meaningless. Where all of this sits Order Step 1 Split off the test set. Nothing below this line may look at it. 2 Understand missingness and record indicator columns. 3 Detect outlier candidates (Lesson 2, Extra 1) and decide what they are (Lesson 3). 4 Encode categorical attributes. 5 Impute remaining gaps — parameters from training data only. 6 Transform skew, then scale — parameters from training data only. 7 Resample for class balance, training folds only. 8 Fit and validate (Module 3), with steps 4 to 7 refitted inside every fold. Steps 3 and 6 are worth noting together: outlier decisions come before scaling, because a scaler fitted on data containing a bad value inherits the damage. And every step from 4 to 7 estimates something, which is why they all have to live inside the fold. quick check answered A sensor reports nothing whenever the true reading exceeds its maximum range. Which missingness mechanism, and what follows? MCAR — the dropouts are a hardware fault and unrelated to the data. MNAR — and imputing the median would replace the largest readings with typical ones. MAR — so k-NN imputation from the other sensors will recover the values. Prep steps for my data click to type; saves automatically Link to Module 3 Why does preparing data belong in the cross-validation loop? Show answer Because every preparation step that estimates something — a mean, a median, a category average — is part of the modelling procedure, not a fixed property of the world. Module 3's whole argument is that you estimate performance by applying the entire procedure to data it has never seen. Fitting the scaler once on everything and then cross-validating measures a procedure that nobody can actually run on new data, and reports a number that is too good."},{"id":"x3","h":"Practice, reading and tools","t":"Extra 3 · not class material Practice, reading and tools Seven problems with the arithmetic worked out, a self-test, and where to read further. Do Problems 1 and 2 by hand — the fences and the modified z-score are the two calculations most likely to be asked for. Part A — the arithmetic Problem 1 — box plot by hand Twelve values: 4, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12, 13, 14, 16, 18, 21, 42. Find the median, quartiles, IQR, the whisker fences, and say which points the box plot flags. Show worked solution Already sorted, n = 12. Median: mean of the 6th and 7th values = (12 + 13)/2 = 12.5 . Q1: median of the lower six (4, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12) = (8 + 9)/2 = 8.5 . Q3: median of the upper six (13, 14, 16, 18, 21, 42) = (16 + 18)/2 = 17 . IQR: 17 − 8.5 = 8.5 . Fences: lower 8.5 − 1.5(8.5) = −4.25; upper 17 + 1.5(8.5) = 29.75 . Flagged: 42 only. The whiskers are drawn to the most extreme values inside the fences, so the upper whisker ends at 21, not at 29.75 — a detail people get wrong. The lower whisker ends at 4. Note what the box plot does not say: nothing about whether 42 is a typing error, a genuine long job, or a different kind of order altogether. That is Lesson 3's question. Problem 2 — classical versus modified z-score Same twelve values. Compute the classical z-score of 42 (mean 14.58, sd 10.05) and the modified z-score (median 12.5, MAD 4.5). Compare. Show worked solution Classical: (42 − 14.58) / 10.05 = 2.73 . Under the common |z| > 3 rule this does not flag. Modified: 0.6745 × (42 − 12.5) / 4.5 = 0.6745 × 6.56 = 4.42 . Against the usual cut-off of 3.5, this flags clearly. The gap is the whole argument for robust rules. 42 has inflated both the mean it is being compared to and the standard deviation it is being divided by, so it shrinks its own score from both directions at once. The median and MAD are computed from the middle of the data and cannot be moved by one extreme point, so the modified score reports the distance honestly. To find the MAD: take |x − 12.5| for every value, giving 8.5, 5.5, 4.5, 3.5, 1.5, 0.5, 0.5, 1.5, 3.5, 5.5, 8.5, 29.5, and take the median of those, which is 4.5. Problem 3 — how many false alarms should you expect? You apply the 1.5 × IQR rule to a clean sample of 5,000 normally distributed measurements. Roughly how many points get flagged, and what should you conclude? Show worked solution Under normality the Tukey fences sit at roughly ±2.7 standard deviations, and about 0.7% of a normal distribution falls beyond them — so roughly 35 points . Every one of them is a perfectly ordinary observation from the intended process. The conclusion is Lesson 3's, arrived at from the other direction: in a large dataset, most flagged points are not errors . The flag rate is a property of the rule and the sample size, not evidence about the data. This is also why \"delete everything outside the whiskers\" is a bad default — on 5,000 points it discards 35 real observations, and if the distribution has any right skew at all, far more than that. For scale, the comparable figures for standard-deviation rules: about 4.6% of normal data lies beyond two standard deviations, and about 0.27% beyond three — roughly 2,700 points per million. Problem 4 — the multivariate blind spot A dataset of drivers has an age column (range 17–90) and a years-of-experience column (range 0–70). One record reads age 22, experience 40. Would a box plot on either column catch it? What would? Show worked solution No. Age 22 is unremarkable; 40 years of experience is unremarkable. Neither column, examined alone, has anything to report. Yet the combination is impossible — the person would have started driving at two. What catches it: Mahalanobis distance , because age and experience are strongly correlated and this record sits far from that relationship even though it is near the centre of each margin. Or, more directly, a domain rule : experience ≤ age − 17. Or a derived feature , age minus experience, which converts the two-dimensional problem into a one-dimensional one that a box plot can see. That last option is worth remembering, because it generalises: when you know which combination is the meaningful one, computing it as a new column is usually simpler and more interpretable than reaching for a multivariate detector. Part B — judgement Problem 5 — four flagged points, four different answers An A&E department's dataset of patient wait times flags four points. For each, say what you would do and why. (a) A wait of −15 minutes. (b) A wait of 1,440 minutes, on a day the hospital's IT system was down. (c) A wait of 611 minutes, on the night of a major road accident. (d) A wait of 380 minutes, on an ordinary Tuesday, with nothing unusual recorded. Show worked solution (a) Remove or correct. A negative wait is physically impossible, so it is an error — most likely swapped timestamps. If the underlying times are recoverable, correct it; if not, treat the value as missing rather than deleting the whole patient record, since the other columns are probably fine. (b) Remove, with documentation. Exactly 1,440 minutes is 24 hours, which is the signature of a default or a fallback value rather than a measurement. Combined with the known IT outage, this is bad data about the recording system rather than data about the patient. Note that you are removing it because you identified the cause, not because it was extreme. (c) Keep — and consider modelling it separately. This is real, explicable, and quite possibly the most informative row in the dataset: it is the only evidence you have about how the department performs under surge. Deleting it produces a model that is confidently wrong about exactly the situation anyone would want it to predict. Lesson 3's \"split one model into two\" applies if there are enough such nights to model. (d) Keep, and investigate. Nothing justifies removal. A long wait on an ordinary day is either genuine variation — which the model should learn — or a signal of something not captured in the columns you have, which is worth finding out. The temptation to delete it because it is inconvenient is precisely the failure the module warns about. Problem 6 — diagnosing a pipeline A colleague's workflow is: load data → standardise all columns → remove points with |z| > 3 → split into train and test → fit → evaluate. Name three separate problems. Show worked solution 1. Leakage. The mean and standard deviation are computed from the whole dataset including the test set, so the test score is optimistic. The split must come first and the scaler must be fitted on the training portion only. 2. The wrong scaler for the job, used to find outliers. Standardisation is computed from the mean and standard deviation, both of which the outliers themselves inflate — so this is the masking problem, and the |z| > 3 step will miss exactly the points it is meant to catch when there is more than one. A modified z-score or IQR rule belongs here. 3. Deleting without asking why. Everything beyond three standard deviations is removed automatically, which on a large dataset discards thousands of genuine observations, and on a right-skewed column discards one entire tail. This is Lesson 3's central warning, automated. A fourth, if you want it: the outlier removal is applied to the test set as well, so the reported performance is on an artificially clean sample that does not resemble what the model will meet in production. Problem 7 — choosing a method Name a detection method for each and say why. (a) One column, 40 values, roughly normal, you suspect one bad reading and need a p-value for a report. (b) 200,000 rows, 30 mixed columns, no distributional assumptions. (c) A fitted regression where one point seems to be bending the line. (d) Hourly energy demand where the suspect reading is high for 3 a.m. but normal for the day overall. Show worked solution (a) Grubbs' test. Designed for exactly one outlier in approximately normal data, and it returns a p-value. If you suspected two or three, generalised ESD instead — never Grubbs applied repeatedly. (b) Isolation Forest. Scales to that size, makes no distributional assumption, handles many columns, and needs almost no tuning. Local Outlier Factor if the data has clusters of clearly different density. (c) Cook's distance. It measures influence directly — how much the fitted values change when the point is dropped — which is the actual question. Check leverage and the standardised residual alongside it to see why : high leverage plus a moderate residual is the damaging combination. (d) Decompose first. Remove the daily and weekly seasonal pattern with STL or fit a forecasting model, then apply any univariate rule to the residuals. This is Lesson 2's contextual outlier, and the general recipe is always the same: model what the value should have been, then examine the error. Part C — self-test self-test answered Why is applying Grubbs' test repeatedly a bad way to find several outliers? Masking can hide the second one, and the significance level is not maintained across repeated tests. It is computationally expensive to recompute the mean and standard deviation each time. Grubbs is only valid for samples smaller than 25, so it fails after the first removal. self-test answered Which of these is not a reason to remove a genuine, correctly recorded outlier? The conditions that produced it cannot occur again in the period being modelled. It belongs to a distinct regime that deserves its own model. It sits far outside the whiskers and is pulling the model's fit around. self-test answered Which models need no feature scaling at all? k-means and ridge regression. Decision trees, random forests and gradient boosting. Neural networks, because the weights adapt to any scale during training. self-test answered You impute missing incomes with the column median, then cross-validate. What is wrong? The median is the wrong statistic for income; the mean would be better. The median was computed from the whole dataset, so information leaks into every fold. Imputing at all is wrong; rows with missing incomes should be dropped. Part D — written questions Four to write out 1. Define masking and swamping, and explain why they argue for median-based rules. Show a model answer Masking is when outliers inflate the estimate of spread so much that they fall back inside the threshold built from it — two extreme points hide each other, and one large one can hide a smaller one. Swamping is the reverse: an extreme point pulls the estimated centre toward itself, so ordinary points on the far side start to look distant and get flagged. Both arise because the mean and standard deviation are computed from the same data that contains the contamination, and both are unbounded — a single value can move them arbitrarily far. The median and the median absolute deviation are computed from the middle of the ordered data and cannot be moved by extreme values at all, so a rule built on them reports the distance of a suspect point honestly regardless of how many other suspects there are. 2. Explain why the 1.5 × IQR rule flags points in perfectly clean data, and what follows for large datasets. Show a model answer The fences sit at roughly ±2.7 standard deviations under normality, and about 0.7% of a normal distribution lies beyond them. The rule therefore has a fixed false-positive rate by construction: it is a convention about where to draw whiskers, not a test with a null hypothesis. In a sample of 5,000 clean measurements it flags around 35 points; in a million, around 7,000. It follows that in any large dataset most flagged points are ordinary, that the flag count carries no information about data quality, and that automatic deletion of everything outside the whiskers discards genuine observations at a predictable rate — worse on skewed data, where one whole tail is legitimately long. 3. A dataset has 15% missing values in one column. Describe how you would decide what to do. Show a model answer Start with why the values are missing, because the mechanism determines what is safe. If missingness is unrelated to anything (MCAR), deletion is unbiased but wasteful at 15%. If it depends on other observed columns (MAR), imputation using those columns — regression, k-NN, or multiple imputation — recovers an unbiased picture. If it depends on the unobserved value itself (MNAR), for example a sensor that fails when readings go out of range or high earners declining to state income, no imputation method can fix it, because every complete case you would learn from comes from the wrong part of the distribution. In that case the fact of missingness is itself information: add an indicator column and let the model use it. Whatever you choose, the imputation statistic must be estimated inside each cross-validation fold, not once on the whole dataset. 4. Why must scaling and imputation happen inside the cross-validation loop? Show a model answer Because both estimate parameters from data — a mean and standard deviation, a median — and those estimates are part of the modelling procedure rather than fixed facts about the world. Module 3 estimates generalisation performance by applying the whole procedure to data it has never seen; if the scaler's mean was computed from the full dataset, the held-out rows contributed to it and the score is optimistic. The fix is to refit every transformation on each fold's training portion and apply it unchanged to that fold's held-out portion, which is what a pipeline object automates. The bug is silent — nothing errors, the number is simply too good — which is why it is the most common leakage mistake in practice. Sources and further reading Source What it gives you NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods , §1.3.5.17 itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook The free, authoritative reference for Grubbs, Tietjen–Moore and the generalised ESD test, with worked examples and critical values. Start here for anything needing a p-value. Iglewicz & Hoaglin (1993), How to Detect and Handle Outliers The source of the modified z-score and the 3.5 cut-off, and still the most practical short treatment of the topic. Barnett & Lewis, Outliers in Statistical Data The comprehensive reference. More than most people need, and the place to look when a specific distribution is involved. Rousseeuw & Leroy, Robust Regression and Outlier Detection Where MCD, robust Mahalanobis and the influence measures are developed properly. Aggarwal, Outlier Analysis The modern machine-learning side: isolation forests, LOF, one-class methods, high-dimensional cases. Little & Rubin, Statistical Analysis with Missing Data The source of MCAR/MAR/MNAR and of multiple imputation. Kuhn & Johnson, Feature Engineering and Selection Free online. The best single treatment of scaling, transformation and encoding as part of a validated workflow rather than as isolated tricks. Tukey (1977), Exploratory Data Analysis . Where the box plot and the 1.5 × IQR convention come from — and where Tukey himself describes it as a screening device rather than a test. Rosner (1983), \"Percentage Points for a Generalized ESD Many-Outlier Procedure\". Liu, Ting & Zhou (2008), \"Isolation Forest\". Short and unusually readable for a method paper. Breunig et al. (2000), \"LOF: Identifying Density-Based Local Outliers\". Leys et al. (2013) on why the median and MAD should replace the mean and standard deviation in outlier rules — a good short argument to hand to a colleague. Tool Language For scikit-learn Python IsolationForest , LocalOutlierFactor , OneClassSVM , EllipticEnvelope ; StandardScaler , MinMaxScaler , RobustScaler , MaxAbsScaler , PowerTransformer (Box–Cox and Yeo–Johnson), QuantileTransformer ; SimpleImputer , KNNImputer , IterativeImputer . The Pipeline and ColumnTransformer objects are what keep all of it out of the leakage trap. PyOD Python Around fifty outlier-detection algorithms behind one interface, with benchmarks. The fastest way to compare several methods on the same data. statsmodels Python OLSInfluence for Cook's distance, leverage, DFFITS and DFBETAS; STL decomposition for the time-series case. outliers , EnvStats , robustbase , mice , recipes R Grubbs and Dixon; Rosner's ESD; MCD and robust regression; multiple imputation; and a pipeline framework that enforces the fit-on-training-only rule. A twenty-minute exercise worth doing Take any column of real data. Apply the box plot, the classical z-score, the modified z-score and an isolation forest, and list which points each one flags. Then add a single fabricated extreme value and rerun all four. Watching the z-score rule quietly stop working while the other three do not move is worth more than any amount of reading about robustness. Reading log click to type; saves automatically Caveat How much of this should you take on trust? Show answer The formulas and the worked arithmetic are standard and checkable. The threshold conventions — 1.5 for the fences, 3.5 for the modified z-score, 3 for the classical one — are conventions rather than derived quantities, and every one of them assumes roughly normal data. Recompute the false-alarm rate for your own distribution rather than inheriting these numbers, and treat the lectures as authoritative wherever the two differ."},{"id":"sec-10","h":"","t":"Written by an AI model from lecture recordings. Not reviewed by any instructor or institution — verify anything you rely on against the original lectures. Lesson 1 is not lecture content in the usual sense. Its source recording is a short preview that names three data-preparation problems and covers no method. The pipeline diagram, the worked comparison of scales, the pointers to where each problem is solved, and both activities in that lesson are orientation material written for these notes. What else goes beyond the transcripts. Every illustration has been replaced with a generic one, so none of the examples here are the ones used in class. The following are additions rather than lecture content: the 1.5 × IQR whisker convention and the twenty-value order-fulfilment dataset in Lesson 2; the perishable-medicine supply chain in Lesson 3; the Priya Raman scenario, which is invented entirely; all cross-references to Modules 2, 3 and 6; and the whole of “Things worth noticing”. One arithmetic figure has been corrected against the recording: the share of normally distributed data beyond three standard deviations is 0.27%, about 2,700 points per million rather than the rounder figure given in the lecture. All quiz feedback, margin questions and exam targets are written here rather than drawn from the recordings. Everything under “Additional resources” is outside material. Extras 1 to 3 — the detection toolbox, the masking and swamping demonstration, the scaling, missing-data, encoding and imbalance material, the leakage rule, the practice problems and the reading list — were assembled from published sources, principally the NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook, Iglewicz & Hoaglin's How to Detect and Handle Outliers , Little & Rubin on missing data, Kuhn & Johnson's Feature Engineering and Selection , the original method papers cited in Extra 3, and the scikit-learn documentation. None of it was taught in this module. All worked numbers there are computed for illustration on invented data. Threshold conventions quoted (1.5 for the fences, 3.5 for the modified z-score) assume approximately normal data; check them against a primary source before relying on them, and treat the lectures as authoritative wherever the two differ."}]},{"f":"module-6-change-detection.html","t":"Module 6: Change Detection","s":[{"id":"l1","h":"Change detection","t":"Lesson 1 Change detection When the unusual points stop being unusual, the question changes from Module 5’s “is this point wrong?” to “has the whole process moved?” — and the answer has to arrive early enough to act on. What it is Change detection Determining whether something has changed. Because change is the subject, the data is nearly always a time series, or at least carries some time attribute. The questions it answers fall into three families. Family Examples Do we need to do something? Does a machine showing variation in performance need maintenance, or is this just random variation? Has global temperature changed? Have sales of a product fallen because a new competitor arrived? Did the action we took have an effect? Did a policy change produce the intended result — did lowering business tax rates lead firms to hire more people? Did a discount promotion actually move sales? What has drifted over time? Have voting patterns shifted? Anything where the point is to understand the change in order to plan or analyse for the future. The first family is the demanding one, because acting late is expensive. The other two can usually be answered at leisure with all the data in hand. quick check answered Three change-detection questions. Which one is under genuine time pressure? Is this machine’s performance drifting, or is it just noise? Did last quarter’s discount move sales at all? Have voting patterns shifted over the last four cycles? quick check answered What actually separates the three families of change-detection question? What counts as a change — each family uses a different definition. When the answer is needed. Whether the data is a time series. Check Why is time-series data almost always involved? Show answer Because a change is a difference between before and after. Without a time attribute there is no before and after to compare, only a distribution of values. Detecting a problem that isn’t a problem yet One class of application is harder than it looks: you need to detect a change now that predicts trouble later, even though nothing is wrong at the moment. Traffic on a busy stretch of motorway is the standard illustration. Loop sensors in the road report vehicle counts and average speeds every few seconds. A jam does not appear from nowhere — density builds, speeds start to vary more, and gaps between vehicles shorten, for several minutes before flow actually collapses. By the time speed has dropped, the queue exists and the options for preventing it are gone. The useful question is therefore not “is traffic bad now?” but “is what I am seeing the beginning of a build-up that will be bad in five minutes?” Why this is a change-detection problem rather than a prediction problem You are not forecasting a number. You are asking whether the current stream of readings still comes from the same regime it did an hour ago. Once you accept that framing, the method has to work on a running basis, with the data that has arrived so far and no more. The noise problem, and why a hypothesis test isn’t enough Consider condition monitoring on a rotating machine — a large pump or motor, with a temperature probe on the bearing housing. Bearings usually fail gradually, and temperature rises before they seize. If the rise can be caught early enough, the machine can be stopped and the bearing replaced during planned downtime instead of failing in service and taking the line with it. In the ideal case the first high reading would be a reliable warning: the temperature is genuinely elevated, the machine comes out of service, and the failure is prevented. Real sensor readings do not behave like that. There is a great deal of random fluctuation between readings — ambient temperature, load, probe contact, plain measurement noise. Individual high readings appear routinely and mean nothing. The difficulty is symmetrical: react to every high reading and you stop a healthy machine repeatedly, but wait until you are really sure and the bearing may already have failed. Same alarm level in both panels. On the left the first reading to cross it is the fault. On the right three healthy readings cross it first, and the genuine rise only becomes unambiguous a reading or two before failure — by which time acting may be too late. A natural first attempt is a hypothesis test: has the mean temperature risen, at the usual level of significance? The trouble is timing. Waiting until a test meets a standard threshold means waiting for enough evidence to be confident, and with data this noisy that can take longer than the component has left. The test gives the right answer too late to be worth anything. The trade that defines the topic What is needed is a method that identifies potential problems sooner, on the explicit understanding that some of its alarms will be false. Accepting false positives is not a weakness of the approach — it is the price of an answer arriving in time. quick check answered Why is a standard hypothesis test a poor fit for the bearing-temperature problem? Because the sensor readings are too noisy for any statistical test to work on. Because reaching the usual significance threshold takes longer than the component survives. Because a hypothesis test can only compare two fixed groups, and this data arrives continuously. Class discussion — Lesson 1 click to type; saves automatically Exam target State the central trade-off of change detection in one sentence. Show answer Detecting a change sooner means accepting more false alarms, and demanding fewer false alarms means detecting real changes later — so the settings you choose depend on which of the two mistakes costs more."},{"id":"l2","h":"CUSUM","t":"Lesson 2 CUSUM CUSUM accumulates how far above expectation the data has been running, with C setting how much evidence is dismissed as noise and T setting how much is needed before you call it a change. The question it answers CUSUM — cumulative sum A running-total method that answers one question: has the mean of the observed distribution gone beyond a critical level? It can detect a move to a higher level, a move to a lower level, or both at once. Set up the notation. Let x t be the observed value at time t — a share price on day t , a bearing temperature at reading t , a reaction time on trial t . Let μ be the expected value of those observations if nothing has changed. Then x t − μ is how far above expectation the process ran at time t . S t = max { 0 , S t−1 + ( x t − μ − C ) } with S 0 = 0 declare a change when S t ≥ T Three moving parts. The running total carries evidence forward; C subtracts a little each period so ordinary noise doesn’t accumulate; the max with zero throws the total away whenever it would go negative. Why each piece is there The running total. A single high reading proves nothing; several in a row is evidence. Adding each period’s excess to the previous total is what turns a sequence of weak signals into a strong one. The reset at zero. If the total would go below zero, set it to zero instead. A run of lower-than-expected observations is irrelevant to the question of whether an increase happens later, and letting it bank negative credit would delay the eventual detection. This is what lets CUSUM react quickly. The constant C. Roughly half the time, an observation will exceed the expectation purely at random. Without something pulling the total down, that noise alone would eventually push S t past any threshold. C is the amount of excess dismissed as noise each period. How the two parameters behave The bigger C is, the harder it is for S t to get large, and the less sensitive the method. The smaller C is, the more sensitive, because S t grows faster. T works the same way in the other place: a high threshold means more evidence before an alarm, so later detection and fewer false ones. Neither has a formula — they are model parameters you fit to data. quick check answered Which sequence describes one period of the CUSUM calculation? Observe x t ; work out x t − μ; subtract C and add to S t−1 ; reset to zero if the result is negative; compare S t against T. Observe x t ; compare it against the threshold T; if it exceeds T, add x t − μ − C to the running total. Observe x t ; subtract C from the previous total S t−1 ; add x t ; reset to zero if the result exceeds T. quick check answered What would break if you dropped the “reset to zero when the total goes negative” step? The method could never raise an alarm at all, since S t would never reach T. A long quiet stretch would bank negative credit the method has to climb back out of before it could report an increase. The method would become more sensitive, raising alarms too readily. Exam target Write the CUSUM recursion for detecting an increase, from memory. Show answer S t = max{0, S t−1 + (x t − μ − C)}, starting from S 0 = 0, with a change declared as soon as S t ≥ T. Choosing C and T is a cost question There is no simple answer for what C and T should be. Part of the decision is statistical, and part of it is about which mistake hurts more. Take a chip manufacturing process where a machine has begun placing a component slightly too far to the left. Every chip produced between the moment the drift starts and the moment it is detected has to be scrapped, so late detection costs material and time. But if CUSUM calls a change when the machine is fine, production stops while a crew inspects and adjusts equipment that was working. Setting C and T is trading those two costs against each other. If you… Then… Lower C or lower T Changes are detected sooner, and false alarms become more frequent. Right when late detection is the expensive mistake. Raise C or raise T False alarms become rarer, and real changes take longer to surface. Right when interrupting a healthy process is the expensive mistake. A worked example Reaction times are measured on the same participant over 18 trials, in milliseconds, to test whether reaction time slows after a while. The expected value with no change is μ = 300 . To check that the method works, 100 ms has been added artificially to every observation from t = 11 onward — so the correct answer is known, and the only question is how long CUSUM takes to notice. t x t x t − μ S t with C = 0 S t with C = 15 1 318 +18 18 3 2 325 +25 43 13 3 291 −9 34 0 4 322 +22 56 7 5 331 +31 87 23 6 286 −14 73 0 7 327 +27 100 12 8 319 +19 119 16 9 266 −34 85 0 10 278 −22 63 0 11 405 +105 168 90 12 389 +89 257 164 13 414 +114 371 263 14 394 +94 465 342 Read three different methods off the same table: C = 0, T = 450 — the alarm fires at t = 14, four periods after the change. Slow, but the highest the total ever reached before the change was 119, so a false alarm was never remotely close. C = 0, T = 150 — the alarm fires at t = 11, the very first period after the change. Much faster, but the total had already reached 119 at t = 8 while nothing was wrong: 79% of the way to the threshold on noise alone. C = 15, T = 150 — the alarm fires at t = 12, one period later than the previous setting, and the total never exceeded 23 before the change. Almost all of the speed, with the near-miss removed. Four regions are clickable. Open all four to finish this activity. This kind of picture is called a control chart: plot S t over time, draw the threshold, and a change is declared wherever the line crosses it. Both series use the same 14 observations from the table; only C differs. Check Both the C = 0 and C = 15 settings detect the change against T = 150. Why prefer the second? Show answer It costs one extra period of delay and removes almost all the false-alarm risk: the pre-change total peaks at 23 rather than 119. Whether that trade is right depends on the costs, but here it is cheap. Detecting decreases, and both at once Nothing about the method is specific to increases. Flip the sign of the deviation inside the sum and the same machinery looks for a drop: increase (upper CUSUM): S + t = max { 0 , S + t−1 + ( x t − μ − C ) } decrease (lower CUSUM): S − t = max { 0 , S − t−1 + ( μ − x t − C ) } Both start at zero. Both raise the alarm on the same test, S t ≥ T. To watch both directions, run the two recursions side by side on the same observations and alarm if either reaches its threshold. What actually differs between the two equations Almost nothing — and the parts that look like they might differ do not. Going through them one at a time is the fastest way to stop making sign errors. Piece Increase Decrease Why it is that way The deviation x t − μ μ − x t This is the only real difference. Each version measures distance in the direction that counts as evidence. A reading of 460 against μ = 500 contributes −40 to the increase sum and +40 to the decrease sum. The allowance C subtracted subtracted C is not a direction, it is a handicap. It always works against accumulation, so it is subtracted in both. Adding it in the decrease version is the single most common error — see below. The floor at zero max{0, …} max{0, …} Identical, and it means the same thing in both: evidence pointing the wrong way is thrown away rather than banked. The alarm test S + t ≥ T S − t ≥ T Both statistics are non-negative by construction, so both are compared against a positive threshold. There is no −T anywhere. What a large value means the process ran high the process ran low S − going up means the process went down . The statistic and the thing it measures move in opposite directions, which is why the notation trips people up. An equivalent way to write the second one, which some texts prefer because it makes the symmetry obvious: S − t = max { 0 , S − t−1 − ( x t − μ ) − C } Same algebra, rearranged. Both forms compute the same number; pick whichever you are less likely to mistype under exam pressure. Three mistakes that account for most wrong answers 1. Writing μ − x t + C. Adding C makes every period contribute more , so the total climbs on ordinary noise and alarms when nothing has happened. On the sales data below this version fires at week 8 — three weeks before the change exists. 2. Expecting S − to go negative and comparing it against −T. It cannot go negative; the max with zero forbids it. A downward change makes S − climb toward a positive T. 3. Running only the increase recursion on a process that fell. S + sits at zero forever and reports \"no change\" while the process quietly collapses. The direction you monitor is a design decision, not something the method works out for you. quick check answered A colleague monitoring for a drop writes S t = max{0, S t−1 + (μ − x t + C)}. What happens? It detects real decreases more slowly, because C now opposes the accumulation twice. Every period gets a free push of size C, so the total climbs even on a healthy process and eventually raises a false alarm. Nothing — it is algebraically equivalent to the correct form once the max with zero is applied. A worked example with both recursions running Weekly unit sales at a store. With no change the expected level is μ = 500 , and the scheme is set at C = 12 , T = 100 . A competitor opens at the start of week 11. Both recursions are computed on every observation, so the method is watching for a move in either direction without knowing in advance which one to expect. t x t x t − μ S + t (increase) S − t (decrease) 1 512 +12 0 0 2 487 −13 0 1 3 503 +3 0 0 4 521 +21 9 0 5 478 −22 0 10 6 495 −5 0 3 7 508 +8 0 0 8 469 −31 0 19 9 516 +16 4 0 10 491 −9 0 0 11 436 −64 0 52 12 451 −49 0 89 13 425 −75 0 152 14 443 −57 0 197 15 418 −82 0 267 16 439 −61 0 316 17 430 −70 0 374 18 447 −53 0 415 Work one row by hand to check you have it. At t = 5, the observation is 478, so μ − x t = 22; subtract C to get 10; add the previous S − of 0; the result is positive, so S − 5 = 10. The increase side gets x t − μ = −22, minus 12 is −34, plus 0 is negative, so S + 5 = 0. Three things to read off the table: The alarm fires at t = 13 , two weeks after the competitor opened. S − reaches 152, clearing T = 100. S + never exceeds 9 , and is zero for the whole second half. The increase side is not broken — it is correctly reporting that no increase occurred. Running both directions costs you nothing in accuracy here. Before the change, S − peaked at 19 , under a fifth of the threshold. There was never a near-miss. Rerunning the same data with different settings shows what each piece of the equation is buying: Version First alarm Highest value before the change C = 12 — correct form t = 13 19 — never close to T C = 0 — no allowance t = 11, immediately 50 — half way to T on noise alone μ − x t + C — sign error on C t = 8 (false) 140 — past T before the change exists The middle row is the familiar trade: drop C and you detect two weeks earlier but hand back most of your margin against false alarms. The bottom row is not a trade at all — it is a broken method that would alarm on any process left running long enough. Two statistics, one data set. The decrease side carries all the evidence; the increase side correctly reports nothing. Note that S − rises as sales fall — the statistic moves opposite to the quantity it is monitoring. Running both sides is not free Each period now gives the scheme two chances to alarm instead of one, so at the same T a two-sided scheme raises roughly twice as many false alarms as a one-sided one. If the false-alarm rate is what you were tuning, raise T a little when you add the second side. The two sides do not have to be symmetric either — if an increase is dangerous and a decrease is merely interesting, give them different C and T. Which to run is a decision about consequences, not about statistics: Situation Run Bearing temperature, contamination level, server latency, defect rate — only one direction is a problem One-sided. Monitoring the harmless direction buys nothing but false alarms. A machined dimension, a chemical concentration, sales, a calibration reading — either direction signals something worth knowing Two-sided. Both recursions, same data, alarm on either. One practical detail that is easy to forget: once the alarm fires and the cause has been dealt with, reset the statistic to zero. Otherwise it stays above T forever and alarms every period from then on. quick check answered In the sales example, S − reaches 415 by week 18 while S + sits at 0. What does the pair of numbers tell you? Sales have fallen to roughly 415 units a week. The process has moved down and has not moved up — and the evidence for the drop is now four times the threshold. The increase recursion has failed and needs its floor removed so it can go negative. Exam target Write both recursions from memory and say what changes between them. Show answer S + t = max{0, S + t−1 + (x t − μ − C)} and S − t = max{0, S − t−1 + (μ − x t − C)}, both from S 0 = 0, both alarming at S t ≥ T. The only change is the sign of the deviation: x t − μ becomes μ − x t . C is subtracted in both, the floor at zero applies to both, and both compare against the same positive threshold. The same equations in industrial notation Quality-control software, textbooks and published tuning tables all write CUSUM with different letters. It is worth being able to read them, because that is where the ready-made parameter values live. C + i = max { 0 , C + i−1 + ( x i − μ 0 ) − K } C − i = max { 0 , C − i−1 − ( x i − μ 0 ) − K } signal when C + i > H or C − i > H Identical to the two recursions above. μ 0 is μ, K is C, H is T. This is the \"tabular CUSUM\"; the older graphical \"V-mask\" version is the same decision drawn as a sideways V laid over the plot. Course notation Industrial notation Usual name μ μ 0 target, or in-control mean C K = k·σ reference value, allowance, or slack T H = h·σ decision interval The useful part is the σ . Expressing the two dials as multiples of the process standard deviation makes them portable: k and h mean the same thing on a temperature probe and on a share price, so published tables of good settings apply to both. The standard starting points: k = δ/2 — half the shift, measured in standard deviations, that you most want to catch quickly. Aiming at a 1σ shift gives k = 0.5, which is the default in most software. This is not a rule of thumb: k = δ/2 is the value that makes each period's contribution the log-likelihood ratio between \"unchanged\" and \"shifted by δ\", which is what makes CUSUM the fastest detector for that particular shift size. h = 4 or 5 — with k = 0.5, h = 4 gives about 168 periods between false alarms and h = 5 about 465. Larger h, rarer false alarms, later detection: the same trade as always, now in numbers you can look up rather than guess. Fast initial response — start the statistic at H/2 instead of 0 if the process might already be out of control when monitoring begins. Detection is much faster at startup, at the cost of some extra early false alarms. The cost of that precision is narrowness: k is tuned to one shift size, and performance falls away for shifts much larger or smaller than δ. If you have no particular δ in mind, EWMA (in the extra material below) is the more forgiving choice. The two numbers that price the two mistakes Average run length (ARL) The average number of periods a scheme runs before it raises an alarm. Quoted twice: ARL 0 , measured while the process is healthy, is the average gap between false alarms and should be large . ARL 1 , measured after a change of a stated size, is the average detection delay and should be small . This is Lesson 1's trade-off turned into two numbers you can actually compute and compare. It also makes the case for CUSUM concrete. Take a plain Shewhart chart, which alarms whenever a single observation falls outside ±3σ: false alarms arrive every 370 periods on average, which sounds excellent, but a sustained 1σ shift takes about 44 periods to catch because a 1σ shift only pushes about 2% of readings past the limit. A CUSUM with k = 0.5 and h = 4 catches the same 1σ shift in about 8 periods. Scheme ARL 0 — periods between false alarms ARL 1 at a 1σ shift Shewhart chart, ±3σ limits ≈ 370 ≈ 44 CUSUM, k = 0.5, h = 4 ≈ 168 ≈ 8 CUSUM, k = 0.5, h = 5 ≈ 465 ≈ 11 Read the last row against the first: CUSUM at h = 5 raises fewer false alarms than the Shewhart chart and still detects the shift four times faster. Accumulating evidence is not a compromise here, it is a straight improvement — for small shifts. For a large sudden jump the ordering reverses, because a 4σ jump puts the very first observation outside the Shewhart limits while CUSUM still needs a period or two to accumulate. That is the whole reason both charts are still in use, and why some schemes run both. What the equations quietly assume CUSUM as written assumes observations are independent and identically distributed around μ when nothing has changed, with a stable spread. Three consequences worth knowing: autocorrelation inflates false alarms badly , because runs of similar values look like accumulating evidence; trend or seasonality must be modelled first , with CUSUM then run on the residuals rather than the raw series; and μ and σ have to be estimated from a clean stretch of data — a \"Phase I\" period known to be in control. Estimating μ from data that already contains the change is a good way to detect nothing. quick check answered A process jumps by 4σ in one step. Which scheme notices first, and why? Shewhart, because a jump that size puts a single observation outside the limits immediately. CUSUM, because accumulating evidence is always faster than judging one point. Neither — for a shift that large the two schemes are mathematically equivalent. Check Why quote ARL 0 and ARL 1 rather than a single accuracy number? Show answer Because the two mistakes have different costs and no single number can express the trade between them. ARL 0 prices crying wolf, ARL 1 prices reacting late, and tuning C and T just slides you along the curve between them. Which point on the curve is right comes from the costs, not from the data. What to take from this lesson CUSUM is one method among several, and the course does not attempt to cover the rest. What generalises is the underlying idea — accumulate evidence over time rather than judging each observation alone — and the trade-off between early detection and false detection, which every change-detection method has to make somewhere. If you want to know what the other methods are, the additional resources below map them out; none of that is class material. Class discussion — Lesson 2 click to type; saves automatically Cross-module link How would you actually choose C and T for a real process? Show answer The same way as any other model parameter: try candidate values, measure how each performs on data where you know the answer, and pick using validation rather than intuition — Module 3’s material. The extra ingredient here is that “performs well” has two components, detection delay and false-alarm rate, whose relative weight comes from the costs rather than from the data."},{"id":"targets","h":"Transfer practice — Change detection","t":"Transfer practice — Change detection Same ideas, unfamiliar settings Why these are here Each prompt below moves the module’s ideas into a situation the lessons never mentioned. If you can only answer a question in the wording it was taught in, what you have memorised is the example rather than the concept — and an exam that changes the setting will find that out. Answer these out loud before opening them. Exam target Server response time is monitored. A false alarm costs a wasted engineer-hour; a missed slowdown costs customers. Which way do you move C and T? Show answer Down, both. Lower values detect real changes sooner at the price of more frequent false alarms, which is the correct trade when late detection is the expensive mistake. The decision is a cost comparison, not a statistical one — the parameters encode which error the organisation would rather make. Exam target Your CUSUM never alarms, even across a period when the process is known to have shifted. Give two candidate causes. Show answer Either the allowance C is large enough to absorb the size of the shift, so each period contributes nothing to the running total, or the threshold T is set higher than the accumulated evidence ever reaches. A third possibility worth checking: μ was estimated from data that already included the shifted period, so the shift is built into the baseline. Exam target Why does the max{0, ·} matter? Predict the behaviour if it were removed. Show answer It discards the running total whenever evidence turns negative, so ordinary variation cannot accumulate a head start toward the threshold. Without it the statistic would drift in both directions and a stretch of high readings before the real change would leave the total already part-way to the threshold — or, after a stretch of low readings, deep in negative territory and slow to respond to a genuine rise. Exam target A colleague proposes setting C to zero and compensating with a much larger T. What behaviour do you predict? Show answer The running total drifts upward on ordinary variation alone, since nothing subtracts from it, so it can sit close to the threshold while nothing is wrong. Detection time also becomes erratic, depending on where the total happened to be when the change began. The two parameters do different jobs: C filters noise period by period, T weighs accumulated evidence. Exam target You need to catch both a slowdown and a speed-up. What do you run? Show answer Both recursions in parallel — one accumulating (x − μ − C) for increases and one accumulating (μ − x − C) for decreases — each against its own threshold. A single one-sided statistic is blind in the other direction by construction. Exam target What would updating μ from recent observations as they arrive do to the scheme? Show answer It would absorb the shift. A baseline that tracks the data gradually redefines the new behaviour as normal, and the deviations that should be accumulating shrink toward zero. The fixed mean is what the whole method is measuring against; letting it move removes the reference point."},{"id":"sec-4","h":"How the module fits together","t":"How the module fits together The through-line across the two lessons Lesson 1 Poses the question, and adds the constraint that makes it hard: the answer has to arrive in time to act on. Lesson 2 Gives one concrete method for meeting that deadline, with two dials that price the two mistakes. Things worth noticing This is Module 5’s question at a different time scale. An outlier is one point that doesn’t fit its context; a change is every subsequent point failing to fit the old context. Module 5’s contextual outlier and this module’s level shift differ mainly in how many points are involved. CUSUM is the residual trick with a memory. Module 5 found outliers by fitting a model and looking at where the error was large. CUSUM does the same thing — μ is the model, x t − μ is the residual — except that it accumulates residuals instead of judging each one alone. That is the entire difference, and it is what buys early detection. The two recursions are one recursion. Increase and decrease differ only in the sign of the deviation term — C is subtracted in both, the floor at zero applies to both, both compare against the same positive threshold. Almost every error people make here comes from assuming more differs than actually does, usually by flipping the sign of C as well or expecting the lower statistic to go negative. The reset at zero is the whole idea, compressed. Evidence for an increase is worth keeping; evidence against one is thrown away, because the question is only ever “has it gone up?”. That asymmetry is what makes CUSUM fast, and it is also why C has to exist. Neither lesson tells you how to set anything. C, T and μ are all left to you. Module 3’s validation material is the missing piece, with one twist: “performs well” here has two components — detection delay and false-alarm rate — whose relative weight comes from costs rather than from data. Statistical correctness is not the goal. Lesson 1 rejects hypothesis testing even though it gives the right answer. That is unusual enough to be worth remembering: a method can be correct and still useless, if the deadline is part of the problem. Likely exam targets Give three kinds of question change detection answers, with an example of each. Explain why time-series data is almost always involved. Explain why hypothesis testing can be the wrong tool even when it is statistically correct. State the central trade-off between early detection and false detection. Write the CUSUM recursion for an increase and for a decrease, and say what each symbol does. State precisely what differs between the two recursions — and what does not. Compute four or five periods of either recursion by hand from a short table of observations, and say whether an alarm fires. Explain why C is subtracted in both directions rather than added in one of them, and what goes wrong if it is not. Explain why the running total resets to zero rather than going negative. Describe the effect on detection delay and on false alarms of raising C, and of raising T. Say what it means when the decrease statistic is large and the increase statistic is zero. Sketch a control chart and mark where a change would be declared. Everything above is class material. Additional resources follow — the other detection methods, the industrial k and h notation, ARL, practice problems and reading — none of it taught in this module. Module 6 — my own summary click to type; saves automatically"},{"id":"extra","h":"Additional resources","t":"Additional resources Everything below this line is not class material Read this first The two lessons above are the module as taught: change detection as a question, and CUSUM as one method for answering it. Everything from here on was assembled from outside sources — textbooks, the NIST engineering statistics handbook, method papers and the documentation of the standard software libraries. None of it is examinable unless your instructor says so, and where a convention differs from the lectures, the lectures win . It is here for three reasons. The lectures state that CUSUM is one method among several without covering the rest, so the first section is the map of what those others are and what each is for. The second section is how to choose between them and how to judge whether a detector is any good. The third is extra practice, because the only reliable way to find out whether you can apply CUSUM is to apply it. The last is where to read further. Extra 1 The full map of change-detection methods, grouped by the question each family answers. Extra 2 Choosing between them, and the metrics used to compare detectors. Extra 3 Practice problems with worked solutions, and a longer self-test. Extra 4 Sources, further reading and the software you would actually use."},{"id":"x1","h":"The full map of methods","t":"Extra 1 · not class material The full map of methods Every method here accumulates or compares evidence about whether the data-generating process has moved. They differ in when you need the answer, what kind of change you are looking for, and how much you are willing to assume. The one distinction that organises everything Before the taxonomy, the split that decides which half of the literature applies to you — and it is Lesson 1's three families of question, in disguise. Online / sequential Offline / retrospective The question \"Has it changed by now ?\" Asked again after every new observation. \"Where, in this completed series, did it change?\" Data available Everything up to t , nothing after. All of it, at once. Output A stopping time — the period at which you declare a change. A set of change-point locations, possibly several. Judged by Detection delay against false-alarm rate. How close the estimated locations are to the true ones. Maps to Lesson 1's \"Do we need to do something?\" \"Did our action have an effect?\" and \"What has drifted?\" Examples CUSUM, Shewhart, EWMA, Page–Hinkley, Shiryaev–Roberts, ADWIN Binary segmentation, PELT, Bai–Perron, BOCPD CUSUM sits squarely in the online column, which is exactly why the lectures introduce it right after arguing that the answer has to arrive in time to act. Family 1 — Sequential monitoring (control charts) All three of the classical charts monitor a stream and signal when a statistic crosses a limit. What separates them is how much memory the statistic has. Method Statistic Memory Best at Shewhart chart Shewhart, 1925/1931 The observation itself, against μ ± 3σ None — each point judged alone Large sudden shifts, roughly 2σ and up. Fastest possible response to a big jump; nearly blind to small sustained drift. CUSUM Page, 1954 Running total of deviations, discounted by C and floored at zero Full, but reset to zero whenever evidence runs out Small sustained shifts of a size you can name in advance. Optimal for that size; performance drops off either side of it. EWMA Roberts, 1959 z t = λx t + (1 − λ)z t−1 , against limits that widen then settle Full, geometrically fading Small shifts, without committing to one size. More forgiving than CUSUM across a range of shifts, slightly slower at any one of them. EWMA: z t = λ x t + ( 1 − λ ) z t−1 , z 0 = μ limits: μ ± L σ √( λ / (2 − λ) · [ 1 − (1 − λ) 2t ] ) λ between 0.05 and 0.25, with 0.2 the common default; L is usually 3. Small λ means a long memory and sensitivity to tiny shifts; large λ approaches a Shewhart chart. λ is EWMA's version of C — the single dial that trades speed against false alarms. The comparison is easiest to see on one data set. Below, thirty observations from a standard normal, with a 1σ shift introduced at t = 16: Same thirty observations in all three panels; the dashed vertical line marks the 1σ shift at t = 16. The largest single observation in the whole series is 2.28σ, so the Shewhart chart never signals. Both memory-based charts signal at t = 18 — two observations after the change. This is the argument for accumulation, in one picture. Two more members of this family worth knowing by name: Page–Hinkley test. A CUSUM variant that tracks the gap between the running mean and the current observation and signals when the cumulative deviation exceeds a threshold. Essentially the same idea with the baseline estimated on the fly rather than fixed — which is why it is the one that reappears in machine-learning drift libraries. Shiryaev–Roberts procedure. Sums likelihood ratios over all possible change points instead of taking the maximum, giving a Bayesian-flavoured alternative to CUSUM with comparable performance and sometimes better properties for detecting a change that happened long ago. Combined Shewhart–CUSUM. Run both, alarm on either. Catches large jumps immediately and small drift eventually, at the cost of a somewhat higher false-alarm rate. Widely used where both failure modes are real. quick check answered What distinguishes a Shewhart chart from CUSUM and EWMA, in one word? Memory — the Shewhart statistic has none, the other two do. Assumptions — only Shewhart requires the data to be normally distributed. Timing — Shewhart is retrospective while CUSUM and EWMA run online. Link to Lesson 2 Where does the course's CUSUM sit in this family? Show answer It is the tabular CUSUM with C in raw units rather than in multiples of σ, run one-sided in the worked example and two-sided when both recursions are used. Everything in Lesson 2 transfers directly; the industrial notation just renames μ, C and T as μ 0 , K and H. Family 2 — Offline segmentation: finding change points after the fact When the series is complete and you want to know where the breaks were — possibly several of them — the problem becomes an optimisation: partition the series so that the data within each segment fits a simple model well, without inventing so many breaks that you are just fitting noise. Every method below is a different way of searching that space. Method How it searches Trade-off Binary segmentation Scott & Knott, 1974 Find the single best split, then recurse into each half. Fast, roughly n log n , and easy to implement. Greedy, so not guaranteed to find the best partition — it can miss change points that are close together. Segment neighbourhood Auger & Lawrence, 1989 Search the whole solution space by dynamic programming. Exact, but expensive enough to be impractical on long series. Dynamic programming (Dynp) Exact minimisation when you already know how many change points there are. Optimal for a fixed number of breaks. You have to supply that number. PELT Killick et al., 2012 Dynamic programming plus pruning of paths that provably cannot be optimal, with a penalty (often BIC) controlling how many breaks are worth having. Exact and roughly linear time when change points are spread through the series. The current default for offline work; its assumption is that breaks grow in number with the length of the series rather than clustering in one stretch. Wild binary segmentation Fryzlewicz, 2014 Run the search on many randomly drawn sub-intervals and aggregate. Much better than plain binary segmentation at multiple or closely spaced change points, at extra computational cost. Bottom-up / window sliding Start over-segmented and merge, or slide a window and score the discrepancy either side of it. Cheap and intuitive; approximate. Window methods are a natural bridge between offline and online. The search method is not the interesting choice — the cost function is All of these algorithms take a cost function that says how badly a stretch of data fits a single regime, and that is what decides what kind of change you can find . An L2 cost detects shifts in the mean. A normal cost detects shifts in mean or variance. A linear cost detects changes in a regression relationship between variables. An autoregressive cost detects changes in the dynamics. A kernel or RBF cost detects a change in the whole distribution, without assuming its shape. Choosing PELT over binary segmentation changes how thoroughly you search; choosing the cost changes what you are searching for. Family 3 — Bayesian change point detection Bayesian online change point detection (BOCPD, Adams & MacKay 2007) maintains a posterior distribution over the run length — how long it has been since the last change — and updates it with every new observation. Instead of a binary alarm it gives you a probability that a change has just occurred, which is exactly what you want when the decision downstream is itself probabilistic, when the sample is small, or when you need to express uncertainty about where the change was rather than just that there was one. The cost is that you must specify prior beliefs about how often changes happen and what the data looks like within a regime. Family 4 — Structural break tests in fitted models Econometrics asks a narrower version of the question: not \"did the series change?\" but \"did the relationship I fitted change?\" — did the coefficients of a regression shift partway through the sample. This is the natural tool for Lesson 1's second family, \"did the action we took have an effect\". Test Break date What it does Chow test Chow, 1960 Known in advance An F-test of whether the regression coefficients before and after a stated date are equal. The baseline everything else generalises. Quandt–Andrews / supF Quandt 1960, Andrews 1993 Unknown Compute the Chow statistic at every candidate date and take the maximum. The date of the maximum is the likely break; the distribution of that maximum is non-standard, which is what Andrews supplied. Andrews–Ploberger 1994 Unknown Mean and exponential averages of the Wald statistics rather than the maximum — more powerful against some alternatives. CUSUM of recursive residuals Brown, Durbin & Evans, 1975 Unknown, and not estimated Accumulate one-step-ahead forecast residuals and see whether the total wanders outside a confidence band. Flags parameter instability without committing to a date. Note the name collision : same accumulate-residuals idea as Lesson 2, different statistic and a different purpose. CUSUM-of-squares is the companion test for changes in variance. Bai–Perron 1998, 2003 Unknown, multiple Estimates how many breaks there are and where, simultaneously, by minimising total residual sum of squares with sequential supF tests or an information criterion deciding the count. The workhorse for multi-regime problems. Family 5 — Non-parametric and distribution-change methods Everything so far assumes you can name the distribution, or at least that the change shows up in the mean. When neither holds — heavy tails, high-dimensional data, a change in shape rather than location — these apply instead. Two-sample Kolmogorov–Smirnov. Compare the empirical distributions of two windows. Simple, assumption-light, and the basis of the KSWIN streaming detector. Kernel methods / maximum mean discrepancy. Map the data into a high-dimensional space through a kernel and look for a mean shift there, which corresponds to a change in the full distribution. Kernel CUSUM and kernel change-point detection are the sequential and offline versions. Rank and energy statistics. Work on the ordering of the data rather than its values, so they are robust to outliers and need no distributional assumption at all. Family 6 — Drift detection in machine-learning systems The version of this problem you are most likely to meet in practice: a deployed model that was accurate at launch and is quietly getting worse. Two things can be moving, and the distinction matters because it changes what you monitor. Term What moved Detectable from Data drift The distribution of the inputs The features alone — no labels needed. Concept drift The relationship between inputs and outcome Prediction errors, so you need labels, and often they arrive late or never. Detector Monitors Idea DDM Gama et al., 2004 Error rate Track the classifier's error rate and its standard deviation; warn, then alarm, when they rise past thresholds. Assumes errors are binomial. EDDM Distance between errors Watches the gap between consecutive errors instead of the rate. Better on gradual drift, weaker on sudden drift. ADWIN Bifet & Gavaldà, 2007 Any stream of values Keeps a window that grows while the data is stable, and whenever two sub-windows differ in mean by more than a bound, drops the older part and declares drift. The window size is the estimate of how long the current regime has lasted. KSWIN Any stream of values Kolmogorov–Smirnov test between a recent window and a reference window. Distribution-free. Page–Hinkley Any stream of values CUSUM, essentially. The direct descendant of the method in Lesson 2. HDDM , RDDM , FHDDM Error rate Refinements of DDM using Hoeffding or McDiarmid bounds, or pruning, for better sensitivity and tighter guarantees. These all inherit Lesson 1's trade-off exactly. Error-rate detectors like DDM and EDDM are cheap in memory because they hold a few summary numbers; window-based detectors like ADWIN and KSWIN hold data and cost more, but detect changes the summary methods miss. And all of them are sensitive to their threshold parameters in the same way C and T are. Family 7 — Model-based monitoring Kalman filter innovations. If you already have a state-space model of the process, its one-step-ahead prediction errors should be white noise with known variance. Running CUSUM or a Shewhart chart on those innovations detects changes in a system that is supposed to be moving — which the raw-value charts cannot handle. Generalised likelihood ratio (GLR). CUSUM needs you to name the post-change parameter. GLR estimates it from the data instead, maximising the likelihood ratio over both the change point and the size of the change. More flexible, considerably more computation, and the standard answer when you cannot specify δ. Two more that answer a different question entirely Interrupted time series and difference-in-differences are worth knowing because they are the correct tools for Lesson 1's second family. There you already know when the intervention happened, and the question is whether the change that followed can be attributed to it rather than to whatever else was going on. Detection is trivial; attribution is the hard part. Reaching for CUSUM there answers a question nobody asked. quick check answered You have five years of completed monthly data and want to know how many regime changes it contains and roughly when. Which family? A control chart — CUSUM run through the series from the start. Offline segmentation — PELT or binary segmentation with a penalty. A drift detector such as ADWIN or DDM. Methods I want to look into further click to type; saves automatically Worth noticing What do all seven families have in common? Show answer Each one compares the evidence for \"same regime\" against the evidence for \"different regime\", and each one needs a threshold to turn that comparison into a decision. The threshold is never supplied by the data — it comes from the relative cost of detecting late and detecting falsely. That is Lesson 1's trade-off, and it survives every change of method."},{"id":"x2","h":"Choosing and comparing","t":"Extra 2 · not class material Choosing and comparing Four questions narrow the field to one or two candidates, and two numbers tell you whether the one you picked is tuned properly. Four questions that pick the method When do you need the answer? Immediately, as data arrives → a sequential method: Shewhart, CUSUM, EWMA, or a drift detector. After the fact, with everything in hand → offline segmentation or a structural break test. This question alone removes about half the options. What is changing? The mean is the easy case and what most methods assume. A change in variance needs a normal cost, CUSUM-of-squares, or a chart built for dispersion. A change in the relationship between variables needs a structural break test or a linear cost. A change in distribution shape with the mean unmoved needs a kernel or rank method. Do you know how big a shift you care about? Yes → CUSUM with k = δ/2, which is optimal for that δ. No → EWMA, which spreads its sensitivity across shift sizes, or GLR, which estimates the shift from the data. How much can you assume? A known distribution and independent observations allow the parametric methods and their published ARL tables. Autocorrelation, heavy tails or high dimensions push you toward non-parametric and kernel methods, or toward modelling the structure first and monitoring the residuals. A summary table If you need to… Use Because Catch a small sustained shift of known size, fast, on a live stream CUSUM Optimal for the shift size you target; roughly five times faster than a Shewhart chart at 1σ. Catch a large sudden jump on a live stream Shewhart A single observation is already conclusive; accumulating only adds delay. Catch small shifts without naming a size EWMA One dial, λ, spreads sensitivity across a range of shift sizes. Catch either, on the same process Combined Shewhart–CUSUM Run both, alarm on either; accept the higher combined false-alarm rate. Find several change points in a completed series PELT Exact, near-linear time, and works out the number of breaks from a penalty. Find one break in a fitted regression, date unknown Quandt–Andrews (supF) Maximum Chow statistic over all candidate dates, with the right critical values. Find several breaks in a fitted regression Bai–Perron Estimates count and locations jointly rather than sequentially by hand. Express uncertainty about whether and where a change occurred BOCPD Returns a posterior over run length instead of a binary alarm. Monitor a deployed model with labels arriving DDM / EDDM / ADWIN Built to watch an error stream and trigger retraining. Monitor a deployed model with no labels KSWIN or a distribution distance on the features Detects data drift, which is all you can see without outcomes. Monitor a process that is supposed to be moving CUSUM on model residuals Fit the expected behaviour first; the residuals are what should be stationary. quick check answered A sensor reading has strong daily seasonality. You run CUSUM on the raw values against a single μ. What happens? It alarms on every seasonal peak, because a predictable run above μ looks exactly like a change. Nothing — the reset at zero cancels the seasonal pattern out over a full cycle. It detects real changes more slowly, because the seasonal variation inflates the estimate of σ. How to tell whether a detector is any good Lesson 2 leaves C and T to you and points at Module 3's validation material. This is what \"performs well\" actually means once you get there — and it is never a single accuracy number, because there are two mistakes with different prices. Metric Measures Want ARL 0 — in-control average run length Average number of periods between false alarms on a healthy process Large. 370 and 500 are conventional targets, chosen to match a Shewhart chart. ARL 1 — out-of-control ARL Average number of periods from a change of stated size to the alarm Small. Always quoted with the shift size, since it is meaningless without one. EDD — expected detection delay The same idea stated as a delay; the standard metric in the sequential-detection literature Small. Usually plotted against log ARL 0 , which is close to a straight line for CUSUM. False alarm rate Alarms per period on a healthy process — roughly 1/ARL 0 Small. The more intuitive form when you are explaining the cost to someone. Detection rate / F1 For offline work: how many true change points were found, against how many were invented High. Needs a tolerance window, since \"found at t = 41 when the truth was 40\" should usually count as a hit. Annotation error, Hausdorff distance For segmentation: difference in the number of change points, and the worst location error Small. The standard pair for comparing segmentations. Why ARL 0 alone is misleading An average of 370 periods between false alarms does not mean a false alarm arrives on schedule every 370 periods. Run lengths are roughly geometric, so the distribution is heavily skewed: with ARL 0 = 370 you will see gaps of 20 periods and gaps of 1,200, and roughly a quarter of the intervals will be shorter than 110. Quote the ARL, but do not promise it as a rhythm. How to actually pick C and T Lesson 2's answer is Module 3's answer, and it holds. Concretely: Get a clean stretch. Find a period where the process was demonstrably in control and estimate μ and σ from it. Everything downstream depends on this being uncontaminated. Name the shift that matters. The smallest change you would actually act on, in σ units. That is δ, and k = δ/2 follows. Pick h from what a false alarm costs. Work out how often you can tolerate stopping a healthy process, convert it to a target ARL 0 , and read h off a table — h = 4 and h = 5 with k = 0.5 give roughly 168 and 465. Simulate. With μ and σ estimated, generate healthy data, inject a shift of size δ at a known point, and measure the delay and the false-alarm rate you actually get. Repeat over a grid of (k, h). This is the validation step, and it costs almost nothing because the data is synthetic. Backtest on real history. Run the chosen settings over past data containing known incidents. Count what you would have caught, how late, and how often you would have stopped production for nothing. Re-estimate periodically. μ and σ drift for legitimate reasons — new equipment, new suppliers, seasonality. A scheme tuned to a baseline that no longer exists is worse than no scheme. quick check answered Two CUSUM schemes both detect a 1σ shift in about 9 periods. Scheme A has ARL 0 = 200, scheme B has ARL 0 = 480. Which is better? A, because a lower ARL 0 means it is more sensitive and will catch unusual behaviour sooner. B, because the detection speed is the same and it raises less than half as many false alarms. Unanswerable without knowing the cost of each mistake. Notes on choosing and tuning click to type; saves automatically Check Why is ARL 1 meaningless without a stated shift size? Show answer Because detection delay depends entirely on how big the change was. The same scheme might take 3 periods on a 2σ shift and 40 on a 0.5σ one. \"ARL 1 = 8\" is not a property of the scheme; \"ARL 1 = 8 at a 1σ shift, with k = 0.5 and h = 4\" is."},{"id":"x3","h":"Practice and self-test","t":"Extra 3 · not class material Practice and self-test Eight worked problems and a mixed self-test. Do the arithmetic by hand at least once — sign errors are invisible until you make one. Part A — computing CUSUM by hand Problem 1 — the increase recursion A process has μ = 20 . You are monitoring for an increase with C = 2 and T = 10 . The first five observations are 23, 19, 24, 26, 22. Compute S t for each period and say whether an alarm fires. Show worked solution t = 1: 23 − 20 − 2 = 1; plus S 0 = 0 gives 1. S 1 = 1. t = 2: 19 − 20 − 2 = −3; plus 1 gives −2, which is negative, so the floor applies. S 2 = 0. t = 3: 24 − 20 − 2 = 2; plus 0 gives 2. S 3 = 2. t = 4: 26 − 20 − 2 = 4; plus 2 gives 6. S 4 = 6. t = 5: 22 − 20 − 2 = 0; plus 6 gives 6. S 5 = 6. No alarm — the maximum is 6 against T = 10. Notice t = 5: an observation genuinely above μ contributed exactly nothing, because it exceeded expectation by precisely C. That is C doing its job. Problem 2 — the decrease recursion Same five observations, same μ = 20. Now compute the decrease statistic with C = 2. What do you get, and what does it tell you? Show worked solution Every period: μ − x t is 20 − 23 = −3, 20 − 19 = +1, 20 − 24 = −4, 20 − 26 = −6, 20 − 22 = −2. Subtract C = 2 from each and the largest is −1. The running total never becomes positive, so S − = 0 in every period. The interpretation is not \"the method failed\" — it is the correct answer. This process has not gone down. A lower statistic pinned at zero is exactly what a healthy or rising process should produce, and is the same behaviour you saw from S + in the sales example in Lesson 2. Problem 3 — a decrease that does fire A machine's output has μ = 100 . Monitoring for a decrease with C = 3 and T = 12 , the observations are 98, 94, 101, 92, 95, 90. When does the alarm fire? Show worked solution t = 1: 100 − 98 − 3 = −1; floored. S − = 0. t = 2: 100 − 94 − 3 = 3; plus 0. S − = 3. t = 3: 100 − 101 − 3 = −4; plus 3 gives −1; floored. S − = 0. t = 4: 100 − 92 − 3 = 5. S − = 5. t = 5: 100 − 95 − 3 = 2; plus 5. S − = 7. t = 6: 100 − 90 − 3 = 7; plus 7 gives 14 ≥ 12. Alarm at t = 6. Note t = 3. A single reading above μ wiped out the accumulated evidence entirely, and the eventual detection was delayed by it. That is the price of the floor — and it is the right price, because the alternative is banking credit from quiet periods. Problem 4 — diagnosing a broken implementation A colleague's decrease detector alarms within about ten periods on every process they point it at, healthy or not. Their code computes s = max(0, s + (mu - x + C)) . Explain the behaviour and give the fix. Show worked solution C is added rather than subtracted, so every period contributes an extra +C regardless of the data. On a healthy process the deviations μ − x t average zero, leaving a steady upward drift of C per period. The total therefore reaches T after roughly T/C periods no matter what the process is doing — which is why the alarms arrive on a schedule rather than in response to anything. Fix: s = max(0, s + (mu - x - C)) . A quick sanity test that catches this class of bug: feed the detector data generated with no change at all and confirm the statistic stays near zero. If it climbs steadily, a sign is wrong. Part B — parameters, tuning and interpretation Problem 5 — converting to industrial notation A process has σ = 4. You want to catch a shift of 1.5σ as quickly as possible and can tolerate a false alarm roughly every 465 periods. Give k, h, K, H and the resulting recursion. Show worked solution k = δ/2 = 0.75 , so K = kσ = 0.75 × 4 = 3 . An ARL 0 near 465 corresponds to h = 5 , so H = hσ = 5 × 4 = 20 . The recursions are S + t = max{0, S + t−1 + (x t − μ − 3)} and S − t = max{0, S − t−1 + (μ − x t − 3)}, alarming at 20. Caveat worth stating: the ARL 0 ≈ 465 figure is for k = 0.5, not 0.75. Changing k moves the ARL, so in practice you would read h off a table for your actual k, or simulate. Quoting a table value for the wrong k is a common slip. Problem 6 — reading a tuning decision A hospital monitors post-operative infection rates with CUSUM. The team proposes halving T to catch outbreaks sooner. What do they need to know before agreeing, and what is the alternative? Show worked solution They need the cost of a false alarm in this setting — an investigation, staff time, possibly suspending a surgeon's list or closing a ward — against the cost of a week's delay in catching a real outbreak. Halving T will roughly halve the detection delay and increase false alarms substantially; whether that is right is a judgement about consequences, not statistics. The alternative worth raising: tuning C instead. In Lesson 2's reaction-time example, lowering C bought most of the speed while leaving the false-alarm margin nearly intact. C and T both move you along the same curve, but not at the same rate, and it is worth checking both before changing either. One more thing to check: whether μ is still right. Infection rates have seasonal and case-mix variation, and a scheme tuned against a stale baseline will look badly calibrated whatever you do to T. Problem 7 — picking a method For each scenario, name a suitable method and say why. (a) A fraud model deployed six months ago; you have labels arriving with a two-week lag. (b) Twenty years of quarterly GDP; you want to know whether the relationship between GDP and interest rates broke, and when. (c) A vibration sensor sampled at 1 kHz where failures announce themselves as a change in the shape of the distribution, not its mean. (d) A filling machine where over-filling wastes product and under-filling breaks a regulation. Show worked solution (a) A drift detector on the error stream — DDM or EDDM if you want a cheap error-rate monitor, ADWIN if you can afford to hold a window. The two-week label lag is the real constraint: it sets a floor on detection delay no method can beat, and it is worth also monitoring the input features with KSWIN so data drift is visible before labels arrive. (b) A structural break test, not a control chart — the question is about a fitted relationship. Bai–Perron if multiple breaks are plausible over twenty years, which they are; Quandt–Andrews if you are confident there is at most one. (c) A non-parametric method: kernel change-point detection or kernel CUSUM offline, KSWIN online. Mean-based methods are blind to a change that leaves the mean where it was. (d) Two-sided CUSUM, with asymmetric parameters. Under-filling is a regulatory breach and over-filling is a cost, so the two directions deserve different thresholds — probably a tighter T on the under-fill side. Problem 8 — why the method failed A CUSUM on daily website response times alarms two or three times a week, and every investigation finds nothing. σ and μ were estimated from a month of clean data and C = 0.5σ, T = 5σ are standard settings. What is the most likely explanation? Show worked solution Autocorrelation and daily seasonality. Response times are not independent from one reading to the next, and they follow a weekday/weekend and time-of-day pattern. CUSUM's whole design assumes independent observations scattered around a fixed μ; runs of similar values are exactly the pattern it is built to treat as evidence, so predictable structure reads as change. Published ARL tables silently assume independence, which is why settings that should give a false alarm every 465 periods give one every two days. The fix is to model the structure first — a seasonal model, or an ARIMA fit — and run CUSUM on the residuals, which are what should be independent and stationary. This is the same \"residual trick\" noted in the module summary, applied to fix the assumption rather than to find the change. Part C — mixed self-test self-test answered S − t−1 = 14, μ = 50, x t = 61, C = 4. What is S − t ? 29 0 −1 self-test answered Two-sided CUSUM, same T as before on each side. Compared with the one-sided version, what happens to the false-alarm rate? Roughly doubles, because there are now two statistics that can cross the threshold. Stays the same, because the two statistics cannot both be large at once. Halves, because each direction only sees half the deviations. self-test answered You want to detect a 2σ shift as fast as possible with CUSUM. What is k? 0.5, the standard default. 1.0, since k = δ/2. 2.0, matching the shift size you are targeting. self-test answered A CUSUM alarms, the engineer investigates, finds a genuine fault and fixes it. What must happen to the statistic? Nothing — it will come back down on its own once the process returns to μ. It must be reset to zero before monitoring resumes. μ must be re-estimated from the data since the alarm. self-test answered Which statement about ARL 0 = 370 is correct? A false alarm will occur close to every 370 periods. The average gap between false alarms is 370 periods, but individual gaps vary enormously. Real changes will be detected within 370 periods. self-test answered A team wants to know whether a policy introduced on a known date changed an outcome. Which approach fits? CUSUM, run from the policy date forward, alarming when the outcome shifts. Interrupted time series or difference-in-differences, since the date is known and the question is attribution. PELT, to locate all the change points and check whether one falls on the policy date. Part D — written questions No multiple choice. Write the answer out before opening it — recognising a correct answer and producing one are different skills, and only the second is tested. Six to write out 1. State both CUSUM recursions and explain, in one sentence each, what the deviation term, the constant C, the max with zero, and the threshold T are for. Show a model answer S + t = max{0, S + t−1 + (x t − μ − C)} and S − t = max{0, S − t−1 + (μ − x t − C)}, both from zero, both alarming at S t ≥ T. The deviation measures how far the process ran from expectation in the direction being monitored. C is the amount of that deviation dismissed as noise each period, so ordinary fluctuation cannot accumulate. The max with zero discards evidence pointing the wrong way, so a quiet stretch does not delay the next detection. T is how much accumulated evidence is required before a change is declared. 2. Explain why a statistically correct hypothesis test can be the wrong tool for change detection. Show a model answer Because reaching a conventional significance threshold requires enough evidence to be confident, and on noisy data that can take longer than the deadline allows — longer than the bearing survives, or longer than the scrap costs justify. The verdict is right and arrives too late to act on. Change detection deliberately trades confidence for speed, accepting false alarms as the price of an answer in time. 3. Describe what happens to detection delay and false-alarm rate when C rises, and when T rises. Why are these not the same dial? Show a model answer Both raise the delay and reduce false alarms, but they act differently. C is subtracted every period, so it suppresses accumulation continuously — it stops noise building up at all, which flattens the statistic during healthy stretches. T only decides how much accumulated evidence is required at the end, and does nothing to stop the total climbing beforehand. That is why, in the reaction-time example, raising C from 0 to 15 kept nearly all the detection speed while cutting the pre-change peak from 119 to 23; raising T instead would have cost several periods of delay to achieve the same margin. 4. Why does the statistic reset to zero rather than being allowed to go negative? Show a model answer Because the question is only ever \"has it moved in this direction?\". A run of lower-than-expected readings is no evidence against a later increase, and letting the total bank negative credit would mean the method had to climb out of a hole before it could report one — delaying every subsequent detection by however long the quiet stretch lasted. The floor makes each new stretch of data start from a clean slate, which is what makes CUSUM fast. 5. A colleague says CUSUM is strictly better than a Shewhart chart. Respond. Show a model answer Only for small sustained shifts. CUSUM with k = 0.5, h = 5 detects a 1σ shift about four times faster than a ±3σ Shewhart chart while raising fewer false alarms, so for that regime it genuinely dominates. But a 4σ jump puts the very next observation outside the Shewhart limits, while CUSUM still needs a period or two to accumulate — so for large sudden changes Shewhart wins. The crossover sits somewhere around 1.5σ, which is why combined Shewhart–CUSUM schemes exist. 6. Give three assumptions CUSUM makes and say what goes wrong when each is violated. Show a model answer Independence: autocorrelated data contains runs of similar values, which look exactly like accumulating evidence, so false alarms rise far above the rate the tables promise. Stationarity around a fixed μ: trend or seasonality produces long predictable runs above expectation and the method alarms on every seasonal peak; fit the structure first and monitor the residuals. μ and σ estimated from clean data: if the baseline period already contains the change, μ is pulled toward the post-change level and the method may never detect anything. Problems I got wrong click to type; saves automatically How to use this Which of these is worth doing twice? Show answer Problems 1 to 3, by hand, on paper. Everything else in this module is conceptual and will stick from reading; the arithmetic will not, and a sign error in the recursion is the single most common way to lose marks on an otherwise correct answer."},{"id":"x4","h":"Sources and further reading","t":"Extra 4 · not class material Sources and further reading Where the material in Extras 1 to 3 came from, and what you would actually reach for if you had to build one of these. Start here Source What it gives you NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook of Statistical Methods , section 6.3 — Univariate and Multivariate Control Charts itl.nist.gov/div898/handbook/pmc/section3/pmc3.htm The free, authoritative reference for control charts. Section 6.3.2.3 covers CUSUM including the V-mask form; 6.3.1.3.1 is the ARL discussion the numbers in Extra 2 come from. Start with this before any textbook. Montgomery, Introduction to Statistical Quality Control The standard textbook. Its CUSUM chapter is where the tabular form, the k and h notation, ARL tables and the fast-initial-response headstart are set out properly. If your library has one statistics text on this, it is this one. Hawkins & Olwell, Cumulative Sum Charts and Charting for Quality Improvement (Springer, 1998) Book-length treatment of CUSUM specifically — self-starting charts, estimated parameters, non-normal data. Where to go when the standard recipe does not fit your process. Basseville & Nikiforov, Detection of Abrupt Changes: Theory and Application The classic reference for the theory, long out of print and freely available online. Heavier going, and the place where the optimality results live. Founding papers, if you want the originals Page, E. S. (1954), \"Continuous Inspection Schemes\", Biometrika . The paper that introduced CUSUM, published a few years after Wald's sequential probability ratio test and built on the same idea. Shewhart, W. A. (1931), Economic Control of Quality of Manufactured Product . The origin of the control chart, and of the idea that variation splits into chance causes and assignable ones. Brown, Durbin & Evans (1975), \"Techniques for Testing the Constancy of Regression Relationships Over Time\", JRSS-B . The econometric CUSUM and CUSUM-of-squares tests. Lucas, J. M. (1976), \"The Design and Use of V-Mask Control Schemes\", and (1982), \"Combined Shewhart-CUSUM Quality Control Schemes\", Journal of Quality Technology . Where the practical design guidance and the ARL tables come from. Bai & Perron (1998, 2003). Multiple structural breaks — estimation and computation respectively. Adams & MacKay (2007), \"Bayesian Online Changepoint Detection\". Short, readable, and the basis of most Bayesian implementations in use. Killick, Fearnhead & Eckley (2012), \"Optimal Detection of Changepoints with a Linear Computational Cost\". The PELT paper. Bifet & Gavaldà (2007), \"Learning from Time-Changing Data with Adaptive Windowing\". ADWIN. Gama et al. (2004), \"Learning with Drift Detection\". DDM, and the paper that organised drift detectors into the three groups used in Extra 1. Software you would actually use Tool Language For ruptures centre-borelli.github.io/ruptures-docs Python Offline change-point detection. Implements PELT, binary segmentation, dynamic programming, bottom-up and window methods, with interchangeable cost functions (l1, l2, normal, linear, autoregressive, RBF kernel, rank). The documentation is unusually good as an introduction to the offline problem in its own right. river riverml.xyz Python Streaming machine learning, including a drift module with ADWIN, KSWIN, Page–Hinkley, DDM, EDDM and the HDDM family. The quickest way to see these detectors behave on real data. changepoint , strucchange , qcc R PELT and friends; Chow, CUSUM-of-residuals and Bai–Perron tests; and classical control charts including CUSUM and EWMA, respectively. strucchange is the standard route to the econometric tests. statsmodels Python breaks_cusumolsresid and related diagnostics for regression stability, plus the state-space models whose residuals you would monitor. Minitab, JMP, SigmaXL, Statgraphics — Where tabular and V-mask CUSUM charts live in industry, with built-in ARL calculators for choosing h and k. Worth knowing the interface exists even if you never use it, because it is what the quality engineer you are talking to has open. A twenty-minute exercise worth doing Generate a few hundred normal observations, inject a 1σ shift somewhere in the middle, and run a Shewhart chart, a CUSUM and an EWMA over the same series. Then vary the shift size from 0.5σ to 4σ and watch the ordering of the three methods change. The three-panel figure in Extra 1 is one instance of this; doing it yourself, with the parameters in your own hands, is what makes the trade-off stop being a slogan. Reading log click to type; saves automatically Caveat How much of this should you take on trust? Show answer The specific numbers — ARL values, k = δ/2, the h = 4 and h = 5 conventions — are standard and consistent across the sources listed here, but they assume independent normally distributed observations. Check any figure you plan to rely on against the NIST handbook or Montgomery, and re-derive it by simulation for your own data rather than quoting a table."},{"id":"sec-10","h":"","t":"Written by an AI model from lecture recordings. Not reviewed by any instructor or institution — verify anything you rely on against the original lectures. What goes beyond the transcripts. Every illustration has been replaced with a generic one, so none of the examples here are the ones used in class — in particular the motorway-congestion and bearing-temperature illustrations in Lesson 1 stand in for the specific examples used in class. The following are additions rather than lecture content: the eighteen-trial reaction-time dataset in Lesson 2 and every number computed from it, including the whole C = 15 comparison and the control chart; the eighteen-week sales dataset and its two-sided CUSUM table, chart and C-versus-sign-error comparison; the ideal-versus-real sensor figure; all cross-references to Modules 3 and 5; and the whole of “Things worth noticing”. All quiz feedback, margin questions and exam targets are written here rather than drawn from the recordings. Everything under “Additional resources” is outside material. Extras 1 to 4 — the map of methods, the industrial k and h notation, ARL figures, the Shewhart/CUSUM/EWMA comparison, the practice problems and the reading list — were assembled from published sources, principally the NIST/SEMATECH e-Handbook, Montgomery’s Introduction to Statistical Quality Control , the original method papers cited in Extra 4, and the documentation of the ruptures and river libraries. None of it was taught in this module. Quantitative claims (ARL values, parameter conventions) are standard in those sources but assume independent, normally distributed observations; check them against a primary source before relying on them, and treat the lectures as authoritative wherever the two differ."}]},{"f":"module-7-time-series.html","t":"Module 7: Time Series Models","s":[{"id":"l1","h":"Exponential smoothing","t":"Lesson 1 Exponential smoothing Today’s best estimate of the true level is a blend of what you just saw and what you believed yesterday — and one number, α, sets the mix. Time series data, and the three things that move it Time series data Data where the same response is recorded for many time periods: the day’s high temperature at one airport, a stock price every two seconds, daily burger sales at one restaurant, a patient’s blood pressure over weeks. Values like these vary for three different reasons, and a model has to tell them apart: Trend — a steady drift up or down. A stock rising as the company gains market share; burger sales slipping as the menu widens. Cyclical (seasonal) variation — a repeating pattern. Warmer summers, busier weekends. Randomness — plain noise. Some days people simply want more burgers. A blood pressure reading taken during a tense match comes out high. Randomness is dealt with first, because it hides the other two. If a patient’s reading today is higher than usual, a doctor needs to know: has the underlying level risen, or was today just a noisy day? Check Name the three sources of variation in a time series. Show answer Trend, cyclical (seasonal) variation, and randomness. Exponential smoothing handles randomness first; Lesson 2 adds the other two. Two extreme answers, and the blend x t The observed response at time t — the reading actually taken S t The expected baseline at time t — what the level “really” is once noise is removed Faced with an unusual reading, there are two opposite beliefs you could hold: The reading is the truth: S t = x t . The baseline has moved. The reading is luck: S t = S t−1 . Nothing has changed. Exponential smoothing takes a weighted average of the two: S t = α·x t + (1 − α)·S t−1 , 0 < α < 1 α is the weight on the new observation; (1 − α) is the weight on yesterday’s belief. At α = 1 you believe every reading; at α = 0 you never update. Choosing α Lots of randomness → α near 0. A fluctuation is probably noise, so lean on yesterday’s baseline. Little randomness → α near 1. A fluctuation probably reflects a real change, so trust what you see. One detail remains: the first period has no yesterday. The standard starting condition is S 1 = x 1 — take the first reading as the first baseline and build up from there. The same readings smoothed two ways. The low-α line ignores most of the noise but takes several periods to believe the step up. The high-α line reacts at once — to the real step and to every random wiggle. (Illustrative data.) quick check answered Home blood-pressure readings from a cheap cuff jump around by 15 points from one reading to the next even when nothing is wrong. Which α is the better starting point? A small α, around 0.1 to 0.2 A large α, around 0.8 to 0.9 α = 1, so the baseline always matches the latest reading α = 0, so noise can never affect the baseline Exam target Write the basic exponential smoothing equation and its starting condition from memory. Show answer S t = αx t + (1 − α)S t−1 , with 0 < α < 1 and S 1 = x 1 . A worked step Yesterday’s baseline was 120. Today’s reading is 135. With α = 0.2: S t = 0.2 × 135 + 0.8 × 120 = 27 + 96 = 123 A 15-point jump in the reading moves the baseline only 3 points. With α = 0.8 the same reading would give 132. calculation answered With α = 0.4, S t−1 = 50 and x t = 40, what is S t ? 44 46 45 40 Class discussion — Lesson 1 click to type; saves automatically Worth noticing S t always lands between which two numbers? Show answer Between x t and S t−1 , because it is a weighted average of them with weights that sum to 1. It moves the fraction α of the way from the old baseline toward the new reading. More examples: where exponential smoothing came from, and where it runs today A little history The method came out of 1950s operations research, not finance. Robert G. Brown developed simple exponential smoothing for US Navy inventory control. Charles Holt added a separate trend in a 1957 Office of Naval Research memo. Peter Winters tested Holt’s methods on real sales data in 1960 and added the seasonal part, which is why triple smoothing carries both names. Its original purpose — deciding how much stock to hold — is still one of its main uses. Example: your internet connection runs this equation Every TCP connection has to decide how long to wait for a reply before assuming a packet was lost. Round-trip times are noisy, so the internet standard for this (RFC 6298) smooths them with exactly the Lesson 1 equation, using α = 1/8: SRTT ← (1 − 1/8)·SRTT + (1/8)·R′ SRTT is the smoothed round-trip time (our S t ); R′ is the newest measurement (our x t ). It also smooths how far each measurement lands from the average, with weight 1/4, and waits for the average plus four times that spread. Suppose SRTT = 100 ms, the smoothed spread is 20 ms, and a slow reply takes 180 ms: Spread 0.75 × 20 + 0.25 × |100 − 180| = 15 + 20 = 35 ms Smoothed RTT 0.875 × 100 + 0.125 × 180 = 87.5 + 22.5 = 110 ms Timeout 110 + 4 × 35 = 250 ms (the standard then raises anything below 1 second up to 1 second) One slow reply moves the average only 10 ms — a small α at work. The spread term is the same idea applied to variability , which is where Lesson 6 ends up. Try it: move α and watch the baseline α = 0.30 Things to try: drag α to about 0.05 and count how many periods the line takes to accept the step up. Then drag it to 0.95 and see how much of the noise it copies. Somewhere in between is the trade-off the optimizer in Lesson 4 settles for you. Worth noticing TCP uses α = 1/8. What does that choice say about how its designers view a single slow reply? Show answer That it is mostly noise. A small α means one unusual round-trip time moves the estimate only an eighth of the way, so the timeout doesn’t lurch around with every delayed packet."},{"id":"l2","h":"Trends and cycles","t":"Lesson 2 Trends and cycles Trend and seasonality get the same treatment as the baseline: each has its own running estimate, its own weight, and its own starting value. Adding a trend Let T t be the trend at time t — how much the baseline changes per period. Two things change. First, “yesterday’s belief” now has to include yesterday’s trend. If the level was 100 and rising by 5 a period, the prior guess for today is 105, not 100: S t = α·x t + (1 − α)·(S t−1 + T t−1 ) Second, the trend itself is smoothed the same way. The observed trend this period is how much the baseline just moved, S t − S t−1 , and it is blended with the previous trend estimate using a new constant β: T t = β·(S t − S t−1 ) + (1 − β)·T t−1 , 0 < β < 1 Starting condition: T 1 = 0 — assume no trend until the data says otherwise, just as S 1 = x 1 assumes nothing about the baseline. Check What is the “observed trend” that β weights? Show answer The change in the baseline estimate from last period to this one, S t − S t−1 — not the change in the raw observations, which would carry all the noise with it. Adding seasonality — multiplicatively A cyclic effect could be added as another additive term, and sometimes that is the right choice. The lecture shows the alternative, a multiplicative seasonal factor, so both are in your toolbox. L The length of one cycle. Daily readings with a weekly cycle: L = 7. Hourly readings with a daily cycle: L = 24. C t The seasonal factor for period t. Above 1 inflates, below 1 deflates. The observation is divided by its seasonal factor before it is blended in — that strips the season out so the baseline sees only the underlying level: S t = α·(x t / C t−L ) + (1 − α)·(S t−1 + T t−1 ) Why C t−L and not C t−1 ? It is the most recent factor from the same stage of the cycle. If today is Monday, use last Monday’s factor. The factor is then updated with a third constant, γ, blending “how far above the baseline was this period?” with the old factor for this stage: C t = γ·(x t / S t ) + (1 − γ)·C t−L , 0 < γ < 1 Starting condition: the first L factors are all 1 — no seasonal effect assumed — and the data fills in the rest. Reading a factor C = 1.1 on a Sunday means Sunday sales run 10% above baseline just because it is Sunday. Sell 550 burgers that day and 500 of them are baseline; the other 50 are the Sunday lift. match the pairs matched Click a symbol, then its meaning. Symbol S t T t C t L C t−L γ Meaning Estimated baseline level at time t Estimated change in baseline per period Multiplicative seasonal factor at time t Number of periods in one cycle Latest factor from the same stage of the cycle Weight on the newly observed seasonal ratio Exam target Give all three starting conditions for triple exponential smoothing. Show answer S 1 = x 1 ; T 1 = 0; and the first L seasonal factors C 1 … C L all equal 1. Each says “assume nothing yet” in the form that suits that component. Names you will see Single exponential smoothing Baseline only (α) Double exponential smoothing Baseline and trend (α, β) Triple exponential smoothing Baseline, trend and seasonality (α, β, γ) — also called Winters’ method or Holt-Winters calculation answered A café’s Saturday seasonal factor is 1.25. It serves 600 coffees this Saturday. How many of those are baseline demand? 450 480 750 575 calculation answered With γ = 0.1, this period’s observation is 660, the new baseline S t is 600, and last cycle’s factor for this stage was 1.2. What is the updated factor C t ? 1.10 1.19 1.11 1.20 Class discussion — Lesson 2 click to type; saves automatically Worth noticing When would an additive seasonal term fit better than a multiplicative one? Show answer When the seasonal swing stays roughly the same size in absolute terms as the level changes — say, always about 50 extra units on Sundays. Multiplicative fits when the swing grows with the level — always about 10% extra. (This rule of thumb is standard practice rather than lecture content.) More examples: real data with each kind of seasonality Multiplicative: airline passengers, 1949–1960 The classic example in almost every time series textbook is Box and Jenkins’ monthly totals of international airline passengers. It is small, real, and has all three components at once. Monthly international airline passengers (thousands), from Box & Jenkins’ “Series G”. Both the level and the seasonal swing grow over time. Year Average month Peak − trough Peak ÷ trough 1949 127 44 1.42 1954 239 114 1.61 1960 476 232 1.59 Read across the table. The average month grew about 3.7 times, and the absolute swing grew more than 5 times. The ratio of peak to trough stayed in a narrow band around 1.4–1.6. A seasonal effect that stays roughly constant as a proportion is exactly what a multiplicative factor C t describes. Lesson 4 fits both versions to this data and compares them. Additive: carbon dioxide at Mauna Loa The Keeling curve — atmospheric CO₂ measured on Mauna Loa, Hawaii, since 1958 — has a strong trend and a yearly cycle. Northern Hemisphere plants pull CO₂ out of the air as they grow in spring and summer, and it returns as they die back. The peak-to-trough swing averages roughly 6 ppm. While the level rose from about 316 ppm to over 420 ppm, that swing changed only modestly. A swing that stays about the same size while the level climbs fits an additive seasonal term better than a multiplicative one. More than one cycle: electricity demand Half-hourly electricity demand has a daily cycle and a weekly one. Basic Holt-Winters allows one L. In 2003, James Taylor extended the method to two seasonal cycles for exactly this problem. When your data has several cycles, look for “double seasonal” or “multiple seasonal” versions in your software. real data answered A theme park’s summer attendance runs about 80% above its winter attendance, and that has held true while total visitors doubled over a decade. Which seasonal form fits? Multiplicative — the effect is a stable percentage Additive — the effect is a stable number of visitors Neither — attendance has no real seasonality A trend term alone captures it Check In the airline table, why is “peak ÷ trough” the more useful column for choosing a model? Show answer Because it tests the multiplicative assumption directly. If the ratio stays roughly steady while the level grows, a multiplicative factor fits. If instead the difference stayed steady, additive would fit."},{"id":"l3","h":"Where the name comes from","t":"Lesson 3 Where the name comes from “Smoothing” because spikes get pulled toward the old baseline; “exponential” because, unrolled, every past observation is in there with a weight that shrinks by a constant factor each step back. Smoothing Take any α strictly between 0 and 1 — think of 0.5. A very high reading x t is only counted with weight α, and the other (1 − α) goes to the older, lower baseline, so S t ends up below the spike. A very low reading gets pulled up the same way. Peaks shave down, valleys fill in, and the line through them is smoother than the data. The trend and seasonal equations work exactly the same way, so everything in this lesson applies to them too. Exponential S t−1 is itself given by the same equation: S t−1 = αx t−1 + (1 − α)S t−2 . Substitute that in, then do the same for S t−2 , S t−3 , and so on: S t = αx t + α(1−α)x t−1 + α(1−α)²x t−2 + α(1−α)³x t−3 + … + (1−α) k S t−k The weight on the observation k periods back is α(1−α) k . The exponent rises by one each step — that is the “exponential”. Four regions are clickable. Open all four to finish this activity. Exam target What weight does x t−3 receive in S t ? Show answer α(1 − α)³. With α = 0.5 that is 0.0625; with α = 0.2 it is 0.1024 — the smaller α gives more weight to an observation three periods old. What the unrolled form reveals The one-line equation makes it look as if only the latest observation matters. The expanded form shows the truth: Every past observation contributes to S t . The older ones are baked into S t−1 . Recent observations count more. Because 1 − α < 1, each step back multiplies the weight by something smaller than one. So the method has a long memory and a recency bias at the same time — which is usually exactly what you want when estimating what is happening now. concept trap answered A colleague says: “Exponential smoothing only looks at the latest reading and yesterday’s baseline, so data from a month ago has no effect.” What is the best reply? They’re right — that is what the equation says. Month-old data is still in there, folded into yesterday’s baseline with a smaller weight. Old data drops out after a fixed window of L periods. All past readings count equally, so month-old data matters as much as today’s. Class discussion — Lesson 3 click to type; saves automatically Worth noticing Do the weights α(1−α) k add up to 1? Show answer Over an infinitely long history, yes — it is a geometric series: α / (1 − (1 − α)) = 1. Over a finite history the leftover (1 − α) k sits on the starting value S 1 = x 1 . That leftover is why the choice of starting value fades in importance as data accumulates. More examples: how long is α’s memory? Two numbers make α concrete. The half-life is how many periods back the weight falls to half of today’s weight: ln(0.5) / ln(1 − α). The average age of the data behind S t — the weighted average of how many periods old each observation is — works out to (1 − α) / α. α Weight on today Half-life Average age of the data Share of weight on the last 10 periods 0.1 10% 6.6 periods 9 periods 65% 0.2 20% 3.1 periods 4 periods 89% 0.3 30% 1.9 periods 2.3 periods 97% 0.5 50% 1 period 1 period 99.9% 0.8 80% 0.4 periods 0.25 periods ≈100% So α = 0.1 on daily data behaves like an average over roughly the last week and a half, and α = 0.5 is essentially looking at yesterday and today. When someone gives you a fitted α, translating it into an average age is the quickest way to explain it to a non-specialist. Compared with a simple moving average A 5-day moving average gives each of the last five days a weight of 0.2 and every earlier day a weight of 0. That cliff causes a quirk: when a big value drops out of the window, the average jumps, even though nothing happened today. Exponential weights fade smoothly instead, so there is no drop-out jump. They also need only one stored number, S t−1 , rather than the whole window — a big reason the method suited 1950s inventory systems. calculation answered A team wants a smoothed daily metric whose data is, on average, about 4 days old. Which α gives that? 0.25 0.20 0.40 0.10 Check With α = 0.5, what fraction of S t ’s weight sits on data more than three periods old? Show answer (1 − α) 4 = 0.5 4 = 6.25%. Today and the three periods before it carry 50 + 25 + 12.5 + 6.25 = 93.75%."},{"id":"l4","h":"Forecasting with exponential smoothing","t":"Lesson 4 Forecasting with exponential smoothing You don’t know next period’s observation, so your best guess for it is your current baseline — and that single substitution turns a smoother into a forecaster. The one-step-ahead forecast Write the smoothing equation for the next period, t + 1: S t+1 = α·x t+1 + (1 − α)·S t x t+1 hasn’t happened yet. The best guess for it is the current baseline S t . Substitute, and the α terms cancel: F t+1 = α·S t + (1 − α)·S t = S t The forecast for next period is simply the latest baseline estimate. The same argument applies to t + 2, t + 3 and beyond, so with baseline only the forecast is a flat line at S t . What does change is the uncertainty: the further ahead you look, the larger the expected forecast error — which is the widening band in the picture at the top of this page. Check Using single exponential smoothing, what is the forecast five periods ahead? Show answer S t — the same as the one-period-ahead forecast. Only the expected size of the error grows. With trend, and with seasonality The best estimate of next period’s baseline is the current baseline; the best estimate of next period’s trend is the current trend. So: F t+1 = S t + T t Add multiplicative seasonality, and the best estimate of next period’s factor is the latest one from that same stage of the cycle, C (t+1)−L : F t+1 = (S t + T t ) · C (t+1)−L Looking more than one step ahead The lecture presents the one-step form. In the standard Holt-Winters method the trend is applied once per step, so k periods ahead the forecast is (S t + k·T t ) · C (t+k)−L , using the factor from the matching stage of the most recent full cycle. Check which form your software reports. A worked example α = 0.2, β = 0.5. Last period: S t−1 = 100, T t−1 = 2. This period’s observation is x t = 120. No seasonality yet. S t = 0.2 × 120 + 0.8 × (100 + 2) = 24 + 81.6 = 105.6 T t = 0.5 × (105.6 − 100) + 0.5 × 2 = 2.8 + 1 = 3.8 F t+1 = 105.6 + 3.8 = 109.4 If next period’s stage of the cycle had a factor of 1.2, the forecast would be 109.4 × 1.2 = 131.28. calculation answered S t = 200, T t = 4, and the seasonal factor from the matching stage last cycle is 0.9. What is F t+1 ? 184.0 183.6 204.0 226.7 Exam target Why does the α term vanish from the single-smoothing forecast? Show answer Because the unknown x t+1 is replaced by S t , both terms of the weighted average are S t , and α + (1 − α) = 1. α still matters — it shaped S t — but it doesn’t appear in the forecasting step. Fitting α, β and γ Every past period gives a forecast you can check. Measure each one’s squared error, (F t − x t )², add them up over the training data, and choose the constants that make the total smallest: minimize Σ t (F t − x t )² over α, β, γ subject to 0 ≤ α, β, γ ≤ 1 This is an optimization problem — the course covers optimization as a topic of its own. Statistical software solves it for you. put in order solved Put the steps for fitting and using a Holt-Winters model in order. Drag, or use the arrows. Choose the cycle length L and set S 1 = x 1 , T 1 = 0, and the first L factors to 1 Pick trial values of α, β and γ between 0 and 1 Run the update equations through the training data, recording each one-step forecast F t Add up the squared errors (F t − x t )² Let the optimizer adjust α, β and γ until that total is as small as possible Forecast forward from the final S t , T t and seasonal factors Check order Class discussion — Lesson 4 click to type; saves automatically Worth noticing Exponential smoothing is described as both descriptive and predictive. What makes it each? Show answer Descriptive: the smoothed S t tells you what the underlying level has been . Predictive: F t+1 uses that same state to say what comes next . It is one model used two ways. More examples: Holt-Winters worked row by row Eight quarters of sales for a small shop, with a busy fourth quarter. L = 4, α = 0.5, β = 0.3, γ = 0.4, and the lecture’s starting conditions: S 1 = x 1 , T 1 = 0, and C 1 to C 4 all equal to 1. t x t Forecast made at t−1 S t T t C t 1 40 — 40.00 0.00 1 (start) 2 28 40.00 34.00 −1.80 1 (start) 3 30 32.20 31.10 −2.13 1 (start) 4 52 28.97 40.48 1.32 1 (start) 5 46 41.81 43.90 1.95 1.019 6 32 45.86 38.93 −0.13 0.929 7 33 38.80 35.90 −1.00 0.968 8 60 34.91 47.45 2.77 1.106 Check one row yourself — t = 5, which uses C 1 = 1: S 5 = 0.5 × 46/1 + 0.5 × (40.48 + 1.32) = 23 + 20.90 = 43.90 T 5 = 0.3 × (43.90 − 40.48) + 0.7 × 1.32 = 1.03 + 0.92 = 1.95 C 5 = 0.4 × 46/43.90 + 0.6 × 1 = 0.419 + 0.6 = 1.019 Then forecast quarter 9, a first quarter, using the latest first-quarter factor C 5 : F 9 = (S 8 + T 8 ) × C 5 = (47.45 + 2.77) × 1.019 ≈ 51.2 Suppose quarter 9 actually comes in at 50 — an error of about +1.2. Three lessons hide in this table: The first cycle is learning time. With every factor set to 1, the model can’t yet tell a busy fourth quarter from a trend, so T t swings from −2.13 to +1.32 and the quarter-4 forecast of 28.97 misses 52 badly. The season shows up slowly. After one update the Q4 factor is only 1.106, though Q4 actually runs about 40% above the yearly average. With γ = 0.4 it takes several cycles to learn that. This is why software usually starts from a rough estimate of the season instead of 1s. Every forecast in the third column was made before its x t was known. Those are the errors the optimizer minimises. Check For t = 6, which seasonal factor divides x 6 , and why? Show answer C 2 = 1. Quarter 6 is a second quarter, and C 2 is the most recent second-quarter factor — that is, C t−L with L = 4. More examples: forecasting real airline traffic Take the airline data from Lesson 2. Fit on 1949–1958, forecast all 24 months of 1959–1960, and compare with what happened. The numbers below come from fitting each model for this page. The Holt-Winters fits started from a rough seasonal estimate taken from the first three years. Forecasts made at the end of 1958 against the actual 1959–1960 traffic (thousands of passengers). Model (fit on 1949–58) Fitted constants MAPE, 1959–60 RMSE Holt-Winters, multiplicative α 0.31, β 0.005, γ 0.98 7.6% 38 Holt-Winters, additive α 0.23, β 0.00, γ 1.00 7.8% 41 Seasonal naive (repeat 1958) — 15.5% 77 Single exponential smoothing α 1.00 23.6% 137 Naive (repeat Dec 1958) — 23.6% 137 What to take from it: Single smoothing collapsed into naive. A series with trend and season breaks single smoothing’s flat-level assumption. The best it can do is chase the latest value, so the optimizer pushed α to 1 — exactly the naive forecast. A fitted α near 1 is often a sign of a missing component. Multiplicative fitted the training years better. Its squared one-step error over 1950–58 was about 29% lower than the additive model’s (11,591 vs 16,257). On the two test years the gap narrowed to a small one. A better fit doesn’t always mean a proportionally better forecast. γ went to about 1. Each month’s factor is re-estimated almost entirely from its latest ratio. The optimizer decided last year’s same month is the best guide to this year’s. Holt-Winters halved the seasonal naive error. That is a model earning its complexity — the check Lesson 8 insists on. real data answered Fitting single exponential smoothing to the airline data returned α = 1. What is the best explanation? The data has almost no noise, so every reading can be trusted. The model has no trend or season, so the best it can do is copy the latest value. The optimizer broke, because α must be strictly below 1. Ten years is too little data for smoothing. Worth noticing Why can the seasonal naive forecast have the right shape but still miss by 15%? Show answer It copies 1958’s seasonal pattern but also 1958’s level. Traffic kept growing, so every month is too low — and further off in 1960 than in 1959. It has season but no trend."},{"id":"l5","h":"ARIMA","t":"Lesson 5 ARIMA Difference the data until it stops drifting, predict those differences from their own recent past and from recent errors — and exponential smoothing turns out to be one special case. ARIMA stands for AutoRegressive Integrated Moving Average . The lecture covers what it does rather than the theory behind it. It has three parts, one per letter group. 1 · Differences (the “I”) Stationary A series whose mean, variance and other measures are expected to stay constant over time. Exponential smoothing’s unrolled equation estimates S t from x t , x t−1 , x t−2 , … — which works well when the data is stationary. Trend or seasonality break that. But even when the data isn’t stationary, its differences may be: 1st-order differences x t − x t−1 , the change between consecutive observations 2nd-order differences The differences of those differences d-th-order differences Repeat the differencing d times 2 · Autoregression (the “AR”) Regression predicts a value from other factors — a child’s adult height from the parents’ heights and the child’s own height at two. Auto regression predicts a value from earlier values of the same thing : the child’s height at eighteen months, at one year, and so on. That only works with time series data. The unrolled exponential smoothing equation is already an autoregression — one that reaches back through all the data, so it is order ∞ . Using only the last p periods gives an order-p autoregressive model. ARIMA applies the autoregression to the d-th-order differences rather than to the raw observations. 3 · Moving average (the “MA”) Besides past values, ARIMA also uses past forecast errors as predictors. Going back q periods gives an order-q moving average . Vocabulary In ARIMA(p, d, q), which letter controls which part? Show answer p — how many past values feed the autoregression. d — how many times the data is differenced. q — how many past errors feed the moving average. Putting it together Writing D t for the d-th-order difference at time t and ε t for the forecast error, one common way to write the model is: D t = μ + Σ i=1..p φ i ·D t−i + Σ i=1..q θ i ·ε t−i + ε t A constant, p autoregressive terms on past differences, and q terms on past errors. Sign conventions for the θ terms differ between textbooks and software; the structure is what matters. Once you choose p, d and q, software finds the best constants — again by solving an optimization problem — and does it quickly. So you can try several (p, d, q) combinations and compare them with the validation approaches from earlier modules. Lesson 8 covers how those approaches need adjusting for time series. Seasonality can be added too. The course doesn’t cover seasonal ARIMA, but good software does it for you. Familiar models in disguise Particular choices of p, d and q reproduce simpler models. That is part of ARIMA’s power: one framework generalises many. match the pairs matched Click an ARIMA setting, then the model it reduces to. Setting ARIMA(0, 0, 0) ARIMA(0, 1, 0) ARIMA(p, 0, 0) ARIMA(0, 0, q) ARIMA(0, 1, 1) Equivalent model White noise — no pattern at all Random walk Pure autoregressive (AR) model Pure moving-average (MA) model Basic exponential smoothing Exam target Which ARIMA model is basic exponential smoothing? Show answer ARIMA(0, 1, 1): first differences, no autoregressive terms, one moving-average term. When to use it ARIMA, like exponential smoothing, is for short-term forecasting . It tends to beat exponential smoothing when the data is more stable — fewer peaks, valleys and outliers. Rule of thumb: you need about 40 past data points for ARIMA to work well. quick check answered What does ARIMA run its autoregression on? The raw observations x t−1 , x t−2 , … The d-th-order differences of the observations The past forecast errors The past squared errors DR Dana Reyes Demand planner, regional truck dealer group Dana has 30 months of pickup truck sales. The series jumps around a lot — end-of-quarter promotions create sharp spikes, and one month a fleet order tripled sales. Her manager: “ARIMA is the more general model, so it must be the better one. Use that.” What should Dana start with? ARIMA, because a general model can always match or beat its special cases. Exponential smoothing, and compare a few ARIMA models against it on held-out months. GARCH, because the series is so volatile. The next request. The dealer group decides how many trucks to order from the forecast. The operations lead asks: “How far off could this number be? I need to know how much buffer stock to hold.” Add a flat 10% to the forecast and call it the buffer. Estimate the variance of the forecast error, and size the buffer from that. Class discussion — Lesson 5 click to type; saves automatically Check State the rule of thumb for how much data ARIMA needs, and the kind of data it prefers. Show answer About 40 past data points; and relatively stable data with few peaks, valleys and outliers. Spiky data tends to favour exponential smoothing. More examples: seeing smoothing inside ARIMA Rewrite smoothing as “last forecast plus a correction” Since F t+1 = S t , the smoothing equation can be rearranged: F t+1 = αx t + (1 − α)F t = F t + α·(x t − F t ) New forecast = old forecast + α × last error. This is called the error-correction form. Now look at it through ARIMA’s eyes. The change in the series, x t+1 − x t , is a first difference (d = 1). Working through the algebra, that difference is explained by the current random error plus a multiple of the previous error (q = 1), with no past values needed (p = 0). That is ARIMA(0, 1, 1). α is doing the job of the moving-average coefficient. Example with α = 0.3: last forecast 200, actual 220, error +20. New forecast = 200 + 0.3 × 20 = 206. A single surprise shifts every future forecast by 30% of its size. Example: an AR(1) model forecast Weekly deviations of a warehouse’s temperature from its setpoint follow D t = 0.6·D t−1 + ε t (ARIMA(1, 0, 0), no constant). This week’s deviation is +5°. next week: 0.6 × 5 = 3.0 in two weeks: 0.6 × 3.0 = 1.8 in three: 1.08 … → 0 A stationary AR(1) forecast decays back toward the mean. Contrast the random walk below, whose forecast never decays. Example: stock prices as a random walk Daily closing prices are often close to ARIMA(0, 1, 0): tomorrow = today + an unpredictable change. The best forecast for any future day is today’s price, and the uncertainty grows with the horizon. That is why the price itself is hard to forecast — and why Lesson 6 turns to forecasting how variable the changes will be. Example: the “airline model” Box and Jenkins modelled the Lesson 2 airline data with what became known as the airline model, written ARIMA(0, 1, 1)(0, 1, 1) 12 and fitted to the log of the passenger counts. The second bracket is the seasonal extension the lecture mentions: log Turns the growing, proportional swings into constant-size ones (0, 1, 1) One ordinary difference and one past error — smoothing from month to month (0, 1, 1) 12 One difference against the same month last year, and one past error from 12 months back — smoothing from year to year Fitted on 1949–1958 for this page, its two error coefficients came out at about −0.32 and −0.57, similar to published fits. Its 1959–60 forecasts had a MAPE of 8.4% (RMSE 43) — in the same range as Holt-Winters in Lesson 4, with just two parameters. Lesson 7 shows how the correlation plots point to this exact model. calculation answered Using the error-correction form with α = 0.25: last period’s forecast was 80 and the actual value was 72. What is the new forecast? 74 78 82 72 Check An AR(1) forecast fades toward the mean; a random-walk forecast stays flat at today’s value. What single difference in the model causes that? Show answer The random walk is differenced (d = 1), so shocks are permanent. The stationary AR(1) has a coefficient below 1 on the undifferenced data, so each shock’s effect shrinks every period."},{"id":"l6","h":"GARCH","t":"Lesson 6 GARCH The same machinery as ARIMA, pointed at variance instead of value — so you can say not just what will happen, but how uncertain that is. GARCH stands for Generalized AutoRegressive Conditional Heteroscedasticity . It estimates or forecasts the variance of something recorded as a time series. Why forecast variance at all? Size the error around a forecast. A pickup truck maker forecasting demand needs to know how far above or below the true figure the forecast might land, so it can plan production and stock. Measure risk in investing. Classic portfolio optimization balances expected return against volatility. Some investors accept more risk for more expected return; others trade return for safety. Variance is the usual stand-in for volatility — and GARCH is a common way to estimate it. Simulated daily returns. Big moves cluster together, and so do quiet ones. A GARCH model tracks that changing variance — the band widens after large moves and narrows in calm spells. That time-varying variance is the “conditional heteroscedasticity” in the name. (Simulated data; parameters chosen for illustration.) Vocabulary What does “heteroscedasticity” mean? Show answer Variance that isn’t constant. “Conditional” adds that the variance at time t depends on what happened in earlier periods. (The word breakdown is standard usage rather than lecture content.) What the model looks like The lecture doesn’t go deep into the mathematics, but the structure is: σ t ² = ω + Σ i=1..p β i ·σ t−i ² + Σ i=1..q γ i ·ε t−i ² Today’s variance from a constant, p past variances, and q past squared errors. Software packages differ over which order is written first — check the documentation before reading off p and q. Set that beside ARIMA and there are only two differences: Variances, not values GARCH works with variances σ² and squared errors ε², where ARIMA works with observations and plain errors. No differencing GARCH uses the variances directly, never differences of them. So there is no d: the model is GARCH(p, q), not GARCH(p, d, q). As with ARIMA, good software fits the constants for any p and q you choose. GARCH(1, 1) is the common default in practice. quick check answered Why does a GARCH model have two order parameters when ARIMA has three? GARCH has no moving-average part. GARCH has no autoregressive part. GARCH doesn’t use differences, so it has no d. GARCH can’t handle seasonality. method choice answered An investment team wants next week’s volatility estimate for each fund to feed a risk-versus-return portfolio model. Which method fits the question? Holt-Winters on each fund’s price ARIMA on each fund’s price GARCH on each fund’s returns CUSUM on each fund’s returns The three methods side by side Exponential smoothing — estimates and forecasts the value, with optional trend and seasonality. ARIMA — generalises exponential smoothing for the value. GARCH — an ARIMA-like model for the variance. Other approaches exist, but these are the most common and most generally useful. Class discussion — Lesson 6 click to type; saves automatically Exam target Name the two structural differences between GARCH and ARIMA. Show answer GARCH models variances and squared errors rather than observations and linear errors; and it doesn’t difference, so it has no d parameter. More examples: GARCH in numbers Where it came from Robert Engle introduced ARCH in 1982: model today’s variance from past squared errors. His student Tim Bollerslev added past variances in 1986 and called it generalized ARCH — GARCH. Engle shared the 2003 Nobel Memorial Prize in Economics for methods of analysing time series whose volatility changes over time. The pattern he captured is that markets swing between calm spells and turbulent ones. Worked example: one day of GARCH(1, 1) Measure daily returns in percent. A fitted model has ω = 0.05, weight β = 0.85 on yesterday’s variance and weight γ = 0.10 on yesterday’s squared surprise. Yesterday’s variance estimate was 1.0 (a 1% standard deviation). Big move: return −3% σ² = 0.05 + 0.85 × 1.0 + 0.10 × 9 = 1.80 , so σ ≈ 1.34% Quiet day: return 0.5% σ² = 0.05 + 0.85 × 1.0 + 0.10 × 0.25 = 0.925 , so σ ≈ 0.96% One large surprise raises tomorrow’s expected spread by a third. That is volatility clustering expressed as an equation. Three more quantities fall straight out of the constants: Persistence β + γ = 0.95. It must be below 1 for the variance to settle; close to 1 means shocks linger. Long-run variance ω / (1 − β − γ) = 0.05 / 0.05 = 1.0, a 1% daily standard deviation. With about 252 trading days a year, that is roughly 1% × √252 ≈ 16% a year. Half-life of a shock ln(0.5) / ln(0.95) ≈ 13.5 trading days for the extra variance to halve — the same half-life idea as Lesson 3. The link back to Lesson 1 Drop ω and make the two weights add to 1, and GARCH(1, 1) becomes: σ t ² = λ·σ t−1 ² + (1 − λ)·ε t−1 ² That is exponential smoothing applied to squared returns — with λ playing the role of (1 − α). J.P. Morgan’s RiskMetrics system popularised this version in the 1990s with λ = 0.94 for daily data. Full GARCH adds ω, which pulls the variance back toward its long-run level over time. Exponential smoothing of squared returns has no long-run level to return to. How the forecast gets used: value at risk Banks use volatility forecasts like these in value-at-risk calculations — the loss a portfolio should exceed only with some small probability — which feed regulatory capital requirements. The Nobel committee’s own illustration: $1 million held in the S&P 500 on 31 July 2002 had, by such a model, a 99% chance of losing no more than about $61,500 the next day. calculation answered A GARCH(1, 1) has ω = 0.04, β = 0.90 on the past variance and γ = 0.05 on the past squared error. Yesterday’s variance was 0.8 and yesterday’s return was 2%. What is today’s variance, and what is the long-run variance? Today 0.96; long run 0.80 Today 0.86; long run 0.80 Today 0.96; long run 0.04 Today 3.64; long run 0.80 Check What happens to a GARCH(1, 1) forecast of variance many days ahead? Show answer It moves toward the long-run variance ω / (1 − β − γ), faster when persistence β + γ is smaller. A turbulent day raises the near-term forecast, but not the distant one."},{"id":"l7","h":"Stationarity and choosing p, d, q","t":"Lesson 7 Stationarity and choosing p, d, q Difference just enough to stop the drift, read the leftover correlation to suggest p and q, then let validation make the final call. Beyond the lectures The lecture says software fits the constants once you pick p, d and q. This lesson adds the standard ways of picking them. It draws on common forecasting practice, not on lecture content. Spotting non-stationarity The mean drifts — a trend, or a level that wanders and never comes back. The pattern repeats — seasonality means the expected value depends on the stage of the cycle. The spread changes — swings get bigger as the level rises, or come in bursts. Differencing fixes the first two. It does not fix the third. A spread that grows with the level is usually handled by taking logs first; a spread that comes in bursts is what GARCH models. How many differences? Difference once and look again. A straight-line trend disappears after one difference; a curving (quadratic) trend needs two. Series 3, 7, 11, 15, 19 1st differences 4, 4, 4, 4 — constant, so d = 1 is enough Stop as soon as the series looks stationary. Differencing more than needed adds noise and makes forecasts worse, so the usual choice is d = 0, 1, or occasionally 2. calculation answered A series runs 2, 5, 10, 17, 26, 37. What is the smallest d that makes it stationary? d = 0 d = 1 d = 2 d = 3 Check A series’ swings double in size as its level doubles. Will differencing make it stationary? Show answer Not on its own. Differencing removes a drifting mean, not a growing spread. Take logs first so a proportional change becomes a constant-size change, then difference if needed. Suggesting p and q Two plots of the differenced series do most of the work: ACF (autocorrelation function) How strongly the series correlates with itself k periods back, including indirect links through the periods in between. PACF (partial autocorrelation) The same, but with the in-between periods’ influence removed — only the direct link. Pattern ACF PACF AR(p) Dies away gradually Cuts off sharply after lag p MA(q) Cuts off sharply after lag q Dies away gradually Mixed ARMA Dies away Dies away — try small p and q and compare Real plots are rarely this clean, so treat them as a shortlist. Information criteria such as AIC and BIC then score each candidate on fit with a penalty for extra parameters. Automated searches, such as auto.arima in R, do exactly that. Whatever they pick, confirm it on held-out data — Lesson 8. diagnosis answered After one difference, the ACF fades slowly over many lags, while the PACF shows two large spikes and then nothing. Which model does that suggest? ARIMA(2, 1, 0) ARIMA(0, 1, 2) ARIMA(2, 2, 0) ARIMA(0, 1, 0) Class discussion — Lesson 7 click to type; saves automatically Worth noticing Stock prices usually need d = 1. What is GARCH normally fitted to, and how does that relate? Show answer To returns — the period-to-period percentage change in price. A return is essentially a first difference of the log price. So GARCH “needs no d” partly because the differencing was done before the data reached it. More examples: finding the airline model from the data Here is the Lesson 7 procedure run on the real airline series. These correlations were computed for this page. Look at the raw series. Its autocorrelations start at 0.95 and fade very slowly (still 0.71 at lag 13). That slow fade is the signature of a trend — not stationary. Fix the growing swing first. Take logs, so a proportional seasonal effect becomes a constant-size one. Take one ordinary difference. The trend goes, but a huge spike remains at lag 12 (0.84), and again at lag 24 (0.74). Each month still looks most like the same month last year — the season is still in the data. Take a seasonal difference as well (this month minus the same month last year). Now almost every bar sits inside the noise band, except two negative spikes: lag 1 (−0.34) and lag 12 (−0.39). Read the spikes. An ACF that cuts off after lag 1 suggests one MA term. A spike at exactly lag 12 suggests one seasonal MA term. With one ordinary and one seasonal difference, that is ARIMA(0, 1, 1)(0, 1, 1) 12 — the airline model from Lesson 5. Autocorrelation of the log airline series after one difference (left) and after an added seasonal difference (right). Bars outside the grey band are larger than pure noise would typically produce. The log step matters for Holt-Winters too. Fitting additive Holt-Winters to the logged series and converting back gave a 1959–60 MAPE of 2.2% (RMSE 14) — far better than either version on the raw counts in Lesson 4. Logging turned the multiplicative pattern into a clean additive one. diagnosis answered Monthly ice-cream sales, after one ordinary difference, still show an ACF spike of 0.8 at lag 12. What should you do next? Take a second ordinary difference (d = 2) Take a seasonal difference at lag 12 Add 12 autoregressive terms (p = 12) Nothing — a spike at one lag is just noise Check Why take logs before differencing in the airline example, rather than after? Show answer Differences of the raw counts would still have swings that grow over time. Taking logs first makes those swings a constant size, so the differences that follow can be stationary. A log difference is also roughly a percentage change, which is easy to interpret."},{"id":"l8","h":"Judging a forecast","t":"Lesson 8 Judging a forecast Validate in time order, score errors in units people understand, and always check you beat the lazy forecast. Beyond the lectures The lecture points to the validation approaches from earlier modules and says time series quality measures are covered later. This lesson shows how the earlier approaches change when order matters. It follows standard forecasting practice. Why ordinary cross-validation cheats Random k-fold cross-validation shuffles rows into folds. With time series, that means training on March and July to “predict” May. Neighbouring periods are strongly related, so the model has effectively seen the answer. The error it reports is optimistic, sometimes wildly so. The fix: always train on the past and test on the future. The simplest version holds out the last stretch of data. A better version repeats that many times: put in order solved Put the steps of walk-forward validation in order. Fit the model on the first stretch of data, say periods 1 to 40 Forecast period 41 Record the error against what actually happened in period 41 Add period 41 to the training data and refit Repeat until the end of the data is reached Average the recorded errors and compare candidate models on that average Check order Exam target In one sentence, what rule must any time series validation scheme obey? Show answer Every forecast being scored must be made using only data from before the period it forecasts. Scoring the errors With error e t = F t − x t : MAE — mean absolute error Average size of the miss, in the data’s own units. Easy to explain. RMSE — root mean squared error Square root of the average squared miss. Punishes big misses more; matches the squared-error fitting in Lesson 4. MAPE — mean absolute percentage error Average of |e t | / x t . Unit-free, so it compares across series — but breaks down when any actual value is zero or near zero. Three periods, actuals 100, 110, 90; forecasts 105, 100, 95. Errors: +5, −10, +5. MAE = (5 + 10 + 5) / 3 ≈ 6.67 RMSE = √((25 + 100 + 25) / 3) = √50 ≈ 7.07 MAPE ≈ 6.5% Note that the plain average error is (5 − 10 + 5) / 3 = 0. Signed errors cancel, which is why accuracy is never measured on them — though their average is a useful check for bias. The benchmarks to beat Naive: F t+1 = x t . Next period equals this period. That is ARIMA(0, 1, 0), or exponential smoothing with α = 1. Seasonal naive: F t+1 = x (t+1)−L . Next Monday equals last Monday. A model that can’t beat these on walk-forward validation hasn’t earned its complexity. Finally, check what the model leaves behind. If the model has captured the pattern, the leftover errors should look like white noise — ARIMA(0, 0, 0). Remaining correlation means there is structure left to model. calculation trap answered A forecaster’s errors over four weeks are +8, −8, +6, −6. They report “average error: zero — the model is perfect.” What are the MAE and RMSE? MAE 0, RMSE 0 MAE 7, RMSE ≈ 7.07 MAE 7, RMSE 50 MAE 28, RMSE ≈ 14.1 ML Marcus Lee Analyst, city water utility Marcus has four years of daily water demand and three candidate models. His usual workflow is 10-fold cross-validation, and on it his ARIMA model scores an RMSE of 2.1 million litres. What should he do before reporting that number? Report it — 10-fold cross-validation is the standard method. Redo the comparison with walk-forward validation, in time order. Switch to 20 folds for a more stable estimate. The honest numbers. Walk-forward RMSE: ARIMA 3.4 million, Holt-Winters 3.1 million, and a seasonal naive forecast (same day last week) 3.2 million. Choose ARIMA, since it is the most flexible model. Choose Holt-Winters, and report how narrowly it beats the seasonal naive benchmark. Class discussion — Lesson 8 click to type; saves automatically Check Why is MAPE a poor choice for daily sales of a slow-moving spare part? Show answer Many days have zero sales, and MAPE divides by the actual value. On those days it is undefined, and near-zero days blow it up. MAE or a scaled error measure works better. More examples: what large forecasting competitions found Since 1982, Spyros Makridakis has run the “M” competitions, which test many forecasting methods on the same large set of real series. The fourth, M4, finished in 2018 with 100,000 series and 61 methods. Pure machine-learning methods did worse than standard statistical ones such as ARIMA and exponential smoothing, and worse still than combinations of them. The winner was a hybrid. Slawek Smyl, then at Uber, combined exponential smoothing with a recurrent neural network: the smoothing handled level and season, and the network learned the rest. The key benchmark was a simple average. “Comb” averaged the forecasts of single, Holt and damped-trend exponential smoothing. M4 method (all 100,000 series) sMAPE Single exponential smoothing 13.09% Holt (with trend) 13.77% Damped-trend smoothing 12.66% Comb — the average of the three 12.55% Two surprises for a student. Adding a trend hurt on average: Holt projects the latest trend forever, and real trends tend to fade. The damped version shrinks the trend a little each step ahead and did better. And averaging three simple methods beat each of them — combining forecasts is one of the most reliable improvements in the field. The airline comparison in Lesson 4 used a single 24-month holdout — one forecast origin. Walk-forward validation, with many origins, would give a steadier ranking, especially where the models are as close as the two Holt-Winters versions were. interpretation answered In M4, Holt’s trend method was less accurate overall than single exponential smoothing, and damped-trend smoothing beat both. What is the most likely explanation? Holt’s method has too many parameters to fit reliably. Real trends tend to fade, so projecting them forever overshoots; damping limits that. Almost none of the series had any trend. sMAPE unfairly penalises trend methods. Worth noticing Why might averaging three forecasts beat the best one of them? Show answer Their errors are partly independent, so they partly cancel. The average is also safer: you don’t have to know in advance which method will be best for a given series, and picking wrong is costly."},{"id":"l9","h":"Midterm and final-style questions","t":"Exam workout Midterm and final-style questions Short calculations, “explain and justify” prompts where the reasoning carries the marks, and multi-part cases where each part builds on the last. How to use this Attempt each question before opening the answer. Recognising a correct answer is much easier than producing one, and exams test producing. Each model answer ends with marking notes on where the marks usually sit. Section A — Multiple choice, midterm style midterm · calculation answered A Holt model (no seasonality) ends the training data with S t = 50 and T t = 2. Using the standard multi-step form, what is the forecast three periods ahead? 52 56 50 54 midterm · calculation answered With α = 0.3, what weight does the observation from two periods ago, x t−2 , carry in S t ? 0.490 0.210 0.147 0.090 midterm · interpretation answered Software fits single exponential smoothing to a series by minimising squared error and returns α = 0.96. What does that suggest? The series is extremely noisy. Movements tend to persist, so the best forecast is close to the latest observation. The optimizer failed, since α should never exceed 0.5. The series has a strong seasonal cycle. midterm · calculation answered Monthly values: 4, 6, 10, 16, 24, 34. What order of differencing makes this series stationary, and what constant does it reach? d = 1, constant 2 d = 2, constant 2 d = 2, constant 4 d = 3, constant 0 final · concept trap answered Which statement about ARIMA and exponential smoothing is false ? Basic exponential smoothing is a special case of ARIMA. Both are mainly short-term forecasting tools. Because ARIMA is more general, it always forecasts at least as well as exponential smoothing. ARIMA tends to work better on stable data with few outliers. final · method choice answered For which question is GARCH the most natural fit? How many meals will the hospital kitchen serve next Tuesday? How much will a currency’s daily returns swing over the next month? Has the average defect rate on a production line shifted? What will a new product sell next quarter, given 18 months of history? final · critical thinking answered A team compares five forecasting models with random 5-fold cross-validation on three years of daily data. What is the main problem? Five folds is too few; ten would be reliable. Training folds contain days after the test days, so future information leaks in. Three years is too little data to validate anything. Cross-validation can only be used to fit one model at a time. final · interpretation answered Why does a single-smoothing forecast stay flat as the horizon grows, while its prediction interval widens? α shrinks as the horizon grows. The best guess stays S t , but each extra unseen period adds more uncertainty. The trend term cancels out over long horizons. The seasonal factors are unknown for later periods. How to read the options Several wrong answers above are the right number for a neighbouring question. Why? Show answer Examiners build wrong answers from specific slips: the one-step instead of three-step forecast, the weight on t−1 instead of t−2, the MSE instead of the RMSE. If your answer appears as an option, check that it answers the question actually asked. Section B — Short answer and discussion, final style Each of these is worth roughly 4–8 marks in a typical paper. Write the answer out before opening it. B1 · Explain the name. Starting from S t = αx t + (1 − α)S t−1 , show why the method is called “exponential smoothing”, and state what the expanded form reveals about how past data is used. Model answer Smoothing: with 0 < α < 1, a high reading is weighted by only α and pulled down by the older baseline; a low reading is pulled up the same way. Peaks and valleys are damped. Exponential: substitute S t−1 = αx t−1 + (1 − α)S t−2 and keep going: S t = αx t + α(1−α)x t−1 + α(1−α)²x t−2 + … The weight on the observation k periods back is α(1−α) k — an exponent that rises by one each step back. What it reveals: every past observation contributes, not just the latest; and because 1 − α < 1, newer observations get more weight than older ones. Marking notes. Most marks go to the substitution and the general weight α(1−α) k . Saying “older data gets less weight” without showing the substitution usually earns about half. Pointing out that the one-line equation hides the older data is what separates good answers from complete ones. B2 · Compute by hand. With α = 0.5, smooth the series 10, 14, 12, 18. Give S 1 to S 4 , the forecast for period 5, and the sum of squared one-step errors for periods 2 to 4. Model answer S 1 = 10 (starting condition). S 2 = 0.5·14 + 0.5·10 = 12. S 3 = 0.5·12 + 0.5·12 = 12. S 4 = 0.5·18 + 0.5·12 = 15. F 5 = S 4 = 15. One-step forecasts are F 2 = S 1 = 10, F 3 = S 2 = 12, F 4 = S 3 = 12. Errors: 10 − 14 = −4, 12 − 12 = 0, 12 − 18 = −6. Sum of squares = 16 + 0 + 36 = 52. Marking notes. The common slip is scoring S t against x t . S t has already seen x t , so that isn’t a forecast. The error for period t uses the forecast made at t − 1. Stating S 1 = x 1 explicitly earns its own mark. B3 · Explain Holt-Winters. Write the three update equations for triple exponential smoothing with multiplicative seasonality. Explain what each term does, why C t−L is used, and give the starting conditions. Model answer S t = α(x t /C t−L ) + (1−α)(S t−1 + T t−1 ) — blend the de-seasonalised observation with last period’s baseline projected forward by the trend. T t = β(S t − S t−1 ) + (1−β)T t−1 — blend the observed change in baseline with the previous trend. C t = γ(x t /S t ) + (1−γ)C t−L — blend this period’s observed ratio to baseline with the previous factor for the same stage. C t−L is the most recent factor from the same point in the cycle (last Monday for a Monday). Starting conditions: S 1 = x 1 , T 1 = 0, and C 1 … C L = 1. Marking notes. Two details are commonly missed: the trend is added to S t−1 inside the (1 − α) term, and the observation is divided by the factor. Starting conditions for all three components are usually a separate mark. B4 · Choosing α. Explain the trade-off α controls. How should the amount of randomness in a system guide it, and how is it chosen in practice? Model answer α trades trust in the new reading against trust in the existing baseline. A large α reacts fast to real changes but also chases noise. A small α filters noise but is slow to recognise a real shift. High randomness means most fluctuations are noise, so α should be small. Low randomness means fluctuations probably reflect real change, so α should be large. In practice α (with β and γ) is chosen by minimising the sum of squared one-step forecast errors over the training data, subject to staying between 0 and 1 — and the result is then checked on held-out data. Marking notes. Full marks name both costs — noise-chasing and lag. Answers that say only “small α for noisy data” miss what the small α costs. B5 · ARIMA in words. Explain the three components of ARIMA(p, d, q), and give three special cases with the models they correspond to. Model answer d — integrated: difference the data d times so a non-stationary series becomes stationary. p — autoregressive: predict the differenced value from its own previous p values. q — moving average: also use the previous q forecast errors as predictors. Special cases: ARIMA(0,0,0) is white noise; ARIMA(0,1,0) is a random walk; ARIMA(0,1,1) is basic exponential smoothing. (Also acceptable: ARIMA(p,0,0) is a pure AR model; ARIMA(0,0,q) is a pure MA model.) Marking notes. A key mark is saying that the autoregression runs on the differences , not the raw data. Naming ARIMA(0,1,1) as exponential smoothing is the special case examiners most often look for, because it links the two halves of the module. B6 · GARCH. What does GARCH estimate, why would a business want that, and how does its structure differ from ARIMA’s? Model answer GARCH estimates and forecasts the variance of a time series. Variance tells you how far a forecast might miss, which matters for planning (production, stock). In investing it stands in for volatility, which portfolio optimization balances against expected return. Structurally it resembles ARIMA with two changes: it models variances and squared errors rather than values and plain errors, and it does not difference, so there is no d — the model is GARCH(p, q). Marking notes. Many answers explain the structure and skip the why . The portfolio-risk use is the example most papers expect. Giving the reason there is no d, not just the fact, earns the last mark. B7 · Validation. Why is shuffled k-fold cross-validation inappropriate for most time series, and what should replace it? Model answer Shuffling places later observations in training for earlier test points. Neighbouring periods are strongly related, so the model is partly shown the answer, and the validation error is optimistic. Use walk-forward validation: fit on data up to time t, forecast t + 1, record the error, add t + 1 to training, and repeat. Average the errors and compare models on that. Compare against a naive or seasonal naive benchmark. Marking notes. The mark sits in the mechanism — “future information leaks into training” — not in “order matters.” Mentioning the naive benchmark shows you know the error needs a reference point. B8 · Choosing a method. You have 28 months of spiky monthly sales. A colleague insists on ARIMA because it “includes exponential smoothing as a special case.” Respond. Model answer Being more general means ARIMA can represent smoothing, not that it will forecast better. It has more parameters to estimate, and the rule of thumb is about 40 data points — 28 is short. ARIMA also tends to do best on stable data, and this series is spiky. Start with exponential smoothing, fit a few low-order ARIMA models, and let walk-forward validation against a naive benchmark decide. Marking notes. Full marks need both lecture criteria — data size and stability — plus a validation-based way to settle it. “Generality is not accuracy” is the core point. Where the marks hide Across B2 and B7, what is the shared idea? Show answer A forecast only counts if it was made before the answer was known. Scoring S t against x t and shuffled cross-validation both break that rule, in different places. Section C — Multi-part cases, final style C1 · Emergency department arrivals. A hospital records patient arrivals every hour for two years. Arrivals peak each evening, and the evening peak has grown as overall demand has risen. (a) What is L? (b) State the starting conditions for Holt-Winters. (c) Additive or multiplicative seasonality — justify. (d) Describe a validation plan. (e) The staffing manager asks how many extra nurses to roster “just in case.” What do you need to estimate, and with what? Model answer (a) L = 24 for the daily cycle in hourly data. (A weekly pattern would also exist; basic Holt-Winters handles one cycle, so note that as a limitation.) (b) S 1 = x 1 , T 1 = 0, and the first 24 seasonal factors set to 1. (c) Multiplicative. The evening peak grows as the level grows, so the seasonal effect is proportional rather than a fixed number of patients. (d) Walk-forward: fit on the first year or so, forecast the next hour or day, record the error, roll forward, repeat. Compare against a seasonal naive forecast (same hour yesterday or last week) using MAE, which staff can read directly as “patients per hour.” (e) The variance of the forecast error — how far actual arrivals may run above forecast. If that variance changes over time (bursty periods), a GARCH-type model estimates it; the extra staffing follows from that spread. Marking notes. (c) earns its marks only with the justification “the peak grows with the level.” (e) is the link between the value half and the variance half of the module; answers that just propose a larger point forecast miss it. C2 · Diagnosing a model from its symptoms. Name the likely cause and the fix. (a) Fitted α is 0.99 and the model barely beats a naive forecast. (b) A model on monthly data leaves errors strongly correlated at lag 12. (c) ARIMA with d = 2 on a series with a straight-line trend gives erratic forecasts. (d) Shuffled cross-validation RMSE is half the walk-forward RMSE. Model answer (a) The series behaves close to a random walk, so the latest value is nearly the best forecast. Not a bug — but it means little predictable structure. Report the naive benchmark alongside the model. (b) Unmodelled yearly seasonality. Add a seasonal component — Holt-Winters with L = 12, or seasonal ARIMA. (c) Over-differencing. One difference removes a straight-line trend; the second only adds noise. Use d = 1. (d) Leakage from future periods into training. Trust the walk-forward figure. Marking notes. Each part rewards naming the mechanism . For (a), saying “α is too high, lower it” loses marks — α was chosen by the data. For (c), the phrase “over-differencing” plus the reason earns the full mark. C3 · Two funds. Funds A and B have the same forecast return next month. Over the past year, A’s daily returns were calm for months then very volatile for weeks; B’s were steadily moderate. (a) Which method forecasts each fund’s variance, and why? (b) Why does “calm then volatile” matter for that method? (c) A risk-averse investor must choose one. What would you tell them, and what is the limit of your advice? Model answer (a) GARCH, fitted to each fund’s returns. The question is about variance, which is what GARCH estimates and forecasts. (b) A’s variance changes over time and depends on recent history — conditional heteroscedasticity. GARCH uses recent variances and squared errors, so its forecast reflects whether A is currently in a calm or volatile spell. A single historical variance would average those spells and misstate next month’s risk either way. (c) With equal expected return, the fund with lower forecast variance is the better fit for a risk-averse investor; that depends on A’s current state. The limit: these are model estimates, variance is only a proxy for risk, and this is analysis, not financial advice. Marking notes. (b) is the core of the question: a constant variance estimate is wrong for A because of the clustering. In (c), answers that always pick B without checking A’s current regime lose the mark. Exam workout — my working click to type; saves automatically Where the marks hide Across all three cases, which habit earns the most marks? Show answer Asking whether the question wants the value or the variance before choosing a method. C1(e) and all of C3 turn on it, and it is the easiest distinction to overlook under time pressure."},{"id":"targets","h":"Transfer practice — Time series","t":"Transfer practice — Time series Same ideas, unfamiliar settings Why these are here Each prompt below moves the module’s ideas into a situation the lessons never mentioned. If you can only answer a question in the wording it was taught in, what you have memorised is the example rather than the concept — and an exam that changes the setting will find that out. Answer these out loud before opening them. Exam target Daily website traffic shows a weekly pattern and a slowly growing baseline. Which formulation, and what is L? Show answer Holt-Winters — baseline, trend and seasonality — with L = 7, since the cycle repeats every seven observations. Simple exponential smoothing would lag the growth, and adding trend alone would leave the day-of-week pattern in the residuals. Exam target The size of the weekly swing grows as overall traffic grows. Additive or multiplicative seasonality? Show answer Multiplicative. Additive seasonality assumes each day adds a fixed quantity regardless of the level; multiplicative assumes each day runs at a fixed ratio to it. A swing that scales with the series is the second pattern, and forcing the first will under-predict peaks late in the series and over-predict them early. Exam target Your forecast lags every turning point by several periods. Which parameter, and which direction? Show answer Raise α, so more weight falls on recent observations and the memory is shorter. The weight on the observation k periods back is α(1−α)ⁱ; a small α spreads weight far into the past, which smooths noise and makes the estimate slow to acknowledge genuine change. The cost is that the forecast also reacts more to noise. Exam target Fitting by minimising squared error on a visibly noisy series gives α = 0.95. What should you suspect? Show answer That the fit is chasing noise. An α near 1 makes the forecast close to the naive last-value rule, which can genuinely minimise one-step-ahead error on a noisy series while forecasting nothing useful further out. Check it against the naive benchmark on held-out data, and fit at the horizon you actually forecast over. Exam target A forecaster is evaluated with shuffled ten-fold cross-validation and scores well. Why is that meaningless? Show answer Most folds train on periods that come after the periods being predicted, and adjacent periods are related, so the model usually has both neighbours of a held-out point in its training data. Interpolating between known values is a much easier task than extrapolating past the end of what is known, which is what forecasting is. Exam target Your model's mean absolute error beats the seasonal naive rule by 1%. What does that tell you? Show answer That the model has barely earned its complexity. The seasonal naive rule — this period equals the same period last cycle — requires no fitting, no parameters and no maintenance. A 1% improvement is unlikely to justify the cost of building, validating and running the alternative, and may not survive on the next stretch of data. Exam target A team wants to forecast how volatile next week's returns will be, not what the returns will be. Which tool? Show answer A GARCH-type model, which forecasts the variance rather than the level. ARIMA and exponential smoothing both target the value of the series. When volatility clusters — calm stretches followed by turbulent ones — and the decision depends on the size of the uncertainty, it is the second quantity that matters."},{"id":"sec-11","h":"How the module fits together","t":"How the module fits together The through-line across all eight lessons, plus the worked examples inside each Lesson 1 Separates signal from noise with one weighted average and one dial, α. Lesson 2 Gives trend and seasonality the same treatment, with β, γ and their own starting values. Lesson 3 Unrolls the equation to show every past point counts, with exponentially shrinking weight. Lesson 4 Turns the smoother into a forecaster, and fits its constants by minimising squared error. Lesson 5 Generalises all of it into ARIMA, with smoothing as the (0, 1, 1) special case. Lesson 6 Points the same machinery at variance, so a forecast can come with its uncertainty. Lesson 7 Shows how to pick d by differencing and suggest p and q from correlation plots. Lesson 8 Adapts validation to time order and adds the naive benchmark every model must beat. Things worth noticing One idea, three times. Baseline, trend and seasonal factor are each “new evidence blended with old belief.” Learn one equation and you have the pattern for all three. α is Module 2’s bias–variance dial again. Small α: stable but slow (bias). Large α: responsive but noisy (variance). It is chosen the same way — by error on data the fit didn’t see. Every model here nests inside the next. Single smoothing is ARIMA(0, 1, 1); the naive forecast is ARIMA(0, 1, 0); good residuals are ARIMA(0, 0, 0). ARIMA is the umbrella. Value and variance are different questions. Smoothing and ARIMA answer “how much?”; GARCH answers “how sure?”. Many real decisions — stock, staffing, portfolios — need both. This module sits next to Module 6. CUSUM asks whether the level has shifted; exponential smoothing tracks where the level is . Both separate real change from noise, for different decisions. Likely exam targets Write S t = αx t + (1 − α)S t−1 , its starting condition, and explain how randomness should guide α. Write the Holt-Winters equations with multiplicative seasonality, and all three starting conditions. Explain why C t−L , not C t−1 , appears in the baseline update. Interpret a seasonal factor and remove it from an observation. Derive the unrolled form and give the weight on x t−k . Explain both halves of the name “exponential smoothing.” Derive F t+1 = S t , and give the forecasts with trend and with seasonality. State how α, β and γ are fitted, including the constraints. Define stationarity and explain what differencing does. Explain p, d and q, and name the ARIMA equivalents of white noise, a random walk and basic exponential smoothing. State when ARIMA tends to beat exponential smoothing, and the rough data requirement. Explain what GARCH estimates, why variance matters, and why GARCH has no d. Explain why shuffled cross-validation is wrong for time series and describe walk-forward validation. Compute MAE and RMSE, and say why signed errors can’t measure accuracy. Convert α into a half-life or an average age of the data. Rewrite smoothing in error-correction form and explain why it is ARIMA(0, 1, 1). Update a GARCH(1, 1) variance by hand, and give its persistence and long-run variance. Read an ACF after differencing and name the model it suggests, as with the airline data. Explain why a fitted α of 1 can signal a missing trend or seasonal component. Module 7 — my own summary click to type; saves automatically Written by an AI model from lecture recordings. Not reviewed by any instructor or institution — verify anything you rely on against the original lectures. Examples here are generic illustrations rather than the ones used in class, and every worked number and chart is invented or simulated for illustration. The Dana Reyes and Marcus Lee cases, the multi-step Holt forecast note in Lesson 4, the written-out ARIMA and GARCH equations, the additive-versus-multiplicative guidance, the “things worth noticing” list, and Lessons 7 to 9 in their entirety are this summary’s own additions rather than lecture content. They follow standard forecasting practice, in particular Forecasting: Principles and Practice (Hyndman & Athanasopoulos). The “More examples” sections are also additions. The airline passenger figures are Box & Jenkins’ Series G; every model fitted to them on this page (Holt-Winters, single smoothing, the airline model, the correlation plots) was computed for this page with simple code, so results from R or Python packages will differ slightly because of different starting values and fitting methods. Other sources: RFC 6298 (TCP timers); the Nobel Prize committee’s 2003 materials on Engle; Bollerslev (1986); Makridakis, Spiliotis & Assimakopoulos on the M4 competition (2018); Taylor (2003) on double seasonal smoothing; published summaries of the Mauna Loa CO₂ record. The TCP, GARCH, shop and warehouse numbers are illustrative. The lecture showed the ARIMA and GARCH equations only as images, so the notation here may differ from the slides; sign conventions and the order of GARCH’s parameters also vary between software packages."}]},{"f":"module-8-basic-regression.html","t":"Module 8: Basic Regression","s":[{"id":"l1","h":"Introduction to regression","t":"Lesson 1 Introduction to regression Regression estimates a response from a set of predictors. The linear version looks for the line — or, with more predictors, the flat surface — whose squared errors add up to as little as possible. Two kinds of question Regression A model that uses a set of factors, called predictors, to estimate a numeric quantity called the response. There are many variations and add-ons; this module covers the basic linear version. Regression answers two different kinds of question, and it is worth knowing which one you are asking before you fit anything. Question type What you want out of it How does the system work? The size of each factor’s effect — how much an extra year of experience is worth in salary, which features matter most in a purchase decision, how strongly an economic indicator moves an outcome. What will happen? A predicted value for a case you haven’t observed yet — next quarter’s electricity demand, the selling price of a house not yet on the market, the remaining life of a machine part. Every use shares the same shape: a set of predictors that you know, and a response you are trying to estimate. quick check answered A hospital wants to know how much each additional nurse on a ward shift changes the rate of patient falls. Which kind of regression question is this? A prediction question — it forecasts next month’s fall rate. A “how does the system work” question — the answer is the size of one factor’s effect. A prescriptive question — it tells the hospital how many nurses to hire. Check 1 Name the two kinds of question regression answers, and say what the “answer” is in each case. Show answer How a system works: the answer is the coefficients — the size of each factor’s effect. What will happen: the answer is the predicted response for a new data point. Simple linear regression Simple linear regression Linear regression with a single predictor. The relationship between predictor and response is assumed to be a straight line. y = a₀ + a₁x₁ One predictor. a₀ is the intercept — the predicted response when x₁ is zero — and a₁ is the slope, the change in predicted response for each one-unit increase in x₁. y = a₀ + Σⱼ₌₁..ₘ aⱼxⱼ m predictors. The picture is no longer a line but a flat surface (a hyperplane) in m + 1 dimensions. Drawings stay two-dimensional only because that is what fits on a page. Five areas are clickable. Open all five to finish this activity. Check 2 In y = a₀ + a₁x₁, which symbols are data and which are what the regression finds? Show answer x₁ and y come from the data. a₀ and a₁ are the unknowns — the regression coefficients — chosen so the line fits the data as closely as possible. What “fits best” means For each data point, the error is the gap between the true response and the model’s estimate. Square each error and add them up; the line with the smallest total is the best fit. eᵢ = yᵢ − ŷᵢ = yᵢ − (a₀ + a₁xᵢ₁) The error for data point i. yᵢ is the observed response, ŷᵢ (“y-hat”) the regression’s estimate. SSE = Σᵢ₌₁..ₙ ( yᵢ − (a₀ + Σⱼ aⱼxᵢⱼ) )² The sum of squared errors over all n data points. As the line turns and slides, a₀ and a₁ change and so does this total. Best-fit regression line The values of a₀, a₁, …, aₘ that minimise the sum of squared errors. Why squared errors rather than some other measure is the subject of Lesson 2. How the minimum is found The sum of squared errors is a convex function of the coefficients — bowl-shaped, with a single lowest point. Take the partial derivative of the SSE with respect to each coefficient. Set every partial derivative equal to zero. Solve those equations simultaneously. The solution is the minimum — the best-fit coefficients. quick check answered Why do we square the errors before adding them, rather than simply adding the errors? Positive and negative errors would cancel out, so a terrible line could score zero. Squaring makes the numbers bigger and easier to compare. Absolute values can’t be computed by software. Class discussion — Lesson 1 click to type; saves automatically Check 3 Why is it enough to set the partial derivatives to zero, rather than having to check every candidate line? Show answer Because the SSE is convex: it has one minimum and no misleading local dips. Where every partial derivative is zero, the bowl is at its bottom, so solving those equations gives the global best fit directly."},{"id":"l2","h":"Likelihood and information criteria","t":"Lesson 2 Likelihood and information criteria Likelihood measures how well a model fits. AIC and BIC charge it for every parameter it uses, so that extra complexity has to earn its place. Likelihood Likelihood For a given set of parameter values, the probability — strictly, the probability density — of seeing the data we actually observed, under an assumed model of the errors. It is the most basic measure of model quality. Maximum likelihood The set of parameters that gives the highest likelihood. That set is the best-fit set of parameters, and the likelihood it reaches is written L*. Lecture vs standard practice — which way round the probability runs Lecture: describes likelihood as the probability that the model would generate the estimates it does, treating the observed data as the correct values. Standard definition: the probability (density) of the observed data given the parameters. The lecture’s worked derivation below uses exactly this form — the probability of observing zᵢ if the true value is yᵢ — so the two agree once the math starts. Learn the standard wording; it is what textbooks and exams use. Check 4 What is being varied, and what is being held fixed, when you maximise a likelihood? Show answer The data are held fixed — they are what was observed. The parameters are varied, and you keep the set that makes the observed data most probable. Why least squares is maximum likelihood Assume the errors are normally distributed with mean 0 and variance σ², and independent from one data point to the next. Let z₁ … zₙ be the observed values and y₁ … yₙ the model’s estimates. P(zᵢ | yᵢ) = ( 1 / (σ√(2π)) ) · exp( −(zᵢ − yᵢ)² / (2σ²) ) Probability density of observing zᵢ if the true value is yᵢ. L = Πᵢ P(zᵢ | yᵢ) = ( 1 / (σ√(2π)) )ⁿ · exp( −(1/(2σ²)) · Σᵢ (zᵢ − yᵢ)² ) Independent errors, so the joint probability is the product — which collapses into a single exponential. The exponential gets larger as its exponent gets larger, so maximising L means maximising the exponent. Strip the constants and what’s left is minus the sum of squared errors . Maximising that is the same as minimising the SSE. maximise L ⇔ minimise Σᵢ ( zᵢ − (a₀ + Σⱼ aⱼxᵢⱼ) )² Substituting the regression estimate for yᵢ. With independent, identically distributed normal errors, least squares is the maximum likelihood fit. put in order solved Put the steps of the argument that least squares is maximum likelihood in order. Drag, or use the arrows. Assume errors are normal with mean 0 and variance σ², independent across data points Write the normal density of observing each zᵢ when the true value is yᵢ Multiply the n densities together, because the errors are independent Combine the product into one exponential, and note that it is largest when its exponent is largest Drop the constants, leaving the negative sum of squared errors Conclude that minimising the sum of squared errors gives the maximum likelihood parameters Check order Likelihood can also compare two models directly: take the likelihood ratio and run a hypothesis test on it. The conditions that test needs are in Extra 2. Beyond simple regression — other estimating formulas, other error assumptions — maximum likelihood gets complex and may not have an easy solution. Good statistical software handles much of that for you. Check 5 Which two assumptions about the errors make least squares equivalent to maximum likelihood? Show answer They are normally distributed (with mean 0 and a common variance), and they are independent of one another — together, independent and identically distributed normal errors. Drop either one and minimising the SSE is no longer guaranteed to be the maximum likelihood fit. Trading fit against complexity: AIC Adding parameters can always improve the fit — but it can also lead to overfitting , where the extra parameters fit random effects rather than real ones. The information criteria balance the two. AIC = 2k − 2 ln(L*) Akaike information criterion. L* is the maximum likelihood value, k the number of parameters being estimated. 2k is the penalty term. Smaller AIC is preferred. AIC = n · ln( SSE / n ) + 2k (+ a constant) The regression version, with normal errors substituted into the likelihood. The constant is the same for every model fitted to the same data, so it never changes which model wins. Making AIC smaller pushes two ways at once: toward higher likelihood and toward fewer parameters . The underlying math comes from information theory. AICc = AIC + 2k(k + 1) / (n − k − 1) Corrected AIC. AIC’s nice properties hold as the number of data points goes to infinity; the correction term handles the ordinary case of a finite dataset. It shrinks toward zero as n grows. Lecture vs standard practice — how many parameters k counts Lecture: for regression, k = m + 1 — one for each coefficient a₀ … aₘ. Common software practice: the error variance σ² is also estimated, so many packages (R’s AIC() on a linear model, for example) count k = m + 2. Every model on the same data gets the same extra 1, so AIC differences — the only thing you compare — come out the same either way. Just don’t compare a hand-computed AIC to a software-reported one. Check 6 Adding a parameter raises ln(L*) by 0.6. Does AIC go up or down? Show answer Up. The penalty rises by 2, and the fit term falls by 2 × 0.6 = 1.2, a net increase of 0.8. The parameter improved the fit, but not by enough to pay for itself. Under AIC, an extra parameter has to raise ln(L*) by more than 1 to be worth keeping. Comparing two AIC values The gap between two models’ AIC values converts into a relative probability that one is better than the other. relative likelihood = exp( (AIC low − AIC high ) / 2 ) How likely the higher-AIC model is to be the better one, relative to the lower-AIC model. Example: two models differ by 4 in AIC. exp(−4/2) = e −2 ≈ 0.135, so the higher-AIC model is about 13.5% as likely as the lower-AIC one to be the better model. Lecture vs standard practice — the exponent The recording’s spoken version of this formula comes through garbled in the transcript. Its own worked number, though — a 5-point AIC gap giving a relative likelihood of 8.2% — is exactly e −2.5 , which is the standard formula above with the difference halved. Learn it as exp(−ΔAIC / 2). quick check answered Model A has AIC 318; model B has AIC 324. Roughly how likely is B, relative to A, to be the better model? About 37% About 98% — the two values are nearly the same size. About 5% About 0.25% Check 7 Why can you compare AIC values between two models on the same data, but not between two models fitted to different datasets? Show answer The likelihood depends on the data. A different dataset — more points, different values — changes ln(L*) for reasons that have nothing to do with the model, so the AIC gap no longer measures which model is better. BIC: a heavier penalty BIC = k · ln(n) − 2 ln(L*) Bayesian information criterion. Same structure as AIC, but the penalty per parameter is ln(n) rather than 2. Smaller is preferred. Because ln(n) exceeds 2 once n is 8 or more, BIC’s penalty is generally larger than AIC’s, and it tends to favour models with fewer parameters. BIC difference Smaller-BIC model is … more than 10 very likely better 6 to 10 likely better 2 to 6 somewhat likely better 0 to 2 only slightly likely better That table is a rule of thumb. It is an inexact science, and there is no hard-and-fast rule for choosing between AIC, BIC or plain maximum likelihood. For readers with a statistics background, AIC comes from a frequentist point of view and BIC from a Bayesian one. All three give valuable information, and looking at them together helps you choose among the models you are testing. Lecture vs standard practice — when BIC “breaks down” Lecture: BIC breaks down as the number of data points gets close to the number of parameters, so only use it when you have a lot more data than parameters. Standard view: the advice is right — BIC’s justification is a large-sample approximation, so it is unreliable when n is not much bigger than k. But the formula itself doesn’t misbehave there; the criterion whose formula literally blows up as n approaches k + 1 is AICc, through its n − k − 1 denominator. Both point to the same practical rule: with few data points per parameter, trust none of these numbers very far. match the pairs matched Click a measure, then what it does. Measure Maximum likelihood AIC AICc BIC Likelihood ratio What it does Picks the parameters that make the observed data most probable, with no charge for complexity Charges 2 per parameter against the log-likelihood Adds a correction for datasets that are not infinitely large Charges ln(n) per parameter, usually more than AIC Compares two models’ likelihoods directly with a hypothesis test quick check answered On 500 data points, AIC prefers a 9-parameter model and BIC prefers a 6-parameter model. What is the most sensible reading? One of the two criteria has been computed incorrectly. Expected: BIC penalises each parameter more heavily at this n, so the three extra parameters buy some fit, but not much. Go with whichever has the higher maximum likelihood. Class discussion — Lesson 2 click to type; saves automatically Check 8 At what sample size does BIC start penalising parameters more heavily than AIC, and why? Show answer From n = 8. BIC charges ln(n) per parameter and AIC charges 2; ln(n) passes 2 when n passes e² ≈ 7.39."},{"id":"l3","h":"Using regression","t":"Lesson 3 Using regression For questions about how a system works, the coefficients are the answer. For forecasts, the predicted responses are. The same fitted equation serves both. Notation Symbol Meaning n Number of data points m Number of predictors yᵢ Response of the i-th data point xᵢⱼ Value of the j-th predictor for data point i a₀ The constant (intercept) a₁ … aₘ The coefficients, one per predictor, chosen so the model best fits the data ŷᵢ = a₀ + a₁xᵢ₁ + a₂xᵢ₂ + … + aₘxᵢₘ The fitted equation. Every use of regression in this lesson reads something off it. Check 9 In xᵢⱼ, which index runs over data points and which over predictors? Show answer i runs over data points (1 to n); j runs over predictors (1 to m). So x₄₂ is the second predictor’s value for the fourth data point. Describing how a system works: read the coefficients Suppose a retailer compiles, for many store-weeks, the number of units sold (the response) and predictors such as price, advertising spend, number of promotions running and whether the week contained a holiday. After fitting, there is one coefficient per predictor. Each coefficient says how much the predicted response changes when that predictor goes up by one unit, with the others unchanged. If the advertising coefficient comes out at 40 with spend measured in thousands of dollars, then every extra thousand dollars of advertising is associated with about 40 more units sold, on average. Coefficient aⱼ (descriptive reading) The change in the predicted response for a one-unit increase in predictor j, holding the other predictors fixed. Its units are “response units per predictor unit”. quick check answered A regression of monthly electricity use (kWh) on floor area (square metres) and occupants gives a floor-area coefficient of 0.8. What does 0.8 mean? 80% of the variation in electricity use is explained by floor area. Floor area accounts for 0.8 of each home’s electricity use. Each extra square metre is associated with about 0.8 kWh more per month, with the number of occupants held the same. Check 10 Why does “holding the other predictors fixed” matter when you read a coefficient? Show answer Because each coefficient is estimated alongside the others. The advertising coefficient answers “what if spend rises and price, promotions and holidays stay as they were?” — which can differ a lot from what happens when advertising tends to rise together with promotions. That caution is also the bridge to Lesson 4: a coefficient describes an association, not automatically a cause. Predicting what will happen: read the responses Suppose instead you want to predict the selling price of a used car. Compile data on many past sales: the response is the sale price, and the predictors might be age, mileage, engine size and a condition rating. Fit the model and you again have one coefficient per predictor. Now take a new car that hasn’t sold. You know its predictors but not its response. Plug the predictors into the fitted equation and the output ŷ is the predicted selling price. Here the coefficients are only the means to an end; the number you act on is the prediction. quick check answered Which of these is not something regression directly answers? How much does an extra year of warranty raise the resale price of an appliance? What will this applicant’s credit-card balance be in six months? What price should the store charge next week to maximise profit? Check 11 Descriptive, predictive and prescriptive analytics: which does regression cover directly, and why is it so widely used? Show answer It covers descriptive (via the coefficients) and predictive (via the fitted responses) — two of the three. Only prescriptive questions fall outside it, which is why regression is one of the most commonly used tools in analytics. Class discussion — Lesson 3 click to type; saves automatically"},{"id":"l4","h":"Causation vs. correlation","t":"Lesson 4 Causation vs. correlation A regression that predicts well shows that two things move together. Whether one drives the other is a separate question that the fit itself cannot settle. The two words Causation One thing causes another. Correlation Two things tend to happen, or not happen, together — but neither of them need cause the other. Run the regression backwards Take a dataset of daily rainfall and umbrella sales across many towns. Regress umbrella sales on rainfall and the coefficient is strongly significant: wetter days sell more umbrellas. Saying “because it rained, people bought umbrellas” is reasonable. Now swap the roles. Regress rainfall on umbrella sales, using the same data. The p-value and the model’s quality measures come out exactly the same . Does that show that buying umbrellas makes it rain? Obviously not — yet the model is just as good at prediction: tell me one town sold far more umbrellas than another today and I can predict it was the wetter one. y = a₀ + a₁x₁ and x₁ = b₀ + b₁y Both directions of a simple regression on the same data have identical p-values and fit quality. The model’s statistics cannot tell you which way the arrow points. Check 12 If a regression’s quality metrics are identical in both directions, what information must decide which direction is causal? Show answer Knowledge from outside the model — which came first, whether a mechanism makes sense, and whether some other factor could be driving both. The data alone, fitted this way, is symmetric. The third factor Sometimes neither variable causes the other, and both are driven by something else. Weekly ice-cream sales and weekly drownings rise and fall together; a regression would predict one from the other quite well. Neither causes the other: hot weather sends more people to buy ice cream and more people into the water. Outside factor (confounder) A factor that influences both the predictor and the response, producing a correlation between them even when there is no causal link between the two. match the pairs matched Click an observed correlation, then the explanation that best fits it. Observed correlation Towns with more fire engines at a blaze see more damage People who carry an asthma inhaler have more asthma attacks A country’s honey production tracks its number of chess grandmasters over a decade Fields given more fertiliser, in a randomised trial, yield more grain Most likely explanation An outside factor drives both The arrow runs the other way Coincidence between two trending series A genuine cause Check 13 Why is “no outside factor” the hardest of the causal rules of thumb to satisfy? Show answer Because it requires thinking of every possible external factor, and no one can do that with certainty. The other two — cause before effect, and the idea making sense — can be checked directly. So when can you claim causation? There is a fair amount of philosophical thought on this, including the view that proving causation is theoretically impossible. Fortunately absolute proof isn’t needed. People draw causal conclusions from rules of thumb all the time and are often right. Most rules of thumb boil down to three conditions: The cause comes before the effect. The idea of causation makes sense. There is a plausible mechanism. No outside factor could be causing the relationship. The hardest to ensure. Randomised experiments and controls help with the third condition, but you may still miss something — which is why so many studies, especially of human behaviour, are controversial. It is usually not hard to find a possible outside factor that might be part of the cause. None of this means models can’t suggest causation. Coming up with insightful causal relationships is an important part of analytics. It means being careful, and thinking through every possibility you can, before claiming that a model shows causation. PR Priya Raman Analyst, regional school district Priya regresses end-of-year maths scores on hours of after-school tutoring, across two thousand students. The tutoring coefficient is negative and highly significant: every extra ten hours is associated with a lower score. A board member: “So tutoring is hurting them. Let’s cut the programme and save the money.” What should Priya say first? Agree — the p-value is tiny, so the effect is real. because their scores are low, so low scores come first. The arrow in the data may run from grades to tutoring, not from tutoring to grades.\"> Ask which came first: are students tutored because they are struggling? Re-run the regression with scores predicting tutoring hours to see which direction is stronger. The board still wants an answer. Is the tutoring worth paying for? Add last year’s score as a predictor and report the new tutoring coefficient as the effect of tutoring. Propose a randomised rollout among eligible students, and describe the current result as correlation only. quick check answered A model predicts a patient’s recovery time very accurately from the number of follow-up appointments they attend. What does the accuracy establish? That follow-up appointments speed recovery. That the two are correlated, and can be used to predict one another — nothing about which causes which. That the correlation is spurious and the model should be discarded. Class discussion — Lesson 4 click to type; saves automatically Check 14 State the three rules of thumb for inferring causation from a correlation. Show answer The cause comes before the effect. The causal idea makes sense. No outside factor could be causing the relationship."},{"id":"l5","h":"Transformations and interactions","t":"Lesson 5 Transformations and interactions “Linear” regression only has to be linear in its coefficients. Squares, logs and products of the attributes all go in as new columns of data, and the fitting works exactly as before. When the data isn’t a straight line In the basic formula each attribute appears linearly: its value xⱼ is multiplied by a coefficient aⱼ. That works when the data looks linear. When it curves, a straight line fits badly, and a curve fits much closer. Left: a straight line misses the bend at both ends and in the middle; a quadratic term follows it. Right: when one attribute changes how strongly the other matters, the lines are not parallel — the product term is what lets the slopes differ. So transform the data to make the fit linear. If the data looks like y = x², include a quadratic term . Doing so simply creates a new column of data, x₁², and the regression treats it like any other attribute. y = a₀ + a₁x₁ + a₂x₁² Still linear regression: the equation is linear in a₀, a₁ and a₂, even though it is curved in x₁. Any other function of the attributes can be used the same way, if it fits the data — a log, a square root, a reciprocal. Check 15 Why is y = a₀ + a₁x₁ + a₂x₁² still “linear” regression? Show answer “Linear” refers to the coefficients, not the attributes. x₁² is just another column of numbers; the response is still a₀ plus a sum of coefficients times columns, so the same least-squares fitting applies unchanged. Transforming the response The response can be transformed too — for example, regress the log of the response instead of the response itself — or both the attributes and the response can be transformed together. Box-Cox transformation A family of power transformations of the response, indexed by a single parameter λ, that most statistical software can choose automatically. y(λ) = (y λ − 1) / λ for λ ≠ 0 , y(0) = ln y The standard Box-Cox form (the lecture names the method without writing it out). λ = 1 leaves the shape unchanged, λ = 0.5 is a square-root transform, λ = 0 a log. It needs y > 0. quick check answered Plotting delivery cost against distance, the points rise steeply at first and then level off. Which change to the model is the natural first try? Add distance² with a positive coefficient. Use ln(distance) as the predictor instead of distance. Add more predictors so the straight line fits better. Check 16 Name three things you can transform in a regression. Show answer An attribute (for example, add x² or use ln x); the response (for example, regress ln y); or both at once. Box-Cox is a family of response transformations that software can fit for you. Interactions Sometimes attributes interact. Take crop yield as a function of irrigation and fertiliser. Yield may depend on each separately, and also on their product : fertiliser may help far more on a well-watered field than on a dry one. The individual terms alone can’t capture that; an interaction term can. y = a₀ + a₁x₁ + a₂x₂ + a₃(x₁x₂) The interaction term x₁x₂ becomes a new column of data and is treated like any other attribute. It can be transformed too. Interaction term A term built from two or more attributes together — typically their product — that lets the effect of one attribute depend on the value of another. Once transformations and interaction terms are included as columns, software finds the best-fit coefficients in exactly the same way as before. quick check answered In y = a₀ + a₁x₁ + a₂x₂ + a₃x₁x₂, by how much does the predicted y change when x₁ rises by one unit? By a₁, always. By a₁ + a₃. By a₁ + a₃x₂ — it depends on the value of x₂. put in order solved Put the steps for adding a transformed term to a regression in order. Plot the response against the attribute and see that the relationship curves Choose a function of the attribute that matches the shape — a square, a log, a product with another attribute Compute that function for every data point, creating a new column Refit the regression with the new column included like any other attribute Compare the new model with the old one — for instance on AIC or BIC — before keeping the term Check order Class discussion — Lesson 5 click to type; saves automatically Check 17 In a model with an interaction, why can’t you read a₁ on its own as “the effect of x₁”? Show answer Because x₁’s effect is a₁ + a₃x₂, which changes with x₂. a₁ alone is the effect of x₁ only at x₂ = 0 — a value that may not even occur in the data."},{"id":"l6","h":"Reading regression output","t":"Lesson 6 Reading regression output Fitting is not the end. The output tells you which attributes are really significant, whether they matter in practice, and how much of the variation the model accounts for — if you know how far to trust each number. What a standard output looks like A delivery firm regresses delivery time in minutes on trip distance, number of stops and a weekend indicator, using 100 past deliveries. The printed summary looks like the one below; every package lays it out in roughly this way. Six areas are clickable. Open all six to finish this activity. Check 18 Four numbers on each coefficient row carry overlapping information. Which three are really the same test? Show answer The p-value, the t-statistic (coefficient ÷ standard error) and the 95% confidence interval. All three ask how far the estimate sits from zero relative to its uncertainty. The estimate itself answers a different question: how big the effect is. p-values p-value (of a coefficient) The result of a hypothesis test on whether the coefficient could really be zero. Small p-values say the attribute very probably matters; large ones say the data are consistent with no effect. The common rule of thumb: if a coefficient’s p-value is greater than 0.05 , remove that attribute from the model. Other thresholds can be used, depending on how conservative you want to be: Threshold Effect higher (e.g. 0.10) More attributes kept; more likely an irrelevant one ends up in the model. lower (e.g. 0.01) Fewer attributes kept; more risk of leaving out a relevant one. For most attributes the decision isn’t close. p-values tend to be very low — 0.001 or less, so the attribute is almost certainly important — or very high, 0.3 or more. Lecture vs standard practice — what a p-value is Lecture: the p-value estimates the probability that the coefficient really might be zero. Standard definition: assuming the true coefficient is zero, the p-value is the probability of getting an estimate at least as far from zero as the one observed. It is a statement about the data given “no effect”, not the probability that “no effect” is true. The practical rule — small p means keep, large p means doubtful — is the same under either reading, but on a written exam, give the standard one. t = aⱼ / SE(aⱼ) · 95% CI ≈ aⱼ ± 2·SE(aⱼ) The t-statistic and the approximate 95% confidence interval, both built from the estimate and its standard error. (The exact multiplier comes from the t distribution and is about 1.96–2 for moderately large samples.) Check 19 Lowering the p-value threshold from 0.05 to 0.01: which mistake becomes more likely, and which less? Show answer Keeping an irrelevant attribute becomes less likely; leaving out a relevant one becomes more likely. A threshold trades one error against the other. Two warnings about p-values 1 · With a great deal of data, p-values get small. They can become tiny even for attributes that have no meaningful relationship to the response, so with very large datasets be careful about relying on them. 2 · They are only probabilities, so some will be wrong. Test many attributes and some that look significant will not be. If you run a hundred tests on attributes that truly have no effect, a 0.02 threshold will let about two of them through by chance. When the findings are acted on — people change what they eat, what they buy, what they prescribe — those false positives have real consequences. Lecture vs standard practice — the two warnings, stated exactly Large data. The lecture says p-values shrink even when an attribute is “not at all related” to the response. Strictly, if the true effect is exactly zero, p-values do not drift toward zero as data grows. What large samples do is make a tiny but non-zero effect highly significant — statistically real, practically meaningless. The lecture’s advice (don’t lean on p-values with huge data; look at effect size) is standard. Many tests. The lecture frames it as “each attribute with p = 0.02 has a 2% chance of not being significant, so about two in a hundred are irrelevant.” The standard framing: 2% is the false-positive rate among attributes whose true effect is zero, so the expected number of spurious findings depends on how many of the tested attributes are truly null. The conclusion — test many things and some “discoveries” will be noise — is exactly the standard multiple-comparisons problem. Corrections for it are in Extra 2. quick check answered A model of household water use, fitted on 3 million homes, gives a coefficient for “house painted light colour” of 0.02 litres per day, p < 0.0001. What should you conclude? Paint colour is an important driver of water use — the p-value is tiny. It is probably a real but negligible effect; with this much data the p-value alone is not a guide to importance. The p-value must be wrong because the effect is so small. Check 20 Why can the number of attributes you test turn a sound analysis into a misleading one? Show answer Each test has some chance of a false positive. Across many tests those chances add up, so a list of “significant” attributes will contain some that are pure noise — and you can’t tell from the p-values which ones. Look at the coefficient itself Even with a very low p-value, a coefficient multiplied by a realistic range of its attribute may make almost no difference. Suppose a model of house sale prices, in the hundreds of thousands of dollars, gives a coefficient of $15 per electrical outlet. Across the realistic range — say 10 to 40 outlets — that moves the prediction by under $500. Not meaningful at that scale, however small its p-value. Practical significance Whether an effect is large enough to matter, judged by coefficient × a realistic change in the attribute, compared with the size of the response. Distinct from statistical significance, which is what the p-value measures. How much the model explains: R² R² (coefficient of determination) An estimate of how much of the variability in the response the model accounts for. R² = 0.59 means about 59% is accounted for; the remaining 41% is randomness or factors the model doesn’t capture. Adjusted R² R² adjusted for the number of attributes used. Plain R² can only rise when attributes are added; the adjusted version can fall if an attribute doesn’t pull its weight. R² = 1 − SSE / SST , SST = Σᵢ (yᵢ − ȳ)² Standard definition: one minus the unexplained share of the total variation around the mean. adjusted R² = 1 − (1 − R²) · (n − 1) / (n − m − 1) n data points, m attributes. The factor (n − 1)/(n − m − 1) grows with m, so extra attributes are charged for. Keep expectations realistic. Textbook exercises often come with clean datasets and R² of 90% or more; real systems rarely allow that, because so much affects them. Where humans are involved — social science, behavioural analytics — R² of 0.3 or 0.4 can be quite good. quick check answered Adding a fifth attribute raises R² from 0.412 to 0.415 and lowers adjusted R² from 0.401 to 0.399. What does that say? The attribute helps — R² went up. The attribute adds less than its cost in complexity; adjusted R² falling is the signal. The model is bad because R² is below 0.5. Check 21 A model predicting whether customers renew a subscription has R² = 0.35. Is that a poor model? Show answer Not necessarily. Renewal is human behaviour, driven by many things no dataset records. R² of 0.3–0.4 is often quite good in that setting. Judge it against what is achievable for the problem, and against simpler models, not against textbook 90%s. TK Tomás Kerr Pricing analyst, online grocer Tomás fits basket value on 40 customer attributes, using 8 million orders. Every one of the 40 p-values is below 0.001. R² is 0.22. His manager: “Forty significant drivers — let’s build a campaign around each of them.” What should Tomás look at next? Lower the p-value threshold to 0.0001 and keep whatever survives. The size of each coefficient times a realistic change in its attribute. Add more attributes until R² passes 0.5, because 0.22 means the model is useless. The manager asks for the recommendation. Recommend the handful of attributes with meaningful effects, and say that these are associations, not proven causes. Recommend all forty, since each passed the significance test. quick check answered A coefficient’s 95% confidence interval runs from −0.8 to 3.1. What is the best reading? The coefficient may be zero; its p-value will be above 0.05, and the estimate is imprecise. The coefficient is significantly positive, since most of the interval is above zero. The attribute only varies between −0.8 and 3.1 in the data. Class discussion — Lesson 6 click to type; saves automatically Check 22 List the four things on a coefficient row you can use to judge an attribute, and the one extra check that the p-value can’t do for you. Show answer The p-value, the 95% confidence interval, the t-statistic, and the coefficient itself. The extra check: multiply the coefficient by a realistic change in the attribute and compare with the size of the response. That tells you whether a significant effect is big enough to matter."},{"id":"targets","h":"Transfer practice — Basic regression","t":"Transfer practice — Basic regression Same ideas, unfamiliar settings Why these are here Each prompt below moves the module’s ideas into a situation the lessons never mentioned. If you can only answer a question in the wording it was taught in, what you have memorised is the example rather than the concept — and an exam that changes the setting will find that out. Answer these out loud before opening them. Exam target Across a hospital’s patients, length of stay rises strongly with the number of prescriptions written, p < 0.001. An administrator proposes capping prescriptions to shorten stays. What is wrong with the reasoning? Show answer It reads a correlation as a cause. Severity of illness is an obvious outside factor: sicker patients both stay longer and receive more prescriptions. The arrow may also partly run the other way — a longer stay gives more occasions to prescribe. A small p-value says the association is unlikely to be chance; it says nothing about which of these stories is true. Capping prescriptions could lengthen stays. Exam target Plant growth rises with watering up to a point and falls when plants are overwatered. A straight-line regression of growth on water gives a slope of nearly zero, p = 0.6. Is watering irrelevant? Show answer No. The relationship is strong but curved: the rising and falling halves cancel in a straight-line fit, so the linear coefficient is near zero. Add a squared term (water²) as a new column. Expect a positive coefficient on water and a negative one on water², giving a hump; the p-values and fit should change dramatically. A large p-value on a linear term means “no linear relationship”, not “no relationship”. Exam target Two models of server response time, fitted to the same logs, have AIC 1,204 and 1,210. How much more likely is the first to be the better model? Show answer exp((1,204 − 1,210)/2) = e −3 ≈ 0.05. The second model is about 5% as likely as the first to be the better one — equivalently, the first is about twenty times as likely. Only the difference matters; the size of the AIC values themselves carries no meaning. Exam target A startup has 12 months of revenue data and a model with 9 parameters. Which of AIC, AICc and BIC would you trust to compare it with a 3-parameter model? Show answer AICc is the appropriate one of the three, and it will be damning: its correction 2k(k+1)/(n−k−1) is 2·9·10/2 = 90 for the large model, against 2·3·4/8 = 3 for the small one. Plain AIC assumes plenty of data and BIC is a large-sample approximation, so neither is reliable here. The deeper point: 12 observations cannot support 9 parameters at all, and the large model is almost certainly fitting random effects. Exam target A price model for 45 wines uses 40 descriptive attributes and reports R² = 0.96. Adjusted R² is 0.56. Explain the gap. Show answer With 40 attributes and only 45 data points, the model has almost one coefficient per observation, so it can follow the data — including its randomness — nearly perfectly. R² can only rise as attributes are added. Adjusted R² = 1 − (1 − 0.96)·44/4 = 0.56 charges for each one. The gap is the signature of overfitting; the 0.96 will not survive new wines. Exam target A study tests 200 genetic markers for association with a condition, one regression each, at the 0.05 level. Fourteen come out significant. How excited should anyone be? Show answer Not very. If none of the 200 markers had any real association, about 200 × 0.05 = 10 would still pass by chance. Fourteen is only modestly more than that, so most of the list may be noise, and the p-values can’t say which entries are real. A multiple-testing correction (Extra 2) or an independent replication is needed before acting on any of them. Exam target An online shop fits spend = a₀ + a₁·discount + a₂·member + a₃·(discount × member), where member is 1 for loyalty-scheme members and 0 otherwise. The fit gives a₁ = 3 and a₃ = 4. What does each extra unit of discount do? Show answer For non-members (member = 0) the discount effect is a₁ = 3 per unit. For members it is a₁ + a₃ = 7 per unit. The interaction term lets the discount’s effect differ between the two groups; without it, the model would force one common slope and report something in between that is right for neither."},{"id":"wrap","h":"How the module fits together","t":"How the module fits together The through-line across all six lessons Lesson 1 Defines the model — a line or hyperplane — and the fit criterion: the smallest sum of squared errors. Lesson 2 Justifies that criterion — least squares is maximum likelihood under normal, independent errors — and adds AIC and BIC to charge for complexity. Lesson 3 Puts the fitted equation to work: coefficients answer how a system works, predictions answer what will happen. Lesson 4 Limits what the coefficients can claim. A good fit shows correlation; causation needs time order, sense, and no outside factor. Lesson 5 Widens the model: squares, logs, Box-Cox and products go in as new columns, and the same fitting applies. Lesson 6 Reads the result: p-values, intervals, t-statistics and coefficient sizes for each attribute; R² and adjusted R² for the whole model. Four things worth noticing Complexity is charged three times over. AIC and BIC in Lesson 2, adjusted R² in Lesson 6, and the p-value threshold in Lesson 6 all exist to stop a model keeping parameters that only fit noise. Every quality number is symmetric about direction. Lesson 4’s reverse regression has the same p-value and R² as the forward one — none of Lesson 6’s output can tell you which way cause runs. “Linear” is about coefficients. Lesson 5’s transformations are why a method that draws straight lines can still fit curves and interactions. Significance and importance are different questions. A p-value asks whether an effect might be zero; the coefficient times a realistic change asks whether it matters. Large datasets pull the two apart. Likely exam targets Write the regression equation and the sum of squared errors, and say what is minimised over. Explain why least squares is the maximum likelihood fit, and which assumptions that needs. State AIC, AICc and BIC; say which penalises parameters most and when to prefer each. Convert an AIC difference into a relative likelihood. Interpret a coefficient in units; distinguish descriptive from predictive use. Give the three rules of thumb for causation and an example of an outside factor. Explain why a model with x² or x₁x₂ is still linear regression; interpret an interaction. Read an output table: p-values, confidence intervals, t, R², adjusted R² — and the two warnings about p-values. Everything above is class material. Additional resources follow — the assumptions behind least squares and how to check them, careful inference, practice problems and reading — none of it taught in this module. Module 8 — my own summary click to type; saves automatically"},{"id":"extra","h":"Additional resources","t":"Additional resources Everything below this line is not class material Read this first The six lessons above are the module as taught: the regression model and its fit, likelihood and the information criteria, what coefficients and predictions answer, the limits of causal claims, transformations and interactions, and how to read the output. Everything from here on was assembled from outside sources — textbooks, method papers and the documentation of the standard libraries. None of it is examinable unless your instructor says so, and where a convention differs from the lectures, the lectures win . The lectures show how to fit and read a regression but leave its assumptions mostly implicit, and they mention hypothesis tests and multiple-testing risk without the machinery for handling them. The appendix fills in both, then provides practice. Extra 1 What least squares assumes, how to check it from the residuals, and the standard fixes. Extra 2 Inference done carefully: p-values, multiple-testing corrections, interval types, and the likelihood ratio test. Extra 3 Practice problems with worked solutions, and where to read further."},{"id":"x1","h":"What least squares assumes, and how to check it","t":"Extra 1 · not class material What least squares assumes, and how to check it The coefficients come out whatever the data look like. Whether the p-values, intervals and R² mean what Lesson 6 says depends on assumptions you can check in the residuals. The assumptions, and what breaks without each Assumption What fails if it doesn’t hold Linearity The mean response really is a₀ + Σaⱼxⱼ (after any transformations). If not, coefficients describe the wrong shape and predictions are biased in systematic places. Independence Errors don’t influence one another. Time-ordered or clustered data (repeat customers, the same patient measured weekly) break this, and standard errors come out too small — p-values too optimistic. Constant variance Errors have the same spread everywhere (homoscedasticity). A fan shape — spread growing with the fitted value — also makes the standard errors wrong. Normal errors Needed for exact p-values and intervals in small samples, and for Lesson 2’s maximum-likelihood argument. With large n the coefficient tests are fairly robust to non-normality; prediction intervals are not. No perfect collinearity No predictor is an exact combination of others. If one is, the coefficients can’t be separated at all; if nearly so, they become unstable. The one plot to always make Plot residuals against fitted values. What you want is a shapeless horizontal band around zero. A curve means a missing transformation (Lesson 5); a fan means non-constant variance, often fixed by logging the response; a few far-off points are candidates for Module 5’s outlier questions. A normal Q–Q plot of the residuals checks the normality assumption. quick check answered A residuals-versus-fitted plot shows residuals tightly packed for small fitted values and widely spread for large ones. What is the usual first move? Delete the points with large fitted values. Transform the response — a log is the common choice — and re-check the plot. Add a squared term for every predictor. Worth noticing Which assumptions affect the coefficients themselves, and which only the uncertainty around them? Show answer Linearity affects the coefficients: fit the wrong shape and they are wrong. Independence, constant variance and normality mainly affect the standard errors — and so the p-values and intervals — while leaving the least-squares estimates themselves unbiased. The closed-form solution Lesson 1 sets the partial derivatives of the SSE to zero and solves. Written in matrices, with X the n × (m+1) data matrix whose first column is all ones, the solution is â = (XᵀX)⁻¹ Xᵀy The normal equations. Software rarely inverts XᵀX directly — it uses a QR decomposition for numerical stability — but the answer is the same. a₁ = Σ(xᵢ − x̄)(yᵢ − ȳ) / Σ(xᵢ − x̄)² , a₀ = ȳ − a₁x̄ The one-predictor case. The fitted line always passes through the point of means (x̄, ȳ). Influence, collinearity and categories Leverage and Cook’s distance Leverage measures how unusual a point’s predictor values are; Cook’s distance measures how much the fitted coefficients change when the point is removed. A point with high leverage and a large residual can move the whole line. Common flags are Cook’s D above 4/n (screening) or above 1 (serious). Variance inflation factor (VIF) VIFⱼ = 1 / (1 − R²ⱼ), where R²ⱼ comes from regressing predictor j on all the other predictors. It says how much collinearity inflates that coefficient’s variance. Rules of thumb treat values above 5 or 10 as a problem. Dummy (indicator) variables A categorical predictor with c levels enters as c − 1 columns of 0/1, one level left out as the reference. Each coefficient is that level’s difference from the reference. Including all c columns alongside the intercept is perfect collinearity. The hierarchy principle If an interaction x₁x₂ or a power x₁² is in the model, keep the main effect x₁ (and x₂) too, even if its own p-value is large. Removing it forces the effect through zero at an arbitrary point. Centering predictors before forming products makes the main effects interpretable and reduces collinearity with the product. quick check answered Two predictors — home floor area and number of rooms — have a VIF of 12 each. The model’s R² is high but neither coefficient is significant. What is happening? They are highly collinear, so the model can’t separate their effects even though together they explain a lot. Neither predictor matters and both should be removed. The sample is too large, making p-values unreliable. quick check answered A “region” predictor has four values: North, South, East, West. How should it enter a regression with an intercept? As one column coded 1 to 4. As four 0/1 columns, one per region. As three 0/1 columns, with the fourth region as the reference. Notes — assumptions click to type; saves automatically Worth noticing Why can collinearity leave predictions fine while ruining interpretation? Show answer Prediction needs only the combined effect of the correlated predictors, which is estimated well. Interpretation needs each one’s separate effect, which the data can’t pin down. Lesson 3’s two uses of regression react very differently to the same problem."},{"id":"x2","h":"Inference done carefully","t":"Extra 2 · not class material Inference done carefully What a p-value can and can’t say, how to correct for testing many things, which interval to report, and when a likelihood ratio test is valid. What p-values are not The American Statistical Association’s 2016 statement on p-values (Wasserstein & Lazar) sets out the standard cautions. A p-value is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true; it does not measure the size or importance of an effect; and a scientific conclusion should not rest on whether it crosses a threshold. Report estimates and intervals alongside it. Multiple testing Bonferroni: test each of M hypotheses at α / M Controls the chance of even one false positive across the whole family. Simple and very conservative. Benjamini–Hochberg: sort p₍₁₎ ≤ … ≤ p₍M₎ ; find the largest k with p₍k₎ ≤ (k/M)·q ; reject hypotheses 1 … k Controls the false discovery rate — the expected share of false positives among the discoveries — at q. Much less conservative when many effects are real. Stepwise selection and p-values Lesson 6’s rule of thumb — drop attributes with p > 0.05 — is sometimes automated as backward elimination. Two standard cautions. Removing one attribute changes the others’ p-values, especially under collinearity, so drop one at a time and refit. And the p-values of the final model no longer mean what they claim, because the model was chosen by looking at them. Validation on held-out data (Module 3) or penalised methods such as the lasso are the usual alternatives. quick check answered You test 50 predictors and want at most a 5% chance of any false positive across all of them. What threshold does Bonferroni give each test? 0.05 0.001 2.5 Worth noticing When would you prefer Benjamini–Hochberg to Bonferroni? Show answer When testing many hypotheses as a screen, where some false leads are acceptable as long as most discoveries are real — gene screens, feature screening. Bonferroni suits a small number of tests where any single false positive is costly. Confidence interval vs prediction interval Around a predicted value there are two intervals. The confidence interval covers the average response at those predictor values; it narrows as n grows. The prediction interval covers a single new observation, so it adds the error variance σ² and never shrinks below it however much data you have. For a forecast of one case — one house, one delivery — the prediction interval is the honest one. The likelihood ratio test LR = 2 · ( ln L* big − ln L* small ) ~ χ² with (k big − k small ) degrees of freedom Valid when the smaller model is nested in the bigger one — obtainable by setting some of its parameters to zero — and the sample is reasonably large. Non-nested models are compared with AIC or BIC instead. Two facts about direction and scale Reverse regression is not the inverse line. In Lesson 4’s swap, the two slopes satisfy a₁ · b₁ = r², the squared correlation. Unless the fit is perfect, b₁ is not 1/a₁: each regression pulls toward its own mean. Reading logged models. With ln y as the response, a coefficient a means each unit of x multiplies y by eᵃ — roughly a 100·a% change when a is small. With both sides logged, the coefficient is an elasticity: a 1% change in x goes with about an a% change in y. quick check answered A model of ln(salary) gives a coefficient of 0.04 on years of experience. What does it say? Each year adds 0.04 currency units to salary. Each year is associated with about 4% higher salary. A 1% increase in experience gives a 0.04% increase in salary. quick check answered You want to test whether adding three interaction terms improves a model significantly. Which comparison is valid? A likelihood ratio test with 3 degrees of freedom, since the smaller model is nested in the larger. Compare raw R²: if it rises, keep the terms. Keep the terms if any one of them has p < 0.05. Notes — inference click to type; saves automatically Worth noticing Why does a prediction interval never collapse to zero width, even with unlimited data? Show answer More data pins down the line — the average — ever more precisely. But a single new case still scatters around that line with the error variance σ², and no amount of data removes that. The interval’s floor is the irreducible noise."},{"id":"x3","h":"Practice, reading and tools","t":"Extra 3 · not class material Practice, reading and tools Six problems with worked solutions, a self-test, and where to go next. Part A — fitting and reading Problem 1 — a fit by hand Data: x = 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and y = 3, 5, 4, 8, 10. Find the least-squares line, the SSE and R². Show worked solution x̄ = 3, ȳ = 6. Σ(x − x̄)(y − ȳ) = 6 + 1 + 0 + 2 + 8 = 17; Σ(x − x̄)² = 10. a₁ = 17 / 10 = 1.7 ; a₀ = 6 − 1.7·3 = 0.9 . Fitted: 2.6, 4.3, 6.0, 7.7, 9.4. Residuals: 0.4, 0.7, −2.0, 0.3, 0.6 — they sum to zero, as least-squares residuals with an intercept always do. SSE = 0.16 + 0.49 + 4 + 0.09 + 0.36 = 5.1 . SST = 9 + 1 + 4 + 4 + 16 = 34. R² = 1 − 5.1/34 = 0.85 . Adjusted R² = 1 − 0.15·4/3 = 0.80. Problem 2 — a coefficient row An estimate is 1.8 with standard error 0.6, on a large sample. Give t, an approximate 95% interval, and whether p is above or below 0.05. Show worked solution t = 1.8 / 0.6 = 3.0 . Interval ≈ 1.8 ± 2·0.6 = 0.6 to 3.0 . Zero is outside it, so p is below 0.05 (about 0.003). All three say the same thing, which is the point of Check 18. Part B — choosing between models Problem 3 — AIC, AICc and BIC disagreeing On n = 50 points, model A has k = 3 and SSE = 400; model B has k = 5 and SSE = 340. Using AIC = n·ln(SSE/n) + 2k and the analogous forms, compare them. Show worked solution AIC : A = 50·ln 8 + 6 = 109.97; B = 50·ln 6.8 + 10 = 105.85. B lower by 4.12; A is exp(−2.06) ≈ 13% as likely to be better. AICc : add 2k(k+1)/(n−k−1): A + 0.52 = 110.49; B + 1.36 = 107.21. B still lower by 3.3. BIC : replace 2k with k·ln 50 = 3.91k: A = 115.71; B = 115.41. B lower by only 0.30 — “only slightly likely” better on the rule-of-thumb table. The heavier BIC penalty nearly cancels the fit improvement. Report that the two extra parameters help modestly, and check on validation data before committing. Problem 4 — how many false positives? A team screens 200 candidate predictors, one test each at α = 0.05, and finds 14 significant. (a) How many would you expect by chance if none mattered? (b) What is the Bonferroni per-test threshold? Show worked solution (a) 200 × 0.05 = 10 . Fourteen is barely above chance. (b) 0.05 / 200 = 0.00025 . Only predictors clearing that bar would be reported under family-wise control; Benjamini–Hochberg would be the less strict alternative for a screen. Part C — transformations and interactions Problem 5 — an interaction with a 0/1 variable y = 20 + 3d + 5m + 4dm, where m is 1 for members. Give the intercept and slope in d for each group. Show worked solution Non-members (m = 0): y = 20 + 3d — intercept 20, slope 3. Members (m = 1): y = 25 + 7d — intercept 20 + 5, slope 3 + 4. A 0/1 interaction is exactly “a separate line per group”, fitted in one model. Problem 6 — a logged response ln(y) = 2.1 + 0.03x. By how much does a one-unit increase in x change y? Show worked solution It multiplies y by e 0.03 ≈ 1.0305, a 3.05% increase — close to the 3% shortcut because 0.03 is small. The absolute change depends on where you start; the percentage doesn’t. Part D — self-test self-test answered Which statement about least-squares residuals, in a model with an intercept, is always true? They are all smaller than the standard deviation of y. They add up to zero. They are normally distributed. self-test answered Adding a predictor of pure random noise to a regression will… lower R². lower AIC. raise R² slightly and usually lower adjusted R². self-test answered An observational study finds that coffee drinkers have fewer heart attacks, adjusting for age and sex. What is the strongest claim it supports? An association that persists after adjusting for age and sex. That coffee protects against heart attacks. Nothing — observational data can never show anything. Sources and further reading Source What it gives you James, Witten, Hastie & Tibshirani, An Introduction to Statistical Learning , ch. 3 Linear regression treated readably: least squares, the output table, interactions, polynomial terms, and the residual diagnostics in Extra 1. Chapter 6 covers AIC, BIC, adjusted R² and the lasso. Free PDF. Hastie, Tibshirani & Friedman, The Elements of Statistical Learning , ch. 3 and 7 The same ground at research level, including the matrix derivation and the theory behind the information criteria. Free PDF. Burnham & Anderson, Model Selection and Multimodel Inference The standard reference for AIC, AICc and relative likelihoods (“Akaike weights”). Hernán & Robins, Causal Inference: What If A careful, free book on when regression estimates can be read causally, and what confounding does to them. Akaike (1974); Schwarz (1978); Hurvich & Tsai (1989). The original AIC, BIC and AICc papers. Kass & Raftery (1995), “Bayes Factors”. Source of the BIC difference bands used in Lesson 2. Box & Cox (1964), “An analysis of transformations”. Wasserstein & Lazar (2016), “The ASA statement on p-values”. Short, and worth reading in full. Benjamini & Hochberg (1995). The false discovery rate procedure. Hill (1965), “The environment and disease: association or causation?”. The fuller list of causal criteria that Lesson 4’s three rules summarise. Tool Language For lm , summary , confint , predict R Fitting, the output table, confidence intervals, and confidence or prediction intervals for new data ( interval= ). AIC() , BIC() , anova() for nested comparisons, p.adjust() for Bonferroni and BH. MASS , car R boxcox() to choose λ; vif() and diagnostic plots. statsmodels sm.OLS Python The full output table, AIC/BIC, influence measures, prediction intervals, and multipletests for corrections. scikit-learn LinearRegression Python Fast fitting and prediction, but no p-values or standard errors — use statsmodels when you need the output table. scipy.stats.boxcox for the transformation. A twenty-minute exercise worth doing Simulate 1,000 rows where y depends on x₁ only, then add 50 columns of pure noise and fit. Count how many noise columns come out “significant” at 0.05, watch R² rise and adjusted R² fall as you add them, and compare AIC and BIC. Repeat with 1,000,000 rows and a tiny true effect on x₂. It demonstrates both of Lesson 6’s warnings more durably than any explanation. Reading log click to type; saves automatically Caveat How much of this should you take on trust? Show answer The formulas and the worked arithmetic are standard and consistent across the sources listed. The VIF, Cook’s distance and BIC-difference cut-offs are conventions rather than tests. Treat the lectures as authoritative wherever the two differ."},{"id":"foot","h":"","t":"Written by an AI model from lecture recordings. Not reviewed by any instructor or institution — verify anything you rely on against the original lectures. Examples here are generic illustrations rather than the ones used in class. The Priya Raman and Tomás Kerr cases, the delivery-time output table, the “Lecture vs standard practice” callouts, and “Four things worth noticing” are this summary’s own additions rather than lecture content. Formulas for quantities the lectures named without writing out — Box-Cox, R², adjusted R², the t-statistic and interval — are given in their standard textbook form. Everything under “Additional resources” is outside material. Extras 1 to 3 — the assumptions and diagnostics, the multiple-testing corrections, interval types, the likelihood ratio test, the practice problems and the reading list — were assembled from published sources, principally An Introduction to Statistical Learning (ch. 3 and 6), The Elements of Statistical Learning , Burnham & Anderson, and the original papers cited in Extra 3. None of it was taught in this module. All worked numbers there are computed for illustration on invented data. Check quantitative claims against a primary source before relying on them, and treat the lectures as authoritative wherever the two differ."}]}]};